span)?);\n1978:     }\n1979:     // The two reductions (kantord/toylang#140): `sum` folds `+` at the element width, `max`\n1980:     // returns `Opt` because an empty Vec has no maximum. Both are checked here rather than\n1981:     // through `builtin()`'s fixed table because the return type is the element type's, which\n1982:     // no fixed signature can express.\n1983:     if func == \"sum\" {\n1984:         return sum_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?);\n1985:     }\n1986:     if func == \"max\" {\n1987:         return max_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?);\n1988:     }\n1989:     if let Some((which, sig)) = builtin(func) {\n1990:         let param_ty = sig\n1991:             .param\n1992:             .as_ref()\n1993:             .expect(\"every builtin in the fixed table is unary\");\n1994:         let arg = expect(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?, param_ty)?;\n1995:         return Ok(Tir::new(\n1996:             sig.ret,\n1997:             Kind::Builtin {\n1998:                 which,\n1999:                 arg: Box::new(arg),\n2000:             },\n2001:         ));\n2002:     }\n2003:     let Some(sig) = ctx.sigs.get(func) else {\n2004:         // A payload constructor is ordinary application (the Q34 path), so `circle{r: 1}` lands\n2005:         // here; it resolves as a variant only after the function namespace declines.\n2006:         if let Some(owners) = ctx.variant_owners.get(func) {\n2007:             let enum_ty = sole_owner(ctx, func, owners, func_span)?.clone();\n2008:             return construct(ctx, &enum_ty, func, func_span, arg.as_deref(), None, false);\n2009:         }\n2010:         return Err(Error::new(func_span, format!(\"`{func}` is not a function\")));\n2011:     };\n2012:     // A non-`pub` definition is a helper for its own file: it may be called from where it was\n2013:     // defined, and nowhere else. `file` is the caller's file, so a `Prelude`-origin private\n2014:     // helper is refused from the program's file while remaining legal inside prelude.toy -- the\n2015:     // same privacy a `pub` prelude function already kept from calling a private helper, now\n2016:     // stated as a per-call-site rule rather than by dropping the helper (gh:166).\n2017:     if let Some((origin, is_pub)) = ctx.visibility.get(func)\n2018:         && !is_pub\n2019:         && *origin != ctx.file\n2020:     {\n2021:         return Err(Error::new(\n2022:             func_span,\n2023:             format!(\"`{func}` is not `pub`, so it can only be called from its own file\"),\n2024:         ));\n2025:     }\n2026:     let arg = call_arg(ctx, func, span, &sig.param, arg)?;\n2027:     Ok(Tir::new(\n2028:         sig.ret.clone(),\n2029:         Kind::Call {\n2030:             func: func.to_string(),\n2031:             arg,\n2032:         },\n2033:     ))\n2034: }\n2035: \n2036: /// A user function's argument against its (optionally nullary) signature: the four arity/type\n2037: /// combinations a call and a signature can be in relative to each other.\n2038: fn call_arg(\n2039:     ctx: &Ctx,\n2040:     func: &str,\n2041:     span: Span,\n2042:     param_ty: &Option<Type>,\n2043:     arg: &Option<Box<Expr>>,\n2044: ) -> Result<Option<Box<Tir>>, Error> {\n2045:     match (param_ty, arg) {\n2046:         (Some(param_ty), Some(arg)) => Ok(Some(Box::new(expect(ctx, arg, param_ty)?))),\n2047:         (None, None) => Ok(None),\n2048:         (Some(param_ty), None) => Err(Error::new(\n2049:             span,\n2050:             format!(\"`{func}` needs an argument of {param_ty}, but was called with none\"),\n2051:         )),\n2052:         (None, Some(arg)) => Err(Error::new(\n2053:             arg.span(),\n2054:             format!(\"`{func}` takes no argument\"),\n2055:         )),\n2056:     }\n2057: }\n2058: \n2059: /// Cardinality-polymorphic: the same subject-context mechanism types `select` over a Vec and\n2060: /// over a Stream, with the element drawn from either's parameter. Stream in, stream out.\n2061: fn select_call(ctx: &Ctx, arg: &Expr, span: Span) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n2062:     let Some((subject, id)) = ctx.subject.clone() else {\n2063:         return Err(Error::new(\n2064:             span,\n2065:             \"`select` needs a subject, so it must follow `|`\",\n2066:         ));\n2067:     };\n2068:     let Some(elem) = mapper_elem(&subject) else {\n2069:         return Err(Error::new(\n2070:             span,\n2071:             format!(\"`select` needs a Vec or a stream, found {subject}\"),\n2072:         ));\n2073:     };\n2074:     let param = ctx.fresh();\n2075:     let inner = mapper_ctx(ctx, elem, param);\n2076:     let pred = expect(&inner, arg, &Type::Bool)?;\n2077:     let source = Tir::new(subject.clone(), Kind::Local(id));\n2078:     Ok(Tir::new(\n2079:         subject,\n2080:         Kind::Select {\n2081:             source: Box::new(source),\n2082:             param,\n2083:             pred: Box::new(pred),\n2084:         },\n2085:     ))\n2086: }\n2087: \n2088: /// `jsonlines(x)`, the one sink builtin, typed `Sink`. A sink is not a value, so a direct call\n2089: /// survives only where `sink_call` recognized the sink position (the program's body or a\n\n(Showing lines 1950-2089 of 3239. Use offset=2090 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"    // rather than a runtime failure.\n    if func == \"tail\" {\n        return tail_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?);\n    }\n    // Flattens a Vec<Vec<T>> into a Vec<T>, the way jq's `add` flattens a list of arrays.\n    // Joining a fixed, known count of Vecs is `+` (kantord/toylang#97); this is for when the\n    // outer Vec's length is not known at the call site.\n    if func == \"flatten\" {\n        return flatten_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?);\n    }\n    // The one exit a stream has: `Stream<T> -> Vec<T>`, polymorphic over the element type like\n    // `length` and the others above, so the argument is synthesised first.\n    if func == \"collect\" {\n        return collect(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?, None);\n    }\n    // Polymorphic over which record, the same reason `length` is checked here: the return type\n    // (`Vec<Str>`) is fixed, but the argument's shape is not.\n    if func == \"fields\" {\n        return fields_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?);\n    }","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/check/mod.rs","text":"    // rather than a runtime failure.\n    if func == \"tail\" {\n        return tail_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?);\n    }\n    // Flattens a Vec<Vec<T>> into a Vec<T>, the way jq's `add` flattens a list of arrays.\n    // Joining a fixed, known count of Vecs is `+` (kantord/toylang#97); this is for when the\n    // outer Vec's length is not known at the call site.\n    if func == \"flatten\" {\n        return flatten_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?);\n    }\n    // The one exit a stream has: `Stream<T> -> Vec<T>`, polymorphic over the element type like\n    // `length` and the others above, so the argument is synthesised first.\n    if func == \"collect\" {\n        return collect(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?, None);\n    }\n    // Polymorphic over which record, the same reason `length` is checked here: the return type\n    // (`Vec<Str>`) is fixed, but the argument's shape is not.\n    if func == \"fields\" {\n        return fields_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?);\n    }\n    // Blocking, one Vec in and one Vec out with no Stream instance (kantord/toylang#86, Q20 in\n    // draft.md), so both are checked here rather than through `select`'s subject-context\n    // mechanism.\n    if func == \"sort\" {\n        return sort_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?);\n    }\n    if func == \"reverse\" {\n        return reverse_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?);\n    }\n    // The two reductions (kantord/toylang#140): `sum` folds `+` at the element width, `max`\n    // returns `Opt` because an empty Vec has no maximum. Both are checked here rather than\n    // through `builtin()`'s fixed table because the return type is the element type's, which\n    // no fixed signature can express.\n    if func == \"sum\" {\n        return sum_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?);\n    }\n    if func == \"max\" {\n        return max_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?);\n    }\n    if let Some((which, sig)) = builtin(func) {\n        let param_ty = sig\n            .param\n            .as_ref()\n            .expect(\"every builtin in the fixed table is unary\");\n        let arg = expect(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?, param_ty)?;\n        return Ok(Tir::new(\n            sig.ret,\n            Kind::Builtin {\n                which,\n                arg: Box::new(arg),\n            },\n        ));\n    }\n    let Some(sig) = ctx.sigs.get(func) else {\n        // A payload constructor is ordinary application (the Q34 path), so `circle{r: 1}` lands\n        // here; it resolves as a variant only after the function namespace declines.\n        if let Some(owners) = ctx.variant_owners.get(func) {\n            let enum_ty = sole_owner(ctx, func, owners, func_span)?.clone();\n            return construct(ctx, &enum_ty, func, func_span, arg.as_deref(), None, false);\n        }\n        return Err(Error::new(func_span, format!(\"`{func}` is not a function\")));\n    };\n    // A non-`pub` definition is a helper for its own file: it may be called from where it was\n    // defined, and nowhere else. `file` is the caller's file, so a `Prelude`-origin private\n    // helper is refused from the program's file while remaining legal inside prelude.toy -- the\n    // same privacy a `pub` prelude function already kept from calling a private helper, now\n    // stated as a per-call-site rule rather than by dropping the helper (gh:166).\n    if let Some((origin, is_pub)) = ctx.visibility.get(func)\n        && !is_pub\n        && *origin != ctx.file\n    {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            func_span,\n            format!(\"`{func}` is not `pub`, so it can only be called from its own file\"),\n        ));\n    }\n    let arg = call_arg(ctx, func, span, &sig.param, arg)?;\n    Ok(Tir::new(\n        sig.ret.clone(),\n        Kind::Call {\n            func: func.to_string(),\n            arg,\n        },\n    ))\n}\n\n/// A user function's argument against its (optionally nullary) signature: the four arity/type\n/// combinations a call and a signature can be in relative to each other.\nfn call_arg(\n    ctx: &Ctx,\n    func: &str,\n    span: Span,\n    param_ty: &Option<Type>,\n    arg: &Option<Box<Expr>>,\n) -> Result<Option<Box<Tir>>, Error> {\n    match (param_ty, arg) {\n        (Some(param_ty), Some(arg)) => Ok(Some(Box::new(expect(ctx, arg, param_ty)?))),\n        (None, None) => Ok(None),\n        (Some(param_ty), None) => Err(Error::new(\n            span,\n            format!(\"`{func}` needs an argument of {param_ty}, but was called with none\"),\n        )),\n        (None, Some(arg)) => Err(Error::new(\n            arg.span(),\n            format!(\"`{func}` takes no argument\"),\n        )),\n    }\n}\n\n/// Cardinality-polymorphic: the same subject-context mechanism types `select` over a Vec and\n/// over a Stream, with the element drawn from either's parameter. Stream in, stream out.\nfn select_call(ctx: &Ctx, arg: &Expr, span: Span) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n    let Some((subject, id)) = ctx.subject.clone() else {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            span,\n            \"`select` needs a subject, so it must follow `|`\",\n        ));\n    };\n    let Some(elem) = mapper_elem(&subject) else {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            span,\n            format!(\"`select` needs a Vec or a stream, found {subject}\"),\n        ));\n    };\n    let param = ctx.fresh();\n    let inner = mapper_ctx(ctx, elem, param);\n    let pred = expect(&inner, arg, &Type::Bool)?;\n    let source = Tir::new(subject.clone(), Kind::Local(id));\n    Ok(Tir::new(\n        subject,\n        Kind::Select {\n            source: Box::new(source),\n            param,\n            pred: Box::new(pred),\n        },\n    ))\n}\n\n/// `jsonlines(x)`, the one sink builtin, typed `Sink`. A sink is not a value, so a direct call\n/// survives only where `sink_call` recognized the sink position (the program's body or a","lineStart":1950,"lineEnd":2089,"totalLines":3239,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/check/mod.rs","time":{"start":1788464464780,"end":1788464464802}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now let me look at the sort_call and max_call in the checker, and the projection machinery for map. Let me read around line 1973.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068ca0388001cvU0eIpqm4YMyl","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","messageID":"msg_068c9fb7b001m6sQ882JMC9CrV"}}
