{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788464491084,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_753d7600a70247e5aa9a74da","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"ls docs/reference/builtins"},"output":"chars.md\ncollect.md\nfields.md\nflatten.md\ni64.md\njsonlines.md\nlength.md\nmap.md\nmax.md\nrange.md\nreverse.md\nselect.md\nsort.md\nstr.md\nsum.md\ntail.md\n","metadata":{"output":"chars.md\ncollect.md\nfields.md\nflatten.md\ni64.md\njsonlines.md\nlength.md\nmap.md\nmax.md\nrange.md\nreverse.md\nselect.md\nsort.md\nstr.md\nsum.md\ntail.md\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"ls docs/reference/builtins","time":{"start":1788464491077,"end":1788464491079}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"So calls are unary — only one argument. How do we express `sort_by(v, .key)` with two arguments? The jq pair `sort_by(.key)` has the Vec piped in via `|` and the projection as a single argument. In toylang, the pipe is used for `map`/`select` — a `Vec | map(.name)` form. So the likely spelling: `v | sort_by(.key)` — pipe the Vec in, projection as the argument. That matches how `map` and `select` work: they require a subject via `|`.\n\nBut the issue says `sort_by(.key) / max_by(.key)`. And sort itself takes `sort(v)`. Hmm. Let me think about how the projection argument would be combined with the Vec.\n\nThe map machinery: `map(.name)` requires a subject from a pipe. So `v | map(.name)`. For sort_by, we could have `v | sort_by(.key)`. The checker would take the subject as the Vec, and the argument as the projection, checked in the mapper context.\n\nBut wait — the issue example says `sort_by(.key)`. Given the language's function-call convention (single arg), could sort_by take a two-element form? Let me look at how jq-style pipes are used in existing corpus programs. Let me look at the Euler example that would use this. Let me look at the sort builtin docs again: `sort([3,1,2])`. \n\nActually, let me reconsider. jq's `sort_by(.foo)` is a filter that sorts the input array by the projection. In toylang, `sort` is a unary function taking the Vec. For sort_by, we need both the Vec and the projection. The natural toylang spellings:\n\n`v | sort_by(.key)` — pipe v in, argument is the projection. That mirrors `map`/`select` which rebind `.`. \n\nBut the issue says \"sort_by(.key) / max_by(.key) order records through a scalar projection\" — the projection in parens. And \"the map(.name) projection machinery already exists in the checker\".\n\nLet me look at how the checker handles the `synth`/`expect` for builtins to understand how `sort_by` could be checked. The subject mechanism: `select`/`map` require a subject. If `sort_by` is a Vec builtin, we want `v | sort_by(.key)` where `.` is the element for the projection.\n\nLet me look for any existing corpus tests or docs examples that might show the intended spelling. Also let me look at how docs.md tests builtin reference pages — each builtin needs a reference page (BUILTIN_NAMES). So I'll need to add docs pages for sort_by and max_by.\n\nLet me check the docs structure.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068ca6a2a0013yOw43zKZLbf3B","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","messageID":"msg_068ca4c31001KbK6LQ156W6NP8"}}
