:false},"title":"ls docs/examples/euler","time":{"start":1788464491082,"end":1788464491083}},"id":"prt_068ca6a390019AkOqNfudHHTXR","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","messageID":"msg_068ca4c31001KbK6LQ156W6NP8"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788464491087,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_f73519c302c04e9e975fbc35","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/docs/examples/euler/26-reciprocal-cycles.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/docs/examples/euler/26-reciprocal-cycles.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # The unit fraction with the longest repeating cycle\n2: \n3: Solves [Project Euler 26](https://projecteuler.net/problem=26). See the\n4: [spoiler warning](00-spoiler-warning.md).\n5: \n6: `1/d`'s repeating cycle length is the smallest `k` with `10^k mod d == 1`, once the factors of\n7: 2 and 5 are stripped out of `d` (they only lengthen the non-repeating prefix, never the cycle\n8: itself). Walking that remainder forward one digit of long division at a time is the direct\n9: way to find `k`, but a plain recursive walk could run up to `d - 1` steps deep -- as many as\n10: 998 frames for `d` just under 1000, the same ceiling [problem 17](17-number-letter-counts.md)\n11: and [problem 19](19-counting-sundays.md) worry about. `cycle_inner` walks a fixed chunk of a\n12: hundred digits and reports where it stopped; `cycle_outer` calls it chunk after chunk,\n13: carrying the remainder forward, so no single call frame goes deeper than about a hundred.\n14: \n15: ```toylang\n16: fn strip2(n: Int) -> Int = n | . % 2 == 0 -> strip2(. / 2) or .\n17: \n18: fn strip5(n: Int) -> Int = n | . % 5 == 0 -> strip5(. / 5) or .\n19: \n20: fn reduced(d: Int) -> Int = strip5(strip2(d))\n21: \n22: fn cycle_inner(p: {m: Int, r: Int, count: Int, steps_left: Int}) -> {done: Int, count: Int, r: Int} =\n23:     p\n24:         | .r == 1 -> {done: 1, count: .count, r: .r} or\n25:               .steps_left == 0 -> {done: 0, count: .count, r: .r} or\n26:               cycle_inner({m: .m, r: .r * 10 % .m, count: .count + 1, steps_left: .steps_left - 1})\n27: \n28: fn cycle_outer(p: {m: Int, r: Int, count: Int, chunks_left: Int}) -> Int =\n29:     p\n30:         | .chunks_left == 0 -> -1 or\n31:               cycle_inner({m: .m, r: .r, count: .count, steps_left: 100}).done == 1 -> cycle_inner({m: .m, r: .r, count: .count, steps_left: 100}).count or\n32:               cycle_outer({m: .m, r: cycle_inner({m: .m, r: .r, count: .count, steps_left: 100}).r, count: cycle_inner({m: .m, r: .r, count: .count, steps_left: 100}).count, chunks_left: .chunks_left - 1})\n33: \n34: fn cycle_length(d: Int) -> Int =\n35:     d\n36:         | reduced(.) == 1 -> 0 or\n37:               cycle_outer({m: reduced(.), r: 10 % reduced(.), count: 1, chunks_left: 10})\n38: \n39: fn best_of(p: {a: {d: Int, len: Int}, b: {d: Int, len: Int}}) -> {d: Int, len: Int} =\n40:     p | .a.len >= .b.len -> .a or .b\n41: \n42: fn find_best(p: {lo: Int, hi: Int}) -> {d: Int, len: Int} =\n43:     p\n44:         | .hi - .lo == 1 -> {d: .lo, len: cycle_length(.lo)} or\n45:               best_of({a: find_best({lo: .lo, hi: (.lo + .hi) / 2}), b: find_best({lo: (.lo + .hi) / 2, hi: .hi})})\n46: \n47: find_best({lo: 2, hi: 1000}).d\n48: ```\n49: \n50: ```output\n51: 983\n52: ```\n\n(End of file - total 52 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# The unit fraction with the longest repeating cycle\n\nSolves [Project Euler 26](https://projecteuler.net/problem=26). See the\n[spoiler warning](00-spoiler-warning.md).\n\n`1/d`'s repeating cycle length is the smallest `k` with `10^k mod d == 1`, once the factors of\n2 and 5 are stripped out of `d` (they only lengthen the non-repeating prefix, never the cycle\nitself). Walking that remainder forward one digit of long division at a time is the direct\nway to find `k`, but a plain recursive walk could run up to `d - 1` steps deep -- as many as\n998 frames for `d` just under 1000, the same ceiling [problem 17](17-number-letter-counts.md)\nand [problem 19](19-counting-sundays.md) worry about. `cycle_inner` walks a fixed chunk of a\nhundred digits and reports where it stopped; `cycle_outer` calls it chunk after chunk,\ncarrying the remainder forward, so no single call frame goes deeper than about a hundred.\n\n```toylang\nfn strip2(n: Int) -> Int = n | . % 2 == 0 -> strip2(. / 2) or .\n\nfn strip5(n: Int) -> Int = n | . % 5 == 0 -> strip5(. / 5) or .\n\nfn reduced(d: Int) -> Int = strip5(strip2(d))","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/docs/examples/euler/26-reciprocal-cycles.md","text":"# The unit fraction with the longest repeating cycle\n\nSolves [Project Euler 26](https://projecteuler.net/problem=26). See the\n[spoiler warning](00-spoiler-warning.md).\n\n`1/d`'s repeating cycle length is the smallest `k` with `10^k mod d == 1`, once the factors of\n2 and 5 are stripped out of `d` (they only lengthen the non-repeating prefix, never the cycle\nitself). Walking that remainder forward one digit of long division at a time is the direct\nway to find `k`, but a plain recursive walk could run up to `d - 1` steps deep -- as many as\n998 frames for `d` just under 1000, the same ceiling [problem 17](17-number-letter-counts.md)\nand [problem 19](19-counting-sundays.md) worry about. `cycle_inner` walks a fixed chunk of a\nhundred digits and reports where it stopped; `cycle_outer` calls it chunk after chunk,\ncarrying the remainder forward, so no single call frame goes deeper than about a hundred.\n\n```toylang\nfn strip2(n: Int) -> Int = n | . % 2 == 0 -> strip2(. / 2) or .\n\nfn strip5(n: Int) -> Int = n | . % 5 == 0 -> strip5(. / 5) or .\n\nfn reduced(d: Int) -> Int = strip5(strip2(d))\n\nfn cycle_inner(p: {m: Int, r: Int, count: Int, steps_left: Int}) -> {done: Int, count: Int, r: Int} =\n    p\n        | .r == 1 -> {done: 1, count: .count, r: .r} or\n              .steps_left == 0 -> {done: 0, count: .count, r: .r} or\n              cycle_inner({m: .m, r: .r * 10 % .m, count: .count + 1, steps_left: .steps_left - 1})\n\nfn cycle_outer(p: {m: Int, r: Int, count: Int, chunks_left: Int}) -> Int =\n    p\n        | .chunks_left == 0 -> -1 or\n              cycle_inner({m: .m, r: .r, count: .count, steps_left: 100}).done == 1 -> cycle_inner({m: .m, r: .r, count: .count, steps_left: 100}).count or\n              cycle_outer({m: .m, r: cycle_inner({m: .m, r: .r, count: .count, steps_left: 100}).r, count: cycle_inner({m: .m, r: .r, count: .count, steps_left: 100}).count, chunks_left: .chunks_left - 1})\n\nfn cycle_length(d: Int) -> Int =\n    d\n        | reduced(.) == 1 -> 0 or\n              cycle_outer({m: reduced(.), r: 10 % reduced(.), count: 1, chunks_left: 10})\n\nfn best_of(p: {a: {d: Int, len: Int}, b: {d: Int, len: Int}}) -> {d: Int, len: Int} =\n    p | .a.len >= .b.len -> .a or .b\n\nfn find_best(p: {lo: Int, hi: Int}) -> {d: Int, len: Int} =\n    p\n        | .hi - .lo == 1 -> {d: .lo, len: cycle_length(.lo)} or\n              best_of({a: find_best({lo: .lo, hi: (.lo + .hi) / 2}), b: find_best({lo: (.lo + .hi) / 2, hi: .hi})})\n\nfind_best({lo: 2, hi: 1000}).d\n```\n\n```output\n983\n```","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":52,"totalLines":52,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/examples/euler/26-reciprocal-cycles.md","time":{"start":1788464491074,"end":1788464491086}},"id":"prt_068ca6a40001SYEruJJ12q2TQ1","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","messageID":"msg_068ca4c31001KbK6LQ156W6NP8"}}
