{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788464504740,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_2dd5169302e54981a3367c22","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/tir.rs","limit":120,"offset":1},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/tir.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! The typed IR the backends consume.\n2: //!\n3: //! Every node carries the type it was checked at, so a backend never has to ask what a value is\n4: //! at runtime and never has to look a type up in a table beside the tree. A static target cannot\n5: //! work from anything less: it has to know that an add is an integer add before it can emit one.\n6: //!\n7: //! Names are deliberately *not* mangled here. Which identifiers are reserved is a property of\n8: //! the target, not of toylang, so each backend renders these as it needs to.\n9: \n10: use crate::ast::{BinOp, LogicOp};\n11: use crate::ty::{Enums, Type};\n12: \n13: pub struct Tir {\n14:     pub ty: Type,\n15:     pub kind: Kind,\n16: }\n17: \n18: impl Tir {\n19:     pub fn new(ty: Type, kind: Kind) -> Tir {\n20:         Tir { ty, kind }\n21:     }\n22: }\n23: \n24: /// A binding the source cannot name, introduced for `|` and for `select`'s parameter.\n25: pub type LocalId = u32;\n26: \n27: pub enum Kind {\n28:     Str(String),\n29:     Int(i64),\n30:     /// A float literal: an IEEE 754 binary64 double (ADR 0007). Like `Int`, the width lives\n31:     /// in the node's own type -- there is only one float width, so a `Float` node is always\n32:     /// the binary64 the ADR picks, and no other backend needs to consult the type to know.\n33:     Float(f64),\n34:     VecLit(Vec<Tir>),\n35:     /// A record literal, its fields in declaration order so a field's position here matches\n36:     /// its position in the type. That is what lets a backend address one by index rather than\n37:     /// searching for it.\n38:     RecordLit {\n39:         fields: Vec<(String, Tir)>,\n40:     },\n41:     /// A constructed enum value. The node's type is the enum, so a backend finds the variant's\n42:     /// position (its tag, where one is needed) and its payload type by asking `ty::variants`\n43:     /// for that type's list rather than by carrying either here. `payload` is `None` for a unit\n44:     /// variant, which every backend renders as the bare variant-name string; a payload variant\n45:     /// is the single-key wrapper (ADR 0009).\n46:     EnumLit {\n47:         variant: String,\n48:         payload: Option<Box<Tir>>,\n49:     },\n50:     /// Rebuilds an Opt: present-preserving, absent-preserving. `body` runs with `param` bound\n51:     /// to the unwrapped payload only when `source` is present; an absent `source` passes\n52:     /// through untouched. Never surface syntax -- Opt has no general map or match a program can\n53:     /// reach (kantord/toylang#47's totality round owns that) -- this is only how the checker's\n54:     /// own reorder pass reaches inside an Opt payload (kantord/toylang#66): every other enum\n55:     /// reorders through `Match`/`EnumLit`, but Opt keeps a representation of its own in three\n56:     /// backends, so those two general nodes cannot reach it.\n57:     OptMap {\n58:         source: Box<Tir>,\n59:         param: LocalId,\n60:         body: Box<Tir>,\n61:     },\n62:     /// A name written in the source: today only a function parameter.\n63:     Var(String),\n64:     Local(LocalId),\n65:     /// The value read from stdin.\n66:     Input,\n67:     /// Every remaining JSON value on stdin, one per line, eagerly collected into a `Vec<T>`.\n68:     Inputs,\n69:     /// The stream of lines read from stdin, read incrementally by whatever consumes it.\n70:     Lines,\n71:     /// Stdin read as raw lines, each split on the delimiter, born `Vec<Vec<Str>>`: the\n72:     /// parameterized DSV source. `csv`/`tsv` arrive here with the delimiter already fixed.\n73:     Dsv {\n74:         delim: String,\n75:     },\n76:     Call {\n77:         func: String,\n78:         /// `None` for a call to a nullary function.\n79:         arg: Option<Box<Tir>>,\n80:     },\n81:     Concat(Box<Tir>, Box<Tir>),\n82:     /// Wrapping integer arithmetic, at the width the node's own type names: 32 bits for `Int`,\n83:     /// 64 for `Int64` (kantord/toylang#83) -- both operands always share it, since nothing\n84:     /// converts implicitly. Division and remainder stop the program on a zero divisor, which\n85:     /// is the only way arithmetic can fail.\n86:     Arith {\n87:         op: BinOp,\n88:         lhs: Box<Tir>,\n89:         rhs: Box<Tir>,\n90:     },\n91:     Compare {\n92:         op: BinOp,\n93:         lhs: Box<Tir>,\n94:         rhs: Box<Tir>,\n95:     },\n96:     /// `and` / `or` over two Bools, short-circuiting: `rhs` is evaluated only when `lhs` leaves\n97:     /// the answer open. That is not a nicety here -- division and `!` can stop the program, so\n98:     /// whether the right side runs is observable -- which is why every backend emits its own\n99:     /// short-circuiting operator rather than two evaluated operands combined afterwards.\n100:     Logic {\n101:         op: LogicOp,\n102:         lhs: Box<Tir>,\n103:         rhs: Box<Tir>,\n104:     },\n105:     /// `not b`.\n106:     Not(Box<Tir>),\n107:     /// `let local = value in body`, which is what `|` becomes once `.` has a name.\n108:     Bind {\n109:         local: LocalId,\n110:         value: Box<Tir>,\n111:         body: Box<Tir>,\n112:     },\n113:     /// Every element replaced by `body`, with `param` bound to each. Same loop as Select, kept\n114:     /// separate because the result's element type is the body's rather than the source's.\n115:     Map {\n116:         source: Box<Tir>,\n117:         param: LocalId,\n118:         body: Box<Tir>,\n119:     },\n120:     Select {\n\n(Showing lines 1-120 of 612. Use offset=121 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! The typed IR the backends consume.\n//!\n//! Every node carries the type it was checked at, so a backend never has to ask what a value is\n//! at runtime and never has to look a type up in a table beside the tree. A static target cannot\n//! work from anything less: it has to know that an add is an integer add before it can emit one.\n//!\n//! Names are deliberately *not* mangled here. Which identifiers are reserved is a property of\n//! the target, not of toylang, so each backend renders these as it needs to.\n\nuse crate::ast::{BinOp, LogicOp};\nuse crate::ty::{Enums, Type};\n\npub struct Tir {\n    pub ty: Type,\n    pub kind: Kind,\n}\n\nimpl Tir {\n    pub fn new(ty: Type, kind: Kind) -> Tir {\n        Tir { ty, kind }","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/tir.rs","text":"//! The typed IR the backends consume.\n//!\n//! Every node carries the type it was checked at, so a backend never has to ask what a value is\n//! at runtime and never has to look a type up in a table beside the tree. A static target cannot\n//! work from anything less: it has to know that an add is an integer add before it can emit one.\n//!\n//! Names are deliberately *not* mangled here. Which identifiers are reserved is a property of\n//! the target, not of toylang, so each backend renders these as it needs to.\n\nuse crate::ast::{BinOp, LogicOp};\nuse crate::ty::{Enums, Type};\n\npub struct Tir {\n    pub ty: Type,\n    pub kind: Kind,\n}\n\nimpl Tir {\n    pub fn new(ty: Type, kind: Kind) -> Tir {\n        Tir { ty, kind }\n    }\n}\n\n/// A binding the source cannot name, introduced for `|` and for `select`'s parameter.\npub type LocalId = u32;\n\npub enum Kind {\n    Str(String),\n    Int(i64),\n    /// A float literal: an IEEE 754 binary64 double (ADR 0007). Like `Int`, the width lives\n    /// in the node's own type -- there is only one float width, so a `Float` node is always\n    /// the binary64 the ADR picks, and no other backend needs to consult the type to know.\n    Float(f64),\n    VecLit(Vec<Tir>),\n    /// A record literal, its fields in declaration order so a field's position here matches\n    /// its position in the type. That is what lets a backend address one by index rather than\n    /// searching for it.\n    RecordLit {\n        fields: Vec<(String, Tir)>,\n    },\n    /// A constructed enum value. The node's type is the enum, so a backend finds the variant's\n    /// position (its tag, where one is needed) and its payload type by asking `ty::variants`\n    /// for that type's list rather than by carrying either here. `payload` is `None` for a unit\n    /// variant, which every backend renders as the bare variant-name string; a payload variant\n    /// is the single-key wrapper (ADR 0009).\n    EnumLit {\n        variant: String,\n        payload: Option<Box<Tir>>,\n    },\n    /// Rebuilds an Opt: present-preserving, absent-preserving. `body` runs with `param` bound\n    /// to the unwrapped payload only when `source` is present; an absent `source` passes\n    /// through untouched. Never surface syntax -- Opt has no general map or match a program can\n    /// reach (kantord/toylang#47's totality round owns that) -- this is only how the checker's\n    /// own reorder pass reaches inside an Opt payload (kantord/toylang#66): every other enum\n    /// reorders through `Match`/`EnumLit`, but Opt keeps a representation of its own in three\n    /// backends, so those two general nodes cannot reach it.\n    OptMap {\n        source: Box<Tir>,\n        param: LocalId,\n        body: Box<Tir>,\n    },\n    /// A name written in the source: today only a function parameter.\n    Var(String),\n    Local(LocalId),\n    /// The value read from stdin.\n    Input,\n    /// Every remaining JSON value on stdin, one per line, eagerly collected into a `Vec<T>`.\n    Inputs,\n    /// The stream of lines read from stdin, read incrementally by whatever consumes it.\n    Lines,\n    /// Stdin read as raw lines, each split on the delimiter, born `Vec<Vec<Str>>`: the\n    /// parameterized DSV source. `csv`/`tsv` arrive here with the delimiter already fixed.\n    Dsv {\n        delim: String,\n    },\n    Call {\n        func: String,\n        /// `None` for a call to a nullary function.\n        arg: Option<Box<Tir>>,\n    },\n    Concat(Box<Tir>, Box<Tir>),\n    /// Wrapping integer arithmetic, at the width the node's own type names: 32 bits for `Int`,\n    /// 64 for `Int64` (kantord/toylang#83) -- both operands always share it, since nothing\n    /// converts implicitly. Division and remainder stop the program on a zero divisor, which\n    /// is the only way arithmetic can fail.\n    Arith {\n        op: BinOp,\n        lhs: Box<Tir>,\n        rhs: Box<Tir>,\n    },\n    Compare {\n        op: BinOp,\n        lhs: Box<Tir>,\n        rhs: Box<Tir>,\n    },\n    /// `and` / `or` over two Bools, short-circuiting: `rhs` is evaluated only when `lhs` leaves\n    /// the answer open. That is not a nicety here -- division and `!` can stop the program, so\n    /// whether the right side runs is observable -- which is why every backend emits its own\n    /// short-circuiting operator rather than two evaluated operands combined afterwards.\n    Logic {\n        op: LogicOp,\n        lhs: Box<Tir>,\n        rhs: Box<Tir>,\n    },\n    /// `not b`.\n    Not(Box<Tir>),\n    /// `let local = value in body`, which is what `|` becomes once `.` has a name.\n    Bind {\n        local: LocalId,\n        value: Box<Tir>,\n        body: Box<Tir>,\n    },\n    /// Every element replaced by `body`, with `param` bound to each. Same loop as Select, kept\n    /// separate because the result's element type is the body's rather than the source's.\n    Map {\n        source: Box<Tir>,\n        param: LocalId,\n        body: Box<Tir>,\n    },\n    Select {","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":120,"totalLines":612,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/tir.rs","time":{"start":1788464504732,"end":1788464504739}},"id":"prt_068ca9f99001wWgVoJkvd36xMc","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","messageID":"msg_068ca7e5b001YrDPdYJn8LP3To"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788464504743,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"call_a031145cad564840b81dca19","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"include":"*.rs","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/parse.rs","pattern":"Comma"},"output":"Found 19 matches\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/parse.rs:\n  Line 65:     Comma,\n\n  Line 117:             Tok::Comma => \"`,`\",\n\n  Line 217:         ',' => single(input, Tok::Comma),\n\n  Line 897:             if sep != Tok::Comma {\n\n  Line 951:                 if sep != Tok::Comma {\n\n  Line 979:                 if sep != Tok::Comma {\n\n  Line 1011:                 if sep != Tok::Comma {\n\n  Line 1046:                 if sep != Tok::Comma {\n\n  Line 1078:                         Ok(Tok::Ident(_) | Tok::Comma | Tok::Dot) => continue,\n\n  Line 1197:                 if sep != Tok::Comma {\n\n  Line 1607:                         if sep != Tok::Comma {\n\n\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/lib.rs:\n  Line 348:     let status = std::process::Command::new(\"cc\")\n\n  Line 372:     let result = std::process::Command::new(\"rustc\")\n\n  Line 399: fn run_subprocess(mut cmd: std::process::Command, label: &str, feed: &Feed) -> Result<String> {\n\n  Line 435:         std::process::Command::new(exe),\n\n  Line 475:     let mut cmd = std::process::Command::new(\"jq\");\n\n  Line 623:     let mut cmd = std::process::Command::new(\"python3\");\n\n  Line 636:     let mut cmd = std::process::Command::new(\"go\");\n\n  Line 649:     let mut cmd = std::process::Command::new(\"node\");\n","metadata":{"matches":19,"truncated":false},"title":"Comma","time":{"start":1788464504727,"end":1788464504741}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I need to figure out the exact syntax for sort_by/max_by. Since the language is unary-function-call only (single arg), and the map/select machinery requires a subject via `|`, the most natural syntax: `v | sort_by(.key)` — pipe the Vec, projection as the argument.\n\nBut wait — how would `max` work then? `max(v)`. And `max_by` needs two things: the Vec and the projection. So either `max_by(v, .key)` isn't possible (unary), or we use the pipe: `v | max_by(.key)`.\n\n\n\nActually hold on. Let me check if there's a \"two-arg\" convention somewhere. In toylang, records are the multi-argument convention (`direction({g: g, dr: 1, ...})`). But sort_by needs a Vec plus a projection. Since the Vec can be piped, the projection is the natural argument: `v | sort_by(.key)`. This mirrors jq: `.[] | sort_by(.key)`? Actually in jq it's `sort_by(.key)` directly on the input. In toylang, `map`/`select` work as `v | map(.name)`, so `v | sort_by(.key)` fits.\n\nBut actually, maybe they want it as a regular two-argument call? Let me check the parser more carefully — does the call parser handle multiple comma-separated args? Let me search for \"comma\" in parse.rs.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068ca9f9000109DndYBd7T60nn","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","messageID":"msg_068ca7e5b001YrDPdYJn8LP3To"}}
