{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788464507974,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_e66346c6c08f49fb81297964","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/docs/reference/builtins/map.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/docs/reference/builtins/map.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # map\n2: \n3: `map(expr)`: applies `expr` to every entry of its subject, yielding a `Vec` of the results\n4: (or a `Stream`, when the subject is one). The subject arrives through a pipe, and inside the\n5: body `.` is the current entry.\n6: \n7: ```toylang\n8: [1, 2, 3] | map(. * 2)\n9: ```\n10: \n11: ```output\n12: [2,4,6]\n13: ```\n14: \n15: The body is any expression, so mapping into a record literal builds one per entry. A record\n16: literal argument may drop its parentheses, the way any application of a record may:\n17: \n18: ```toylang\n19: [1, 2] | map({n: ., squared: . * .})\n20: ```\n21: \n22: ```output\n23: [{\"n\":1,\"squared\":1},{\"n\":2,\"squared\":4}]\n24: ```\n25: \n26: Over records, projections reach into the current entry:\n27: \n28: ```case\n29: map_records\n30: ```\n31: \n32: Like [`select`](select.md), `map` accepts a `Stream` subject and yields a `Stream` back,\n33: one entry at a time; and like `select`'s predicate, its body cannot read a source, since the\n34: body runs once per entry.\n\n(End of file - total 34 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# map\n\n`map(expr)`: applies `expr` to every entry of its subject, yielding a `Vec` of the results\n(or a `Stream`, when the subject is one). The subject arrives through a pipe, and inside the\nbody `.` is the current entry.\n\n```toylang\n[1, 2, 3] | map(. * 2)\n```\n\n```output\n[2,4,6]\n```\n\nThe body is any expression, so mapping into a record literal builds one per entry. A record\nliteral argument may drop its parentheses, the way any application of a record may:\n\n```toylang\n[1, 2] | map({n: ., squared: . * .})\n```","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/docs/reference/builtins/map.md","text":"# map\n\n`map(expr)`: applies `expr` to every entry of its subject, yielding a `Vec` of the results\n(or a `Stream`, when the subject is one). The subject arrives through a pipe, and inside the\nbody `.` is the current entry.\n\n```toylang\n[1, 2, 3] | map(. * 2)\n```\n\n```output\n[2,4,6]\n```\n\nThe body is any expression, so mapping into a record literal builds one per entry. A record\nliteral argument may drop its parentheses, the way any application of a record may:\n\n```toylang\n[1, 2] | map({n: ., squared: . * .})\n```\n\n```output\n[{\"n\":1,\"squared\":1},{\"n\":2,\"squared\":4}]\n```\n\nOver records, projections reach into the current entry:\n\n```case\nmap_records\n```\n\nLike [`select`](select.md), `map` accepts a `Stream` subject and yields a `Stream` back,\none entry at a time; and like `select`'s predicate, its body cannot read a source, since the\nbody runs once per entry.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":34,"totalLines":34,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/reference/builtins/map.md","time":{"start":1788464507967,"end":1788464507970}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Calls have a single argument. So sort_by's syntax needs the Vec + projection. The most natural: pipe the Vec in like map/select: `v | sort_by(.key)`. Let me check if there's any precedent in docs or corpus. Actually, let me check what jq does and how the docs reference pages are structured for map.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068caac3d001QrcmfxIXNc6pln","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","messageID":"msg_068ca9fb8001zeFBpVoKSiR8TC"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788464507980,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_af3a9e794b434642a4b605b6","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/docs/reference/builtins/max.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/docs/reference/builtins/max.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # max\n2: \n3: `max(v)`, of type `Vec<Int> -> Opt<Int>` and `Vec<Int64> -> Opt<Int64>`: the greatest of `v`'s\n4: entries (kantord/toylang#140). An empty `Vec` has no maximum, so the result is `Opt<T>` -- the\n5: same answer indexing gives to absence, and more honest than Python's `max([])` exception or jq's\n6: `add` returning null untyped.\n7: \n8: ```toylang\n9: max([3, 1, 4, 1, 5])\n10: ```\n11: \n12: ```output\n13: 5\n14: ```\n15: \n16: A maximum can be negative; the least negative is the greatest:\n17: \n18: ```toylang\n19: max([-5, -1, -3])\n20: ```\n21: \n22: ```output\n23: -1\n24: ```\n25: \n26: An empty `Vec` yields the absent `Opt`, which prints as `null`:\n27: \n28: ```toylang\n29: fn nothing() -> Vec<Int> = []\n30: \n31: max(nothing())\n32: ```\n33: \n34: ```output\n35: null\n36: ```\n37: \n38: Defined only for the two integer element types. A `Vec` of records has no total order to\n39: reduce to a maximum over, so it is refused:\n40: \n41: ```toylang\n42: max([{n: 1}])\n43: ```\n44: \n45: ```error\n46: `max` needs a Vec of Int or Int64, found Vec<{n: Int}> (at byte 4)\n47: ```\n\n(End of file - total 47 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# max\n\n`max(v)`, of type `Vec<Int> -> Opt<Int>` and `Vec<Int64> -> Opt<Int64>`: the greatest of `v`'s\nentries (kantord/toylang#140). An empty `Vec` has no maximum, so the result is `Opt<T>` -- the\nsame answer indexing gives to absence, and more honest than Python's `max([])` exception or jq's\n`add` returning null untyped.\n\n```toylang\nmax([3, 1, 4, 1, 5])\n```\n\n```output\n5\n```\n\nA maximum can be negative; the least negative is the greatest:\n\n```toylang\nmax([-5, -1, -3])\n```","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/docs/reference/builtins/max.md","text":"# max\n\n`max(v)`, of type `Vec<Int> -> Opt<Int>` and `Vec<Int64> -> Opt<Int64>`: the greatest of `v`'s\nentries (kantord/toylang#140). An empty `Vec` has no maximum, so the result is `Opt<T>` -- the\nsame answer indexing gives to absence, and more honest than Python's `max([])` exception or jq's\n`add` returning null untyped.\n\n```toylang\nmax([3, 1, 4, 1, 5])\n```\n\n```output\n5\n```\n\nA maximum can be negative; the least negative is the greatest:\n\n```toylang\nmax([-5, -1, -3])\n```\n\n```output\n-1\n```\n\nAn empty `Vec` yields the absent `Opt`, which prints as `null`:\n\n```toylang\nfn nothing() -> Vec<Int> = []\n\nmax(nothing())\n```\n\n```output\nnull\n```\n\nDefined only for the two integer element types. A `Vec` of records has no total order to\nreduce to a maximum over, so it is refused:\n\n```toylang\nmax([{n: 1}])\n```\n\n```error\n`max` needs a Vec of Int or Int64, found Vec<{n: Int}> (at byte 4)\n```","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":47,"totalLines":47,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/reference/builtins/max.md","time":{"start":1788464507972,"end":1788464507976}},"id":"prt_068caac43001z3UkknIloWFCxl","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","messageID":"msg_068ca9fb8001zeFBpVoKSiR8TC"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788464507984,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_0494c7f16dc6467dafafa79e","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/docs/reference/builtins/sum.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/docs/reference/builtins/sum.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # sum\n2: \n3: `sum(v)`, of type `Vec<Int> -> Int` and `Vec<Int64> -> Int64`: the reduction of `+` over `v`'s\n4: entries, at the element type's own width (kantord/toylang#140). An empty `Vec` sums to `0`.\n5: \n6: ```toylang\n7: sum([1, 2, 3, 4])\n8: ```\n9: \n10: ```output\n11: 10\n12: ```\n13: \n14: Each addition wraps the way the language's `+` does, so a sum that leaves the 32-bit width is\n15: the same number a hand-written fold would produce:\n16: \n17: ```toylang\n18: sum([2147483647, 1])\n19: ```\n20: \n21: ```output\n22: -2147483648\n23: ```\n24: \n25: The result keeps the element width, so a `Vec<Int64>` sums to an `Int64`:\n26: \n27: ```toylang\n28: fn wide(x: Int) -> Int64 = i64(x)\n29: \n30: sum([wide(3), wide(1), wide(2)])\n31: ```\n32: \n33: ```output\n34: 6\n35: ```\n36: \n37: Defined only for the two integer element types. A `Vec<Str>` is refused rather than summed,\n38: since there is no caller for it:\n39: \n40: ```toylang\n41: sum([\"a\"])\n42: ```\n43: \n44: ```error\n45: `sum` needs a Vec of Int or Int64, found Vec<Str> (at byte 4)\n46: ```\n\n(End of file - total 46 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# sum\n\n`sum(v)`, of type `Vec<Int> -> Int` and `Vec<Int64> -> Int64`: the reduction of `+` over `v`'s\nentries, at the element type's own width (kantord/toylang#140). An empty `Vec` sums to `0`.\n\n```toylang\nsum([1, 2, 3, 4])\n```\n\n```output\n10\n```\n\nEach addition wraps the way the language's `+` does, so a sum that leaves the 32-bit width is\nthe same number a hand-written fold would produce:\n\n```toylang\nsum([2147483647, 1])\n```\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/docs/reference/builtins/sum.md","text":"# sum\n\n`sum(v)`, of type `Vec<Int> -> Int` and `Vec<Int64> -> Int64`: the reduction of `+` over `v`'s\nentries, at the element type's own width (kantord/toylang#140). An empty `Vec` sums to `0`.\n\n```toylang\nsum([1, 2, 3, 4])\n```\n\n```output\n10\n```\n\nEach addition wraps the way the language's `+` does, so a sum that leaves the 32-bit width is\nthe same number a hand-written fold would produce:\n\n```toylang\nsum([2147483647, 1])\n```\n\n```output\n-2147483648\n```\n\nThe result keeps the element width, so a `Vec<Int64>` sums to an `Int64`:\n\n```toylang\nfn wide(x: Int) -> Int64 = i64(x)\n\nsum([wide(3), wide(1), wide(2)])\n```\n\n```output\n6\n```\n\nDefined only for the two integer element types. A `Vec<Str>` is refused rather than summed,\nsince there is no caller for it:\n\n```toylang\nsum([\"a\"])\n```\n\n```error\n`sum` needs a Vec of Int or Int64, found Vec<Str> (at byte 4)\n```","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":46,"totalLines":46,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/reference/builtins/sum.md","time":{"start":1788464507978,"end":1788464507983}},"id":"prt_068caac49001cvLdDtiHZUer3Q","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","messageID":"msg_068ca9fb8001zeFBpVoKSiR8TC"}}
