{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788464511240,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_cd078ec484ba42b58dbf009f","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/tir.rs","limit":110,"offset":120},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/tir.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n120:     Select {\n121:         source: Box<Tir>,\n122:         param: LocalId,\n123:         pred: Box<Tir>,\n124:     },\n125:     /// Read `name` off `base`. How many Vec layers to descend through is `base.ty`'s doing and\n126:     /// is not stored, so it cannot disagree with the type.\n127:     Field {\n128:         base: Box<Tir>,\n129:         name: String,\n130:     },\n131:     /// Collapse one dimension of `base` at `index`, `depth` layers down.\n132:     ///\n133:     /// Unlike a field access this has to store its depth. A field access leaves a record behind,\n134:     /// so the depth is every Vec layer of the base; an index leaves a Vec behind, so the layers\n135:     /// below the one being collapsed are indistinguishable from the ones above it.\n136:     /// A unary builtin. Unary like every other function, so it needs no special call form.\n137:     Builtin {\n138:         which: Builtin,\n139:         arg: Box<Tir>,\n140:     },\n141:     /// Insist an Opt is present, `depth` layers down. Like a field access and unlike an index,\n142:     /// the depth is every Vec layer of the base, because an Opt is not a dimension.\n143:     Unwrap {\n144:         base: Box<Tir>,\n145:     },\n146:     Index {\n147:         base: Box<Tir>,\n148:         index: Box<Tir>,\n149:         depth: usize,\n150:         /// Whether an entry is a record, which decides if collapsing has to gather columns.\n151:         elem_is_record: bool,\n152:     },\n153:     /// Narrow a dimension to the `[start, end)` window, `depth` layers down, counting negative\n154:     /// bounds from the end. Out-of-range bounds clamp to the valid range rather than answering\n155:     /// `Opt` the way a collapsing `Index` does (kantord/toylang#143), jq's `.[a:b]`; `None`\n156:     /// means the dimension's own boundary (0 and its length), and `start >= end` after\n157:     /// clamping yields empty.\n158:     Slice {\n159:         base: Box<Tir>,\n160:         start: Option<Box<Tir>>,\n161:         end: Option<Box<Tir>>,\n162:         depth: usize,\n163:     },\n164:     /// First-match-wins dispatch over the subject: variant arms test its shape, guard arms\n165:     /// evaluate a Bool of their own. The checker has already resolved every name a pattern\n166:     /// bound, so an arm is only a test, a payload local to bind, and a body.\n167:     ///\n168:     /// When the chain is total (`partial` is false) -- every variant covered, or a default at\n169:     /// the end -- a backend may take the last arm without a test. A partial chain has no such\n170:     /// arm: every test is emitted, and falling off the end produces the absent `Opt`, which is\n171:     /// why the node's type is then `Opt` of the arms' common body type.\n172:     Match {\n173:         subject: Box<Tir>,\n174:         arms: Vec<MatchArm>,\n175:         partial: bool,\n176:     },\n177: }\n178: \n179: pub struct MatchArm {\n180:     /// `Some` tests the subject for this variant. `None` with a `guard` is a guard arm; `None`\n181:     /// with no guard is the default arm, which the checker keeps last.\n182:     pub variant: Option<String>,\n183:     /// The Bool deciding a guard arm, already checked in the subject's scope.\n184:     pub guard: Option<Tir>,\n185:     /// The local the payload binds to in a payload-variant arm; `.` and destructured field\n186:     /// names in the body both read through it.\n187:     pub payload: Option<LocalId>,\n188:     pub body: Tir,\n189: }\n190: \n191: /// The functions the language provides. Each is unary, and so is every user function: something\n192: /// wanting two arguments takes a record, which is what a record literal is for in argument\n193: /// position.\n194: #[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\n195: pub enum Builtin {\n196:     /// `str(n)`, rendering an Int the way the printer does but reachable from a program.\n197:     IntToStr,\n198:     /// `i64(n)`, `Int -> Int64`: the one bridge between the two integer types, explicit\n199:     /// because nothing widens implicitly (kantord/toylang#83). A no-op on every backend whose\n200:     /// runtime integers are already 64 bits wide; JS builds a BigInt, and Go and Rust spell\n201:     /// the cast.\n202:     IntToI64,\n203:     /// `range(n)`, the integers from zero up to but not including n. Zero-based, matching jq,\n204:     /// Python, and this language's own indices.\n205:     Range,\n206:     /// `collect(s)`, `Stream<T> -> Vec<T>`: the one place a stream stops being a stream. What\n207:     /// comes back is an ordinary value, exactly as sized as it needs to be, with no trace left\n208:     /// of how it arrived.\n209:     Collect,\n210:     /// `jsonlines(v)`, printing each element of a `Vec<T>` on its own line rather than wrapping\n211:     /// the whole thing in `[...]`. Named for the format (jsonlines.org, also called NDJSON):\n212:     /// one JSON value per line. Polymorphic over the element type, so it is not in the fixed\n213:     /// signature table `builtin()` reads from; `synth` checks it directly, the way `map` and\n214:     /// `select` are checked from their own arm rather than through a table.\n215:     JsonLines,\n216:     /// `length(v)`, a Vec's length. A dense Vec already tracks this at runtime, so reading it out\n217:     /// costs nothing -- there is no fold or scan hiding behind the name.\n218:     Length,\n219:     /// `flatten(vv)`, flattening a `Vec<Vec<T>>` into one `Vec<T>`, for the case where the\n220:     /// number of inner Vecs is not known at the call site. Joining a fixed, known count of Vecs\n221:     /// is `+` instead (kantord/toylang#97, the add-trait reading of Q2 in plans/questions.md); this\n222:     /// builtin used to be named `concat` and cover both cases before that split.\n223:     Flatten,\n224:     /// `tail(v)`, every element but the first, `None` when `v` is empty. Consistent with how\n225:     /// `Index` already turns \"reaching past what's there\" into `Opt` rather than a runtime\n226:     /// failure.\n227:     Tail,\n228:     /// `fields(r)`, a record's field names in declaration order (kantord/toylang#63, the\n229:     /// accessor #60's ratification promised once order left type identity). The names come from\n\n(Showing lines 120-229 of 612. Use offset=230 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"    Select {\n        source: Box<Tir>,\n        param: LocalId,\n        pred: Box<Tir>,\n    },\n    /// Read `name` off `base`. How many Vec layers to descend through is `base.ty`'s doing and\n    /// is not stored, so it cannot disagree with the type.\n    Field {\n        base: Box<Tir>,\n        name: String,\n    },\n    /// Collapse one dimension of `base` at `index`, `depth` layers down.\n    ///\n    /// Unlike a field access this has to store its depth. A field access leaves a record behind,\n    /// so the depth is every Vec layer of the base; an index leaves a Vec behind, so the layers\n    /// below the one being collapsed are indistinguishable from the ones above it.\n    /// A unary builtin. Unary like every other function, so it needs no special call form.\n    Builtin {\n        which: Builtin,\n        arg: Box<Tir>,","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/tir.rs","text":"    Select {\n        source: Box<Tir>,\n        param: LocalId,\n        pred: Box<Tir>,\n    },\n    /// Read `name` off `base`. How many Vec layers to descend through is `base.ty`'s doing and\n    /// is not stored, so it cannot disagree with the type.\n    Field {\n        base: Box<Tir>,\n        name: String,\n    },\n    /// Collapse one dimension of `base` at `index`, `depth` layers down.\n    ///\n    /// Unlike a field access this has to store its depth. A field access leaves a record behind,\n    /// so the depth is every Vec layer of the base; an index leaves a Vec behind, so the layers\n    /// below the one being collapsed are indistinguishable from the ones above it.\n    /// A unary builtin. Unary like every other function, so it needs no special call form.\n    Builtin {\n        which: Builtin,\n        arg: Box<Tir>,\n    },\n    /// Insist an Opt is present, `depth` layers down. Like a field access and unlike an index,\n    /// the depth is every Vec layer of the base, because an Opt is not a dimension.\n    Unwrap {\n        base: Box<Tir>,\n    },\n    Index {\n        base: Box<Tir>,\n        index: Box<Tir>,\n        depth: usize,\n        /// Whether an entry is a record, which decides if collapsing has to gather columns.\n        elem_is_record: bool,\n    },\n    /// Narrow a dimension to the `[start, end)` window, `depth` layers down, counting negative\n    /// bounds from the end. Out-of-range bounds clamp to the valid range rather than answering\n    /// `Opt` the way a collapsing `Index` does (kantord/toylang#143), jq's `.[a:b]`; `None`\n    /// means the dimension's own boundary (0 and its length), and `start >= end` after\n    /// clamping yields empty.\n    Slice {\n        base: Box<Tir>,\n        start: Option<Box<Tir>>,\n        end: Option<Box<Tir>>,\n        depth: usize,\n    },\n    /// First-match-wins dispatch over the subject: variant arms test its shape, guard arms\n    /// evaluate a Bool of their own. The checker has already resolved every name a pattern\n    /// bound, so an arm is only a test, a payload local to bind, and a body.\n    ///\n    /// When the chain is total (`partial` is false) -- every variant covered, or a default at\n    /// the end -- a backend may take the last arm without a test. A partial chain has no such\n    /// arm: every test is emitted, and falling off the end produces the absent `Opt`, which is\n    /// why the node's type is then `Opt` of the arms' common body type.\n    Match {\n        subject: Box<Tir>,\n        arms: Vec<MatchArm>,\n        partial: bool,\n    },\n}\n\npub struct MatchArm {\n    /// `Some` tests the subject for this variant. `None` with a `guard` is a guard arm; `None`\n    /// with no guard is the default arm, which the checker keeps last.\n    pub variant: Option<String>,\n    /// The Bool deciding a guard arm, already checked in the subject's scope.\n    pub guard: Option<Tir>,\n    /// The local the payload binds to in a payload-variant arm; `.` and destructured field\n    /// names in the body both read through it.\n    pub payload: Option<LocalId>,\n    pub body: Tir,\n}\n\n/// The functions the language provides. Each is unary, and so is every user function: something\n/// wanting two arguments takes a record, which is what a record literal is for in argument\n/// position.\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\npub enum Builtin {\n    /// `str(n)`, rendering an Int the way the printer does but reachable from a program.\n    IntToStr,\n    /// `i64(n)`, `Int -> Int64`: the one bridge between the two integer types, explicit\n    /// because nothing widens implicitly (kantord/toylang#83). A no-op on every backend whose\n    /// runtime integers are already 64 bits wide; JS builds a BigInt, and Go and Rust spell\n    /// the cast.\n    IntToI64,\n    /// `range(n)`, the integers from zero up to but not including n. Zero-based, matching jq,\n    /// Python, and this language's own indices.\n    Range,\n    /// `collect(s)`, `Stream<T> -> Vec<T>`: the one place a stream stops being a stream. What\n    /// comes back is an ordinary value, exactly as sized as it needs to be, with no trace left\n    /// of how it arrived.\n    Collect,\n    /// `jsonlines(v)`, printing each element of a `Vec<T>` on its own line rather than wrapping\n    /// the whole thing in `[...]`. Named for the format (jsonlines.org, also called NDJSON):\n    /// one JSON value per line. Polymorphic over the element type, so it is not in the fixed\n    /// signature table `builtin()` reads from; `synth` checks it directly, the way `map` and\n    /// `select` are checked from their own arm rather than through a table.\n    JsonLines,\n    /// `length(v)`, a Vec's length. A dense Vec already tracks this at runtime, so reading it out\n    /// costs nothing -- there is no fold or scan hiding behind the name.\n    Length,\n    /// `flatten(vv)`, flattening a `Vec<Vec<T>>` into one `Vec<T>`, for the case where the\n    /// number of inner Vecs is not known at the call site. Joining a fixed, known count of Vecs\n    /// is `+` instead (kantord/toylang#97, the add-trait reading of Q2 in plans/questions.md); this\n    /// builtin used to be named `concat` and cover both cases before that split.\n    Flatten,\n    /// `tail(v)`, every element but the first, `None` when `v` is empty. Consistent with how\n    /// `Index` already turns \"reaching past what's there\" into `Opt` rather than a runtime\n    /// failure.\n    Tail,\n    /// `fields(r)`, a record's field names in declaration order (kantord/toylang#63, the\n    /// accessor #60's ratification promised once order left type identity). The names come from","lineStart":120,"lineEnd":229,"totalLines":612,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/tir.rs","time":{"start":1788464511235,"end":1788464511238}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand the docs style. The map docs use `[1, 2,  .,  | map(. * 2)`. So for sort_by, the natural spelling is `v | sort_by(.key)`.\n\n.\n\nNow, the question is: does the issue intend the syntax `v | sort_by(.key)` or `sort_by(v, .key)`? Since calls are unary, the Vec must be piped. The plan says \"sort_by(.key) and max_by(.key) order records through a scalar projection\". And \"the same map(.name) projection machinery already in the checker\". The map machinery requires the subject via pipe. So the syntax is: `v | sort_by(.key)`.\n\n.\n\nLet me look at how `max` is emitted across backends and how `sort` is, so I can mirror those for sort_by/max_by. Let me look at each backend's handling of Builtin::Sort and Builtin::Max in detail.\n\n Let me read the emit files relevant sections.\n\n.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068cab8ff001V3CUPZX5fl0A51","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","messageID":"msg_068caac600012ubKTUK6xQUOK8"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788464511260,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"id":"prt_068cab9180018ARtqSETbDrfPc","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"4b2797779b42b25cca6cb1f7660061fcb9d31aa4","messageID":"msg_068caac600012ubKTUK6xQUOK8","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":61917,"input":1444,"output":113,"reasoning":200,"cache":{"write":0,"read":60160}},"cost":0.00111276}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788464512478,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"id":"prt_068cabdd90016RCnle8G2J0RCv","messageID":"msg_068cab9270017Qz5zlYK09qS45","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","snapshot":"4b2797779b42b25cca6cb1f7660061fcb9d31aa4","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788464515623,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_a409c94e7b61404abb7e1be7","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_py.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_py.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! The Python backend.\n2: //!\n3: //! Python is dynamically typed, so the depth-polymorphic helpers that Go could not have are\n4: //! available again: one `tl_field(v, k, depth)` serves every shape. What it stresses instead is\n5: //! arithmetic, from a direction none of the other five come from. Its integers are exact and\n6: //! unbounded, so wrapping is emulation like jq's -- but unlike jq nothing is lost on the way,\n7: //! so the whole rule is one modulo rather than a split into 16-bit halves. Its `//` and `%` are\n8: //! floored, so truncated division needs writing out.\n9: //!\n10: //! One mapping is better here than anywhere else: a record is a dict, which is also what\n11: //! `json.loads` produces, so reading input is the parse and nothing more. Go needed a declared\n12: //! struct and a decoder to reach the same place.\n13: \n14: use crate::ast::BinOp;\n15: use crate::tir::{self, Builtin, Kind, LocalId, Program, Tir};\n16: use crate::ty::{self, Enums, Type};\n17: \n18: /// The binding the input value is read into. Unspellable in source, since every source name is\n19: /// prefixed.\n20: const INPUT: &str = \"t_input\";\n21: const INPUTS: &str = \"t_inputs\";\n22: \n23: const FAIL_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_fail(msg):\n24:     sys.stderr.write(\"toylang: \" + msg + \"\\n\")\n25:     sys.exit(1)\n26: \"#;\n27: \n28: /// Python's integers do not overflow, so the 32-bit rule is entirely emulated. It is exact\n29: /// arithmetic all the way through, which is what lets one modulo stand for the whole thing:\n30: /// jq needed a 16-bit split only because a double loses the low bits of a 62-bit product.\n31: const I32_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_i32(n):\n32:     return ((n + 2147483648) % 4294967296) - 2147483648\n33: \"#;\n34: \n35: /// The same one-modulo emulation at 64 bits, for `Int64` (kantord/toylang#83). Exact for the\n36: /// same reason `tl_i32` is: Python's integers never lose bits on the way to the modulo.\n37: const I64_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_i64(n):\n38:     return ((n + 9223372036854775808) % 18446744073709551616) - 9223372036854775808\n39: \"#;\n40: \n41: /// Python floors, and `int(a / b)` would truncate through a float and lose bits on the way. The\n42: /// remainder takes its sign from the dividend, so `a == tl_div(a, b) * b + tl_rem(a, b)` holds.\n43: const ARITH_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_div(a, b):\n44:     if b == 0:\n45:         tl_fail(\"divided by zero\")\n46:     q = abs(a) // abs(b)\n47:     return tl_i32(-q if (a < 0) != (b < 0) else q)\n48: \n49: \n50: def tl_rem(a, b):\n51:     if b == 0:\n52:         tl_fail(\"divided by zero\")\n53:     r = abs(a) % abs(b)\n54:     return -r if a < 0 else r\n55: \"#;\n56: \n57: /// `tl_div`/`tl_rem` at 64 bits. Only the quotient can leave the range (`MIN / -1`), so the\n58: /// remainder needs no wrap: its magnitude is strictly below the divisor's.\n59: const ARITH64_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_div64(a, b):\n60:     if b == 0:\n61:         tl_fail(\"divided by zero\")\n62:     q = abs(a) // abs(b)\n63:     return tl_i64(-q if (a < 0) != (b < 0) else q)\n64: \n65: \n66: def tl_rem64(a, b):\n67:     if b == 0:\n68:         tl_fail(\"divided by zero\")\n69:     r = abs(a) % abs(b)\n70:     return -r if a < 0 else r\n71: \"#;\n72: \n73: const FIELD_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_field(v, k, depth):\n74:     if depth == 0:\n75:         return v[k]\n76:     return [tl_field(e, k, depth - 1) for e in v]\n77: \"#;\n78: \n79: /// An Opt is its enum's own runtime shape (ADR 0009): `{\"some\": v}` present, `\"none\"` absent.\n80: /// Tagged, so two levels of absence stay two values; only the printer flattens to null.\n81: const AT_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_at(v, i, depth):\n82:     if depth > 0:\n83:         return [tl_at(e, i, depth - 1) for e in v]\n84:     n = len(v)\n85:     if i < 0:\n86:         i = n + i\n87:     if i < 0 or i >= n:\n88:         return \"none\"\n89:     return {\"some\": v[i]}\n90: \"#;\n91: \n92: /// Python's own slicing already clamps out-of-range bounds and counts negatives from the end,\n93: /// so jq's boundary behaviour is the target's native one; `None` is a bound left out.\n94: const SLICE_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_slice(v, lo, hi, depth):\n95:     if depth > 0:\n96:         return [tl_slice(e, lo, hi, depth - 1) for e in v]\n97:     return v[lo:hi]\n98: \"#;\n99: \n100: const UNWRAP_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_unwrap(v, depth):\n101:     if depth > 0:\n102:         return [tl_unwrap(e, depth - 1) for e in v]\n103:     if v == \"none\":\n104:         tl_fail(\"unwrapped a value that is not there\")\n105:     return v[\"some\"]\n106: \"#;\n107: \n108: const TAIL_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_tail(v):\n109:     if len(v) == 0:\n110:         return \"none\"\n111:     return {\"some\": v[1:]}\n112: \"#;\n113: \n114: const FLATTEN_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_flatten(vv):\n115:     return [e for sub in vv for e in sub]\n116: \"#;\n117: \n118: const COLLECT_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_collect_lines():\n119:     out = []\n120:     for line in sys.stdin:\n121:         out.append(line[:-1] if line.endswith(\"\\n\") else line)\n122:     return out\n123: \"#;\n124: \n125: const RANGE_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_range(n):\n126:     return list(range(max(0, n)))\n127: \"#;\n128: \n129: /// Python 3 strings are already sequences of Unicode scalar values, so iterating one already\n130: /// decodes by codepoint; `ord` is the cast down to the number every other backend represents a\n131: /// `Char` as.\n132: const CHARS_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_chars(s):\n133:     return [ord(c) for c in s]\n134: \"#;\n135: \n136: const JOIN_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_join(v, f):\n137:     return \"[\" + \",\".join(f(e) for e in v) + \"]\"\n138: \"#;\n139: \n140: const JSONLINES_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_jsonlines(v, f):\n141:     return \"\\n\".join(f(e) for e in v)\n142: \"#;\n143: \n144: /// Python's integers are unbounded, so both widths wrap by emulation -- `tl_i32` for Int and\n145: /// `tl_i64` for Int64, the same two helpers arithmetic already uses.\n146: const SUM_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_sum(v):\n147:     acc = 0\n148:     for x in v:\n149:         acc = tl_i32(acc + x)\n150:     return acc\n151: \n152: \n153: def tl_sum64(v):\n154:     acc = 0\n155:     for x in v:\n156:         acc = tl_i64(acc + x)\n157:     return acc\n158: \"#;\n159: \n160: const MAX_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_max(v):\n161:     if len(v) == 0:\n162:         return \"none\"\n163:     return {\"some\": max(v)}\n164: \"#;\n165: \n166: /// Iterating characters rather than bytes, which agrees with the C runtime's byte loop because\n167: /// the two differ only above U+007F, where both pass the value through unchanged.\n168: const QUOTE_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_quote(s):\n169:     out = ['\"']\n170:     for c in s:\n171:         if c == '\"':\n172:             out.append('\\\\\"')\n173:         elif c == \"\\\\\":\n174:             out.append(\"\\\\\\\\\")\n175:         elif c == \"\\n\":\n176:             out.append(\"\\\\n\")\n177:         elif c == \"\\r\":\n178:             out.append(\"\\\\r\")\n179:         elif c == \"\\t\":\n180:             out.append(\"\\\\t\")\n181:         elif ord(c) < 0x20:\n182:             out.append(\"\\\\u%04x\" % ord(c))\n183:         else:\n184:             out.append(c)\n185:     out.append('\"')\n186:     return \"\".join(out)\n187: \"#;\n188: \n189: pub fn emit(program: &Program) -> String {\n190:     let enums = &program.enums;\n191:     // Before the program's own functions, so a printer is defined by the time anything calls\n192:     // one, and so the helper scan below sees what a printer body uses.\n193:     let mut decls = printers(program);\n194: \n195:     // Module-level functions are looked up when called, not when defined, so the forward\n196:     // reference the checker accepts costs nothing here. Lua needed declarations, JavaScript\n197:     // relied on hoisting, and jq could not express it at all.\n198:     for f in &program.funcs {\n199:         let param = f.param.as_deref().map(user);\n200:         if tir::has_tail_call(&f.name, &f.body) {\n201:             // The contract: a self-tail-call runs in constant stack. A tail call becomes\n202:             // `param = arg` then `continue` in a loop, so 100k-deep self-recursion cannot hit\n203:             // Python's recursion limit the way a real call would (kantord/toylang#141).\n204:             let mut fresh = 0;\n205:             decls.push_str(&format!(\n206:                 \"def {}({}):\\n    while True:\\n{}\\n\\n\\n\",\n207:                 user(&f.name),\n208:                 param.as_deref().unwrap_or_default(),\n209:                 tail_stmts(enums, &f.name, param.as_deref(), &mut fresh, &f.body, 8)\n210:             ));\n211:         } else {\n212:             decls.push_str(&format!(\n213:                 \"def {}({}):\\n    return {}\\n\\n\\n\",\n214:                 user(&f.name),\n215:                 param.as_deref().unwrap_or_default(),\n216:                 expr(enums, &f.body)\n217:             ));\n218:         }\n219:     }\n220: \n221:     if let Some(fusion) = tir::fusion(program) {\n222:         decls.push_str(&fused_main(program, &fusion));\n223:     } else {\n224:         if program.input.is_some() {\n225:             decls.push_str(&format!(\n226:                 \"{INPUT} = json.loads(sys.stdin.buffer.read().decode(\\\"utf-8\\\"))\\n\"\n227:             ));\n228:         }\n229:         if program.inputs.is_some() {\n230:             decls.push_str(&format!(\n231:                 \"{INPUTS} = [json.loads(_l) for _l in sys.stdin]\\n\"\n232:             ));\n233:         }\n234: \n235:         let body = expr(enums, &program.body);\n236:         // A top-level Str prints raw, the way jq's -r does; anything else prints as JSON.\n237:         let printed = if matches!(program.body.ty, Type::Str | Type::Sink) {\n238:             body\n239:         } else {\n240:             show(enums, &program.body.ty, &body, 0)\n241:         };\n242:         // Bytes rather than `print`, so output does not depend on the locale the interpreter was\n243:         // started under. The native backend writes bytes for the same reason.\n244:         decls.push_str(&format!(\n245:             \"sys.stdout.buffer.write(({printed} + \\\"\\\\n\\\").encode(\\\"utf-8\\\"))\\n\"\n246:         ));\n247:     }\n248: \n249:     // Which helpers to include is read off the emitted text. Python objects to neither an unused\n250:     // function nor an unused import, so nothing here can break the way it would in Go; the\n251:     // helper-to-helper edges are still stated rather than read, since inclusion is what pulls\n252:     // them in.\n253:     let uses = |name: &str| decls.contains(name);\n254:     let unwrap = uses(\"tl_unwrap(\");\n255:     let arith = uses(\"tl_div(\") || uses(\"tl_rem(\");\n256:     let arith64 = uses(\"tl_div64(\") || uses(\"tl_rem64(\");\n257: \n258:     let mut helpers = false;\n259:     let mut out = String::from(\"import sys\\n\");\n260:     if program.input.is_some() || program.inputs.is_some() {\n261:         out.push_str(\"import json\\n\");\n262:     }\n263:     // Python's default ceiling (1000) is far below what a compiled-style recursive program\n264:     // needs: Euler 11's self-tail-recursive fold alone is ~1300 frames deep. Raise it before\n265:     // any program code runs; 100000 is comfortably above the thousands real programs reach.\n266:     out.push_str(\"sys.setrecursionlimit(100000)\\n\");\n267:     out.push('\\n');\n268:     for (on, text) in [\n269:         (unwrap || arith || arith64, FAIL_HELPER),\n270:         (arith || uses(\"tl_i32(\") || uses(\"tl_sum(\"), I32_HELPER),\n271:         (arith64 || uses(\"tl_i64(\") || uses(\"tl_sum64(\"), I64_HELPER),\n272:         (arith, ARITH_HELPER),\n273:         (arith64, ARITH64_HELPER),\n274:         (uses(\"tl_field(\"), FIELD_HELPER),\n275:         (uses(\"tl_at(\"), AT_HELPER),\n276:         (uses(\"tl_slice(\"), SLICE_HELPER),\n277:         (uses(\"tl_tail(\"), TAIL_HELPER),\n278:         (uses(\"tl_flatten(\"), FLATTEN_HELPER),\n279:         (unwrap, UNWRAP_HELPER),\n280:         (uses(\"tl_range(\"), RANGE_HELPER),\n281:         (uses(\"tl_chars(\"), CHARS_HELPER),\n282:         (uses(\"tl_sum(\") || uses(\"tl_sum64(\"), SUM_HELPER),\n283:         (uses(\"tl_max(\"), MAX_HELPER),\n284:         (uses(\"tl_collect_lines(\"), COLLECT_HELPER),\n285:         (uses(\"tl_join(\"), JOIN_HELPER),\n286:         (uses(\"tl_jsonlines(\"), JSONLINES_HELPER),\n287:         (uses(\"tl_quote(\"), QUOTE_HELPER),\n288:     ] {\n289:         if on {\n290:             out.push_str(\"\\n\\n\");\n291:             out.push_str(text);\n292:             helpers = true;\n293:         }\n294:     }\n295:     if helpers {\n296:         out.push_str(\"\\n\\n\");\n297:     }\n298:     out.push_str(&decls);\n299:     out\n300: }\n301: \n302: /// A stream-typed `jsonlines` program, compiled as a loop reading one entry at a time from\n303: /// `sys.stdin` (which iterates lazily) rather than the eager path's read-everything-first.\n304: /// `sys.stdout.buffer` is block-buffered whenever it is not a terminal, the same as the eager\n305: /// path already writes bytes rather than using `print` for, so the explicit `.flush()` after\n306: /// each line is what makes a record appear before the next one arrives rather than after the\n307: /// whole run ends.\n308: fn fused_main(program: &Program, fusion: &tir::Fusion) -> String {\n309:     let enums = &program.enums;\n310:     let mut out = String::new();\n311:     let (mut current, mut current_ty) = match fusion.source {\n312:         tir::Source::Inputs => {\n313:             out.push_str(\"for _line in sys.stdin:\\n\");\n314:             out.push_str(\"    _line = _line[:-1] if _line.endswith(\\\"\\\\n\\\") else _line\\n\");\n315:             out.push_str(\"    if _line.strip() == \\\"\\\":\\n        continue\\n\");\n316:             out.push_str(\"    t_line = json.loads(_line)\\n\");\n317:             let elem = program\n318:                 .inputs\n319:                 .as_ref()\n320:                 .expect(\"an inputs source recorded its element\");\n321:             (\"t_line\".to_string(), elem.clone())\n322:         }\n323:         // A raw line is already the element, blank ones included: `lines` keeps them.\n324:         tir::Source::Lines => {\n325:             out.push_str(\"for _line in sys.stdin:\\n\");\n326:             out.push_str(\"    _line = _line[:-1] if _line.endswith(\\\"\\\\n\\\") else _line\\n\");\n327:             (\"_line\".to_string(), Type::Str)\n328:         }\n329:         // The bound is evaluated once; the loop counter is the element. A negative bound makes\n330:         // Python's own range empty, the same answer `tl_range` gives eagerly.\n331:         tir::Source::Range(bound) => {\n332:             out.push_str(&format!(\"for t_i in range({}):\\n\", expr(enums, bound)));\n333:             (\"t_i\".to_string(), Type::Int)\n334:         }\n335:     };\n336:     for stage in &fusion.stages {\n337:         match stage {\n338:             tir::Stage::Map { param, body } => {\n339:                 out.push_str(&format!(\"    {} = {}\\n\", local(*param), current));\n340:                 current = expr(enums, body);\n341:                 current_ty = body.ty.clone();\n342:             }\n343:             tir::Stage::Select { param, pred } => {\n344:                 out.push_str(&format!(\"    {} = {}\\n\", local(*param), current));\n345:                 out.push_str(&format!(\n346:                     \"    if not ({}):\\n        continue\\n\",\n347:                     expr(enums, pred)\n348:                 ));\n349:                 current = local(*param);\n350:             }\n351:         }\n352:     }\n353:     let printed = show(enums, &current_ty, &current, 0);\n354:     out.push_str(&format!(\n355:         \"    sys.stdout.buffer.write(({printed} + \\\"\\\\n\\\").encode(\\\"utf-8\\\"))\\n\"\n356:     ));\n357:     out.push_str(\"    sys.stdout.buffer.flush()\\n\");\n358:     out\n359: }\n360: \n361: /// The printer is built from the type rather than by inspecting the value, as on every backend.\n362: /// Here the type is doing one thing it does nowhere else: `str` on a Bool gives `True`, so the\n363: /// two JSON words have to be written out.\n364: fn show(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type, value: &str, depth: usize) -> String {\n365:     match ty {\n366:         Type::Param(_) => unreachable!(\"params are substituted before emit\"),\n367:         // The checker refuses a program whose result contains a stream, since there is nothing to\n368:         // print: a stream has no value, only a promise that collect can redeem.\n369:         Type::Stream(_) => unreachable!(\"a stream cannot reach the printer\"),\n370:         Type::Char => unreachable!(\"Char cannot reach the printer, refused by the checker\"),\n371:         Type::Str => format!(\"tl_quote({value})\"),\n372:         Type::Sink => unreachable!(\"a sink only ever prints raw, never through the printer\"),\n373:         Type::Int | Type::Int64 => format!(\"str({value})\"),\n374:         Type::Float => unreachable!(\"Float is JS-only in this row\"),\n375:         Type::Bool => format!(\"(\\\"true\\\" if {value} else \\\"false\\\")\"),\n376:         Type::Vec(elem) => {\n377:             let e = format!(\"e{depth}\");\n378:             format!(\n379:                 \"tl_join({value}, lambda {e}: {})\",\n380:                 show(enums, elem, &e, depth + 1)\n381:             )\n382:         }\n383:         Type::Enum { .. } if ty.as_opt().is_some() => {\n384:             let inner = ty.as_opt().expect(\"guarded\");\n385:             let v = format!(\"o{depth}\");\n386:             format!(\n387:                 \"(lambda {v}: \\\"null\\\" if {v} == \\\"none\\\" else {})({value})\",\n388:                 show(enums, inner, &format!(\"{v}[\\\"some\\\"]\"), depth + 1)\n389:             )\n390:         }\n391:         // A recursive enum prints through a function of its own (`printers`), because expanding\n392:         // one here has no bottom: its payload leads back to the same type.\n393:         Type::Enum { .. } if ty::is_recursive(enums, ty) => format!(\"{}({value})\", ty.show_fn()),\n394:         Type::Enum { .. } => show_enum(enums, ty, value, depth),\n395:         Type::Record(fields) => {\n396:             if fields.is_empty() {\n397:                 return \"\\\"{}\\\"\".to_string();\n398:             }\n399:             // The type's field list is declaration order, so this prints as declared. Field\n400:             // names are identifiers, so the JSON key needs no escaping and is one literal.\n401:             let parts: Vec<String> = fields\n402:                 .iter()\n403:                 .map(|(name, fty)| {\n404:                     let read = format!(\"{value}[{}]\", py_string(name));\n405:                     let key = py_string(&format!(\"\\\"{name}\\\":\"));\n406:                     format!(\"{key} + {}\", show(enums, fty, &read, depth + 1))\n407:                 })\n408:                 .collect();\n409:             format!(\"(\\\"{{\\\" + {} + \\\"}}\\\")\", parts.join(\" + \\\",\\\" + \"))\n410:         }\n411:     }\n412: }\n413: \n414: fn user(name: &str) -> String {\n415:     format!(\"v_{name}\")\n416: }\n417: \n418: fn local(id: LocalId) -> String {\n419:     format!(\"t_{id}\")\n420: }\n421: \n422: fn expr(enums: &Enums, t: &Tir) -> String {\n423:     match &t.kind {\n424:         Kind::Str(s) => py_string(s),\n425:         Kind::Int(n) => n.to_string(),\n426:         Kind::Float(_) => unreachable!(\"Float is JS-only in this row\"),\n427:         Kind::Var(name) => user(name),\n428:         Kind::Local(id) => local(*id),\n429:         Kind::Input => INPUT.to_string(),\n430:         Kind::Inputs => INPUTS.to_string(),\n431:         // The stream, materialized eagerly: whatever consumes it -- `collect`, a mapper --\n432:         // works on the Vec of its entries. Fusion is what will remove this materialization.\n433:         Kind::Lines => \"tl_collect_lines()\".to_string(),\n434:         // Same raw lines as `lines`, each split on the delimiter into one row.\n435:         Kind::Dsv { delim } => format!(\n436:             \"[l.split({}) for l in tl_collect_lines()]\",\n437:             py_string(delim)\n438:         ),\n439:         Kind::RecordLit { fields } => {\n440:             let parts: Vec<String> = fields\n441:                 .iter()\n442:                 .map(|(name, value)| format!(\"{}: {}\", py_string(name), expr(enums, value)))\n443:                 .collect();\n444:             format!(\"{{{}}}\", parts.join(\", \"))\n445:         }\n446: \n447:         // The value is its JSON shape: a unit variant is the variant-name string, a payload\n448:         // variant the single-key dict a record already is.\n449:         Kind::EnumLit { variant, payload } => match payload {\n450:             None => py_string(variant),\n451:             Some(p) => format!(\"{{{}: {}}}\", py_string(variant), expr(enums, p)),\n452:         },\n453: \n454:         Kind::VecLit(items) => {\n455:             let parts: Vec<String> = items.iter().map(|i| expr(enums, i)).collect();\n456:             format!(\"[{}]\", parts.join(\", \"))\n457:         }\n458:         Kind::Call { func, arg } => format!(\n459:             \"{}({})\",\n460:             user(func),\n461:             arg.as_deref().map_or_else(String::new, |a| expr(enums, a))\n462:         ),\n463:         // Python's own `+` already concatenates two lists the way it concatenates two strings,\n464:         // so a Vec needs no different spelling here than Str does.\n465:         Kind::Concat(l, r) => format!(\"({} + {})\", expr(enums, l), expr(enums, r)),\n466:         Kind::Arith { op, lhs, rhs } => arith(&t.ty, *op, expr(enums, lhs), expr(enums, rhs)),\n467:         Kind::Builtin { which, arg } => match which {\n468:             Builtin::IntToStr => format!(\"str({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n469:             // Python's integers are one type at every width, so the bridge has nothing to do.\n470:             Builtin::IntToI64 => expr(enums, arg),\n471:             Builtin::Range => format!(\"tl_range({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n472:             Builtin::Chars => format!(\"tl_chars({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n473:             Builtin::JsonLines => {\n474:                 let elem = tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty).expect(\"checked to be a Vec or a stream\");\n475:                 let e = \"e0\".to_string();\n476:                 format!(\n477:                     \"tl_jsonlines({}, lambda {e}: {})\",\n478:                     expr(enums, arg),\n479:                     show(enums, elem, &e, 1)\n480:                 )\n481:             }\n482:             // The source already materialized, so the exit has nothing left to do.\n483:             Builtin::Collect => expr(enums, arg),\n484:             Builtin::Length => format!(\"len({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n485:             Builtin::Tail => format!(\"tl_tail({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n486:             Builtin::Flatten => format!(\"tl_flatten({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n487:             // Python compares both numbers and strings (by codepoint) with `<` natively, so\n488:             // `sorted` needs no key or comparator.\n489:             Builtin::Sort => format!(\"sorted({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n490:             Builtin::Reverse => format!(\"({})[::-1]\", expr(enums, arg)),\n491:             // Which fold depends on the element width only for the wrap: Python has one integer\n492:             // type, so the two differ in which modulo they apply, not in the value type.\n493:             Builtin::Sum => {\n494:                 if tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty) == Some(&Type::Int) {\n495:                     format!(\"tl_sum({})\", expr(enums, arg))\n496:                 } else {\n497:                     format!(\"tl_sum64({})\", expr(enums, arg))\n498:                 }\n499:             }\n500:             Builtin::Max => format!(\"tl_max({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n501:             // The names come from the checked type, not the dict value, so `arg` runs as the\n502:             // lambda's ignored argument -- the same shape `Bind` uses -- purely for whatever\n503:             // else it does.\n504:             Builtin::Fields => {\n505:                 let Type::Record(fields) = &arg.ty else {\n506:                     unreachable!(\"checked to be a record\")\n507:                 };\n508:                 let names: Vec<String> = fields.iter().map(|(n, _)| py_string(n)).collect();\n509:                 format!(\"(lambda _: [{}])({})\", names.join(\", \"), expr(enums, arg))\n510:             }\n511:         },\n512:         Kind::Compare { op, lhs, rhs } => {\n513:             format!(\"({} {} {})\", expr(enums, lhs), py_op(*op), expr(enums, rhs))\n514:         }\n515:         // Python spells and short-circuits both of these the way toylang does.\n516:         Kind::Logic { op, lhs, rhs } => format!(\"({} {op} {})\", expr(enums, lhs), expr(enums, rhs)),\n517:         Kind::Not(base) => format!(\"(not {})\", expr(enums, base)),\n518:         Kind::Bind {\n519:             local: id,\n520:             value,\n521:             body,\n522:         } => {\n523:             format!(\n524:                 \"(lambda {}: {})({})\",\n525:                 local(*id),\n526:                 expr(enums, body),\n527:                 expr(enums, value)\n528:             )\n529:         }\n530:         // A comprehension rather than `map`, which returns an iterator here and would need a\n531:         // `list` around it anyway.\n532:         Kind::Map {\n533:             source,\n534:             param,\n535:             body,\n536:         } => {\n537:             format!(\n538:                 \"[{} for {} in {}]\",\n539:                 expr(enums, body),\n540:                 local(*param),\n541:                 expr(enums, source)\n542:             )\n543:         }\n544:         Kind::Select {\n545:             source,\n546:             param,\n547:             pred,\n548:         } => {\n549:             let p = local(*param);\n550:             format!(\n551:                 \"[{p} for {p} in {} if {}]\",\n552:                 expr(enums, source),\n553:                 expr(enums, pred)\n554:             )\n555:         }\n556:         Kind::Unwrap { base } => {\n557:             format!(\n558:                 \"tl_unwrap({}, {})\",\n559:                 expr(enums, base),\n560:                 tir::vec_depth(&base.ty)\n561:             )\n562:         }\n563:         // Opt's reorder pass (kantord/toylang#66): the same `== \"none\"`/`[\"some\"]` shape the\n564:         // printer and Match already read, generalised to rebuild the dict instead.\n565:         Kind::OptMap {\n566:             source,\n567:             param,\n568:             body,\n569:         } => {\n570:             format!(\n571:                 \"(lambda __opt: \\\"none\\\" if __opt == \\\"none\\\" else {{\\\"some\\\": (lambda {}: {})(__opt[\\\"some\\\"])}})({})\",\n572:                 local(*param),\n573:                 expr(enums, body),\n574:                 expr(enums, source)\n575:             )\n576:         }\n577:         Kind::Index {\n578:             base, index, depth, ..\n579:         } => {\n580:             format!(\n581:                 \"tl_at({}, {}, {})\",\n582:                 expr(enums, base),\n583:                 expr(enums, index),\n584:                 depth\n585:             )\n586:         }\n587:         Kind::Slice {\n588:             base, start, end, depth,\n589:         } => {\n590:             let lo = match start {\n591:                 Some(s) => expr(enums, s),\n592:                 None => \"None\".to_string(),\n593:             };\n594:             let hi = match end {\n595:                 Some(e) => expr(enums, e),\n596:                 None => \"None\".to_string(),\n597:             };\n598:             format!(\n599:                 \"tl_slice({}, {}, {}, {})\",\n600:                 expr(enums, base),\n601:                 lo,\n602:                 hi,\n603:                 depth\n604:             )\n605:         }\n606:         Kind::Field { base, name } => {\n607:             let depth = tir::vec_depth(&base.ty);\n608:             if depth == 0 {\n609:                 format!(\"{}[{}]\", expr(enums, base), py_string(name))\n610:             } else {\n611:                 format!(\n612:                     \"tl_field({}, {}, {})\",\n613:                     expr(enums, base),\n614:                     py_string(name),\n615:                     depth\n616:                 )\n617:             }\n618:         }\n619:         // A right-fold of conditional expressions over the subject, tests first-match-wins; a\n620:         // guard arm's test is the guard itself. A total chain's last arm carries no test, the\n621:         // checker having proved nothing else can reach it; a partial chain tests every arm and\n622:         // bottoms out at `None`, the absent Opt. The payload key test needs the isinstance\n623:         // guard: `\"a\" in v` on a unit variant's *string* would be a substring test, and\n624:         // `\"a\" in \"ab\"` answers yes.\n625:         Kind::Match {\n626:             subject,\n627:             arms,\n628:             partial,\n629:         } => {\n630:             let subj = expr(enums, subject);\n631:             let mut out = String::new();\n632:             let mut closing = 0;\n633:             for (i, arm) in arms.iter().enumerate() {\n634:                 let run = match arm.payload {\n635:                     Some(pid) => {\n636:                         let variant = arm\n637:                             .variant\n638:                             .as_ref()\n639:                             .expect(\"only a variant arm has a payload\");\n640:                         format!(\n641:                             \"(lambda {}: {})({subj}[{}])\",\n642:                             local(pid),\n643:                             expr(enums, &arm.body),\n644:                             py_string(variant)\n645:                         )\n646:                     }\n647:                     None => expr(enums, &arm.body),\n648:                 };\n649:                 let test = match (&arm.variant, &arm.guard) {\n650:                     (Some(v), _) if arm.payload.is_some() => Some(format!(\n651:                         \"(isinstance({subj}, dict) and {} in {subj})\",\n652:                         py_string(v)\n653:                     )),\n654:                     (Some(v), _) => Some(format!(\"{subj} == {}\", py_string(v))),\n655:                     (None, Some(g)) => Some(expr(enums, g)),\n656:                     (None, None) => None,\n657:                 };\n658:                 // A partial chain's yield is an Opt, so a present arm is tagged.\n659:                 let run = if *partial {\n660:                     format!(\"{{\\\"some\\\": {run}}}\")\n661:                 } else {\n662:                     run\n663:                 };\n664:                 match test {\n665:                     Some(test) if *partial || i + 1 < arms.len() => {\n666:                         out.push_str(&format!(\"({run} if {test} else \"));\n667:                         closing += 1;\n668:                     }\n669:                     _ => out.push_str(&run),\n670:                 }\n671:             }\n672:             if *partial {\n673:                 out.push_str(\"\\\"none\\\"\");\n674:             }\n675:             out.push_str(&\")\".repeat(closing));\n676:             format!(\"({out})\")\n677:         }\n678:     }\n679: }\n680: \n681: /// `t` as statements in the tail position, each line already padded to `level` columns:\n682: /// `return <expr>` for a base case, and for a tail call `param = <arg>` followed by `continue`\n683: /// so the emitted `while True` rewinds instead of recursing against Python's interpreter\n684: /// recursion limit. A total `Match`'s arm bodies nest one indent deeper, the way Python needs.\n685: fn tail_stmts(\n686:     enums: &Enums,\n687:     name: &str,\n688:     param: Option<&str>,\n689:     fresh: &mut usize,\n690:     t: &Tir,\n691:     level: usize,\n692: ) -> String {\n693:     let pad = \" \".repeat(level);\n694:     match &t.kind {\n695:         Kind::Call { func, arg } if func == name => {\n696:             let assign = param.map_or_else(String::new, |p| {\n697:                 format!(\n698:                     \"{pad}{p} = {}\\n\",\n699:                     arg.as_deref().map_or_else(String::new, |a| expr(enums, a))\n700:                 )\n701:             });\n702:             format!(\"{assign}{pad}continue\\n\")\n703:         }\n704:         Kind::Bind {\n705:             local: id,\n706:             value,\n707:             body,\n708:         } => format!(\n709:             \"{pad}{} = {}\\n{}\",\n710:             local(*id),\n711:             expr(enums, value),\n712:             tail_stmts(enums, name, param, fresh, body, level),\n713:         ),\n714:         Kind::Match {\n715:             subject,\n716:             arms,\n717:             partial,\n718:         } if !partial => {\n719:             // The subject is read into a temp the way `expr`'s lambda chain reads it, but there\n720:             // is no lambda to contain it here, so the name has to be fresh rather than the\n721:             // fixed `subj`.\n722:             let subj = format!(\"tl_sub{}\", *fresh);\n723:             *fresh += 1;\n724:             let mut out = format!(\"{pad}{subj} = {}\\n\", expr(enums, subject));\n725:             for (i, arm) in arms.iter().enumerate() {\n726:                 let test = match (&arm.variant, &arm.guard) {\n727:                     (Some(v), _) if arm.payload.is_some() => Some(format!(\n728:                         \"(isinstance({subj}, dict) and {} in {subj})\",\n729:                         py_string(v)\n730:                     )),\n731:                     (Some(v), _) => Some(format!(\"{subj} == {}\", py_string(v))),\n732:                     (None, Some(g)) => Some(expr(enums, g)),\n733:                     (None, None) => None,\n734:                 };\n735:                 // A total chain's last arm carries no test, the checker having proved nothing\n736:                 // else can reach it -- the same rule `expr`'s match arm follows. Its statements\n737:                 // sit at the block level, not nested under a test.\n738:                 let unconditional = i + 1 == arms.len();\n739:                 let arm_level = if unconditional { level } else { level + 4 };\n740:                 let arm_pad = \" \".repeat(arm_level);\n741:                 let mut run = String::new();\n742:                 if let Some(pid) = arm.payload {\n743:                     let variant = arm\n744:                         .variant\n745:                         .as_ref()\n746:                         .expect(\"only a variant arm has a payload\");\n747:                     run.push_str(&format!(\n748:                         \"{arm_pad}{} = {subj}[{}]\\n\",\n749:                         local(pid),\n750:                         py_string(variant)\n751:                     ));\n752:                 }\n753:                 run.push_str(&tail_stmts(enums, name, param, fresh, &arm.body, arm_level));\n754:                 match (test, unconditional) {\n755:                     (Some(test), false) => out.push_str(&format!(\"{pad}if {test}:\\n{run}\")),\n756:                     _ => out.push_str(&run),\n757:                 }\n758:             }\n759:             out\n760:         }\n761:         // A partial match wraps every arm body, so no arm body is a tail position; the whole\n762:         // match stays an expression, exactly as it would outside tail position.\n763:         Kind::Match { .. } => format!(\"{pad}return {}\\n\", expr(enums, t)),\n764:         _ => format!(\"{pad}return {}\\n\", expr(enums, t)),\n765:     }\n766: }\n767: \n768: /// One arithmetic expression at the width the node's type names (kantord/toylang#83): the\n769: /// same emulation either way, through the 64-bit helpers when the type says so.\n770: fn arith(ty: &Type, op: BinOp, l: String, r: String) -> String {\n771:     if *ty == Type::Int64 {\n772:         match op {\n773:             BinOp::Div => format!(\"tl_div64({l}, {r})\"),\n774:             BinOp::Rem => format!(\"tl_rem64({l}, {r})\"),\n775:             BinOp::Add => format!(\"tl_i64({l} + {r})\"),\n776:             BinOp::Sub => format!(\"tl_i64({l} - {r})\"),\n777:             BinOp::Mul => format!(\"tl_i64({l} * {r})\"),\n778:             other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n779:         }\n780:     } else {\n781:         match op {\n782:             BinOp::Div => format!(\"tl_div({l}, {r})\"),\n783:             BinOp::Rem => format!(\"tl_rem({l}, {r})\"),\n784:             BinOp::Add => format!(\"tl_i32({l} + {r})\"),\n785:             BinOp::Sub => format!(\"tl_i32({l} - {r})\"),\n786:             BinOp::Mul => format!(\"tl_i32({l} * {r})\"),\n787:             other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n788:         }\n789:     }\n790: }\n791: \n792: fn py_op(op: BinOp) -> &'static str {\n793:     match op {\n794:         BinOp::Eq => \"==\",\n795:         BinOp::Ne => \"!=\",\n796:         BinOp::Lt => \"<\",\n797:         BinOp::Le => \"<=\",\n798:         BinOp::Gt => \">\",\n799:         BinOp::Ge => \">=\",\n800:         other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not a comparison\"),\n801:     }\n802: }\n803: \n804: /// The printer for one enum, inline. One type, two runtime shapes (ADR 0009): a unit variant is\n805: /// a bare string, a payload variant a single-key dict, so the shape is inspected before\n806: /// rendering. Which payload follows which key is still the type's knowledge, as everywhere else.\n807: fn show_enum(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type, value: &str, depth: usize) -> String {\n808:     let variants = ty::variants(enums, ty);\n809:     let n = format!(\"n{depth}\");\n810:     let payloads: Vec<&(String, Option<Type>)> =\n811:         variants.iter().filter(|(_, p)| p.is_some()).collect();\n812:     if payloads.is_empty() {\n813:         return format!(\"tl_quote({value})\");\n814:     }\n815:     let mut body = String::new();\n816:     if payloads.len() < variants.len() {\n817:         body.push_str(&format!(\"tl_quote({n}) if isinstance({n}, str) else \"));\n818:     }\n819:     for (i, (vname, pty)) in payloads.iter().enumerate() {\n820:         let pty = pty.as_ref().expect(\"filtered to payload variants\");\n821:         let read = format!(\"{n}[{}]\", py_string(vname));\n822:         let wrapped = format!(\n823:             \"({} + {} + \\\"}}\\\")\",\n824:             py_string(&format!(\"{{\\\"{vname}\\\":\")),\n825:             show(enums, pty, &read, depth + 1)\n826:         );\n827:         if i + 1 < payloads.len() {\n828:             body.push_str(&format!(\"{wrapped} if {} in {n} else \", py_string(vname)));\n829:         } else {\n830:             // The last payload variant needs no test: the type says nothing else is left.\n831:             body.push_str(&wrapped);\n832:         }\n833:     }\n834:     format!(\"(lambda {n}: {body})({value})\")\n835: }\n836: \n837: /// A named printer for every recursive enum the program prints. The call in `show` above is\n838: /// what a nested occurrence renders as, so the recursion in the type becomes recursion in the\n839: /// emitted function rather than in this compiler (kantord/toylang#94).\n840: fn printers(program: &Program) -> String {\n841:     let mut out = String::new();\n842:     for ty in tir::printed_recursive_enums(program) {\n843:         out.push_str(&format!(\n844:             \"def {}(v):\\n    return {}\\n\\n\\n\",\n845:             ty.show_fn(),\n846:             show_enum(&program.enums, &ty, \"v\", 0)\n847:         ));\n848:     }\n849:     out\n850: }\n851: \n852: fn py_string(s: &str) -> String {\n853:     let mut out = String::with_capacity(s.len() + 2);\n854:     out.push('\"');\n855:     for c in s.chars() {\n856:         match c {\n857:             '\"' => out.push_str(\"\\\\\\\"\"),\n858:             '\\\\' => out.push_str(\"\\\\\\\\\"),\n859:             '\\n' => out.push_str(\"\\\\n\"),\n860:             '\\r' => out.push_str(\"\\\\r\"),\n861:             '\\t' => out.push_str(\"\\\\t\"),\n862:             c if (c as u32) < 0x20 || c == '\\u{7f}' => {\n863:                 out.push_str(&format!(\"\\\\u{:04x}\", c as u32));\n864:             }\n865:             c => out.push(c),\n866:         }\n867:     }\n868:     out.push('\"');\n869:     out\n870: }\n\n(End of file - total 870 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! The Python backend.\n//!\n//! Python is dynamically typed, so the depth-polymorphic helpers that Go could not have are\n//! available again: one `tl_field(v, k, depth)` serves every shape. What it stresses instead is\n//! arithmetic, from a direction none of the other five come from. Its integers are exact and\n//! unbounded, so wrapping is emulation like jq's -- but unlike jq nothing is lost on the way,\n//! so the whole rule is one modulo rather than a split into 16-bit halves. Its `//` and `%` are\n//! floored, so truncated division needs writing out.\n//!\n//! One mapping is better here than anywhere else: a record is a dict, which is also what\n//! `json.loads` produces, so reading input is the parse and nothing more. Go needed a declared\n//! struct and a decoder to reach the same place.\n\nuse crate::ast::BinOp;\nuse crate::tir::{self, Builtin, Kind, LocalId, Program, Tir};\nuse crate::ty::{self, Enums, Type};\n\n/// The binding the input value is read into. Unspellable in source, since every source name is\n/// prefixed.\nconst INPUT: &str = \"t_input\";","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_py.rs","text":"//! The Python backend.\n//!\n//! Python is dynamically typed, so the depth-polymorphic helpers that Go could not have are\n//! available again: one `tl_field(v, k, depth)` serves every shape. What it stresses instead is\n//! arithmetic, from a direction none of the other five come from. Its integers are exact and\n//! unbounded, so wrapping is emulation like jq's -- but unlike jq nothing is lost on the way,\n//! so the whole rule is one modulo rather than a split into 16-bit halves. Its `//` and `%` are\n//! floored, so truncated division needs writing out.\n//!\n//! One mapping is better here than anywhere else: a record is a dict, which is also what\n//! `json.loads` produces, so reading input is the parse and nothing more. Go needed a declared\n//! struct and a decoder to reach the same place.\n\nuse crate::ast::BinOp;\nuse crate::tir::{self, Builtin, Kind, LocalId, Program, Tir};\nuse crate::ty::{self, Enums, Type};\n\n/// The binding the input value is read into. Unspellable in source, since every source name is\n/// prefixed.\nconst INPUT: &str = \"t_input\";\nconst INPUTS: &str = \"t_inputs\";\n\nconst FAIL_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_fail(msg):\n    sys.stderr.write(\"toylang: \" + msg + \"\\n\")\n    sys.exit(1)\n\"#;\n\n/// Python's integers do not overflow, so the 32-bit rule is entirely emulated. It is exact\n/// arithmetic all the way through, which is what lets one modulo stand for the whole thing:\n/// jq needed a 16-bit split only because a double loses the low bits of a 62-bit product.\nconst I32_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_i32(n):\n    return ((n + 2147483648) % 4294967296) - 2147483648\n\"#;\n\n/// The same one-modulo emulation at 64 bits, for `Int64` (kantord/toylang#83). Exact for the\n/// same reason `tl_i32` is: Python's integers never lose bits on the way to the modulo.\nconst I64_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_i64(n):\n    return ((n + 9223372036854775808) % 18446744073709551616) - 9223372036854775808\n\"#;\n\n/// Python floors, and `int(a / b)` would truncate through a float and lose bits on the way. The\n/// remainder takes its sign from the dividend, so `a == tl_div(a, b) * b + tl_rem(a, b)` holds.\nconst ARITH_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_div(a, b):\n    if b == 0:\n        tl_fail(\"divided by zero\")\n    q = abs(a) // abs(b)\n    return tl_i32(-q if (a < 0) != (b < 0) else q)\n\n\ndef tl_rem(a, b):\n    if b == 0:\n        tl_fail(\"divided by zero\")\n    r = abs(a) % abs(b)\n    return -r if a < 0 else r\n\"#;\n\n/// `tl_div`/`tl_rem` at 64 bits. Only the quotient can leave the range (`MIN / -1`), so the\n/// remainder needs no wrap: its magnitude is strictly below the divisor's.\nconst ARITH64_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_div64(a, b):\n    if b == 0:\n        tl_fail(\"divided by zero\")\n    q = abs(a) // abs(b)\n    return tl_i64(-q if (a < 0) != (b < 0) else q)\n\n\ndef tl_rem64(a, b):\n    if b == 0:\n        tl_fail(\"divided by zero\")\n    r = abs(a) % abs(b)\n    return -r if a < 0 else r\n\"#;\n\nconst FIELD_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_field(v, k, depth):\n    if depth == 0:\n        return v[k]\n    return [tl_field(e, k, depth - 1) for e in v]\n\"#;\n\n/// An Opt is its enum's own runtime shape (ADR 0009): `{\"some\": v}` present, `\"none\"` absent.\n/// Tagged, so two levels of absence stay two values; only the printer flattens to null.\nconst AT_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_at(v, i, depth):\n    if depth > 0:\n        return [tl_at(e, i, depth - 1) for e in v]\n    n = len(v)\n    if i < 0:\n        i = n + i\n    if i < 0 or i >= n:\n        return \"none\"\n    return {\"some\": v[i]}\n\"#;\n\n/// Python's own slicing already clamps out-of-range bounds and counts negatives from the end,\n/// so jq's boundary behaviour is the target's native one; `None` is a bound left out.\nconst SLICE_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_slice(v, lo, hi, depth):\n    if depth > 0:\n        return [tl_slice(e, lo, hi, depth - 1) for e in v]\n    return v[lo:hi]\n\"#;\n\nconst UNWRAP_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_unwrap(v, depth):\n    if depth > 0:\n        return [tl_unwrap(e, depth - 1) for e in v]\n    if v == \"none\":\n        tl_fail(\"unwrapped a value that is not there\")\n    return v[\"some\"]\n\"#;\n\nconst TAIL_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_tail(v):\n    if len(v) == 0:\n        return \"none\"\n    return {\"some\": v[1:]}\n\"#;\n\nconst FLATTEN_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_flatten(vv):\n    return [e for sub in vv for e in sub]\n\"#;\n\nconst COLLECT_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_collect_lines():\n    out = []\n    for line in sys.stdin:\n        out.append(line[:-1] if line.endswith(\"\\n\") else line)\n    return out\n\"#;\n\nconst RANGE_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_range(n):\n    return list(range(max(0, n)))\n\"#;\n\n/// Python 3 strings are already sequences of Unicode scalar values, so iterating one already\n/// decodes by codepoint; `ord` is the cast down to the number every other backend represents a\n/// `Char` as.\nconst CHARS_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_chars(s):\n    return [ord(c) for c in s]\n\"#;\n\nconst JOIN_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_join(v, f):\n    return \"[\" + \",\".join(f(e) for e in v) + \"]\"\n\"#;\n\nconst JSONLINES_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_jsonlines(v, f):\n    return \"\\n\".join(f(e) for e in v)\n\"#;\n\n/// Python's integers are unbounded, so both widths wrap by emulation -- `tl_i32` for Int and\n/// `tl_i64` for Int64, the same two helpers arithmetic already uses.\nconst SUM_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_sum(v):\n    acc = 0\n    for x in v:\n        acc = tl_i32(acc + x)\n    return acc\n\n\ndef tl_sum64(v):\n    acc = 0\n    for x in v:\n        acc = tl_i64(acc + x)\n    return acc\n\"#;\n\nconst MAX_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_max(v):\n    if len(v) == 0:\n        return \"none\"\n    return {\"some\": max(v)}\n\"#;\n\n/// Iterating characters rather than bytes, which agrees with the C runtime's byte loop because\n/// the two differ only above U+007F, where both pass the value through unchanged.\nconst QUOTE_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_quote(s):\n    out = ['\"']\n    for c in s:\n        if c == '\"':\n            out.append('\\\\\"')\n        elif c == \"\\\\\":\n            out.append(\"\\\\\\\\\")\n        elif c == \"\\n\":\n            out.append(\"\\\\n\")\n        elif c == \"\\r\":\n            out.append(\"\\\\r\")\n        elif c == \"\\t\":\n            out.append(\"\\\\t\")\n        elif ord(c) < 0x20:\n            out.append(\"\\\\u%04x\" % ord(c))\n        else:\n            out.append(c)\n    out.append('\"')\n    return \"\".join(out)\n\"#;\n\npub fn emit(program: &Program) -> String {\n    let enums = &program.enums;\n    // Before the program's own functions, so a printer is defined by the time anything calls\n    // one, and so the helper scan below sees what a printer body uses.\n    let mut decls = printers(program);\n\n    // Module-level functions are looked up when called, not when defined, so the forward\n    // reference the checker accepts costs nothing here. Lua needed declarations, JavaScript\n    // relied on hoisting, and jq could not express it at all.\n    for f in &program.funcs {\n        let param = f.param.as_deref().map(user);\n        if tir::has_tail_call(&f.name, &f.body) {\n            // The contract: a self-tail-call runs in constant stack. A tail call becomes\n            // `param = arg` then `continue` in a loop, so 100k-deep self-recursion cannot hit\n            // Python's recursion limit the way a real call would (kantord/toylang#141).\n            let mut fresh = 0;\n            decls.push_str(&format!(\n                \"def {}({}):\\n    while True:\\n{}\\n\\n\\n\",\n                user(&f.name),\n                param.as_deref().unwrap_or_default(),\n                tail_stmts(enums, &f.name, param.as_deref(), &mut fresh, &f.body, 8)\n            ));\n        } else {\n            decls.push_str(&format!(\n                \"def {}({}):\\n    return {}\\n\\n\\n\",\n                user(&f.name),\n                param.as_deref().unwrap_or_default(),\n                expr(enums, &f.body)\n            ));\n        }\n    }\n\n    if let Some(fusion) = tir::fusion(program) {\n        decls.push_str(&fused_main(program, &fusion));\n    } else {\n        if program.input.is_some() {\n            decls.push_str(&format!(\n                \"{INPUT} = json.loads(sys.stdin.buffer.read().decode(\\\"utf-8\\\"))\\n\"\n            ));\n        }\n        if program.inputs.is_some() {\n            decls.push_str(&format!(\n                \"{INPUTS} = [json.loads(_l) for _l in sys.stdin]\\n\"\n            ));\n        }\n\n        let body = expr(enums, &program.body);\n        // A top-level Str prints raw, the way jq's -r does; anything else prints as JSON.\n        let printed = if matches!(program.body.ty, Type::Str | Type::Sink) {\n            body\n        } else {\n            show(enums, &program.body.ty, &body, 0)\n        };\n        // Bytes rather than `print`, so output does not depend on the locale the interpreter was\n        // started under. The native backend writes bytes for the same reason.\n        decls.push_str(&format!(\n            \"sys.stdout.buffer.write(({printed} + \\\"\\\\n\\\").encode(\\\"utf-8\\\"))\\n\"\n        ));\n    }\n\n    // Which helpers to include is read off the emitted text. Python objects to neither an unused\n    // function nor an unused import, so nothing here can break the way it would in Go; the\n    // helper-to-helper edges are still stated rather than read, since inclusion is what pulls\n    // them in.\n    let uses = |name: &str| decls.contains(name);\n    let unwrap = uses(\"tl_unwrap(\");\n    let arith = uses(\"tl_div(\") || uses(\"tl_rem(\");\n    let arith64 = uses(\"tl_div64(\") || uses(\"tl_rem64(\");\n\n    let mut helpers = false;\n    let mut out = String::from(\"import sys\\n\");\n    if program.input.is_some() || program.inputs.is_some() {\n        out.push_str(\"import json\\n\");\n    }\n    // Python's default ceiling (1000) is far below what a compiled-style recursive program\n    // needs: Euler 11's self-tail-recursive fold alone is ~1300 frames deep. Raise it before\n    // any program code runs; 100000 is comfortably above the thousands real programs reach.\n    out.push_str(\"sys.setrecursionlimit(100000)\\n\");\n    out.push('\\n');\n    for (on, text) in [\n        (unwrap || arith || arith64, FAIL_HELPER),\n        (arith || uses(\"tl_i32(\") || uses(\"tl_sum(\"), I32_HELPER),\n        (arith64 || uses(\"tl_i64(\") || uses(\"tl_sum64(\"), I64_HELPER),\n        (arith, ARITH_HELPER),\n        (arith64, ARITH64_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tl_field(\"), FIELD_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tl_at(\"), AT_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tl_slice(\"), SLICE_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tl_tail(\"), TAIL_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tl_flatten(\"), FLATTEN_HELPER),\n        (unwrap, UNWRAP_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tl_range(\"), RANGE_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tl_chars(\"), CHARS_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tl_sum(\") || uses(\"tl_sum64(\"), SUM_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tl_max(\"), MAX_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tl_collect_lines(\"), COLLECT_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tl_join(\"), JOIN_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tl_jsonlines(\"), JSONLINES_HELPER),\n        (uses(\"tl_quote(\"), QUOTE_HELPER),\n    ] {\n        if on {\n            out.push_str(\"\\n\\n\");\n            out.push_str(text);\n            helpers = true;\n        }\n    }\n    if helpers {\n        out.push_str(\"\\n\\n\");\n    }\n    out.push_str(&decls);\n    out\n}\n\n/// A stream-typed `jsonlines` program, compiled as a loop reading one entry at a time from\n/// `sys.stdin` (which iterates lazily) rather than the eager path's read-everything-first.\n/// `sys.stdout.buffer` is block-buffered whenever it is not a terminal, the same as the eager\n/// path already writes bytes rather than using `print` for, so the explicit `.flush()` after\n/// each line is what makes a record appear before the next one arrives rather than after the\n/// whole run ends.\nfn fused_main(program: &Program, fusion: &tir::Fusion) -> String {\n    let enums = &program.enums;\n    let mut out = String::new();\n    let (mut current, mut current_ty) = match fusion.source {\n        tir::Source::Inputs => {\n            out.push_str(\"for _line in sys.stdin:\\n\");\n            out.push_str(\"    _line = _line[:-1] if _line.endswith(\\\"\\\\n\\\") else _line\\n\");\n            out.push_str(\"    if _line.strip() == \\\"\\\":\\n        continue\\n\");\n            out.push_str(\"    t_line = json.loads(_line)\\n\");\n            let elem = program\n                .inputs\n                .as_ref()\n                .expect(\"an inputs source recorded its element\");\n            (\"t_line\".to_string(), elem.clone())\n        }\n        // A raw line is already the element, blank ones included: `lines` keeps them.\n        tir::Source::Lines => {\n            out.push_str(\"for _line in sys.stdin:\\n\");\n            out.push_str(\"    _line = _line[:-1] if _line.endswith(\\\"\\\\n\\\") else _line\\n\");\n            (\"_line\".to_string(), Type::Str)\n        }\n        // The bound is evaluated once; the loop counter is the element. A negative bound makes\n        // Python's own range empty, the same answer `tl_range` gives eagerly.\n        tir::Source::Range(bound) => {\n            out.push_str(&format!(\"for t_i in range({}):\\n\", expr(enums, bound)));\n            (\"t_i\".to_string(), Type::Int)\n        }\n    };\n    for stage in &fusion.stages {\n        match stage {\n            tir::Stage::Map { param, body } => {\n                out.push_str(&format!(\"    {} = {}\\n\", local(*param), current));\n                current = expr(enums, body);\n                current_ty = body.ty.clone();\n            }\n            tir::Stage::Select { param, pred } => {\n                out.push_str(&format!(\"    {} = {}\\n\", local(*param), current));\n                out.push_str(&format!(\n                    \"    if not ({}):\\n        continue\\n\",\n                    expr(enums, pred)\n                ));\n                current = local(*param);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    let printed = show(enums, &current_ty, &current, 0);\n    out.push_str(&format!(\n        \"    sys.stdout.buffer.write(({printed} + \\\"\\\\n\\\").encode(\\\"utf-8\\\"))\\n\"\n    ));\n    out.push_str(\"    sys.stdout.buffer.flush()\\n\");\n    out\n}\n\n/// The printer is built from the type rather than by inspecting the value, as on every backend.\n/// Here the type is doing one thing it does nowhere else: `str` on a Bool gives `True`, so the\n/// two JSON words have to be written out.\nfn show(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type, value: &str, depth: usize) -> String {\n    match ty {\n        Type::Param(_) => unreachable!(\"params are substituted before emit\"),\n        // The checker refuses a program whose result contains a stream, since there is nothing to\n        // print: a stream has no value, only a promise that collect can redeem.\n        Type::Stream(_) => unreachable!(\"a stream cannot reach the printer\"),\n        Type::Char => unreachable!(\"Char cannot reach the printer, refused by the checker\"),\n        Type::Str => format!(\"tl_quote({value})\"),\n        Type::Sink => unreachable!(\"a sink only ever prints raw, never through the printer\"),\n        Type::Int | Type::Int64 => format!(\"str({value})\"),\n        Type::Float => unreachable!(\"Float is JS-only in this row\"),\n        Type::Bool => format!(\"(\\\"true\\\" if {value} else \\\"false\\\")\"),\n        Type::Vec(elem) => {\n            let e = format!(\"e{depth}\");\n            format!(\n                \"tl_join({value}, lambda {e}: {})\",\n                show(enums, elem, &e, depth + 1)\n            )\n        }\n        Type::Enum { .. } if ty.as_opt().is_some() => {\n            let inner = ty.as_opt().expect(\"guarded\");\n            let v = format!(\"o{depth}\");\n            format!(\n                \"(lambda {v}: \\\"null\\\" if {v} == \\\"none\\\" else {})({value})\",\n                show(enums, inner, &format!(\"{v}[\\\"some\\\"]\"), depth + 1)\n            )\n        }\n        // A recursive enum prints through a function of its own (`printers`), because expanding\n        // one here has no bottom: its payload leads back to the same type.\n        Type::Enum { .. } if ty::is_recursive(enums, ty) => format!(\"{}({value})\", ty.show_fn()),\n        Type::Enum { .. } => show_enum(enums, ty, value, depth),\n        Type::Record(fields) => {\n            if fields.is_empty() {\n                return \"\\\"{}\\\"\".to_string();\n            }\n            // The type's field list is declaration order, so this prints as declared. Field\n            // names are identifiers, so the JSON key needs no escaping and is one literal.\n            let parts: Vec<String> = fields\n                .iter()\n                .map(|(name, fty)| {\n                    let read = format!(\"{value}[{}]\", py_string(name));\n                    let key = py_string(&format!(\"\\\"{name}\\\":\"));\n                    format!(\"{key} + {}\", show(enums, fty, &read, depth + 1))\n                })\n                .collect();\n            format!(\"(\\\"{{\\\" + {} + \\\"}}\\\")\", parts.join(\" + \\\",\\\" + \"))\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn user(name: &str) -> String {\n    format!(\"v_{name}\")\n}\n\nfn local(id: LocalId) -> String {\n    format!(\"t_{id}\")\n}\n\nfn expr(enums: &Enums, t: &Tir) -> String {\n    match &t.kind {\n        Kind::Str(s) => py_string(s),\n        Kind::Int(n) => n.to_string(),\n        Kind::Float(_) => unreachable!(\"Float is JS-only in this row\"),\n        Kind::Var(name) => user(name),\n        Kind::Local(id) => local(*id),\n        Kind::Input => INPUT.to_string(),\n        Kind::Inputs => INPUTS.to_string(),\n        // The stream, materialized eagerly: whatever consumes it -- `collect`, a mapper --\n        // works on the Vec of its entries. Fusion is what will remove this materialization.\n        Kind::Lines => \"tl_collect_lines()\".to_string(),\n        // Same raw lines as `lines`, each split on the delimiter into one row.\n        Kind::Dsv { delim } => format!(\n            \"[l.split({}) for l in tl_collect_lines()]\",\n            py_string(delim)\n        ),\n        Kind::RecordLit { fields } => {\n            let parts: Vec<String> = fields\n                .iter()\n                .map(|(name, value)| format!(\"{}: {}\", py_string(name), expr(enums, value)))\n                .collect();\n            format!(\"{{{}}}\", parts.join(\", \"))\n        }\n\n        // The value is its JSON shape: a unit variant is the variant-name string, a payload\n        // variant the single-key dict a record already is.\n        Kind::EnumLit { variant, payload } => match payload {\n            None => py_string(variant),\n            Some(p) => format!(\"{{{}: {}}}\", py_string(variant), expr(enums, p)),\n        },\n\n        Kind::VecLit(items) => {\n            let parts: Vec<String> = items.iter().map(|i| expr(enums, i)).collect();\n            format!(\"[{}]\", parts.join(\", \"))\n        }\n        Kind::Call { func, arg } => format!(\n            \"{}({})\",\n            user(func),\n            arg.as_deref().map_or_else(String::new, |a| expr(enums, a))\n        ),\n        // Python's own `+` already concatenates two lists the way it concatenates two strings,\n        // so a Vec needs no different spelling here than Str does.\n        Kind::Concat(l, r) => format!(\"({} + {})\", expr(enums, l), expr(enums, r)),\n        Kind::Arith { op, lhs, rhs } => arith(&t.ty, *op, expr(enums, lhs), expr(enums, rhs)),\n        Kind::Builtin { which, arg } => match which {\n            Builtin::IntToStr => format!(\"str({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            // Python's integers are one type at every width, so the bridge has nothing to do.\n            Builtin::IntToI64 => expr(enums, arg),\n            Builtin::Range => format!(\"tl_range({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            Builtin::Chars => format!(\"tl_chars({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            Builtin::JsonLines => {\n                let elem = tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty).expect(\"checked to be a Vec or a stream\");\n                let e = \"e0\".to_string();\n                format!(\n                    \"tl_jsonlines({}, lambda {e}: {})\",\n                    expr(enums, arg),\n                    show(enums, elem, &e, 1)\n                )\n            }\n            // The source already materialized, so the exit has nothing left to do.\n            Builtin::Collect => expr(enums, arg),\n            Builtin::Length => format!(\"len({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            Builtin::Tail => format!(\"tl_tail({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            Builtin::Flatten => format!(\"tl_flatten({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            // Python compares both numbers and strings (by codepoint) with `<` natively, so\n            // `sorted` needs no key or comparator.\n            Builtin::Sort => format!(\"sorted({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            Builtin::Reverse => format!(\"({})[::-1]\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            // Which fold depends on the element width only for the wrap: Python has one integer\n            // type, so the two differ in which modulo they apply, not in the value type.\n            Builtin::Sum => {\n                if tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty) == Some(&Type::Int) {\n                    format!(\"tl_sum({})\", expr(enums, arg))\n                } else {\n                    format!(\"tl_sum64({})\", expr(enums, arg))\n                }\n            }\n            Builtin::Max => format!(\"tl_max({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            // The names come from the checked type, not the dict value, so `arg` runs as the\n            // lambda's ignored argument -- the same shape `Bind` uses -- purely for whatever\n            // else it does.\n            Builtin::Fields => {\n                let Type::Record(fields) = &arg.ty else {\n                    unreachable!(\"checked to be a record\")\n                };\n                let names: Vec<String> = fields.iter().map(|(n, _)| py_string(n)).collect();\n                format!(\"(lambda _: [{}])({})\", names.join(\", \"), expr(enums, arg))\n            }\n        },\n        Kind::Compare { op, lhs, rhs } => {\n            format!(\"({} {} {})\", expr(enums, lhs), py_op(*op), expr(enums, rhs))\n        }\n        // Python spells and short-circuits both of these the way toylang does.\n        Kind::Logic { op, lhs, rhs } => format!(\"({} {op} {})\", expr(enums, lhs), expr(enums, rhs)),\n        Kind::Not(base) => format!(\"(not {})\", expr(enums, base)),\n        Kind::Bind {\n            local: id,\n            value,\n            body,\n        } => {\n            format!(\n                \"(lambda {}: {})({})\",\n                local(*id),\n                expr(enums, body),\n                expr(enums, value)\n            )\n        }\n        // A comprehension rather than `map`, which returns an iterator here and would need a\n        // `list` around it anyway.\n        Kind::Map {\n            source,\n            param,\n            body,\n        } => {\n            format!(\n                \"[{} for {} in {}]\",\n                expr(enums, body),\n                local(*param),\n                expr(enums, source)\n            )\n        }\n        Kind::Select {\n            source,\n            param,\n            pred,\n        } => {\n            let p = local(*param);\n            format!(\n                \"[{p} for {p} in {} if {}]\",\n                expr(enums, source),\n                expr(enums, pred)\n            )\n        }\n        Kind::Unwrap { base } => {\n            format!(\n                \"tl_unwrap({}, {})\",\n                expr(enums, base),\n                tir::vec_depth(&base.ty)\n            )\n        }\n        // Opt's reorder pass (kantord/toylang#66): the same `== \"none\"`/`[\"some\"]` shape the\n        // printer and Match already read, generalised to rebuild the dict instead.\n        Kind::OptMap {\n            source,\n            param,\n            body,\n        } => {\n            format!(\n                \"(lambda __opt: \\\"none\\\" if __opt == \\\"none\\\" else {{\\\"some\\\": (lambda {}: {})(__opt[\\\"some\\\"])}})({})\",\n                local(*param),\n                expr(enums, body),\n                expr(enums, source)\n            )\n        }\n        Kind::Index {\n            base, index, depth, ..\n        } => {\n            format!(\n                \"tl_at({}, {}, {})\",\n                expr(enums, base),\n                expr(enums, index),\n                depth\n            )\n        }\n        Kind::Slice {\n            base, start, end, depth,\n        } => {\n            let lo = match start {\n                Some(s) => expr(enums, s),\n                None => \"None\".to_string(),\n            };\n            let hi = match end {\n                Some(e) => expr(enums, e),\n                None => \"None\".to_string(),\n            };\n            format!(\n                \"tl_slice({}, {}, {}, {})\",\n                expr(enums, base),\n                lo,\n                hi,\n                depth\n            )\n        }\n        Kind::Field { base, name } => {\n            let depth = tir::vec_depth(&base.ty);\n            if depth == 0 {\n                format!(\"{}[{}]\", expr(enums, base), py_string(name))\n            } else {\n                format!(\n                    \"tl_field({}, {}, {})\",\n                    expr(enums, base),\n                    py_string(name),\n                    depth\n                )\n            }\n        }\n        // A right-fold of conditional expressions over the subject, tests first-match-wins; a\n        // guard arm's test is the guard itself. A total chain's last arm carries no test, the\n        // checker having proved nothing else can reach it; a partial chain tests every arm and\n        // bottoms out at `None`, the absent Opt. The payload key test needs the isinstance\n        // guard: `\"a\" in v` on a unit variant's *string* would be a substring test, and\n        // `\"a\" in \"ab\"` answers yes.\n        Kind::Match {\n            subject,\n            arms,\n            partial,\n        } => {\n            let subj = expr(enums, subject);\n            let mut out = String::new();\n            let mut closing = 0;\n            for (i, arm) in arms.iter().enumerate() {\n                let run = match arm.payload {\n                    Some(pid) => {\n                        let variant = arm\n                            .variant\n                            .as_ref()\n                            .expect(\"only a variant arm has a payload\");\n                        format!(\n                            \"(lambda {}: {})({subj}[{}])\",\n                            local(pid),\n                            expr(enums, &arm.body),\n                            py_string(variant)\n                        )\n                    }\n                    None => expr(enums, &arm.body),\n                };\n                let test = match (&arm.variant, &arm.guard) {\n                    (Some(v), _) if arm.payload.is_some() => Some(format!(\n                        \"(isinstance({subj}, dict) and {} in {subj})\",\n                        py_string(v)\n                    )),\n                    (Some(v), _) => Some(format!(\"{subj} == {}\", py_string(v))),\n                    (None, Some(g)) => Some(expr(enums, g)),\n                    (None, None) => None,\n                };\n                // A partial chain's yield is an Opt, so a present arm is tagged.\n                let run = if *partial {\n                    format!(\"{{\\\"some\\\": {run}}}\")\n                } else {\n                    run\n                };\n                match test {\n                    Some(test) if *partial || i + 1 < arms.len() => {\n                        out.push_str(&format!(\"({run} if {test} else \"));\n                        closing += 1;\n                    }\n                    _ => out.push_str(&run),\n                }\n            }\n            if *partial {\n                out.push_str(\"\\\"none\\\"\");\n            }\n            out.push_str(&\")\".repeat(closing));\n            format!(\"({out})\")\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n/// `t` as statements in the tail position, each line already padded to `level` columns:\n/// `return <expr>` for a base case, and for a tail call `param = <arg>` followed by `continue`\n/// so the emitted `while True` rewinds instead of recursing against Python's interpreter\n/// recursion limit. A total `Match`'s arm bodies nest one indent deeper, the way Python needs.\nfn tail_stmts(\n    enums: &Enums,\n    name: &str,\n    param: Option<&str>,\n    fresh: &mut usize,\n    t: &Tir,\n    level: usize,\n) -> String {\n    let pad = \" \".repeat(level);\n    match &t.kind {\n        Kind::Call { func, arg } if func == name => {\n            let assign = param.map_or_else(String::new, |p| {\n                format!(\n                    \"{pad}{p} = {}\\n\",\n                    arg.as_deref().map_or_else(String::new, |a| expr(enums, a))\n                )\n            });\n            format!(\"{assign}{pad}continue\\n\")\n        }\n        Kind::Bind {\n            local: id,\n            value,\n            body,\n        } => format!(\n            \"{pad}{} = {}\\n{}\",\n            local(*id),\n            expr(enums, value),\n            tail_stmts(enums, name, param, fresh, body, level),\n        ),\n        Kind::Match {\n            subject,\n            arms,\n            partial,\n        } if !partial => {\n            // The subject is read into a temp the way `expr`'s lambda chain reads it, but there\n            // is no lambda to contain it here, so the name has to be fresh rather than the\n            // fixed `subj`.\n            let subj = format!(\"tl_sub{}\", *fresh);\n            *fresh += 1;\n            let mut out = format!(\"{pad}{subj} = {}\\n\", expr(enums, subject));\n            for (i, arm) in arms.iter().enumerate() {\n                let test = match (&arm.variant, &arm.guard) {\n                    (Some(v), _) if arm.payload.is_some() => Some(format!(\n                        \"(isinstance({subj}, dict) and {} in {subj})\",\n                        py_string(v)\n                    )),\n                    (Some(v), _) => Some(format!(\"{subj} == {}\", py_string(v))),\n                    (None, Some(g)) => Some(expr(enums, g)),\n                    (None, None) => None,\n                };\n                // A total chain's last arm carries no test, the checker having proved nothing\n                // else can reach it -- the same rule `expr`'s match arm follows. Its statements\n                // sit at the block level, not nested under a test.\n                let unconditional = i + 1 == arms.len();\n                let arm_level = if unconditional { level } else { level + 4 };\n                let arm_pad = \" \".repeat(arm_level);\n                let mut run = String::new();\n                if let Some(pid) = arm.payload {\n                    let variant = arm\n                        .variant\n                        .as_ref()\n                        .expect(\"only a variant arm has a payload\");\n                    run.push_str(&format!(\n                        \"{arm_pad}{} = {subj}[{}]\\n\",\n                        local(pid),\n                        py_string(variant)\n                    ));\n                }\n                run.push_str(&tail_stmts(enums, name, param, fresh, &arm.body, arm_level));\n                match (test, unconditional) {\n                    (Some(test), false) => out.push_str(&format!(\"{pad}if {test}:\\n{run}\")),\n                    _ => out.push_str(&run),\n                }\n            }\n            out\n        }\n        // A partial match wraps every arm body, so no arm body is a tail position; the whole\n        // match stays an expression, exactly as it would outside tail position.\n        Kind::Match { .. } => format!(\"{pad}return {}\\n\", expr(enums, t)),\n        _ => format!(\"{pad}return {}\\n\", expr(enums, t)),\n    }\n}\n\n/// One arithmetic expression at the width the node's type names (kantord/toylang#83): the\n/// same emulation either way, through the 64-bit helpers when the type says so.\nfn arith(ty: &Type, op: BinOp, l: String, r: String) -> String {\n    if *ty == Type::Int64 {\n        match op {\n            BinOp::Div => format!(\"tl_div64({l}, {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Rem => format!(\"tl_rem64({l}, {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Add => format!(\"tl_i64({l} + {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Sub => format!(\"tl_i64({l} - {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Mul => format!(\"tl_i64({l} * {r})\"),\n            other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n        }\n    } else {\n        match op {\n            BinOp::Div => format!(\"tl_div({l}, {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Rem => format!(\"tl_rem({l}, {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Add => format!(\"tl_i32({l} + {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Sub => format!(\"tl_i32({l} - {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Mul => format!(\"tl_i32({l} * {r})\"),\n            other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn py_op(op: BinOp) -> &'static str {\n    match op {\n        BinOp::Eq => \"==\",\n        BinOp::Ne => \"!=\",\n        BinOp::Lt => \"<\",\n        BinOp::Le => \"<=\",\n        BinOp::Gt => \">\",\n        BinOp::Ge => \">=\",\n        other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not a comparison\"),\n    }\n}\n\n/// The printer for one enum, inline. One type, two runtime shapes (ADR 0009): a unit variant is\n/// a bare string, a payload variant a single-key dict, so the shape is inspected before\n/// rendering. Which payload follows which key is still the type's knowledge, as everywhere else.\nfn show_enum(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type, value: &str, depth: usize) -> String {\n    let variants = ty::variants(enums, ty);\n    let n = format!(\"n{depth}\");\n    let payloads: Vec<&(String, Option<Type>)> =\n        variants.iter().filter(|(_, p)| p.is_some()).collect();\n    if payloads.is_empty() {\n        return format!(\"tl_quote({value})\");\n    }\n    let mut body = String::new();\n    if payloads.len() < variants.len() {\n        body.push_str(&format!(\"tl_quote({n}) if isinstance({n}, str) else \"));\n    }\n    for (i, (vname, pty)) in payloads.iter().enumerate() {\n        let pty = pty.as_ref().expect(\"filtered to payload variants\");\n        let read = format!(\"{n}[{}]\", py_string(vname));\n        let wrapped = format!(\n            \"({} + {} + \\\"}}\\\")\",\n            py_string(&format!(\"{{\\\"{vname}\\\":\")),\n            show(enums, pty, &read, depth + 1)\n        );\n        if i + 1 < payloads.len() {\n            body.push_str(&format!(\"{wrapped} if {} in {n} else \", py_string(vname)));\n        } else {\n            // The last payload variant needs no test: the type says nothing else is left.\n            body.push_str(&wrapped);\n        }\n    }\n    format!(\"(lambda {n}: {body})({value})\")\n}\n\n/// A named printer for every recursive enum the program prints. The call in `show` above is\n/// what a nested occurrence renders as, so the recursion in the type becomes recursion in the\n/// emitted function rather than in this compiler (kantord/toylang#94).\nfn printers(program: &Program) -> String {\n    let mut out = String::new();\n    for ty in tir::printed_recursive_enums(program) {\n        out.push_str(&format!(\n            \"def {}(v):\\n    return {}\\n\\n\\n\",\n            ty.show_fn(),\n            show_enum(&program.enums, &ty, \"v\", 0)\n        ));\n    }\n    out\n}\n\nfn py_string(s: &str) -> String {\n    let mut out = String::with_capacity(s.len() + 2);\n    out.push('\"');\n    for c in s.chars() {\n        match c {\n            '\"' => out.push_str(\"\\\\\\\"\"),\n            '\\\\' => out.push_str(\"\\\\\\\\\"),\n            '\\n' => out.push_str(\"\\\\n\"),\n            '\\r' => out.push_str(\"\\\\r\"),\n            '\\t' => out.push_str(\"\\\\t\"),\n            c if (c as u32) < 0x20 || c == '\\u{7f}' => {\n                out.push_str(&format!(\"\\\\u{:04x}\", c as u32));\n            }\n            c => out.push(c),\n        }\n    }\n    out.push('\"');\n    out\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":870,"totalLines":870,"truncated":false}},"title":"src/emit_py.rs","time":{"start":1788464515616,"end":1788464515620}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The Builtin enum is unary. For sort_by and max_by, we need a Vec argument plus a projection. Since the projection is a function-like expression (`map(.name)` machinery), the natural TIR shape mirrors `Map`: `source`, `param`, `body`. Actually wait — the projection body is an expression that can reference `.` (the element). But how does the pipe mechanism work for `map`? `[1,2] | map(. * 2)` becomes `Kind::Map { source, param, body }`.\n\nFor `v | sort_by(.key)`, we'd want a new Kind like `SortBy { source, param, body }` where `body` is the projection (checked in mapper context), and the ordering is by the projection's value.\n\n Similarly `MaxBy { source, param, body }`.\n\nAlternatively, we could reuse the `Map` mechanism by first mapping to keys and sorting... but that loses the original elements. jq's `sort_by(f)` sorts by the projected value while keeping the original. So we need the element + key. The TIR should carry the projection body, and backends compute key per element then sort by (key, element) with stable sort or by key only (with tie-breaking by original order for stability?).\n\njq's sort_by is stable. For sort_by, ties should retain original order (stable sort). Python's sorted is stable. The simplest: sort indices or pair (key, index) then stable-sort. \n\nLet me look at how each backend emits Map/Select to understand the runtime shapes, and how sort/max are emitted. Then I'll design the SortBy/MaxBy lowering.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068caca1e001F4tv9EMPPs8c5R","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","messageID":"msg_068cab9270017Qz5zlYK09qS45"}}
