{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788464524054,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_9c48ab36847847faa3703327","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/check/mod.rs","limit":130,"offset":1780},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/check/mod.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1780: \n1781:         // `v[]` with nothing after it is the identity: \"keep every entry\" is what a Vec (or\n1782:         // stream) already is, with no field/index/unwrap left to distribute across it.\n1783:         Expr::Project { .. } => {\n1784:             let access = access(ctx, expr)?;\n1785:             Ok(access.tir)\n1786:         }\n1787: \n1788:         // `input` names one value, so its first checked use fixes the type for the whole\n1789:         // program -- and a later use in a position that expects nothing can borrow what the\n1790:         // first use fixed. With no use typed yet, nothing says what it contains, and guessing\n1791:         // is what the annotation rule avoids.\n1792:         Expr::Input { span } => match ctx.input.borrow().as_ref() {\n1793:             Some(ty) => Ok(Tir::new(ty.clone(), Kind::Input)),\n1794:             None => Err(Error::new(*span, \"cannot tell what `input` contains\")),\n1795:         },\n1796:         Expr::Inputs { span } => match ctx.in_fn {\n1797:             Some(func) => Err(source_in_fn(*span, \"inputs\", func)),\n1798:             None => Err(Error::new(*span, \"cannot tell what `inputs` contains\")),\n1799:         },\n1800: \n1801:         Expr::Call {\n1802:             func,\n1803:             func_span,\n1804:             arg,\n1805:             span,\n1806:         } => call(ctx, func, *func_span, arg, *span),\n1807: \n1808:         Expr::Match { arms, span } => match_chain(ctx, arms, *span, None),\n1809: \n1810:         Expr::Variant {\n1811:             enum_name,\n1812:             enum_span,\n1813:             variant,\n1814:             variant_span,\n1815:             payload,\n1816:             ..\n1817:         } => {\n1818:             let Some(enum_ty) = ctx.enums.get(enum_name) else {\n1819:                 return Err(Error::new(\n1820:                     *enum_span,\n1821:                     format!(\"`{enum_name}` is not an enum\"),\n1822:                 ));\n1823:             };\n1824:             construct(\n1825:                 ctx,\n1826:                 &enum_ty.clone(),\n1827:                 variant,\n1828:                 *variant_span,\n1829:                 payload.as_deref(),\n1830:                 None,\n1831:                 false,\n1832:             )\n1833:         }\n1834: \n1835:         Expr::Neg { base, span } => {\n1836:             // A minus directly on a literal is part of the literal, so the most negative Int can\n1837:             // be written even though its magnitude is one past the most positive. This is the\n1838:             // rule Rust uses, and it is why `-` was not folded into the lexer: `a -1` has to\n1839:             // stay `a - 1`.\n1840:             if let Expr::Int { value, span: lit } = base.as_ref() {\n1841:                 if *value > -(i32::MIN as i64) {\n1842:                     return Err(Error::new(\n1843:                         *lit,\n1844:                         format!(\n1845:                             \"integer `-{value}` does not fit in Int, which is 32 bits; only a \\\n1846:                              position that expects Int64 can hold it\"\n1847:                         ),\n1848:                     ));\n1849:                 }\n1850:                 return Ok(Tir::new(Type::Int, Kind::Int(-value)));\n1851:             }\n1852:             if let Expr::Float { value, .. } = base.as_ref() {\n1853:                 return Ok(Tir::new(Type::Float, Kind::Float(-value)));\n1854:             }\n1855:             // Synthesise first, so `-x` works at whichever integer width `x` already has; a\n1856:             // form that can only be checked (`-input` fixing input to Int) falls back to the\n1857:             // Int expectation this arm always applied.\n1858:             let inner = match synth(ctx, base) {\n1859:                 Ok(inner) if matches!(inner.ty, Type::Int | Type::Int64 | Type::Float) => inner,\n1860:                 Ok(inner) => {\n1861:                     return Err(Error::new(\n1862:                         base.span(),\n1863:                         format!(\"expected Int, found {}\", inner.ty),\n1864:                     ));\n1865:                 }\n1866:                 Err(_) => expect(ctx, base, &Type::Int)?,\n1867:             };\n1868:             let width = inner.ty.clone();\n1869:             let zero = Tir::new(width.clone(), Kind::Int(0));\n1870:             let _ = span;\n1871:             Ok(Tir::new(\n1872:                 width,\n1873:                 Kind::Arith {\n1874:                     op: BinOp::Sub,\n1875:                     lhs: Box::new(zero),\n1876:                     rhs: Box::new(inner),\n1877:                 },\n1878:             ))\n1879:         }\n1880: \n1881:         Expr::Binary { op, lhs, rhs, .. } => binary(ctx, *op, lhs, rhs),\n1882: \n1883:         // The connectives are the one place a subexpression may go unevaluated: `and` runs its\n1884:         // right side only when the left is true, `or` only when it is false. Nothing here has\n1885:         // to say so -- every backend emits its own short-circuiting operator -- but it is why\n1886:         // `x != 0 and 10 / x > 1` is a program and not a division by zero.\n1887:         Expr::Logic { op, lhs, rhs, .. } => {\n1888:             let lhs = expect(ctx, lhs, &Type::Bool)?;\n1889:             let rhs = expect(ctx, rhs, &Type::Bool)?;\n1890:             Ok(Tir::new(\n1891:                 Type::Bool,\n1892:                 Kind::Logic {\n1893:                     op: *op,\n1894:                     lhs: Box::new(lhs),\n1895:                     rhs: Box::new(rhs),\n1896:                 },\n1897:             ))\n1898:         }\n1899: \n1900:         Expr::Not { base, .. } => {\n1901:             let base = expect(ctx, base, &Type::Bool)?;\n1902:             Ok(Tir::new(Type::Bool, Kind::Not(Box::new(base))))\n1903:         }\n1904: \n1905:         // A `let` block is only reachable as a function body, which is always checked against an\n1906:         // expected return type, so `expect_inner` owns it. `synth` has no want to hand the block's\n1907:         // value, so this arm is unreachable in practice but must exist for the match to be total.\n1908:         Expr::Let { bindings, body, .. } => match let_bind(ctx, bindings, body, None)? {\n1909:             Expected::Checked(_) => unreachable!(\"synth has no want, so nothing is Checked\"),\n\n(Showing lines 1780-1909 of 3239. Use offset=1910 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"\n        // `v[]` with nothing after it is the identity: \"keep every entry\" is what a Vec (or\n        // stream) already is, with no field/index/unwrap left to distribute across it.\n        Expr::Project { .. } => {\n            let access = access(ctx, expr)?;\n            Ok(access.tir)\n        }\n\n        // `input` names one value, so its first checked use fixes the type for the whole\n        // program -- and a later use in a position that expects nothing can borrow what the\n        // first use fixed. With no use typed yet, nothing says what it contains, and guessing\n        // is what the annotation rule avoids.\n        Expr::Input { span } => match ctx.input.borrow().as_ref() {\n            Some(ty) => Ok(Tir::new(ty.clone(), Kind::Input)),\n            None => Err(Error::new(*span, \"cannot tell what `input` contains\")),\n        },\n        Expr::Inputs { span } => match ctx.in_fn {\n            Some(func) => Err(source_in_fn(*span, \"inputs\", func)),\n            None => Err(Error::new(*span, \"cannot tell what `inputs` contains\")),\n        },","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/check/mod.rs","text":"\n        // `v[]` with nothing after it is the identity: \"keep every entry\" is what a Vec (or\n        // stream) already is, with no field/index/unwrap left to distribute across it.\n        Expr::Project { .. } => {\n            let access = access(ctx, expr)?;\n            Ok(access.tir)\n        }\n\n        // `input` names one value, so its first checked use fixes the type for the whole\n        // program -- and a later use in a position that expects nothing can borrow what the\n        // first use fixed. With no use typed yet, nothing says what it contains, and guessing\n        // is what the annotation rule avoids.\n        Expr::Input { span } => match ctx.input.borrow().as_ref() {\n            Some(ty) => Ok(Tir::new(ty.clone(), Kind::Input)),\n            None => Err(Error::new(*span, \"cannot tell what `input` contains\")),\n        },\n        Expr::Inputs { span } => match ctx.in_fn {\n            Some(func) => Err(source_in_fn(*span, \"inputs\", func)),\n            None => Err(Error::new(*span, \"cannot tell what `inputs` contains\")),\n        },\n\n        Expr::Call {\n            func,\n            func_span,\n            arg,\n            span,\n        } => call(ctx, func, *func_span, arg, *span),\n\n        Expr::Match { arms, span } => match_chain(ctx, arms, *span, None),\n\n        Expr::Variant {\n            enum_name,\n            enum_span,\n            variant,\n            variant_span,\n            payload,\n            ..\n        } => {\n            let Some(enum_ty) = ctx.enums.get(enum_name) else {\n                return Err(Error::new(\n                    *enum_span,\n                    format!(\"`{enum_name}` is not an enum\"),\n                ));\n            };\n            construct(\n                ctx,\n                &enum_ty.clone(),\n                variant,\n                *variant_span,\n                payload.as_deref(),\n                None,\n                false,\n            )\n        }\n\n        Expr::Neg { base, span } => {\n            // A minus directly on a literal is part of the literal, so the most negative Int can\n            // be written even though its magnitude is one past the most positive. This is the\n            // rule Rust uses, and it is why `-` was not folded into the lexer: `a -1` has to\n            // stay `a - 1`.\n            if let Expr::Int { value, span: lit } = base.as_ref() {\n                if *value > -(i32::MIN as i64) {\n                    return Err(Error::new(\n                        *lit,\n                        format!(\n                            \"integer `-{value}` does not fit in Int, which is 32 bits; only a \\\n                             position that expects Int64 can hold it\"\n                        ),\n                    ));\n                }\n                return Ok(Tir::new(Type::Int, Kind::Int(-value)));\n            }\n            if let Expr::Float { value, .. } = base.as_ref() {\n                return Ok(Tir::new(Type::Float, Kind::Float(-value)));\n            }\n            // Synthesise first, so `-x` works at whichever integer width `x` already has; a\n            // form that can only be checked (`-input` fixing input to Int) falls back to the\n            // Int expectation this arm always applied.\n            let inner = match synth(ctx, base) {\n                Ok(inner) if matches!(inner.ty, Type::Int | Type::Int64 | Type::Float) => inner,\n                Ok(inner) => {\n                    return Err(Error::new(\n                        base.span(),\n                        format!(\"expected Int, found {}\", inner.ty),\n                    ));\n                }\n                Err(_) => expect(ctx, base, &Type::Int)?,\n            };\n            let width = inner.ty.clone();\n            let zero = Tir::new(width.clone(), Kind::Int(0));\n            let _ = span;\n            Ok(Tir::new(\n                width,\n                Kind::Arith {\n                    op: BinOp::Sub,\n                    lhs: Box::new(zero),\n                    rhs: Box::new(inner),\n                },\n            ))\n        }\n\n        Expr::Binary { op, lhs, rhs, .. } => binary(ctx, *op, lhs, rhs),\n\n        // The connectives are the one place a subexpression may go unevaluated: `and` runs its\n        // right side only when the left is true, `or` only when it is false. Nothing here has\n        // to say so -- every backend emits its own short-circuiting operator -- but it is why\n        // `x != 0 and 10 / x > 1` is a program and not a division by zero.\n        Expr::Logic { op, lhs, rhs, .. } => {\n            let lhs = expect(ctx, lhs, &Type::Bool)?;\n            let rhs = expect(ctx, rhs, &Type::Bool)?;\n            Ok(Tir::new(\n                Type::Bool,\n                Kind::Logic {\n                    op: *op,\n                    lhs: Box::new(lhs),\n                    rhs: Box::new(rhs),\n                },\n            ))\n        }\n\n        Expr::Not { base, .. } => {\n            let base = expect(ctx, base, &Type::Bool)?;\n            Ok(Tir::new(Type::Bool, Kind::Not(Box::new(base))))\n        }\n\n        // A `let` block is only reachable as a function body, which is always checked against an\n        // expected return type, so `expect_inner` owns it. `synth` has no want to hand the block's\n        // value, so this arm is unreachable in practice but must exist for the match to be total.\n        Expr::Let { bindings, body, .. } => match let_bind(ctx, bindings, body, None)? {\n            Expected::Checked(_) => unreachable!(\"synth has no want, so nothing is Checked\"),","lineStart":1780,"lineEnd":1909,"totalLines":3239,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/check/mod.rs","time":{"start":1788464524043,"end":1788464524052}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand the Python backend well. Let me now look at how the checker handles `synth` for calls — specifically where `select` and `map` are dispatched (they need a subject via pipe). For sort_by, we want `v | sort_by(.key)`. Let me look at the dispatch in `synth`/`expect` that handles `select` and `map` to understand where to add sort_by/max_by dispatch. Let me read the check/mod.rs around line 1801 and 3064.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068caeb02001ORIFCjrYx83gVi","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","messageID":"msg_068caca3e001OgLBqEJJh0gRcJ"}}
