{"type":"text","timestamp":1788464540022,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"id":"prt_068cb2830001SbcjDNVRrVevao","messageID":"msg_068caf068001A022sbM0z8kN0A","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","type":"text","text":"\n\n","time":{"start":1788464539696,"end":1788464540020}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788464540034,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_06c624bbe20d4424ba3f7178","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/docs/examples/euler/22-names-scores.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/docs/examples/euler/22-names-scores.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Scoring a sorted list of names (skipped)\n2: \n3: Skipped. [Project Euler 22](https://projecteuler.net/problem=22) scores a text file of over\n4: five thousand names by alphabetical rank, and it is blocked twice over. The names are\n5: problem-given data with nowhere to live in this repo, the blocker that also skips\n6: [problem 13](13-large-sum.md) and its neighbours\n7: ([kantord/toylang#129](https://github.com/kantord/toylang/issues/129)). Independently of the\n8: data, there is the rank: toylang has no `Vec` sort\n9: ([kantord/toylang#86](https://github.com/kantord/toylang/issues/86)),\n10: and every shape a hand-written one takes recurses once per element merged or inserted --\n11: linear in the list's length, not its depth. Five thousand-odd frames is five times the ceiling\n12: that already gates [problem 17](17-number-letter-counts.md) and\n13: [problem 19](19-counting-sundays.md) at one thousand. See the\n14: [spoiler warning](00-spoiler-warning.md).\n\n(End of file - total 14 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Scoring a sorted list of names (skipped)\n\nSkipped. [Project Euler 22](https://projecteuler.net/problem=22) scores a text file of over\nfive thousand names by alphabetical rank, and it is blocked twice over. The names are\nproblem-given data with nowhere to live in this repo, the blocker that also skips\n[problem 13](13-large-sum.md) and its neighbours\n([kantord/toylang#129](https://github.com/kantord/toylang/issues/129)). Independently of the\ndata, there is the rank: toylang has no `Vec` sort\n([kantord/toylang#86](https://github.com/kantord/toylang/issues/86)),\nand every shape a hand-written one takes recurses once per element merged or inserted --\nlinear in the list's length, not its depth. Five thousand-odd frames is five times the ceiling\nthat already gates [problem 17](17-number-letter-counts.md) and\n[problem 19](19-counting-sundays.md) at one thousand. See the\n[spoiler warning](00-spoiler-warning.md).","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/docs/examples/euler/22-names-scores.md","text":"# Scoring a sorted list of names (skipped)\n\nSkipped. [Project Euler 22](https://projecteuler.net/problem=22) scores a text file of over\nfive thousand names by alphabetical rank, and it is blocked twice over. The names are\nproblem-given data with nowhere to live in this repo, the blocker that also skips\n[problem 13](13-large-sum.md) and its neighbours\n([kantord/toylang#129](https://github.com/kantord/toylang/issues/129)). Independently of the\ndata, there is the rank: toylang has no `Vec` sort\n([kantord/toylang#86](https://github.com/kantord/toylang/issues/86)),\nand every shape a hand-written one takes recurses once per element merged or inserted --\nlinear in the list's length, not its depth. Five thousand-odd frames is five times the ceiling\nthat already gates [problem 17](17-number-letter-counts.md) and\n[problem 19](19-counting-sundays.md) at one thousand. See the\n[spoiler warning](00-spoiler-warning.md).","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":14,"totalLines":14,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/examples/euler/22-names-scores.md","time":{"start":1788464540013,"end":1788464540029}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand the call dispatch. For `sort_by`/`max_by`, I'll add dispatch in `call` that handles the subject form. The syntax: `v | sort_by(.key)` — the argument is checked with `.` rebound to the element, same as map/select.\n\nNow, the TIR shape. I need a new Kind variant for SortBy and MaxBy. Let me design:\n\n```rust\n/// `sort_by(v, .key)`: `v`'s entries ordered by the value `body` projects each entry to.\nSortBy {\n    source: Box<Tir>,\n    param: LocalId,\n    body: Box<Tir>,\n},\n/// `max_by(v, .key))`: the entry whose projection is greatest, `Opt<T>` on empty.\nMaxBy {\n    source: Box<Tir>,\n    param: LocalId,\n    body: Box<Tir>,\n},\n```\n\nHmm, but wait — should these be `Kind::Builtin`-style or separate Kind variants? The projection body involves a `param` binding (like Map/Select). The `Builtin` kind carries only an `arg`. So these need their own Kind variants, similar to Map/Select.\n\n.\n\nNow the syntax question again: `v | sort_by(.key)`. How does pipe work? `pipe` synthesizes lhs, binds it, then checks rhs. But sort_by needs BOTH the Vec (the piped subject) and the projection (arg). So `sort_by` in the call dispatch would read the subject from `ctx.subject` — like `select_call`/`map_call` do. Wait — `map_call` gets `ctx.subject` — the piped value. So `v | sort_by(.key)` would have `ctx.subject = Some((Vec<T>, id))`. The arg `.key` is checked in mapper context. \n\nBut hold on — could it also be called without a pipe? Like `sort_by(v, .key)`? No, calls are unary. The only way to pass two things is... hmm, what about a record? Like `sort_by({v: v, key: .key})`? No — `.` wouldn't refer to the element.\n\n.\n\nHmm wait, maybe I should reconsider. Perhaps sort_by takes a record argument combining the Vec and the projection. Let me re-read the issue:\n\n\"Implement sort_by and max_by as Vec builtins ordering through a scalar projection function, e.g. sort_by(.key) / max_by(.key).\"\n\n\"The map(.name) projection machinery already exists in the checker) and order/reduce by the projected scalar instead of the element itself.\"\n\n\"See plans/euler-ergonomics.md (\"sort_by, max_by: comparing through a projection\")...\"\n\nThe plan says: \"sort_by(.key)and max_by(.key) order records through a scalar projection, which every backend can do with a key comparator, no general record ordering required. jq has exactly this pair for exactly this reason. Depends on max existing first; the projection-body machinery (map(.name)) already exists to hang it on.\"\n\nSo the syntax is `sort_by(.key)` — the projection in parens. The Vec arrives via the pipe (since that's how map/select work in this language. The issue example \"sort_by(.key) / max_by(.key\" suggests the call form with the projection arg. In context of pipe, `v | sort_by(.key)`. This is exactly how map is written: `[1,2] | map(. * 2)`. And select: `v | select(pred)`.\n\n.\n\nSo I'll go with: `v | sort_by(.key)`. The checker reads the subject from ctx.subject, same as map/select, and checks arg in mapper context. Result type is `Vec<T>` (same element type as subject). For max_by: `v | max_by(.key)`, result `Opt<T>` where T is the subject's element type.\n\n.\n\nNow — max_by's projection must produce an orderable type (Int, Int64, Str, Char). sort_by's projection likewise must produce an orderable type. The plan says \"order/reduce by the projected scalar\". So:\n\n- sort_by(v, .key): projection must typecheck and be orderable (Int, Int64, Str, Char).\n- max_by(v, .key: projection must be reducible? Hmm. max itself is restricted to Int/Int64 element types. max_by orders by the projected scalar. So should max_by's projection be restricted to Int/Int64 (like max), or to orderable types (like sort)?\n\nThe issue: \"order/reduce by the projected scalar instead of the element itself.\" And \"max is already landed (gh:140) as the reduction to build max_by on top of.\" The plan: \"sort_by(.key)and max_by(.key) order records through a scalar projection\". \n\nHmm. max_by reduces to the entry with the greatest projected scalar. What scalar types can be compared? jq's max_by works on any comparable values (numbers, strings, arrays...). In toylang, the comparator `<` works on Int, Int64, Str, Char (the orderable types. max is restricted to integers because empty→Opt and the reduction over natively-ordered element types — but max's restriction to ints was a \"no caller\" decision (reducible() only Int/Int64.). \n\nFor max_by, the projection produces a scalar. Should it be restricted to the two int types (like max), or to all four orderable types?\n\nConsider problem 26: max over `{d, len}` pairs compared by `.len`. `.len` is an Int. So integers suffice there.\n\n Consider problem 22: needs rank-after-sorting, which is sort_by. Not max_by.\n\nWhat about max_by over Str projections? Hmm. The issue says \"order/reduce by the projected scalar\". \"The projected scalar\". jq's max_by handles any orderable.\n\n I think for consistency with the sort builtin (which accepts all four orderable types), sort_by accepts projections of any orderable type. And max_by — since it's \"the reduction to build max_by on top of\" and max restricts to ints... Hmm.\n\n\n\nLet me look at how the docs will read. The docs for sort.md say \"Restricted to the element types ordering already typechecks on — Int, Int64, Str, and Char\". The docs for max.md say \"Defined only for the two integer element types.\"\n\nFor max_by, I think the projection should be orderable — the same four types sort accepts — because max_by orders by the projection (it doesn't reduce the projection itself; it reduces the *elements*, keeping the one with the greatest key.). The `Opt` on empty comes from the elements, not the projection. So max_by over a Vec of records with an Int projection: result Opt<record>. The projection being Str should be fine for ordering (and jq max_by handles strings fine. But — does any corpus case need it? Problem 26 needs Int. There's no caller for Str projection in max_by. Per AGENTS.md, \"Build for callers that exist.\" Hmm. But sort_by over Str projections... Problem 22 sorts names (Str projections?). Let me look at problem 22 to see what it needs.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068cb2963001BP00Q7efZMRWJr","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","messageID":"msg_068caf068001A022sbM0z8kN0A"}}
