{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788464542947,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"id":"prt_068cb34df001m2UXNYsoGipLaa","messageID":"msg_068cb29c0001uWn5EdB1p1Ua9y","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","snapshot":"4b2797779b42b25cca6cb1f7660061fcb9d31aa4","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1788464547423,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"id":"prt_068cb457a001v55xn5eLaygy5c","messageID":"msg_068cb29c0001uWn5EdB1p1Ua9y","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","type":"text","text":"\n\n","time":{"start":1788464547194,"end":1788464547420}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788464547433,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_5f23a058d2d0413f9234e4e1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/tags.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/tags.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! Human-readable tags for the AST shapes a program exercises.\n2: //!\n3: //! Drawn from `tir::Kind`, not `ast::Expr`: `select`, `map`, and even `+` only resolve into\n4: //! their real meaning once type checking has run (`ast::Expr::Call` covers a user function, a\n5: //! builtin, and `select`/`map` alike, and `+` covers both `Concat` and `Arith::Add`). Every\n6: //! corpus case compiles, so `Tir` is always available.\n7: //!\n8: //! Where CONTEXT.md already names a concept, the tag uses that name -- `application` for a call,\n9: //! `projection` for a field access, `selection` for both `select(...)` and an index, since the\n10: //! glossary treats those as the same idea under different specs. `map-over` is the one deliberate\n11: //! departure: CONTEXT.md reserves `Map` for the not-yet-built dict-like type, so the `map(f)`\n12: //! builtin gets a different word rather than colliding with it.\n13: \n14: use std::collections::BTreeSet;\n15: \n16: use crate::ast::BinOp;\n17: use crate::tir::{Builtin, Kind, Program, Tir};\n18: \n19: /// Every tag exercised by `program`, sorted and deduplicated. A tag with no sub-case is its bare\n20: /// name (`projection`); one with a sub-case is `parent.child` (`arith.add`, `builtin.range`).\n21: pub fn node_types(program: &Program) -> Vec<String> {\n22:     let mut tags = BTreeSet::new();\n23:     for func in &program.funcs {\n24:         walk(&func.body, &mut tags);\n25:     }\n26:     walk(&program.body, &mut tags);\n27:     tags.into_iter().collect()\n28: }\n29: \n30: fn walk(tir: &Tir, tags: &mut BTreeSet<String>) {\n31:     tags.insert(tag(tir));\n32:     match &tir.kind {\n33:         Kind::Str(_)\n34:         | Kind::Int(_)\n35:         | Kind::Float(_)\n36:         | Kind::Var(_)\n37:         | Kind::Local(_)\n38:         | Kind::Input\n39:         | Kind::Inputs\n40:         | Kind::Lines\n41:         | Kind::Dsv { .. } => {}\n42:         Kind::VecLit(items) => items.iter().for_each(|i| walk(i, tags)),\n43:         Kind::RecordLit { fields } => fields.iter().for_each(|(_, v)| walk(v, tags)),\n44:         Kind::EnumLit { payload, .. } => {\n45:             if let Some(p) = payload {\n46:                 walk(p, tags);\n47:             }\n48:         }\n49:         Kind::Call { arg, .. } => {\n50:             if let Some(a) = arg {\n51:                 walk(a, tags);\n52:             }\n53:         }\n54:         Kind::Concat(lhs, rhs) => {\n55:             walk(lhs, tags);\n56:             walk(rhs, tags);\n57:         }\n58:         Kind::Arith { lhs, rhs, .. }\n59:         | Kind::Compare { lhs, rhs, .. }\n60:         | Kind::Logic { lhs, rhs, .. } => {\n61:             walk(lhs, tags);\n62:             walk(rhs, tags);\n63:         }\n64:         Kind::Bind { value, body, .. }\n65:         | Kind::Map {\n66:             source: value,\n67:             body,\n68:             ..\n69:         }\n70:         | Kind::OptMap {\n71:             source: value,\n72:             body,\n73:             ..\n74:         } => {\n75:             walk(value, tags);\n76:             walk(body, tags);\n77:         }\n78:         Kind::Select { source, pred, .. } => {\n79:             walk(source, tags);\n80:             walk(pred, tags);\n81:         }\n82:         Kind::Field { base, .. } | Kind::Unwrap { base } | Kind::Not(base) => walk(base, tags),\n83:         Kind::Builtin { arg, .. } => walk(arg, tags),\n84:         Kind::Index { base, index, .. } => {\n85:             walk(base, tags);\n86:             walk(index, tags);\n87:         }\n88:         Kind::Slice { base, start, end, .. } => {\n89:             walk(base, tags);\n90:             if let Some(s) = start {\n91:                 walk(s, tags);\n92:             }\n93:             if let Some(e) = end {\n94:                 walk(e, tags);\n95:             }\n96:         }\n97:         Kind::Match { subject, arms, .. } => {\n98:             walk(subject, tags);\n99:             for a in arms {\n100:                 if let Some(g) = &a.guard {\n101:                     walk(g, tags);\n102:                 }\n103:                 walk(&a.body, tags);\n104:             }\n105:         }\n106:     }\n107: }\n108: \n109: fn tag(tir: &Tir) -> String {\n110:     match &tir.kind {\n111:         Kind::Str(_) => \"str\".into(),\n112:         // One literal kind, two widths: which one this literal has is the node's type's to\n113:         // say (kantord/toylang#83), so the tag reads it rather than the kind.\n114:         Kind::Int(_) if tir.ty == crate::ty::Type::Int64 => \"int64\".into(),\n115:         Kind::Int(_) => \"int\".into(),\n116:         Kind::Float(_) => \"float\".into(),\n117:         Kind::VecLit(_) => \"vec-literal\".into(),\n118:         Kind::RecordLit { .. } => \"record-literal\".into(),\n119:         // CONTEXT.md's terms: a `variant` is one alternative, a `unit variant` carries nothing.\n120:         Kind::EnumLit { payload: None, .. } => \"variant.unit\".into(),\n121:         Kind::EnumLit {\n122:             payload: Some(_), ..\n123:         } => \"variant.payload\".into(),\n124:         Kind::Var(_) => \"var\".into(),\n125:         Kind::Local(_) => \"local\".into(),\n126:         Kind::Input => \"input\".into(),\n127:         Kind::Lines => \"lines\".into(),\n128:         Kind::Dsv { .. } => \"dsv\".into(),\n129:         Kind::Call { .. } => \"application\".into(),\n130:         Kind::Concat(..) => \"concat\".into(),\n131:         Kind::Arith { op, .. } => format!(\"arith.{}\", binop_tag(*op)),\n132:         Kind::Compare { op, .. } => format!(\"compare.{}\", binop_tag(*op)),\n133:         Kind::Logic { op, .. } => format!(\"logic.{op}\"),\n134:         Kind::Not(_) => \"logic.not\".into(),\n135:         Kind::Bind { .. } => \"pipe\".into(),\n136:         Kind::Map { .. } => \"map-over\".into(),\n137:         Kind::OptMap { .. } => \"opt-map\".into(),\n138:         Kind::Select { .. } => \"selection.narrow\".into(),\n139:         Kind::Field { .. } => \"projection\".into(),\n140:         Kind::Builtin { which, .. } => format!(\"builtin.{}\", builtin_tag(*which)),\n141:         Kind::Unwrap { .. } => \"unwrap\".into(),\n142:         Kind::Index { .. } => \"selection.collapse\".into(),\n143:         // A slice narrows by position where `select` narrows by predicate, so both are\n144:         // the same kind of spec (CONTEXT.md's narrow), and share the tag.\n145:         Kind::Slice { .. } => \"selection.narrow\".into(),\n146:         Kind::Inputs => \"inputs\".into(),\n147:         Kind::Match { .. } => \"match\".into(),\n148:     }\n149: }\n150: \n151: fn binop_tag(op: BinOp) -> &'static str {\n152:     match op {\n153:         BinOp::Add => \"add\",\n154:         BinOp::Sub => \"sub\",\n155:         BinOp::Mul => \"mul\",\n156:         BinOp::Div => \"div\",\n157:         BinOp::Rem => \"rem\",\n158:         BinOp::Eq => \"eq\",\n159:         BinOp::Ne => \"ne\",\n160:         BinOp::Lt => \"lt\",\n161:         BinOp::Le => \"le\",\n162:         BinOp::Gt => \"gt\",\n163:         BinOp::Ge => \"ge\",\n164:     }\n165: }\n166: \n167: fn builtin_tag(which: Builtin) -> &'static str {\n168:     match which {\n169:         Builtin::IntToStr => \"str\",\n170:         Builtin::IntToI64 => \"i64\",\n171:         Builtin::Range => \"range\",\n172:         Builtin::Collect => \"collect\",\n173:         Builtin::JsonLines => \"jsonlines\",\n174:         Builtin::Length => \"length\",\n175:         Builtin::Flatten => \"flatten\",\n176:         Builtin::Tail => \"tail\",\n177:         Builtin::Fields => \"fields\",\n178:         Builtin::Chars => \"chars\",\n179:         Builtin::Sort => \"sort\",\n180:         Builtin::Reverse => \"reverse\",\n181:         Builtin::Sum => \"sum\",\n182:         Builtin::Max => \"max\",\n183:     }\n184: }\n\n(End of file - total 184 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! Human-readable tags for the AST shapes a program exercises.\n//!\n//! Drawn from `tir::Kind`, not `ast::Expr`: `select`, `map`, and even `+` only resolve into\n//! their real meaning once type checking has run (`ast::Expr::Call` covers a user function, a\n//! builtin, and `select`/`map` alike, and `+` covers both `Concat` and `Arith::Add`). Every\n//! corpus case compiles, so `Tir` is always available.\n//!\n//! Where CONTEXT.md already names a concept, the tag uses that name -- `application` for a call,\n//! `projection` for a field access, `selection` for both `select(...)` and an index, since the\n//! glossary treats those as the same idea under different specs. `map-over` is the one deliberate\n//! departure: CONTEXT.md reserves `Map` for the not-yet-built dict-like type, so the `map(f)`\n//! builtin gets a different word rather than colliding with it.\n\nuse std::collections::BTreeSet;\n\nuse crate::ast::BinOp;\nuse crate::tir::{Builtin, Kind, Program, Tir};\n\n/// Every tag exercised by `program`, sorted and deduplicated. A tag with no sub-case is its bare\n/// name (`projection`); one with a sub-case is `parent.child` (`arith.add`, `builtin.range`).","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/tags.rs","text":"//! Human-readable tags for the AST shapes a program exercises.\n//!\n//! Drawn from `tir::Kind`, not `ast::Expr`: `select`, `map`, and even `+` only resolve into\n//! their real meaning once type checking has run (`ast::Expr::Call` covers a user function, a\n//! builtin, and `select`/`map` alike, and `+` covers both `Concat` and `Arith::Add`). Every\n//! corpus case compiles, so `Tir` is always available.\n//!\n//! Where CONTEXT.md already names a concept, the tag uses that name -- `application` for a call,\n//! `projection` for a field access, `selection` for both `select(...)` and an index, since the\n//! glossary treats those as the same idea under different specs. `map-over` is the one deliberate\n//! departure: CONTEXT.md reserves `Map` for the not-yet-built dict-like type, so the `map(f)`\n//! builtin gets a different word rather than colliding with it.\n\nuse std::collections::BTreeSet;\n\nuse crate::ast::BinOp;\nuse crate::tir::{Builtin, Kind, Program, Tir};\n\n/// Every tag exercised by `program`, sorted and deduplicated. A tag with no sub-case is its bare\n/// name (`projection`); one with a sub-case is `parent.child` (`arith.add`, `builtin.range`).\npub fn node_types(program: &Program) -> Vec<String> {\n    let mut tags = BTreeSet::new();\n    for func in &program.funcs {\n        walk(&func.body, &mut tags);\n    }\n    walk(&program.body, &mut tags);\n    tags.into_iter().collect()\n}\n\nfn walk(tir: &Tir, tags: &mut BTreeSet<String>) {\n    tags.insert(tag(tir));\n    match &tir.kind {\n        Kind::Str(_)\n        | Kind::Int(_)\n        | Kind::Float(_)\n        | Kind::Var(_)\n        | Kind::Local(_)\n        | Kind::Input\n        | Kind::Inputs\n        | Kind::Lines\n        | Kind::Dsv { .. } => {}\n        Kind::VecLit(items) => items.iter().for_each(|i| walk(i, tags)),\n        Kind::RecordLit { fields } => fields.iter().for_each(|(_, v)| walk(v, tags)),\n        Kind::EnumLit { payload, .. } => {\n            if let Some(p) = payload {\n                walk(p, tags);\n            }\n        }\n        Kind::Call { arg, .. } => {\n            if let Some(a) = arg {\n                walk(a, tags);\n            }\n        }\n        Kind::Concat(lhs, rhs) => {\n            walk(lhs, tags);\n            walk(rhs, tags);\n        }\n        Kind::Arith { lhs, rhs, .. }\n        | Kind::Compare { lhs, rhs, .. }\n        | Kind::Logic { lhs, rhs, .. } => {\n            walk(lhs, tags);\n            walk(rhs, tags);\n        }\n        Kind::Bind { value, body, .. }\n        | Kind::Map {\n            source: value,\n            body,\n            ..\n        }\n        | Kind::OptMap {\n            source: value,\n            body,\n            ..\n        } => {\n            walk(value, tags);\n            walk(body, tags);\n        }\n        Kind::Select { source, pred, .. } => {\n            walk(source, tags);\n            walk(pred, tags);\n        }\n        Kind::Field { base, .. } | Kind::Unwrap { base } | Kind::Not(base) => walk(base, tags),\n        Kind::Builtin { arg, .. } => walk(arg, tags),\n        Kind::Index { base, index, .. } => {\n            walk(base, tags);\n            walk(index, tags);\n        }\n        Kind::Slice { base, start, end, .. } => {\n            walk(base, tags);\n            if let Some(s) = start {\n                walk(s, tags);\n            }\n            if let Some(e) = end {\n                walk(e, tags);\n            }\n        }\n        Kind::Match { subject, arms, .. } => {\n            walk(subject, tags);\n            for a in arms {\n                if let Some(g) = &a.guard {\n                    walk(g, tags);\n                }\n                walk(&a.body, tags);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn tag(tir: &Tir) -> String {\n    match &tir.kind {\n        Kind::Str(_) => \"str\".into(),\n        // One literal kind, two widths: which one this literal has is the node's type's to\n        // say (kantord/toylang#83), so the tag reads it rather than the kind.\n        Kind::Int(_) if tir.ty == crate::ty::Type::Int64 => \"int64\".into(),\n        Kind::Int(_) => \"int\".into(),\n        Kind::Float(_) => \"float\".into(),\n        Kind::VecLit(_) => \"vec-literal\".into(),\n        Kind::RecordLit { .. } => \"record-literal\".into(),\n        // CONTEXT.md's terms: a `variant` is one alternative, a `unit variant` carries nothing.\n        Kind::EnumLit { payload: None, .. } => \"variant.unit\".into(),\n        Kind::EnumLit {\n            payload: Some(_), ..\n        } => \"variant.payload\".into(),\n        Kind::Var(_) => \"var\".into(),\n        Kind::Local(_) => \"local\".into(),\n        Kind::Input => \"input\".into(),\n        Kind::Lines => \"lines\".into(),\n        Kind::Dsv { .. } => \"dsv\".into(),\n        Kind::Call { .. } => \"application\".into(),\n        Kind::Concat(..) => \"concat\".into(),\n        Kind::Arith { op, .. } => format!(\"arith.{}\", binop_tag(*op)),\n        Kind::Compare { op, .. } => format!(\"compare.{}\", binop_tag(*op)),\n        Kind::Logic { op, .. } => format!(\"logic.{op}\"),\n        Kind::Not(_) => \"logic.not\".into(),\n        Kind::Bind { .. } => \"pipe\".into(),\n        Kind::Map { .. } => \"map-over\".into(),\n        Kind::OptMap { .. } => \"opt-map\".into(),\n        Kind::Select { .. } => \"selection.narrow\".into(),\n        Kind::Field { .. } => \"projection\".into(),\n        Kind::Builtin { which, .. } => format!(\"builtin.{}\", builtin_tag(*which)),\n        Kind::Unwrap { .. } => \"unwrap\".into(),\n        Kind::Index { .. } => \"selection.collapse\".into(),\n        // A slice narrows by position where `select` narrows by predicate, so both are\n        // the same kind of spec (CONTEXT.md's narrow), and share the tag.\n        Kind::Slice { .. } => \"selection.narrow\".into(),\n        Kind::Inputs => \"inputs\".into(),\n        Kind::Match { .. } => \"match\".into(),\n    }\n}\n\nfn binop_tag(op: BinOp) -> &'static str {\n    match op {\n        BinOp::Add => \"add\",\n        BinOp::Sub => \"sub\",\n        BinOp::Mul => \"mul\",\n        BinOp::Div => \"div\",\n        BinOp::Rem => \"rem\",\n        BinOp::Eq => \"eq\",\n        BinOp::Ne => \"ne\",\n        BinOp::Lt => \"lt\",\n        BinOp::Le => \"le\",\n        BinOp::Gt => \"gt\",\n        BinOp::Ge => \"ge\",\n    }\n}\n\nfn builtin_tag(which: Builtin) -> &'static str {\n    match which {\n        Builtin::IntToStr => \"str\",\n        Builtin::IntToI64 => \"i64\",\n        Builtin::Range => \"range\",\n        Builtin::Collect => \"collect\",\n        Builtin::JsonLines => \"jsonlines\",\n        Builtin::Length => \"length\",\n        Builtin::Flatten => \"flatten\",\n        Builtin::Tail => \"tail\",\n        Builtin::Fields => \"fields\",\n        Builtin::Chars => \"chars\",\n        Builtin::Sort => \"sort\",\n        Builtin::Reverse => \"reverse\",\n        Builtin::Sum => \"sum\",\n        Builtin::Max => \"max\",\n    }\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":184,"totalLines":184,"truncated":false}},"title":"src/tags.rs","time":{"start":1788464547415,"end":1788464547426}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Problem 22 needs sorting a Vec of Str by... the names themselves. `sort(names)` already exists for Vec<Str>. But the issue says problem 22 \"needs rank-after-sorting\". Hmm. The blocked reason was sort missing. Now sort exists. The issue says sort_by/max_by unblocks 26 (best_of comparator) and 22 (rank-after-sorting).. \n\nHmm — rank-after-sorting: for problem 22, you sort names (Vec<Str>, using sort — already exists), then compute the score: sum of (position+1) * (alphabetical value of name).. \"rank-after-sorting\" — maybe sort_by isn't even needed for 22? Let me think... Problem 22: names sorted alphabetically, score = sum over positions of (position * sum of letter-values). With sort(v) existing, rank-after-sorting is possible directly. But wait — why does the issue say sort_by unblocks it? Maybe because with `sort_by`, you could sort records of {name, score}? Or maybe 22's data shape involves... Hmm.\n\n.\n\nActually re-reading the issue: \"Unblocks docs/examples/euler/26-reciprocal-cycles.md (replaces its hand-written best_of comparator) and docs/examples/euler/22-names-scores.md (rank-after-sorting.\"\n\nSo 22 needs rank-after-sorting. The data (names list) is problem-given and has nowhere to live (blocker #129 remains. So maybe 22 stays skipped regardless. The issue lists these as \"unblocks\" — meaning once sort_by/max_by land, the remaining blocker for those pages is only the data problem. Hmm, or maybe the implementation makes them writable.\n\n.\n\nI shouldn't overthink the docs examples right now. Let me focus on implementing sort_by/max_by across all backends. The core deliverable.\n\n.\n\nLet me now check the tag_corpus.rs and tags.rs to add tags for the new builtins. And look at how emit_toylang, emit_jq, emit_js, emit_lua, emit_go, emit_rs, emit_llvm handle Sort/Max. Let me read the relevant sections of each backend.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068cb4651001ViKkd2gYnWWutL","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","messageID":"msg_068cb29c0001uWn5EdB1p1Ua9y"}}
