{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788464592940,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_0d4e754d0d2c4aabad5a5ab7","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_rs.rs","limit":80,"offset":1333},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_rs.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1333:             Kind::Field { base, name } => {\n1334:                 let depth = tir::vec_depth(&base.ty);\n1335:                 self.distribute(&self.expr(base), &base.ty, &t.ty, depth, &|v| {\n1336:                     format!(\"{v}.{}\", rs_field(name))\n1337:                 })\n1338:             }\n1339:             Kind::Unwrap { base } => {\n1340:                 let depth = tir::vec_depth(&base.ty);\n1341:                 self.distribute(&self.expr(base), &base.ty, &t.ty, depth, &|v| {\n1342:                     format!(\"tl_unwrap({v})\")\n1343:                 })\n1344:             }\n1345:             Kind::Index {\n1346:                 base, index, depth, ..\n1347:             } => {\n1348:                 let i = self.expr(index);\n1349:                 self.distribute(&self.expr(base), &base.ty, &t.ty, *depth, &|v| {\n1350:                     format!(\"tl_at(&{v}, {i})\")\n1351:                 })\n1352:             }\n1353:             Kind::Slice {\n1354:                 base, start, end, depth,\n1355:             } => {\n1356:                 let lo = match start {\n1357:                     Some(s) => self.expr(s),\n1358:                     None => \"i32::MIN\".to_string(),\n1359:                 };\n1360:                 let hi = match end {\n1361:                     Some(e) => self.expr(e),\n1362:                     None => \"i32::MAX\".to_string(),\n1363:                 };\n1364:                 self.distribute(&self.expr(base), &base.ty, &t.ty, *depth, &|v| {\n1365:                     format!(\"tl_slice(&{v}, {lo}, {hi})\")\n1366:                 })\n1367:             }\n1368:             // The one target with the construct itself: a toylang match is a Rust match, and\n1369:             // for a chain of variant arms rustc re-proves the exhaustiveness the checker\n1370:             // already established. A default arm is `_`, a guard arm `_ if cond` (the guard\n1371:             // reads the subject's own local, not the scrutinee, so no borrow of the match is\n1372:             // involved); a duplicate or dead arm costs a warning, not an error. A partial\n1373:             // chain wraps each body in `Some` and ends `_ => None`, which is also what keeps\n1374:             // rustc's exhaustiveness proof satisfied there.\n1375:             Kind::Match {\n1376:                 subject,\n1377:                 arms,\n1378:                 partial,\n1379:             } => {\n1380:                 let ename = self.rs_type(&subject.ty);\n1381:                 let mut rendered: Vec<String> = arms\n1382:                     .iter()\n1383:                     .map(|arm| {\n1384:                         let body = if *partial {\n1385:                             format!(\"Some({})\", self.expr(&arm.body))\n1386:                         } else {\n1387:                             self.expr(&arm.body)\n1388:                         };\n1389:                         match (&arm.variant, &arm.guard) {\n1390:                             (None, Some(g)) => format!(\"_ if {} => {body}\", self.expr(g)),\n1391:                             (None, None) => format!(\"_ => {body}\"),\n1392:                             (Some(v), _) => {\n1393:                                 let has_payload = ty::variants(self.registry, &subject.ty)\n1394:                                     .iter()\n1395:                                     .find(|(n, _)| n == v)\n1396:                                     .expect(\"the checker resolved the variant\")\n1397:                                     .1\n1398:                                     .is_some();\n1399:                                 if has_payload {\n1400:                                     let pid = arm\n1401:                                         .payload\n1402:                                         .expect(\"a payload arm always binds its payload\");\n1403:                                     format!(\"{ename}::V_{v}({}) => {body}\", self.local(pid))\n1404:                                 } else {\n1405:                                     format!(\"{ename}::V_{v} => {body}\")\n1406:                                 }\n1407:                             }\n1408:                         }\n1409:                     })\n1410:                     .collect();\n1411:                 if *partial {\n1412:                     rendered.push(\"_ => None\".to_string());\n\n(Showing lines 1333-1412 of 1587. Use offset=1413 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"            Kind::Field { base, name } => {\n                let depth = tir::vec_depth(&base.ty);\n                self.distribute(&self.expr(base), &base.ty, &t.ty, depth, &|v| {\n                    format!(\"{v}.{}\", rs_field(name))\n                })\n            }\n            Kind::Unwrap { base } => {\n                let depth = tir::vec_depth(&base.ty);\n                self.distribute(&self.expr(base), &base.ty, &t.ty, depth, &|v| {\n                    format!(\"tl_unwrap({v})\")\n                })\n            }\n            Kind::Index {\n                base, index, depth, ..\n            } => {\n                let i = self.expr(index);\n                self.distribute(&self.expr(base), &base.ty, &t.ty, *depth, &|v| {\n                    format!(\"tl_at(&{v}, {i})\")\n                })\n            }","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_rs.rs","text":"            Kind::Field { base, name } => {\n                let depth = tir::vec_depth(&base.ty);\n                self.distribute(&self.expr(base), &base.ty, &t.ty, depth, &|v| {\n                    format!(\"{v}.{}\", rs_field(name))\n                })\n            }\n            Kind::Unwrap { base } => {\n                let depth = tir::vec_depth(&base.ty);\n                self.distribute(&self.expr(base), &base.ty, &t.ty, depth, &|v| {\n                    format!(\"tl_unwrap({v})\")\n                })\n            }\n            Kind::Index {\n                base, index, depth, ..\n            } => {\n                let i = self.expr(index);\n                self.distribute(&self.expr(base), &base.ty, &t.ty, *depth, &|v| {\n                    format!(\"tl_at(&{v}, {i})\")\n                })\n            }\n            Kind::Slice {\n                base, start, end, depth,\n            } => {\n                let lo = match start {\n                    Some(s) => self.expr(s),\n                    None => \"i32::MIN\".to_string(),\n                };\n                let hi = match end {\n                    Some(e) => self.expr(e),\n                    None => \"i32::MAX\".to_string(),\n                };\n                self.distribute(&self.expr(base), &base.ty, &t.ty, *depth, &|v| {\n                    format!(\"tl_slice(&{v}, {lo}, {hi})\")\n                })\n            }\n            // The one target with the construct itself: a toylang match is a Rust match, and\n            // for a chain of variant arms rustc re-proves the exhaustiveness the checker\n            // already established. A default arm is `_`, a guard arm `_ if cond` (the guard\n            // reads the subject's own local, not the scrutinee, so no borrow of the match is\n            // involved); a duplicate or dead arm costs a warning, not an error. A partial\n            // chain wraps each body in `Some` and ends `_ => None`, which is also what keeps\n            // rustc's exhaustiveness proof satisfied there.\n            Kind::Match {\n                subject,\n                arms,\n                partial,\n            } => {\n                let ename = self.rs_type(&subject.ty);\n                let mut rendered: Vec<String> = arms\n                    .iter()\n                    .map(|arm| {\n                        let body = if *partial {\n                            format!(\"Some({})\", self.expr(&arm.body))\n                        } else {\n                            self.expr(&arm.body)\n                        };\n                        match (&arm.variant, &arm.guard) {\n                            (None, Some(g)) => format!(\"_ if {} => {body}\", self.expr(g)),\n                            (None, None) => format!(\"_ => {body}\"),\n                            (Some(v), _) => {\n                                let has_payload = ty::variants(self.registry, &subject.ty)\n                                    .iter()\n                                    .find(|(n, _)| n == v)\n                                    .expect(\"the checker resolved the variant\")\n                                    .1\n                                    .is_some();\n                                if has_payload {\n                                    let pid = arm\n                                        .payload\n                                        .expect(\"a payload arm always binds its payload\");\n                                    format!(\"{ename}::V_{v}({}) => {body}\", self.local(pid))\n                                } else {\n                                    format!(\"{ename}::V_{v} => {body}\")\n                                }\n                            }\n                        }\n                    })\n                    .collect();\n                if *partial {\n                    rendered.push(\"_ => None\".to_string());","lineStart":1333,"lineEnd":1412,"totalLines":1587,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/emit_rs.rs","time":{"start":1788464592924,"end":1788464592938}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now let me think carefully about the design.\n\n### The syntax question — need to nail this down.\n\nLet me re-read the issue's exact wording:\n\n\"Implement sort_by and max_by as Vec builtins ordering through a scalar projection function, e.g. sort_by(.key) / max_by(.key).\"\n\n\"the map(.name) projection machinery already exists in the checker) and order/reduce by the projected scalar instead of the element itself.\"\n\nSo `sort_by(.key)`. Since the language's function calls are unary, `sort_by(v, .key)` isn't possible. The projection function syntax `.key` requires `.` bound to the element. The map machinery binds `.` through a pipe (`v | map(.name)`) and through the checker's subject mechanism. So `v | sort_by(.key)` is the only spelling that works.\n\n.\n\nLet me verify this is consistent with how the map builtin docs describe itself: \"The subject arrives through a pipe\". Yes.\n\nSo the implementation: in `call()`, dispatch `sort_by`/`max_by` like `select`/`map`:\n\n```rust\nif func == \"sort_by\" {\n    return sort_by_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?, span);\n}\nif func == \"max_by\" {\n    return max_by_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?, span);\n}\n```\n\nWait — but the issue says \"sort_by(.key) / max_by(.key)\". Hmm — could the intended syntax be `sort_by(v | .key)`? No, that doesn't work either since `.` wouldn't bind.\n\n.\n\nActually hold on. Let me reconsider. Maybe sort_by takes a *record* argument: `sort_by({v: v, key: .key})`? No — `.key` needs `.` bound to the element of v, which isn't the case there.\n\nThe pipe form is the only one consistent with the map machinery. I'll go with `v | sort_by(.key)`. Note that the issue example \"sort_by(.key)\" reads exactly like the map example \"[1,2,3] | map(. * 2)\" would be abbreviated. Fine.\n\n.\n\n### TIR Kind design\n\nAdd to tir::Kind:\n\n```rust\n/// `v | sort_by(.key)`: `v`'s entries ordered ascending by the value `body` projects each\n/// entry to. The projection is checked in a mapper context (the same `.`-rebinding\n/// `map` uses), so a backend can sort by key with no general record ordering.\nSortBy {\n    source: Box<Tir>,\n    param: LocalId,\n    body: Box<Tir>,\n},\n/// `v | max_by(.key)`: the entry whose projection is greatest, `Opt<T>` on empty --\n/// the same absence answer `max` gives. Ties resolve to the first entry, the way a stable\n/// maximum does.\nMaxBy {\n    source: Box<Tir>,\n    param: LocalId,\n    body: Box<Tir>,\n},\n```\n\nActually, for stability: with sort_by, ties should preserve original order (stable sort). For max_by, ties... any is fine but let's pick first (stable). Backends: using `sort_by_key` with a stable sort where available.\n\n.\n\nLet me think about what each backend can do:\n\n1. **Python**: `sorted(v, key=f)` — stable. For max_by: iterate, compare keys, keep first max (stable).\n\n2. **jq**: `sort_by(f)` is built-in (stable. For max_by: jq has `max_by(f)` built-in! Let me check: jq's `max_by(f)` exists. Yes, jq 1.7+ has `max_by(f)`. But jq's `max_by([])` on empty errors. So need the empty check like max.\n\n\n\n3. **JavaScript**: `[...v].sort((a,b) => cmp(key(a), key(b)))`. JS's sort is stable (ES2019+. For max_by: manual loop.\n\n.\n\n4. **Lua**: `table.sort(v, comparator)` — not stable! Hmm. Lua's table.sort is not stable. For sort_by, need stable behavior. How to ensure stability? Sort indices then map back: build a list of indices, sort by (key, index), then reorder. Or use a decorate-sort-undecorate with the original index as tiebreaker. Let me check how tl_sort is implemented in Lua. Let me read the Lua helpers.\n\n5. **Go**: `sort.SliceStable`. Go has `sort.SliceStable` which takes a less func. Could emit that with the key computed inline. But Go needs the key type... the comparator closure computes keys per comparison (recomputing each time). Alternatively decorate-sort-undecorate: build pairs (key, elem) using tlMap then sort.SliceStable by key then map back. That's cleaner: Go is statically typed, so a helper `tlSortBy[T, K]` generics? Go version? Let me check go.mod / how tlSort is written. Let me look at the Go helper for sort.\n\n6. **Rust**: `v.iter().cloned().sorted_by(...)`? No itertools. Use `sort_by_key`: `out.sort_by_key(|e| key(e))`. But the key closure takes &T and returns K — but K must be Ord. The key projection body is an expression emitting a value (by clone? For Str, the projection `.name` gives a String? Hmm — in Rust backend, a field access on a record... let me look at how Field works in Rust. A record is a struct; reading `.name` on `&T`... The Map closure takes `&T` and the body emits... let me check how tl_map's body handles `.name`. The body `self.expr(body)` — the body for `.name` would be a Field access which distributes... Actually in mapper context, `.name` on the element... Let me look at Rust Field emission: it emits `self.distribute(&self.expr(base),...)`. The base is a Local (the param), a `&T` reference. So `.name` on `&T` reads a field by reference? This is getting deep. Let me look at how Rust emits a Field read on a local inside a map closure. \n\nActually, for Rust sort_by: I can emit a helper `tl_sort_by` that takes `v: &[T]`, key closure `F: Fn(&T) -> K` where `K: Ord`. But the key closure in emitted code is a closure `|e: &T| -> K { ... }` — the body expression emitting a K value. For `.name` on a record, the body would be `e.name.clone()`? Let me check how Map emits `.name` to see what the body expression looks like. Let me look at Rust Field emission more carefully.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068cbf8190010li60Xx60ETKBp","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","messageID":"msg_068cbc4e9001G0Hwu3hzLAqaeh"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788464592942,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_ec5ac7ae54934907a755a9d0","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_lua.rs","limit":70,"offset":150},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_lua.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n150: local function tl_flatten(vv)\n151:   local out = {}\n152:   for i = 1, #vv do\n153:     local inner = vv[i]\n154:     for j = 1, #inner do out[#out + 1] = inner[j] end\n155:   end\n156:   return out\n157: end\n158: \";\n159: \n160: // `table.sort`'s default comparator is Lua's own `<`, which already agrees with every other\n161: // backend's ordering on both numbers and (byte-wise, so codepoint-wise for valid UTF-8) strings,\n162: // so nothing here has to branch on the element type.\n163: const SORT_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n164: local function tl_sort(v)\n165:   local out = {}\n166:   for i = 1, #v do out[i] = v[i] end\n167:   table.sort(out)\n168:   return out\n169: end\n170: \";\n171: \n172: const REVERSE_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n173: local function tl_reverse(v)\n174:   local out = {}\n175:   local n = #v\n176:   for i = 1, n do out[i] = v[n - i + 1] end\n177:   return out\n178: end\n179: \";\n180: \n181: // `narrow` is true exactly when the element type is Int: both widths live in the same 64-bit\n182: // integer here, so the only difference is whether each addition is brought back to 32 bits the\n183: // way `+` does.\n184: const SUM_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n185: local function tl_sum(v, narrow)\n186:   local acc = 0\n187:   for i = 1, #v do\n188:     acc = acc + v[i]\n189:     if narrow then acc = tl_i32(acc) end\n190:   end\n191:   return acc\n192: end\n193: \";\n194: \n195: const MAX_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n196: local function tl_max(v)\n197:   if #v == 0 then return \\\"none\\\" end\n198:   local m = v[1]\n199:   for i = 2, #v do\n200:     if v[i] > m then m = v[i] end\n201:   end\n202:   return { some = m }\n203: end\n204: \";\n205: \n206: const UNWRAP_HELPER: &str = r#\"local function tl_unwrap(v, depth)\n207:   if depth > 0 then\n208:     local out = {}\n209:     for k = 1, #v do out[k] = tl_unwrap(v[k], depth - 1) end\n210:     return out\n211:   end\n212:   if v == \"none\" then error(\"toylang: unwrapped a value that is not there\", 0) end\n213:   return v.some\n214: end\n215: \"#;\n216: \n217: // A record and a payload-carrying variant are both tables, and Lua's `==` on two tables is\n218: // identity, so `{a = 1} == {a = 1}` was false here and true on Python (kantord/toylang#68).\n219: // Structural equality is the ratified answer, so composites walk. Keyed rather than ordered:\n\n(Showing lines 150-219 of 1096. Use offset=220 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"local function tl_flatten(vv)\n  local out = {}\n  for i = 1, #vv do\n    local inner = vv[i]\n    for j = 1, #inner do out[#out + 1] = inner[j] end\n  end\n  return out\nend\n\";\n\n// `table.sort`'s default comparator is Lua's own `<`, which already agrees with every other\n// backend's ordering on both numbers and (byte-wise, so codepoint-wise for valid UTF-8) strings,\n// so nothing here has to branch on the element type.\nconst SORT_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_sort(v)\n  local out = {}\n  for i = 1, #v do out[i] = v[i] end\n  table.sort(out)\n  return out\nend","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_lua.rs","text":"local function tl_flatten(vv)\n  local out = {}\n  for i = 1, #vv do\n    local inner = vv[i]\n    for j = 1, #inner do out[#out + 1] = inner[j] end\n  end\n  return out\nend\n\";\n\n// `table.sort`'s default comparator is Lua's own `<`, which already agrees with every other\n// backend's ordering on both numbers and (byte-wise, so codepoint-wise for valid UTF-8) strings,\n// so nothing here has to branch on the element type.\nconst SORT_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_sort(v)\n  local out = {}\n  for i = 1, #v do out[i] = v[i] end\n  table.sort(out)\n  return out\nend\n\";\n\nconst REVERSE_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_reverse(v)\n  local out = {}\n  local n = #v\n  for i = 1, n do out[i] = v[n - i + 1] end\n  return out\nend\n\";\n\n// `narrow` is true exactly when the element type is Int: both widths live in the same 64-bit\n// integer here, so the only difference is whether each addition is brought back to 32 bits the\n// way `+` does.\nconst SUM_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_sum(v, narrow)\n  local acc = 0\n  for i = 1, #v do\n    acc = acc + v[i]\n    if narrow then acc = tl_i32(acc) end\n  end\n  return acc\nend\n\";\n\nconst MAX_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_max(v)\n  if #v == 0 then return \\\"none\\\" end\n  local m = v[1]\n  for i = 2, #v do\n    if v[i] > m then m = v[i] end\n  end\n  return { some = m }\nend\n\";\n\nconst UNWRAP_HELPER: &str = r#\"local function tl_unwrap(v, depth)\n  if depth > 0 then\n    local out = {}\n    for k = 1, #v do out[k] = tl_unwrap(v[k], depth - 1) end\n    return out\n  end\n  if v == \"none\" then error(\"toylang: unwrapped a value that is not there\", 0) end\n  return v.some\nend\n\"#;\n\n// A record and a payload-carrying variant are both tables, and Lua's `==` on two tables is\n// identity, so `{a = 1} == {a = 1}` was false here and true on Python (kantord/toylang#68).\n// Structural equality is the ratified answer, so composites walk. Keyed rather than ordered:","lineStart":150,"lineEnd":219,"totalLines":1096,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/emit_lua.rs","time":{"start":1788464592931,"end":1788464592941}},"id":"prt_068cbf820001WbOFYV5BqS7kGr","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","messageID":"msg_068cbc4e9001G0Hwu3hzLAqaeh"}}
