{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788464604111,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_a56ab9bebe6a4f1bb75a421f","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"rg -l \"map\\(\\.\\w+\" tests/corpus docs/examples --no-messages | head -30"},"output":"tests/corpus/match_partial_opt_arms.yaml\ntests/corpus/map_records.yaml\ntests/corpus/map_guard_chain_over_vec_field.yaml\n","metadata":{"output":"tests/corpus/match_partial_opt_arms.yaml\ntests/corpus/map_records.yaml\ntests/corpus/map_guard_chain_over_vec_field.yaml\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"rg -l \"map\\(\\.\\w+\" tests/corpus docs/examples --no-messages | head -30","time":{"start":1788464604107,"end":1788464604109}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I have a good picture. Let me now think about how to emit sort_by/max_by on each backend.\n\n**The key insight**: The projection body is checked in mapper context and its TIR is a normal expression. The emitted body expression, given a local var name for the element, produces the key. Each backend can either:\n- (a) compute keys eagerly into a parallel array, then sort pairs (key, element) stably, then extract elements — works everywhere.\n- (b) use a native keyed sort (Python `sorted(v, key=f)`, jq `sort_by(f)`, JS stable sort with comparator computing keys twice per compare.\n\nThe eager approach (a) is simplest and uniform across all backends, and handles stability trivially by including the original index. But it costs an extra pass. Actually for each backend, let me think about the cleanest emission:\n\nLet me define a generic strategy: **decorate-sort-undecorate**:\n1. Build an array of `{key, idx}` or `{key, elem}` pairs.\n. For backends where elements can be held in a parallel array alongside keys, use pairs.\n\nActually simpler: build a Vec of (key, original_index) pairs. Sort the pairs by (key, idx). Then build the output by mapping each sorted pair to its original element via the original source array. This handles stability. And `max_by`: iterate over pairs, track max key (ties → lower idx.Then return the element at that idx.\n\nHmm — but the pair approach requires an indexable source and an index-to-element lookup. All backends have Vecs. For Rust, indexing a Vec by usize is trivial. For jq, `.[i]`. For Go, `v[i]`. For Lua, `v[i+1]`. Fine.\n\n\n\nBut wait — for some backends, computing the key eagerly may be more work than the comparator approach. E.g. Rust: `sort_by_key` recomputes keys lazily per comparison — simpler: just call `out.sort_by_key(|e| key(e))`. But the key closure returns K by value; if the key is `.name` (a String field), `e.name` on `&Record` would be a String field read by reference... `sort_by_key` needs `K: Ord` — a `&String` implements Ord? `&T: Ord` where T: Ord, yes (`&String: Ord` if String: Ord — yes, references compare by pointee). Hmm. But `sort_by_key(|e| e.name)` where e: &Record — e.name would be... field access on a reference auto-derefs, giving a String by value? In Rust, `e.name` on `&Record` produces a place expression of type String; the closure returns it by value → moves out of a shared reference → error! Unless the field is Copy. So for a String field, `e.name` would fail to compile. `e.name.clone()` works. Hmm. How does Map handle this today? Map's body for `.name`: Field access → `self.distribute(&self.expr(base),...)`. Base is a Local in mapper context: the param binds to... In Rust map: `.iter().map(|e: &T| -> U { body })`. The body `.name` would emit `e.name` — by-value move of a non-Copy field → compile error?!\n\nWait, maybe the checker rebases the projection differently. Let me look at how Map's param is bound in Rust: `self.expr(source).iter().map(|{param}: &{elem}| -> {body_ty} { {body} }).collect()`. The body expression `.name` is a Field { base: Local(param), name }. `self.expr(base)` gives the local name (e.g. `t_5`), which is `&T`. Then distribute applies `{v}.{name}` → `t_5.name`. If T is a record with field `name: Str`, then `t_5.name` is a String place... In the closure return position, that's a move out of a shared reference — but wait, does the checker or emitter handle this? Let me look for how the docs/corpus use map(.name) today to see if it compiles. Let me search the corpus for map with a field access.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068cc23a5001mDlx4obcjb5Z0g","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","messageID":"msg_068cbf851001bLOYQ8p6iP6qZi"}}
