{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788464609957,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"call_c1c4cb40d96d47539febc44d","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"include":"emit_rs.rs","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src","pattern":"fn distribute|fn rebase|fn rs_type"},"output":"Found 2 matches\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_rs.rs:\n  Line 901:     fn rs_type(&self, ty: &Type) -> String {\n\n  Line 1146:     fn distribute(\n","metadata":{"matches":2,"truncated":false},"title":"fn distribute|fn rebase|fn rs_type","time":{"start":1788464609939,"end":1788464609955}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The map(.name) projection compiles across all backends (this corpus case runs on all backends). So the Rust backend handles `.name` in a map closure. How? The Field base is the Local, which in the map closure is `&T`. Reading `t_5.name` on `&Record`... Let me check the Rust emission for Map again: `.iter().map(|{param}: &{elem}| -> {body_ty} { {body} })`. The body for `.name` is `{v}.{name}` where v = `t_5` (type &T). `t_5.name` — auto-deref through the reference gives the field place of type String. Returning it moves out of the reference...\n\nUnless `show`/the surrounding context clones. Hmm — but wait, `.name + \" is \" + str(.age)` is a Concat — the `.name` operand. `String + &str` — `t_5.name + ...` — that consumes the String by value (moves out of &T). That should be a compile error!\n\nLet me actually test: build the corpus case and run `just check` to see if it currently passes. Actually let me just run the test suite for this specific case. Actually, the case is in the corpus, and `just check` presumably runs all corpus tests. So it must compile. Let me look at how Rust emits a Field access more carefully. Maybe the checker's reorder/rebasing pass converts Field-on-Local into something with a clone. Let me search for \"rebase\" or \"clone\" in the check module. Actually — `distribute` in Rust: let me read it.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068cc3a8a001DvwSCEf03WecXW","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","messageID":"msg_068cc2a87001y48QO7RgfLLFLR"}}
