{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788464619111,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_749877077a1043218ef866bc","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_go.rs","limit":90,"offset":120},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_go.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n120: const FLATTEN_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlFlatten[T any](vv [][]T) []T {\n121: \tout := []T{}\n122: \tfor _, v := range vv {\n123: \t\tout = append(out, v...)\n124: \t}\n125: \treturn out\n126: }\n127: \"#;\n128: \n129: // `cmp.Ordered` is exactly the constraint the checker's own `orderable` restricts `sort`'s\n130: // element type to, so nothing here has to name Int, Int64, Str, or Char individually.\n131: const SORT_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlSort[T cmp.Ordered](v []T) []T {\n132: \tout := append([]T{}, v...)\n133: \tslices.Sort(out)\n134: \treturn out\n135: }\n136: \"#;\n137: \n138: const REVERSE_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlReverse[T any](v []T) []T {\n139: \tout := make([]T, len(v))\n140: \tfor i, x := range v {\n141: \t\tout[len(v)-1-i] = x\n142: \t}\n143: \treturn out\n144: }\n145: \"#;\n146: \n147: // `int32`/`int64` `+=` wraps by definition, so a fold needs nothing but the loop itself -- the\n148: // same \"the width wraps on its own\" story ARITH_HELPER and ARITH64_HELPER tell for one operator.\n149: const SUM_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlSum(v []int32) int32 {\n150: \tvar acc int32\n151: \tfor _, x := range v {\n152: \t\tacc += x\n153: \t}\n154: \treturn acc\n155: }\n156: \n157: func tlSum64(v []int64) int64 {\n158: \tvar acc int64\n159: \tfor _, x := range v {\n160: \t\tacc += x\n161: \t}\n162: \treturn acc\n163: }\n164: \"#;\n165: \n166: // `cmp.Ordered` covers both integer widths, so one helper serves Int and Int64. Absence is the\n167: // zero `tlOpt`, the same answer an out-of-range index gives.\n168: const MAX_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlMax[T cmp.Ordered](v []T) tlOpt[T] {\n169: \tif len(v) == 0 {\n170: \t\treturn tlOpt[T]{}\n171: \t}\n172: \tm := v[0]\n173: \tfor _, x := range v[1:] {\n174: \t\tif x > m {\n175: \t\t\tm = x\n176: \t\t}\n177: \t}\n178: \treturn tlOpt[T]{true, m}\n179: }\n180: \"#;\n181: \n182: /// Go's `int32` wraps on overflow by definition, and its `/` and `%` truncate toward zero, so\n183: /// only the zero divisor needs a guard. `MIN / -1` is defined to be `MIN` here, which is the\n184: /// wrapping answer the other backends were made to give.\n185: const ARITH_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlDiv(a, b int32) int32 {\n186: \tif b == 0 {\n187: \t\ttlFail(\"divided by zero\")\n188: \t}\n189: \treturn a / b\n190: }\n191: \n192: func tlRem(a, b int32) int32 {\n193: \tif b == 0 {\n194: \t\ttlFail(\"divided by zero\")\n195: \t}\n196: \treturn a % b\n197: }\n198: \"#;\n199: \n200: /// The same shape at 64 bits (kantord/toylang#83): Go defines `MIN / -1` as `MIN` for int64\n201: /// exactly as for int32, so only the zero divisor needs the guard here too.\n202: const ARITH64_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlDiv64(a, b int64) int64 {\n203: \tif b == 0 {\n204: \t\ttlFail(\"divided by zero\")\n205: \t}\n206: \treturn a / b\n207: }\n208: \n209: func tlRem64(a, b int64) int64 {\n\n(Showing lines 120-209 of 1488. Use offset=210 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"const FLATTEN_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlFlatten[T any](vv [][]T) []T {\n\tout := []T{}\n\tfor _, v := range vv {\n\t\tout = append(out, v...)\n\t}\n\treturn out\n}\n\"#;\n\n// `cmp.Ordered` is exactly the constraint the checker's own `orderable` restricts `sort`'s\n// element type to, so nothing here has to name Int, Int64, Str, or Char individually.\nconst SORT_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlSort[T cmp.Ordered](v []T) []T {\n\tout := append([]T{}, v...)\n\tslices.Sort(out)\n\treturn out\n}\n\"#;\n\nconst REVERSE_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlReverse[T any](v []T) []T {\n\tout := make([]T, len(v))","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_go.rs","text":"const FLATTEN_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlFlatten[T any](vv [][]T) []T {\n\tout := []T{}\n\tfor _, v := range vv {\n\t\tout = append(out, v...)\n\t}\n\treturn out\n}\n\"#;\n\n// `cmp.Ordered` is exactly the constraint the checker's own `orderable` restricts `sort`'s\n// element type to, so nothing here has to name Int, Int64, Str, or Char individually.\nconst SORT_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlSort[T cmp.Ordered](v []T) []T {\n\tout := append([]T{}, v...)\n\tslices.Sort(out)\n\treturn out\n}\n\"#;\n\nconst REVERSE_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlReverse[T any](v []T) []T {\n\tout := make([]T, len(v))\n\tfor i, x := range v {\n\t\tout[len(v)-1-i] = x\n\t}\n\treturn out\n}\n\"#;\n\n// `int32`/`int64` `+=` wraps by definition, so a fold needs nothing but the loop itself -- the\n// same \"the width wraps on its own\" story ARITH_HELPER and ARITH64_HELPER tell for one operator.\nconst SUM_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlSum(v []int32) int32 {\n\tvar acc int32\n\tfor _, x := range v {\n\t\tacc += x\n\t}\n\treturn acc\n}\n\nfunc tlSum64(v []int64) int64 {\n\tvar acc int64\n\tfor _, x := range v {\n\t\tacc += x\n\t}\n\treturn acc\n}\n\"#;\n\n// `cmp.Ordered` covers both integer widths, so one helper serves Int and Int64. Absence is the\n// zero `tlOpt`, the same answer an out-of-range index gives.\nconst MAX_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlMax[T cmp.Ordered](v []T) tlOpt[T] {\n\tif len(v) == 0 {\n\t\treturn tlOpt[T]{}\n\t}\n\tm := v[0]\n\tfor _, x := range v[1:] {\n\t\tif x > m {\n\t\t\tm = x\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn tlOpt[T]{true, m}\n}\n\"#;\n\n/// Go's `int32` wraps on overflow by definition, and its `/` and `%` truncate toward zero, so\n/// only the zero divisor needs a guard. `MIN / -1` is defined to be `MIN` here, which is the\n/// wrapping answer the other backends were made to give.\nconst ARITH_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlDiv(a, b int32) int32 {\n\tif b == 0 {\n\t\ttlFail(\"divided by zero\")\n\t}\n\treturn a / b\n}\n\nfunc tlRem(a, b int32) int32 {\n\tif b == 0 {\n\t\ttlFail(\"divided by zero\")\n\t}\n\treturn a % b\n}\n\"#;\n\n/// The same shape at 64 bits (kantord/toylang#83): Go defines `MIN / -1` as `MIN` for int64\n/// exactly as for int32, so only the zero divisor needs the guard here too.\nconst ARITH64_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlDiv64(a, b int64) int64 {\n\tif b == 0 {\n\t\ttlFail(\"divided by zero\")\n\t}\n\treturn a / b\n}\n\nfunc tlRem64(a, b int64) int64 {","lineStart":120,"lineEnd":209,"totalLines":1488,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/emit_go.rs","time":{"start":1788464619065,"end":1788464619074}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I see: `Kind::Local(id)` emits `t_5.clone()` in Rust! So inside the map closure, the param local reads are `.clone()` — the closure param `|e: &T|` and `e.clone()` gives an owned T. So `.name` on the local: base = `t_5.clone()` (an owned Record), then `.name` — moves the String field out of the owned record. Good — no borrow issue.\n\nSo for Rust sort_by: the projection body emitted with the param local will clone the element first. So I can emit:\n\n```rust\nlet mut out = v.to_vec();\nout.sort_by(|a, b| key(a).cmp(&key(b));\nout\n```\n\nBut key(a) evaluates key twice per comparison (a and b each compared multiple times). Alternatively `sort_by_key` with key computed once per element? `sort_by_key(|a| key(a))` — the closure takes `&T` and returns K (owned. `key(a)` = the body expression with local `a`... but the body expects a `Local` which emits `{local}.clone()`. If I bind the closure param `a: &T`, then `a.clone()` is owned T, and `.name` reads out a String. Good: `out.sort_by_key(|a: &T| -> K { key(a) })`. This computes the key once per element (sort_by_key caches keys. Yes, `sort_by_key` uses a key cache in the slice sort — it's the \"Schwartzian transform\" internally. So stable! Rust's `sort_by_key` is stable? `slice::sort_by_key` is stable (yes, stable sort. \n\nSo Rust helper:\n\n```rust\nfn tl_sort_by<T, K: Ord, F: Fn(&T) -> K>(v: &[T], key: F) -> Vec<T> {\n    let mut out = v.to_vec();\n    out.sort_by_key(key);\n    out\n}\n```\n\nAnd emission: `tl_sort_by(&{source}, |{param}: &{elem}| -> {key_ty} {{ {body} }})`.\n\nWait — the body emits `{local}.clone()` for the param; so `|a: &T| -> K { a.clone().name }` works. But the param type in closure: `&T`. The body's local read clones. Good.\n\nBut hold on — the body expression may reference the param multiple times or inside nested mappers... but the checker limits that. Fine.\n\n\n\nFor max_by Rust:\n\n```rust\nfn tl_max_by<T, K: Ord, F: Fn(&T) -> K>(v: &[T], key: F) -> Option<T> {\n    let mut best: Option<(K, usize)> = None;\n    // hmm — need ties resolve to first\n}\n```\n\nActually simpler with stable max: iterate, keep first on ties:\n\n```rust\nfn tl_max_by<T: Clone, K: Ord, F: Fn(&T) -> K>(v: &[T], key: F) -> Option<T> {\n    let mut best_key: Option<K> = None;\n    let mut best: Option<T> = None;\n    for e in v {\n        let k = key(e);\n        if best_key.as_ref().map_or(true, |bk| &k > bk) {\n            best_key = Some(k);\n            best = Some(e.clone());\n        }\n    }\n    best\n}\n```\n\nGood.\n\n\n\nNow let me look at Go's helpers for sort to see the pattern (tlSort etc.. Let me read the Go helper definitions.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068cc5e350016MyHoXr4sEVXdT","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","messageID":"msg_068cc4029001RUmBejMbCgICzL"}}
