{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788464629265,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_f5d11789994643ffa7a8470f","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cat runtime/go.mod 2>/dev/null; rg -n \"slices\\.|cmp\\.\" src/emit_go.rs | head"},"output":"129:// `cmp.Ordered` is exactly the constraint the checker's own `orderable` restricts `sort`'s\n131:const SORT_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlSort[T cmp.Ordered](v []T) []T {\n133:\tslices.Sort(out)\n166:// `cmp.Ordered` covers both integer widths, so one helper serves Int and Int64. Absence is the\n168:const MAX_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlMax[T cmp.Ordered](v []T) tlOpt[T] {\n","metadata":{"output":"129:// `cmp.Ordered` is exactly the constraint the checker's own `orderable` restricts `sort`'s\n131:const SORT_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlSort[T cmp.Ordered](v []T) []T {\n133:\tslices.Sort(out)\n166:// `cmp.Ordered` covers both integer widths, so one helper serves Int and Int64. Absence is the\n168:const MAX_HELPER: &str = r#\"func tlMax[T cmp.Ordered](v []T) tlOpt[T] {\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cat runtime/go.mod 2>/dev/null; rg -n \"slices\\.|cmp\\.\" src/emit_go.rs | head","time":{"start":1788464629258,"end":1788464629263}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Go uses `cmp.Ordered` and `slices.Sort`. For Go, I can write generic helpers:\n\n```go\nfunc tlSortBy[T any, K cmp.Ordered](v []T, key func(T) K) []T {\n\tout := append([]T{}, v...)\n\tslices.SortStableFunc(out, func(a, b T) int {\n\t\treturn cmp.Compare(key(a), key(b))\n\t})\n\treturn out\n}\n```\n\nWait — `slices.SortStableFunc` sorts by a compare func that returns int. Go 1.21+ has `slices.SortStableFunc`. Let me check what Go version the repo targets. Let me look at how the Go backend emits comparators — e.g. the JS `tl_str_cmp` or sort. Actually, Go has `cmp.Compare` for cmp.Ordered. So:\n\n```go\nfunc tlSortBy[T any, K cmp.Ordered](v []T, key func(T) K) []T {\n\tout := append([]T{}, v...)\n\tslices.SortStableFunc(out, func(a, b T) int { return cmp.Compare(key(a), key(b)) })\n\treturn out\n}\n```\n\nBut this recomputes keys per comparison (O(n log n) key evals. Fine — behavior-correct; but note the key function may have side effects? The projection body could include division (traps)? Keys must be evaluated for each element at least once. Recomputing could double-trap... but key is pure-ish (no I/O; division by zero traps always the same). Fine. Actually, to be safe and efficient, use the decorate approach: compute keys once. But for Go, the comparator closure approach is simplest. Since the key function is pure (a projection, possibly with arithmetic that always yields the same result per element;, double evaluation is harmless semantically. jq's sort_by also may evaluate f multiple times? Actually jq evaluates f per element once (it's a Schwartzian).. \n\nFor stability: `slices.SortStableFunc` gives stability. Good.\n\nBut — what Go version? Let me check whether `slices.SortStableFunc` and `cmp.Compare` are available (Go 1.21+; 1.21? `cmp` package added in 1.21, `slices.SortStableFunc` also 1.21. Let me check the go.mod in the runtime folder.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068cc85e4001z02MWEr1dfh3gw","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","messageID":"msg_068cc5e84001cOvgVUgjgcDA6j"}}
