{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788464642353,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_5b6b471116e741468d0f53f0","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_js.rs","limit":80,"offset":120},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_js.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n120:   return out;\n121: }\n122: \"#;\n123: \n124: const JOIN_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n125: function tl_join(v, f) {\n126:   const parts = [];\n127:   for (let i = 0; i < v.length; i++) parts.push(f(v[i]));\n128:   return \\\"[\\\" + parts.join(\\\",\\\") + \\\"]\\\";\n129: }\n130: \";\n131: \n132: // JS string comparison compares UTF-16 code units, which disagrees with codepoint order on any\n133: // pair straddling a surrogate pair: a BMP character above U+DFFF (e.g. U+E000) sorts below an\n134: // astral character's lead surrogate even though its codepoint is smaller. Iterating with the\n135: // string iterator (rather than indexing) steps one codepoint at a time, surrogate pairs included.\n136: const STR_CMP_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n137: function tl_str_cmp(a, b) {\n138:   const ai = a[Symbol.iterator]();\n139:   const bi = b[Symbol.iterator]();\n140:   for (;;) {\n141:     const x = ai.next();\n142:     const y = bi.next();\n143:     if (x.done || y.done) return (x.done ? 0 : 1) - (y.done ? 0 : 1);\n144:     const cx = x.value.codePointAt(0);\n145:     const cy = y.value.codePointAt(0);\n146:     if (cx !== cy) return cx < cy ? -1 : 1;\n147:   }\n148: }\n149: \";\n150: \n151: // A record and a payload-carrying variant are both objects, and JS `===` on two objects is\n152: // identity, so `{a: 1} === {a: 1}` was false here and true on Python (kantord/toylang#68).\n153: // Structural equality is the ratified answer, so composites walk. Keyed rather than ordered:\n154: // two spellings of one record type may reach here with their keys in different orders.\n155: const EQ_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n156: function tl_eq(a, b) {\n157:   if (a === b) return true;\n158:   if (typeof a !== \\\"object\\\" || a === null || typeof b !== \\\"object\\\" || b === null) return false;\n159:   const keys = Object.keys(a);\n160:   if (keys.length !== Object.keys(b).length) return false;\n161:   return keys.every((k) => Object.hasOwn(b, k) && tl_eq(a[k], b[k]));\n162: }\n163: \";\n164: \n165: const JSONLINES_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n166: function tl_jsonlines(v, f) {\n167:   const parts = [];\n168:   for (let i = 0; i < v.length; i++) parts.push(f(v[i]));\n169:   return parts.join(\\\"\\\\n\\\");\n170: }\n171: \";\n172: \n173: // The string iterator steps one codepoint at a time, surrogate pairs included (the same\n174: // iterator STR_CMP_HELPER uses above), so there is no decoding to get right here.\n175: const CHARS_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n176: function tl_chars(s) {\n177:   return Array.from(s, (c) => c.codePointAt(0));\n178: }\n179: \";\n180: \n181: // Int and Int64 are different runtime types here -- Number and BigInt -- so `+` cannot mix\n182: // them and each width needs its own fold. `|0` is the 32-bit wrap (the same ToInt32 ARITH_HELPER\n183: // relies on); `BigInt.asIntN(64, ...)` is the 64-bit one ARITH64_HELPER uses.\n184: const SUM_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n185: function tl_sum(v) {\n186:   let acc = 0;\n187:   for (let i = 0; i < v.length; i++) acc = (acc + v[i]) | 0;\n188:   return acc;\n189: }\n190: function tl_sum64(v) {\n191:   let acc = 0n;\n192:   for (let i = 0; i < v.length; i++) acc = BigInt.asIntN(64, acc + v[i]);\n193:   return acc;\n194: }\n195: \";\n196: \n197: // `>` orders Number and BigInt alike, so one maximum serves both widths.\n198: const MAX_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n199: function tl_max(v) {\n\n(Showing lines 120-199 of 1139. Use offset=200 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"  return out;\n}\n\"#;\n\nconst JOIN_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nfunction tl_join(v, f) {\n  const parts = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i < v.length; i++) parts.push(f(v[i]));\n  return \\\"[\\\" + parts.join(\\\",\\\") + \\\"]\\\";\n}\n\";\n\n// JS string comparison compares UTF-16 code units, which disagrees with codepoint order on any\n// pair straddling a surrogate pair: a BMP character above U+DFFF (e.g. U+E000) sorts below an\n// astral character's lead surrogate even though its codepoint is smaller. Iterating with the\n// string iterator (rather than indexing) steps one codepoint at a time, surrogate pairs included.\nconst STR_CMP_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nfunction tl_str_cmp(a, b) {\n  const ai = a[Symbol.iterator]();\n  const bi = b[Symbol.iterator]();","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_js.rs","text":"  return out;\n}\n\"#;\n\nconst JOIN_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nfunction tl_join(v, f) {\n  const parts = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i < v.length; i++) parts.push(f(v[i]));\n  return \\\"[\\\" + parts.join(\\\",\\\") + \\\"]\\\";\n}\n\";\n\n// JS string comparison compares UTF-16 code units, which disagrees with codepoint order on any\n// pair straddling a surrogate pair: a BMP character above U+DFFF (e.g. U+E000) sorts below an\n// astral character's lead surrogate even though its codepoint is smaller. Iterating with the\n// string iterator (rather than indexing) steps one codepoint at a time, surrogate pairs included.\nconst STR_CMP_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nfunction tl_str_cmp(a, b) {\n  const ai = a[Symbol.iterator]();\n  const bi = b[Symbol.iterator]();\n  for (;;) {\n    const x = ai.next();\n    const y = bi.next();\n    if (x.done || y.done) return (x.done ? 0 : 1) - (y.done ? 0 : 1);\n    const cx = x.value.codePointAt(0);\n    const cy = y.value.codePointAt(0);\n    if (cx !== cy) return cx < cy ? -1 : 1;\n  }\n}\n\";\n\n// A record and a payload-carrying variant are both objects, and JS `===` on two objects is\n// identity, so `{a: 1} === {a: 1}` was false here and true on Python (kantord/toylang#68).\n// Structural equality is the ratified answer, so composites walk. Keyed rather than ordered:\n// two spellings of one record type may reach here with their keys in different orders.\nconst EQ_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nfunction tl_eq(a, b) {\n  if (a === b) return true;\n  if (typeof a !== \\\"object\\\" || a === null || typeof b !== \\\"object\\\" || b === null) return false;\n  const keys = Object.keys(a);\n  if (keys.length !== Object.keys(b).length) return false;\n  return keys.every((k) => Object.hasOwn(b, k) && tl_eq(a[k], b[k]));\n}\n\";\n\nconst JSONLINES_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nfunction tl_jsonlines(v, f) {\n  const parts = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i < v.length; i++) parts.push(f(v[i]));\n  return parts.join(\\\"\\\\n\\\");\n}\n\";\n\n// The string iterator steps one codepoint at a time, surrogate pairs included (the same\n// iterator STR_CMP_HELPER uses above), so there is no decoding to get right here.\nconst CHARS_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nfunction tl_chars(s) {\n  return Array.from(s, (c) => c.codePointAt(0));\n}\n\";\n\n// Int and Int64 are different runtime types here -- Number and BigInt -- so `+` cannot mix\n// them and each width needs its own fold. `|0` is the 32-bit wrap (the same ToInt32 ARITH_HELPER\n// relies on); `BigInt.asIntN(64, ...)` is the 64-bit one ARITH64_HELPER uses.\nconst SUM_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nfunction tl_sum(v) {\n  let acc = 0;\n  for (let i = 0; i < v.length; i++) acc = (acc + v[i]) | 0;\n  return acc;\n}\nfunction tl_sum64(v) {\n  let acc = 0n;\n  for (let i = 0; i < v.length; i++) acc = BigInt.asIntN(64, acc + v[i]);\n  return acc;\n}\n\";\n\n// `>` orders Number and BigInt alike, so one maximum serves both widths.\nconst MAX_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nfunction tl_max(v) {","lineStart":120,"lineEnd":199,"totalLines":1139,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/emit_js.rs","time":{"start":1788464642336,"end":1788464642352}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"In Go, Local emits `t_5` directly (no clone). Go copies values on assignment; the closure param is a value T (not a reference). So `.name` on a Go record value reads the field (a copy. Fine.\n\n.\n\nNow the JS backend: Sort emission already branches on elem type. For sort_by: I'll emit a helper `tl_sort_by(v, key)` that does the decorate-sort-undecorate or uses a comparator. JS sort is stable. A comparator `(a, b) => tl_cmp3(key(a), key(b))` — but keys can be Str or numbers/BigInt. The existing `tl_str_cmp` returns -1/0/1 for strings. For numbers: `a < b ? -1 : a > b ? 1 : 0`. For mixed key types — key type is fixed per sort_by call. I could emit a helper per key type... but simpler: emit `tl_sort_by(v, key)` where key returns the projected value, and inside helper use a generic compare. JS's `<` works on both Number and BigInt mixed? BigInt vs Number comparison works with relational operators (yes, `<` between BigInt and Number works fine. And strings compare lexicographically with `<`/`>` too. But mixing Str with numbers can't happen (fixed key type). So a single `cmp3(a, b)` helper: `(a, b) => a < b ? -1 : a > b ? 1 : 0` works for Number, BigInt, and Str alike. Actually for Str, `a < b` compares lexicographically by UTF-16 code units — which is wrong for codepoint ordering! The existing `tl_str_cmp` handles Str properly. For sort_by over a Str projection, need tl_str_cmp. Over Int/Int64 projections: the plain compare. Char: Char is an i32 number in JS backend. So — I can branch on the projection type like the existing Sort emission does: if key type is Str, use `tl_str_cmp`, else the plain three-way. Let me check `tl_str_cmp`.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068ccb91a001UZlE48AGxOmcgX","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","messageID":"msg_068cca332001zujmX2OZl49HoG"}}
