{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788464643221,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"id":"prt_068ccbc91001Dg0u20V8nqzAIg","messageID":"msg_068ccb948001lrzQj43c511S8X","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","snapshot":"4b2797779b42b25cca6cb1f7660061fcb9d31aa4","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788464655843,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_656dfda1e0b64ae482996806","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"jq --version"},"output":"jq-1.8.2\n","metadata":{"output":"jq-1.8.2\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"jq --version","time":{"start":1788464655838,"end":1788464655841}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"OK. Now let me think about the JS sort_by approach. Since sort_by keys can be Str (needing tl_str_cmp), or Int/Int64 (Number/BigInt, needing three-way `a < b ? -1 : a > b ? 1 : 0`), or Char (Number. I'll emit the comparator inline per the projection's type, using a helper that is a generic `tl_sort_by(v, cmp, key)`:\n\n```js\nfunction tl_sort_by(v, cmp, key) {\n  return [...v].sort((a, b) => cmp(key(a), key(b)));\n}\n```\n\nThat way the key is computed per comparison (twice per compare. Since key is pure, fine. And stability: JS `Array.prototype.sort` is stable (spec since ES2019. \n\nAlternatively compute keys once: `v.map((e, i) => [key(e), i, e]).sort(...).map(...` — more complex. Simplicity wins; the comparator approach with a pure key is fine. But — careful: the projection body may contain a division by zero? Re-evaluating a key per comparison would trap multiple times — but trapping stops the program anyway, so no observable difference. Fine.\n\nWait — but the JS emission for Map emits `{source}.map(({param}) => {body})` — for sort_by I'd emit `tl_sort_by({source}, {cmp}, ({param}) => {body})`. The cmp is chosen by the projection type.\n\n.\n\nActually — hold on. Is there an issue with `tl_sort_by` receiving the key closure as a callback and then calling `key(a)` inside a comparator — fine.\n\n.\n\nNow Lua: `table.sort` is NOT stable. So I need a stable approach. The decorate-sort-undecorate: build pairs (key, idx) and sort by key then idx. Or sort with a comparator that receives a and b (the elements) and computes keys — but stability lost. To get stability: include original index. Since Lua's table.sort comparator can't see indices directly, the decorate approach works:\n\n```lua\nlocal function tl_sort_by(v, key)\n  local out = {}\n  for i = 1, #v do\n    out[i] = { key = key(v[i]), i = i }\n  end\n  table.sort(out, function(a, b) return a.key < b.key or (a.key == b.key and a.i < b.i) end)\n  local res = {}\n  for i =  ., #out do res[i] = v[out[i].i] end\n  return res\nend\n```\n\nHmm — but Lua's `==` on tables is identity — keys are scalars (Int/Int64/Str/Char/, all scalars in Lua: numbers, strings. Char is a number. So key equality by `==` is fine. But wait — could the projection be a record? No — restricted to orderable scalars. Good.\n\n\n\nActually — wait. Do I even need stability for sort_by? jq's sort_by is stable; Python's sorted is stable; JS's sort stable; Go's slices.SortStableFunc stable; Rust's sort_by_key stable. So the language's sort_by should be stable. For Lua, table.sort isn't stable, so decorate with index tiebreak gives stability. Good.\n\n\n\nNow — Python: `sorted(v, key=f)` is stable. So emission: `sorted({source}, key=lambda {param}: {body})`. The body emits `{param}` as the local name — in Python, Local(id) → `t_5`. Lambda param named `t_5`. Body `.name` → `t_5[\"name\"]`. Good.\n\n\n\nmax_by Python: helper:\n\n```python\ndef tl_max_by(v, key):\n    best = None\n    best_key = None\n    for e in v:\n        k = key(e)\n        if best_key is None or k > best_key:\n            best_key = k\n            best = e\n    if best is None: return \"none\"\n    return {\"some\": best}\n```\n\nHmm — for ties, this keeps the first (strict >. Good.\n\n\n\nNow jq: `sort_by(f)` builtin is exactly this. And `max_by(f)` builtin exists too! jq 1.7+ has max_by. Let me verify that max_by exists in jq: Yes, jq has `max_by(f)` since 1.6? Actually jq manual: \"max_by(f) — The element of the input array with the greatest f\". Yes. But empty array errors. So:\n\n```\nsort_by: \"({} | sort_by(.key))\" — but wait, the projection body references the param local, not `.`. In jq, `Kind::Map` emits `[ {}[] | . as {param} | {body} ]`. So for sort_by: `[ {}[] | . as {param} | sort_by({body}) ]`? Hmm — jq's sort_by operates on the array itself, not on each element. The proper jq emission: `({} | sort_by(. as {param} | {body}))`? No — jq's sort_by takes a filter f and applies it to each element. So: `({source} | sort_by({param} as {body}))` — wait, in jq, the body references `{param}` (a variable bound via `as` earlier). Actually the source is an array; `sort_by(f)` where f is a filter that runs once per element with `.` = element. So I want f = the body with `.` replaced by `{param}`... but the body TIR references the param local, not `.`. In jq's Kind::Bind: `({value} as {local} | {body})`. So the projection filter would be `.{body-with-param}`? Hmm.\n\nLet me look at how jq emits `Kind::Map`: `[ {source}[] | . as {param} | {body} ]`. So inside the map, `. as t_5 | body. For sort_by, I need: sort the array by a key filter f where f is computed as `. as {param} | {body}`. So: `({source} | sort_by(. as {param} | {body}))`. \n\nWait — does that work? `sort_by(. as t_5 | t_5.name)`? `as` binds `.` to t_5 then pipes to `t_5.name` — which evaluates the field read on t_5. Hmm — jq's `as` binds and pipes; `(. as $x | f)` — yes that's a valid filter expression. Actually the toylang emitter already uses this pattern for Map: `[ {source}[] | . as {param} | {body} ]`. So the pattern `. as {param} | {body}` is the projection body as a jq filter over `.`. So sort_by: `({source} | sort_by(. as {param} | {body}))`. \n\nHmm, wait — jq's `as` in `sort_by(. as $x | ...)` — `$x` binds `.`'s value. Yes that works.\n\n.\n\nmax_by jq: `({source} | if length == 0 then \"none\" else {some: max_by(. as {param} | {body})} end)`. Need to confirm max_by exists in the installed jq version. Let me check the jq version.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068ccedc6001p4PPyWXE8OvRly","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","messageID":"msg_068ccb948001lrzQj43c511S8X"}}
