{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788464660348,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_e9753e50e9424d3a88ff39c2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/runtime/toylang.c"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/runtime/toylang.c</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: /* The native backend's runtime.\n2:  *\n3:  * Compiled and linked into every native binary by the `cc` invocation that already links the\n4:  * object file. The generated LLVM IR declares these and calls them; nothing here knows about\n5:  * toylang's types beyond what its signatures say.\n6:  *\n7:  * Nothing frees. Prototype 1.5 leaks deliberately: choosing between refcounting and tracing\n8:  * belongs with the mutation model, and a half-built refcount would be worse than an honest\n9:  * leak in a program that runs once and exits. Keeping every allocation in this file keeps that\n10:  * decision in one visible place.\n11:  */\n12: \n13: #include <stdint.h>\n14: #include <stdio.h>\n15: #include <stdlib.h>\n16: #include <string.h>\n17: #include <unistd.h>\n18: \n19: /* A toylang Str: bytes and a length, never null-terminated by contract. The bytes a literal\n20:  * points at happen to carry a trailing NUL so that a debugger can print them, but len excludes\n21:  * it and no code here reads past len. */\n22: typedef struct {\n23:     const char *ptr;\n24:     int64_t len;\n25: } tl_str;\n26: \n27: static void *tl_alloc(size_t n) {\n28:     void *p = malloc(n);\n29:     if (p == NULL) {\n30:         const char *msg = \"toylang: out of memory\\n\";\n31:         write(2, msg, strlen(msg));\n32:         exit(1);\n33:     }\n34:     return p;\n35: }\n36: \n37: static tl_str *tl_str_new(char *bytes, int64_t len) {\n38:     tl_str *s = tl_alloc(sizeof(tl_str));\n39:     s->ptr = bytes;\n40:     s->len = len;\n41:     return s;\n42: }\n43: \n44: tl_str *tl_concat(const tl_str *a, const tl_str *b) {\n45:     char *bytes = tl_alloc((size_t)(a->len + b->len));\n46:     memcpy(bytes, a->ptr, (size_t)a->len);\n47:     memcpy(bytes + a->len, b->ptr, (size_t)b->len);\n48:     return tl_str_new(bytes, a->len + b->len);\n49: }\n50: \n51: tl_str *tl_int_to_str(int64_t n) {\n52:     /* -9223372036854775808 is 20 characters plus a terminator. */\n53:     char buf[24];\n54:     int len = snprintf(buf, sizeof buf, \"%lld\", (long long)n);\n55:     char *bytes = tl_alloc((size_t)len);\n56:     memcpy(bytes, buf, (size_t)len);\n57:     return tl_str_new(bytes, len);\n58: }\n59: \n60: int64_t tl_str_eq(const tl_str *a, const tl_str *b) {\n61:     if (a->len != b->len) {\n62:         return 0;\n63:     }\n64:     return memcmp(a->ptr, b->ptr, (size_t)a->len) == 0;\n65: }\n66: \n67: /* Byte order, which is what Lua does. JavaScript compares UTF-16 code units, so the three\n68:  * backends agree on ASCII and are not guaranteed to beyond it. */\n69: int64_t tl_str_cmp(const tl_str *a, const tl_str *b) {\n70:     int64_t shared = a->len < b->len ? a->len : b->len;\n71:     int diff = memcmp(a->ptr, b->ptr, (size_t)shared);\n72:     if (diff != 0) {\n73:         return diff < 0 ? -1 : 1;\n74:     }\n75:     if (a->len == b->len) {\n76:         return 0;\n77:     }\n78:     return a->len < b->len ? -1 : 1;\n79: }\n80: \n81: /* One write for the payload and one for the newline, rather than copying to join them. */\n82: void tl_print(const tl_str *s) {\n83:     if (s->len > 0) {\n84:         (void)!write(1, s->ptr, (size_t)s->len);\n85:     }\n86:     (void)!write(1, \"\\n\", 1);\n87: }\n88: \n89: /* A Vec: a length and `ncols` columns, each holding `len` raw 8-byte slots.\n90:  *\n91:  * The layout is struct of arrays. A Vec of scalars has one column; a Vec of records has one\n92:  * column per field, which is what makes reading a field off a Vec a column rather than a gather.\n93:  *\n94:  * Every scalar toylang has fits one slot: an Int is an i64, and a Str, a record or a nested Vec\n95:  * is a pointer. That is what lets one set of functions serve every element type instead of one\n96:  * per width.\n97:  */\n98: typedef struct {\n99:     int64_t len;\n100:     int64_t ncols;\n101:     int64_t **cols;\n102: } tl_vec;\n103: \n104: tl_vec *tl_vec_new(int64_t len, int64_t ncols) {\n105:     tl_vec *v = tl_alloc(sizeof(tl_vec));\n106:     v->len = len;\n107:     v->ncols = ncols;\n108:     v->cols = tl_alloc((size_t)ncols * sizeof(int64_t *));\n109:     for (int64_t c = 0; c < ncols; c++) {\n110:         v->cols[c] = len > 0 ? tl_alloc((size_t)len * sizeof(int64_t)) : NULL;\n111:     }\n112:     return v;\n113: }\n114: \n115: int64_t tl_vec_len(const tl_vec *v) {\n116:     return v->len;\n117: }\n118: \n119: int64_t tl_vec_get(const tl_vec *v, int64_t col, int64_t i) {\n120:     return v->cols[col][i];\n121: }\n122: \n123: void tl_vec_set(tl_vec *v, int64_t col, int64_t i, int64_t value) {\n124:     v->cols[col][i] = value;\n125: }\n126: \n127: /* One field of a Vec of records, as a Vec of that field's type.\n128:  *\n129:  * The column is shared rather than copied, so `.name` on a Vec<User> costs one small header and\n130:  * no element work. This is the whole reason the layout is struct of arrays.\n131:  */\n132: tl_vec *tl_vec_column(const tl_vec *v, int64_t col) {\n133:     tl_vec *out = tl_alloc(sizeof(tl_vec));\n134:     out->len = v->len;\n135:     out->ncols = 1;\n136:     out->cols = tl_alloc(sizeof(int64_t *));\n137:     out->cols[0] = v->cols[col];\n138:     return out;\n139: }\n140: \n141: /* select in two passes: count survivors, then fill. Two passes over a mask beats growing an\n142:  * array, and every column is compacted with the same surviving indices. */\n143: tl_vec *tl_vec_from_mask(const tl_vec *src, const int8_t *keep) {\n144:     int64_t n = 0;\n145:     for (int64_t i = 0; i < src->len; i++) {\n146:         n += keep[i] != 0;\n147:     }\n148:     tl_vec *out = tl_vec_new(n, src->ncols);\n149:     int64_t j = 0;\n150:     for (int64_t i = 0; i < src->len; i++) {\n151:         if (keep[i]) {\n152:             for (int64_t c = 0; c < src->ncols; c++) {\n153:                 out->cols[c][j] = src->cols[c][i];\n154:             }\n155:             j++;\n156:         }\n157:     }\n158:     return out;\n159: }\n160: \n161: int8_t *tl_mask_new(int64_t len) {\n162:     return len > 0 ? tl_alloc((size_t)len) : NULL;\n163: }\n164: \n165: void tl_mask_set(int8_t *mask, int64_t i, int64_t value) {\n166:     mask[i] = value != 0;\n167: }\n168: \n169: /* A record: one slot per field, in the field order the type declares. Records only ever\n170:  * arrive from input, since the language has no expression that builds one. */\n171: int64_t *tl_rec_new(int64_t nfields) {\n172:     return tl_alloc((size_t)nfields * sizeof(int64_t));\n173: }\n174: \n175: int64_t tl_rec_get(const int64_t *r, int64_t field) {\n176:     return r[field];\n177: }\n178: \n179: void tl_rec_set(int64_t *r, int64_t field, int64_t value) {\n180:     r[field] = value;\n181: }\n182: \n183: /* JSON string escaping, matching what the Lua and JavaScript printers emit. Anything below\n184:  * space goes out as \\u00xx, which is what both of the others do for control characters. */\n185: tl_str *tl_quote(const tl_str *s) {\n186:     /* Worst case every byte becomes \\u00xx, plus the two quotes. */\n187:     char *out = tl_alloc((size_t)(s->len * 6 + 2));\n188:     int64_t n = 0;\n189:     out[n++] = '\"';\n190:     for (int64_t i = 0; i < s->len; i++) {\n191:         unsigned char c = (unsigned char)s->ptr[i];\n192:         switch (c) {\n193:             case '\"': out[n++] = '\\\\'; out[n++] = '\"'; break;\n194:             case '\\\\': out[n++] = '\\\\'; out[n++] = '\\\\'; break;\n195:             case '\\n': out[n++] = '\\\\'; out[n++] = 'n'; break;\n196:             case '\\r': out[n++] = '\\\\'; out[n++] = 'r'; break;\n197:             case '\\t': out[n++] = '\\\\'; out[n++] = 't'; break;\n198:             default:\n199:                 if (c < 0x20) {\n200:                     n += snprintf(out + n, 7, \"\\\\u%04x\", c);\n201:                 } else {\n202:                     out[n++] = (char)c;\n203:                 }\n204:         }\n205:     }\n206:     out[n++] = '\"';\n207:     return tl_str_new(out, n);\n208: }\n209: \n210: /* Concatenate `parts`, which holds tl_str pointers, between `open` and `close` with `sep`\n211:  * between elements. One allocation, so printing a Vec is not quadratic in its length. */\n212: tl_str *tl_str_join(const tl_vec *parts, const tl_str *open, const tl_str *sep,\n213:                     const tl_str *close) {\n214:     int64_t total = open->len + close->len;\n215:     for (int64_t i = 0; i < parts->len; i++) {\n216:         total += ((const tl_str *)tl_vec_get(parts, 0, i))->len;\n217:         if (i > 0) {\n218:             total += sep->len;\n219:         }\n220:     }\n221: \n222:     char *out = tl_alloc((size_t)total);\n223:     int64_t n = 0;\n224:     memcpy(out + n, open->ptr, (size_t)open->len);\n225:     n += open->len;\n226:     for (int64_t i = 0; i < parts->len; i++) {\n227:         if (i > 0) {\n228:             memcpy(out + n, sep->ptr, (size_t)sep->len);\n229:             n += sep->len;\n230:         }\n231:         const tl_str *p = (const tl_str *)tl_vec_get(parts, 0, i);\n232:         memcpy(out + n, p->ptr, (size_t)p->len);\n233:         n += p->len;\n234:     }\n235:     memcpy(out + n, close->ptr, (size_t)close->len);\n236:     n += close->len;\n237:     return tl_str_new(out, n);\n238: }\n239: \n240: /* Reading input.\n241:  *\n242:  * The parser is driven by a type descriptor the compiler emits as one string, so it only ever\n243:  * looks for the shape the program declared. The grammar is:\n244:  *\n245:  *   s            Str\n246:  *   i            Int\n247:  *   b            Bool\n248:  *   [T           Vec of T\n249:  *   {n,name:T,...}   record with n fields, in the type's declared order\n250:  *   e{n,Name,variant,variant:T,...}   enum with n variants, in declaration order (the\n251:  *                variant's position is its tag); a variant with no `:T` is a unit variant.\n252:  *                Name is what a mismatch says it expected, and what `@` names.\n253:  *   @Name        the enum called Name, whose descriptor is still open around this one. A\n254:  *                recursive enum's payload names itself back rather than spelling itself out\n255:  *                again, which would have no end (kantord/toylang#94).\n256:  *\n257:  * It re-validates rather than trusting a pre-checked shape, so a built binary works on its own\n258:  * with `./adults < data.json` and not only under the test harness. A mismatch names the path\n259:  * that failed and exits non-zero, which is what the Rust-side check does too.\n260:  */\n261: \n262: typedef struct {\n263:     const char *p;\n264:     const char *end;\n265: } tl_json;\n266: \n267: /* The enums whose descriptors are open around the type being parsed, innermost first. `@Name`\n268:  * resolves by walking up this chain, so a name is always the nearest enclosing enum of that\n269:  * name -- which is what makes two instantiations of one generic enum, nested inside each other,\n270:  * resolve to the right one. */\n271: typedef struct tl_open_enum {\n272:     const char *name;\n273:     int64_t name_len;\n274:     /* The `e{...}` this name was opened at, which is what `@Name` parses as. */\n275:     const char *desc;\n276:     const struct tl_open_enum *up;\n277: } tl_open_enum;\n278: \n279: static void tl_fail(const char *what, const char *path) {\n280:     char buf[512];\n281:     int n = snprintf(buf, sizeof buf, \"toylang: input: %s at %s\\n\", what,\n282:                      path[0] ? path : \"input\");\n283:     (void)!write(2, buf, (size_t)n);\n284:     exit(1);\n285: }\n286: \n287: static void tl_skip_ws(tl_json *j) {\n288:     while (j->p < j->end && (*j->p == ' ' || *j->p == '\\t' || *j->p == '\\n' || *j->p == '\\r')) {\n289:         j->p++;\n290:     }\n291: }\n292: \n293: static void tl_expect(tl_json *j, char c, const char *path) {\n294:     tl_skip_ws(j);\n295:     if (j->p >= j->end || *j->p != c) {\n296:         char what[32];\n297:         snprintf(what, sizeof what, \"expected `%c`\", c);\n298:         tl_fail(what, path);\n299:     }\n300:     j->p++;\n301: }\n302: \n303: /* A growable list of slots, used while an array's length is still unknown. */\n304: typedef struct {\n305:     int64_t *data;\n306:     int64_t len;\n307:     int64_t cap;\n308: } tl_list;\n309: \n310: static void tl_list_push(tl_list *l, int64_t v) {\n311:     if (l->len == l->cap) {\n312:         int64_t cap = l->cap ? l->cap * 2 : 8;\n313:         int64_t *data = tl_alloc((size_t)cap * sizeof(int64_t));\n314:         memcpy(data, l->data, (size_t)l->len * sizeof(int64_t));\n315:         l->data = data;\n316:         l->cap = cap;\n317:     }\n318:     l->data[l->len++] = v;\n319: }\n320: \n321: /* Advance `t` past one complete type in the descriptor. */\n322: static const char *tl_type_skip(const char *t) {\n323:     switch (*t) {\n324:         case 's': case 'i': case 'b':\n325:             return t + 1;\n326:         case '[':\n327:             return tl_type_skip(t + 1);\n328:         case '@':\n329:             /* A back-reference is one token: the name runs to whatever delimits the type. */\n330:             t++;\n331:             while (*t != ',' && *t != '}') {\n332:                 t++;\n333:             }\n334:             return t;\n335:         case '{': {\n336:             t++;\n337:             int64_t n = 0;\n338:             while (*t != ',') {\n339:                 n = n * 10 + (*t++ - '0');\n340:             }\n341:             t++;\n342:             for (int64_t f = 0; f < n; f++) {\n343:                 while (*t != ':') {\n344:                     t++;\n345:                 }\n346:                 t = tl_type_skip(t + 1);\n347:                 if (*t == ',') {\n348:                     t++;\n349:                 }\n350:             }\n351:             return t + 1; /* past '}' */\n352:         }\n353:         case 'e': {\n354:             t += 2; /* past \"e{\" */\n355:             int64_t n = 0;\n356:             while (*t != ',') {\n357:                 n = n * 10 + (*t++ - '0');\n358:             }\n359:             t++;\n360:             while (*t != ',' && *t != '}') {\n361:                 t++; /* the enum's name */\n362:             }\n363:             for (int64_t v = 0; v < n; v++) {\n364:                 t++; /* past ',' */\n365:                 while (*t != ':' && *t != ',' && *t != '}') {\n366:                     t++;\n367:                 }\n368:                 if (*t == ':') {\n369:                     t = tl_type_skip(t + 1);\n370:                 }\n371:             }\n372:             return t + 1; /* past '}' */\n373:         }\n374:         default:\n375:             tl_fail(\"bad type descriptor\", \"\");\n376:             return t;\n377:     }\n378: }\n379: \n380: static int64_t tl_parse(tl_json *j, const char *t, const char *path,\n381:                         const tl_open_enum *scope);\n382: \n383: /* Consumes exactly 4 hex digits at j->p and returns them as a UTF-16 code unit. */\n384: static uint32_t tl_hex4(tl_json *j, const char *path) {\n385:     uint32_t v = 0;\n386:     for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\n387:         if (j->p >= j->end) {\n388:             tl_fail(\"unterminated \\\\u escape\", path);\n389:         }\n390:         char c = *j->p;\n391:         int d = (c >= '0' && c <= '9')   ? c - '0'\n392:                 : (c >= 'a' && c <= 'f') ? c - 'a' + 10\n393:                 : (c >= 'A' && c <= 'F') ? c - 'A' + 10\n394:                                          : -1;\n395:         if (d < 0) {\n396:             tl_fail(\"bad \\\\u escape\", path);\n397:         }\n398:         v = v * 16 + (uint32_t)d;\n399:         j->p++;\n400:     }\n401:     return v;\n402: }\n403: \n404: /* Appends cp's UTF-8 encoding to out, which tl_parse_string has already sized generously\n405:  * enough: every multi-byte encoding here is shorter than the \\u escape(s) that produced it. */\n406: static void tl_utf8_encode(char *out, int64_t *n, uint32_t cp) {\n407:     if (cp < 0x80) {\n408:         out[(*n)++] = (char)cp;\n409:     } else if (cp < 0x800) {\n410:         out[(*n)++] = (char)(0xC0 | (cp >> 6));\n411:         out[(*n)++] = (char)(0x80 | (cp & 0x3F));\n412:     } else if (cp < 0x10000) {\n413:         out[(*n)++] = (char)(0xE0 | (cp >> 12));\n414:         out[(*n)++] = (char)(0x80 | ((cp >> 6) & 0x3F));\n415:         out[(*n)++] = (char)(0x80 | (cp & 0x3F));\n416:     } else {\n417:         out[(*n)++] = (char)(0xF0 | (cp >> 18));\n418:         out[(*n)++] = (char)(0x80 | ((cp >> 12) & 0x3F));\n419:         out[(*n)++] = (char)(0x80 | ((cp >> 6) & 0x3F));\n420:         out[(*n)++] = (char)(0x80 | (cp & 0x3F));\n421:     }\n422: }\n423: \n424: static tl_str *tl_parse_string(tl_json *j, const char *path) {\n425:     tl_expect(j, '\"', path);\n426:     char *out = tl_alloc((size_t)(j->end - j->p));\n427:     int64_t n = 0;\n428:     while (j->p < j->end && *j->p != '\"') {\n429:         if (*j->p == '\\\\') {\n430:             j->p++;\n431:             if (j->p >= j->end) {\n432:                 tl_fail(\"unterminated escape\", path);\n433:             }\n434:             // A control character with no named shorthand (NUL, say) is the only escape\n435:             // that reaches this in practice: src/lib.rs re-serializes input through\n436:             // serde_json before any backend sees it, and that writer emits raw UTF-8 for\n437:             // everything else. A surrogate pair is still combined into one codepoint below,\n438:             // to be a JSON decoder rather than one tied to that upstream behavior.\n439:             if (*j->p == 'u') {\n440:                 j->p++;\n441:                 uint32_t cu = tl_hex4(j, path);\n442:                 uint32_t cp;\n443:                 if (cu >= 0xD800 && cu <= 0xDBFF) {\n444:                     if (j->p + 1 >= j->end || j->p[0] != '\\\\' || j->p[1] != 'u') {\n445:                         tl_fail(\"unpaired surrogate\", path);\n446:                     }\n447:                     j->p += 2;\n448:                     uint32_t lo = tl_hex4(j, path);\n449:                     if (lo < 0xDC00 || lo > 0xDFFF) {\n450:                         tl_fail(\"unpaired surrogate\", path);\n451:                     }\n452:                     cp = 0x10000 + ((cu - 0xD800) << 10) + (lo - 0xDC00);\n453:                 } else if (cu >= 0xDC00 && cu <= 0xDFFF) {\n454:                     tl_fail(\"unpaired surrogate\", path);\n455:                 } else {\n456:                     cp = cu;\n457:                 }\n458:                 tl_utf8_encode(out, &n, cp);\n459:                 continue;\n460:             }\n461:             switch (*j->p) {\n462:                 case '\"': out[n++] = '\"'; break;\n463:                 case '\\\\': out[n++] = '\\\\'; break;\n464:                 case '/': out[n++] = '/'; break;\n465:                 case 'n': out[n++] = '\\n'; break;\n466:                 case 't': out[n++] = '\\t'; break;\n467:                 case 'r': out[n++] = '\\r'; break;\n468:                 case 'b': out[n++] = '\\b'; break;\n469:                 case 'f': out[n++] = '\\f'; break;\n470:                 default: tl_fail(\"unsupported escape\", path);\n471:             }\n472:             j->p++;\n473:         } else {\n474:             out[n++] = *j->p++;\n475:         }\n476:     }\n477:     tl_expect(j, '\"', path);\n478:     return tl_str_new(out, n);\n479: }\n480: \n481: /* Skip one JSON value without interpreting it, for fields the type does not declare. */\n482: static void tl_skip_value(tl_json *j, const char *path) {\n483:     tl_skip_ws(j);\n484:     if (j->p >= j->end) {\n485:         tl_fail(\"unexpected end of input\", path);\n486:     }\n487:     if (*j->p == '\"') {\n488:         tl_parse_string(j, path);\n489:         return;\n490:     }\n491:     if (*j->p == '[' || *j->p == '{') {\n492:         char open = *j->p;\n493:         char close = open == '[' ? ']' : '}';\n494:         int depth = 0;\n495:         while (j->p < j->end) {\n496:             if (*j->p == '\"') {\n497:                 tl_parse_string(j, path);\n498:                 continue;\n499:             }\n500:             if (*j->p == open) {\n501:                 depth++;\n502:             } else if (*j->p == close) {\n503:                 depth--;\n504:                 if (depth == 0) {\n505:                     j->p++;\n506:                     return;\n507:                 }\n508:             }\n509:             j->p++;\n510:         }\n511:         tl_fail(\"unterminated value\", path);\n512:     }\n513:     while (j->p < j->end && *j->p != ',' && *j->p != '}' && *j->p != ']') {\n514:         j->p++;\n515:     }\n516: }\n517: \n518: static int64_t tl_parse_record(tl_json *j, const char *t, const char *path,\n519:                                const tl_open_enum *scope) {\n520:     /* Field names and types, read off the descriptor once. */\n521:     t++;\n522:     int64_t n = 0;\n523:     while (*t != ',') {\n524:         n = n * 10 + (*t++ - '0');\n525:     }\n526:     t++;\n527: \n528:     const char *names[64];\n529:     int64_t name_lens[64];\n530:     const char *types[64];\n531:     if (n > 64) {\n532:         tl_fail(\"too many record fields\", path);\n533:     }\n534:     for (int64_t f = 0; f < n; f++) {\n535:         names[f] = t;\n536:         while (*t != ':') {\n537:             t++;\n538:         }\n539:         name_lens[f] = t - names[f];\n540:         types[f] = t + 1;\n541:         t = tl_type_skip(t + 1);\n542:         if (*t == ',') {\n543:             t++;\n544:         }\n545:     }\n546: \n547:     int64_t *rec = tl_rec_new(n);\n548:     int8_t seen[64];\n549:     memset(seen, 0, sizeof seen);\n550: \n551:     tl_expect(j, '{', path);\n552:     tl_skip_ws(j);\n553:     if (j->p < j->end && *j->p == '}') {\n554:         j->p++;\n555:     } else {\n556:         for (;;) {\n557:             tl_str *key = tl_parse_string(j, path);\n558:             tl_expect(j, ':', path);\n559: \n560:             int64_t match = -1;\n561:             for (int64_t f = 0; f < n; f++) {\n562:                 if (key->len == name_lens[f] && memcmp(key->ptr, names[f], (size_t)key->len) == 0) {\n563:                     match = f;\n564:                     break;\n565:                 }\n566:             }\n567:             if (match < 0) {\n568:                 /* Fields the program did not declare are ignored, matching the Rust-side check. */\n569:                 tl_skip_value(j, path);\n570:             } else {\n571:                 char sub[256];\n572:                 snprintf(sub, sizeof sub, \"%s.%.*s\", path, (int)key->len, key->ptr);\n573:                 rec[match] = tl_parse(j, types[match], sub, scope);\n574:                 seen[match] = 1;\n575:             }\n576: \n577:             tl_skip_ws(j);\n578:             if (j->p < j->end && *j->p == ',') {\n579:                 j->p++;\n580:                 continue;\n581:             }\n582:             break;\n583:         }\n584:         tl_expect(j, '}', path);\n585:     }\n586: \n587:     for (int64_t f = 0; f < n; f++) {\n588:         if (!seen[f]) {\n589:             char what[256];\n590:             snprintf(what, sizeof what, \"missing field `%.*s`\", (int)name_lens[f], names[f]);\n591:             tl_fail(what, path);\n592:         }\n593:     }\n594:     return (int64_t)rec;\n595: }\n596: \n597: /* One enum value spans two JSON shapes (ADR 0009): a bare string for a unit variant, a\n598:  * single-key object for a payload one. What comes back is the same two-slot box the compiler\n599:  * builds for a constructed enum: slot 0 the variant's declaration index, slot 1 the payload. */\n600: static int64_t tl_parse_enum(tl_json *j, const char *t, const char *path,\n601:                              const tl_open_enum *scope) {\n602:     const char *self = t;\n603:     t += 2; /* past \"e{\" */\n604:     int64_t n = 0;\n605:     while (*t != ',') {\n606:         n = n * 10 + (*t++ - '0');\n607:     }\n608:     t++;\n609:     const char *ename = t;\n610:     while (*t != ',' && *t != '}') {\n611:         t++;\n612:     }\n613:     int64_t ename_len = t - ename;\n614: \n615:     const char *names[64];\n616:     int64_t name_lens[64];\n617:     const char *types[64]; /* NULL marks a unit variant */\n618:     if (n > 64) {\n619:         tl_fail(\"too many variants\", path);\n620:     }\n621:     for (int64_t v = 0; v < n; v++) {\n622:         t++;\n623:         names[v] = t;\n624:         while (*t != ':' && *t != ',' && *t != '}') {\n625:             t++;\n626:         }\n627:         name_lens[v] = t - names[v];\n628:         if (*t == ':') {\n629:             types[v] = t + 1;\n630:             t = tl_type_skip(t + 1);\n631:         } else {\n632:             types[v] = NULL;\n633:         }\n634:     }\n635: \n636:     tl_skip_ws(j);\n637:     if (j->p < j->end && *j->p == '\"') {\n638:         tl_str *s = tl_parse_string(j, path);\n639:         for (int64_t v = 0; v < n; v++) {\n640:             if (types[v] == NULL && s->len == name_lens[v] &&\n641:                 memcmp(s->ptr, names[v], (size_t)s->len) == 0) {\n642:                 int64_t *box = tl_rec_new(2);\n643:                 box[0] = v;\n644:                 return (int64_t)box;\n645:             }\n646:         }\n647:         char what[256];\n648:         snprintf(what, sizeof what, \"`%.*s` is not a unit variant of %.*s\", (int)s->len,\n649:                  s->ptr, (int)ename_len, ename);\n650:         tl_fail(what, path);\n651:     }\n652:     if (j->p < j->end && *j->p == '{') {\n653:         j->p++;\n654:         tl_str *key = tl_parse_string(j, path);\n655:         tl_expect(j, ':', path);\n656:         for (int64_t v = 0; v < n; v++) {\n657:             if (types[v] != NULL && key->len == name_lens[v] &&\n658:                 memcmp(key->ptr, names[v], (size_t)key->len) == 0) {\n659:                 char sub[256];\n660:                 snprintf(sub, sizeof sub, \"%s.%.*s\", path, (int)key->len, key->ptr);\n661:                 int64_t *box = tl_rec_new(2);\n662:                 box[0] = v;\n663:                 /* Open around the payload, so a `@` inside it names this enum. */\n664:                 tl_open_enum open = {ename, ename_len, self, scope};\n665:                 box[1] = tl_parse(j, types[v], sub, &open);\n666:                 /* One key is the whole shape, so the wrapper closes right here. */\n667:                 tl_expect(j, '}', path);\n668:                 return (int64_t)box;\n669:             }\n670:         }\n671:         char what[256];\n672:         snprintf(what, sizeof what, \"`%.*s` is not a payload variant of %.*s\", (int)key->len,\n673:                  key->ptr, (int)ename_len, ename);\n674:         tl_fail(what, path);\n675:     }\n676:     char what[256];\n677:     snprintf(what, sizeof what, \"expected %.*s\", (int)ename_len, ename);\n678:     tl_fail(what, path);\n679:     return 0;\n680: }\n681: \n682: /* A Vec of records is parsed element by element and then transposed into columns. Filling the\n683:  * columns directly would avoid materialising each record, and is worth doing when the parser\n684:  * stops being the cheapest part of reading input. */\n685: static int64_t tl_parse_vec(tl_json *j, const char *t, const char *path,\n686:                             const tl_open_enum *scope) {\n687:     const char *elem = t + 1;\n688:     /* Whether the element is a record is a separate question from how many columns it has: a\n689:      * record with one field also has one column, so the count cannot stand in for the test. */\n690:     int is_record = *elem == '{';\n691:     int64_t ncols = 1;\n692:     if (is_record) {\n693:         const char *c = elem + 1;\n694:         ncols = 0;\n695:         while (*c != ',') {\n696:             ncols = ncols * 10 + (*c++ - '0');\n697:         }\n698:     }\n699: \n700:     tl_list items = {NULL, 0, 0};\n701:     tl_expect(j, '[', path);\n702:     tl_skip_ws(j);\n703:     if (j->p < j->end && *j->p == ']') {\n704:         j->p++;\n705:     } else {\n706:         for (;;) {\n707:             char sub[256];\n708:             snprintf(sub, sizeof sub, \"%s[%lld]\", path, (long long)items.len);\n709:             tl_list_push(&items, tl_parse(j, elem, sub, scope));\n710:             tl_skip_ws(j);\n711:             if (j->p < j->end && *j->p == ',') {\n712:                 j->p++;\n713:                 continue;\n714:             }\n715:             break;\n716:         }\n717:         tl_expect(j, ']', path);\n718:     }\n719: \n720:     tl_vec *v = tl_vec_new(items.len, ncols);\n721:     for (int64_t i = 0; i < items.len; i++) {\n722:         if (!is_record) {\n723:             v->cols[0][i] = items.data[i];\n724:         } else {\n725:             const int64_t *rec = (const int64_t *)items.data[i];\n726:             for (int64_t c = 0; c < ncols; c++) {\n727:                 v->cols[c][i] = rec[c];\n728:             }\n729:         }\n730:     }\n731:     return (int64_t)v;\n732: }\n733: \n734: static int64_t tl_parse(tl_json *j, const char *t, const char *path,\n735:                         const tl_open_enum *scope) {\n736:     tl_skip_ws(j);\n737:     if (j->p >= j->end) {\n738:         tl_fail(\"unexpected end of input\", path);\n739:     }\n740:     switch (*t) {\n741:         case 's': {\n742:             if (*j->p != '\"') {\n743:                 tl_fail(\"expected a string\", path);\n744:             }\n745:             return (int64_t)tl_parse_string(j, path);\n746:         }\n747:         case 'i': {\n748:             const char *start = j->p;\n749:             if (j->p < j->end && (*j->p == '-' || *j->p == '+')) {\n750:                 j->p++;\n751:             }\n752:             while (j->p < j->end && *j->p >= '0' && *j->p <= '9') {\n753:                 j->p++;\n754:             }\n755:             if (j->p == start) {\n756:                 tl_fail(\"expected an integer\", path);\n757:             }\n758:             /* A float where Int was declared is an error, not a truncation. */\n759:             if (j->p < j->end && (*j->p == '.' || *j->p == 'e' || *j->p == 'E')) {\n760:                 tl_fail(\"expected an integer, found a non-integer number\", path);\n761:             }\n762:             char buf[32];\n763:             int64_t n = j->p - start;\n764:             if (n >= (int64_t)sizeof buf) {\n765:                 tl_fail(\"integer is out of range\", path);\n766:             }\n767:             memcpy(buf, start, (size_t)n);\n768:             buf[n] = 0;\n769:             return strtoll(buf, NULL, 10);\n770:         }\n771:         case 'b': {\n772:             if (j->end - j->p >= 4 && memcmp(j->p, \"true\", 4) == 0) {\n773:                 j->p += 4;\n774:                 return 1;\n775:             }\n776:             if (j->end - j->p >= 5 && memcmp(j->p, \"false\", 5) == 0) {\n777:                 j->p += 5;\n778:                 return 0;\n779:             }\n780:             tl_fail(\"expected a boolean\", path);\n781:             return 0;\n782:         }\n783:         case '[':\n784:             if (*j->p != '[') {\n785:                 tl_fail(\"expected an array\", path);\n786:             }\n787:             return tl_parse_vec(j, t, path, scope);\n788:         case '{':\n789:             if (*j->p != '{') {\n790:                 tl_fail(\"expected an object\", path);\n791:             }\n792:             return tl_parse_record(j, t, path, scope);\n793:         case 'e':\n794:             /* Which of its two shapes arrived is tl_parse_enum's own question. */\n795:             return tl_parse_enum(j, t, path, scope);\n796:         case '@': {\n797:             /* The enum this names is open around here, and its descriptor is where to resume. */\n798:             const char *name = t + 1;\n799:             int64_t len = 0;\n800:             while (name[len] != ',' && name[len] != '}') {\n801:                 len++;\n802:             }\n803:             for (const tl_open_enum *e = scope; e != NULL; e = e->up) {\n804:                 if (e->name_len == len && memcmp(e->name, name, (size_t)len) == 0) {\n805:                     return tl_parse(j, e->desc, path, scope);\n806:                 }\n807:             }\n808:             tl_fail(\"bad type descriptor\", path);\n809:             return 0;\n810:         }\n811:         default:\n812:             tl_fail(\"bad type descriptor\", path);\n813:             return 0;\n814:     }\n815: }\n816: \n817: int64_t tl_read_input(const tl_str *descriptor) {\n818:     size_t cap = 1 << 16;\n819:     size_t len = 0;\n820:     char *buf = tl_alloc(cap);\n821:     for (;;) {\n822:         if (len == cap) {\n823:             cap *= 2;\n824:             char *bigger = tl_alloc(cap);\n825:             memcpy(bigger, buf, len);\n826:             buf = bigger;\n827:         }\n828:         ssize_t n = read(0, buf + len, cap - len);\n829:         if (n < 0) {\n830:             tl_fail(\"could not read stdin\", \"\");\n831:         }\n832:         if (n == 0) {\n833:             break;\n834:         }\n835:         len += (size_t)n;\n836:     }\n837: \n838:     /* The descriptor is a literal the compiler emitted, so it is NUL-terminated in practice;\n839:      * copy it anyway so the C string functions above have one for certain. */\n840:     char *t = tl_alloc((size_t)descriptor->len + 1);\n841:     memcpy(t, descriptor->ptr, (size_t)descriptor->len);\n842:     t[descriptor->len] = 0;\n843: \n844:     tl_json j = {buf, buf + len};\n845:     int64_t value = tl_parse(&j, t, \"input\", NULL);\n846:     tl_skip_ws(&j);\n847:     if (j.p != j.end) {\n848:         tl_fail(\"trailing content after the value\", \"input\");\n849:     }\n850:     return value;\n851: }\n852: \n853: /* Every remaining JSON value on stdin, one per line, parsed with the same descriptor-driven\n854:  * grammar tl_read_input uses above and assembled into a proper Vec -- spread into columns when\n855:  * the element is a record, the same invariant vec_lit and tl_at already keep, since tl_parse\n856:  * hands back a record as one packed blob rather than already-columnar data.\n857:  *\n858:  * A blank line is skipped rather than fed to tl_parse, which would fail trying to parse nothing.\n859:  */\n860: tl_vec *tl_read_inputs(const tl_str *descriptor) {\n861:     char *t = tl_alloc((size_t)descriptor->len + 1);\n862:     memcpy(t, descriptor->ptr, (size_t)descriptor->len);\n863:     t[descriptor->len] = 0;\n864: \n865:     int is_record = t[0] == '{';\n866:     int64_t ncols = 1;\n867:     if (is_record) {\n868:         const char *p = t + 1;\n869:         ncols = 0;\n870:         while (*p != ',') {\n871:             ncols = ncols * 10 + (*p++ - '0');\n872:         }\n873:     }\n874: \n875:     tl_list items = {NULL, 0, 0};\n876:     char *line = NULL;\n877:     size_t cap = 0;\n878:     ssize_t len;\n879:     while ((len = getline(&line, &cap, stdin)) != -1) {\n880:         tl_json j = {line, line + len};\n881:         tl_skip_ws(&j);\n882:         if (j.p == j.end) {\n883:             continue;\n884:         }\n885:         j.p = line;\n886:         int64_t value = tl_parse(&j, t, \"inputs\", NULL);\n887:         tl_skip_ws(&j);\n888:         if (j.p != j.end) {\n889:             tl_fail(\"trailing content after the value\", \"inputs\");\n890:         }\n891:         tl_list_push(&items, value);\n892:     }\n893:     free(line);\n894: \n895:     tl_vec *out = tl_vec_new(items.len, ncols);\n896:     for (int64_t i = 0; i < items.len; i++) {\n897:         if (is_record) {\n898:             const int64_t *rec = (const int64_t *)items.data[i];\n899:             for (int64_t c = 0; c < ncols; c++) {\n900:                 out->cols[c][i] = rec[c];\n901:             }\n902:         } else {\n903:             out->cols[0][i] = items.data[i];\n904:         }\n905:     }\n906:     return out;\n907: }\n908: \n909: /* One JSON value from stdin per call, parsed the same descriptor-driven way tl_read_inputs\n910:  * assembles its whole Vec from, but one line at a time and with no Vec ever built: the caller\n911:  * drives its own loop and decides when to stop, which is what lets the native backend fuse\n912:  * `jsonlines(f(inputs))` into a read-one/transform-one/write-one loop (see tir::fusion)\n913:  * instead of collecting everything before printing anything.\n914:  *\n915:  * Returns 0 at EOF (*out untouched) and 1 with *out set otherwise. A separate flag rather than a\n916:  * sentinel value in *out, because a record pointer or an Int result can legitimately be any\n917:  * int64_t, including whatever a sentinel would need to reserve.\n918:  *\n919:  * getline's buffer is not reused across calls the way tl_read_inputs's is within its own single\n920:  * call: this function is called once per record from the caller's own loop, so there is no one\n921:  * long-lived buffer to reuse, and toylang optimises for none of this to begin with. */\n922: int tl_read_one_input(const tl_str *descriptor, int64_t *out) {\n923:     char *t = tl_alloc((size_t)descriptor->len + 1);\n924:     memcpy(t, descriptor->ptr, (size_t)descriptor->len);\n925:     t[descriptor->len] = 0;\n926: \n927:     char *line = NULL;\n928:     size_t cap = 0;\n929:     for (;;) {\n930:         ssize_t len = getline(&line, &cap, stdin);\n931:         if (len == -1) {\n932:             free(line);\n933:             return 0;\n934:         }\n935:         tl_json j = {line, line + len};\n936:         tl_skip_ws(&j);\n937:         if (j.p == j.end) {\n938:             continue;\n939:         }\n940:         j.p = line;\n941:         int64_t value = tl_parse(&j, t, \"inputs\", NULL);\n942:         tl_skip_ws(&j);\n943:         if (j.p != j.end) {\n944:             tl_fail(\"trailing content after the value\", \"inputs\");\n945:         }\n946:         *out = value;\n947:         free(line);\n948:         return 1;\n949:     }\n950: }\n951: \n952: /* One raw line of stdin at a time: the streaming counterpart of tl_collect_lines below, the\n953:  * same way tl_read_one_input above is tl_read_inputs's. Returns 0 at EOF (*out untouched) and\n954:  * 1 with *out set to the line's tl_str otherwise. Same trailing-newline rule as\n955:  * tl_collect_lines; a blank line is a line, since `lines` keeps them. A non-UTF-8 line is\n956:  * refused rather than carried: a Str is Unicode scalar values (kantord/toylang#102). */\n957: static int tl_utf8_valid(const char *s, size_t len) {\n958:     size_t i = 0;\n959:     while (i < len) {\n960:         unsigned char b = (unsigned char)s[i];\n961:         if (b < 0x80) {\n962:             i++;\n963:         } else if (b >= 0xC2 && b < 0xE0) {\n964:             if (i + 1 >= len || ((unsigned char)s[i + 1] & 0xC0) != 0x80) return 0;\n965:             i += 2;\n966:         } else if (b >= 0xE0 && b < 0xF0) {\n967:             if (i + 2 >= len || ((unsigned char)s[i + 1] & 0xC0) != 0x80\n968:                 || ((unsigned char)s[i + 2] & 0xC0) != 0x80) return 0;\n969:             i += 3;\n970:         } else if (b >= 0xF0 && b < 0xF5) {\n971:             if (i + 3 >= len || ((unsigned char)s[i + 1] & 0xC0) != 0x80\n972:                 || ((unsigned char)s[i + 2] & 0xC0) != 0x80\n973:                 || ((unsigned char)s[i + 3] & 0xC0) != 0x80) return 0;\n974:             i += 4;\n975:         } else {\n976:             return 0;\n977:         }\n978:     }\n979:     return 1;\n980: }\n981: \n982: int tl_read_one_line(int64_t *out) {\n983:     char *line = NULL;\n984:     size_t cap = 0;\n985:     ssize_t len = getline(&line, &cap, stdin);\n986:     if (len == -1) {\n987:         free(line);\n988:         return 0;\n989:     }\n990:     if (len > 0 && line[len - 1] == '\\n') {\n991:         len--;\n992:     }\n993:     if (!tl_utf8_valid(line, (size_t)len)) {\n994:         tl_fail(\"stdin is not valid UTF-8\", \"lines\");\n995:     }\n996:     char *bytes = tl_alloc((size_t)len);\n997:     memcpy(bytes, line, (size_t)len);\n998:     *out = (int64_t)tl_str_new(bytes, len);\n999:     free(line);\n1000:     return 1;\n1001: }\n1002: \n1003: /* One line of stdin at a time via getline, in contrast to tl_read_input just above, which reads\n1004:  * the whole stream before anything else can run. getline reuses the same growable buffer across\n1005:  * calls, so each line is copied out to its own allocation before being stored; the buffer would\n1006:  * otherwise be overwritten, and every previously stored line, on the next call.\n1007:  *\n1008:  * The trailing newline getline includes is stripped; a final line with none is still yielded,\n1009:  * matching wc -l's undercount being the mistake to avoid rather than the convention to follow.\n1010:  * A bare \\r is left untouched as ordinary content, matching jq -R and Python's own stdin\n1011:  * iteration, neither of which treats CRLF specially. */\n1012: tl_vec *tl_collect_lines(void) {\n1013:     tl_list items = {NULL, 0, 0};\n1014:     char *line = NULL;\n1015:     size_t cap = 0;\n1016:     ssize_t len;\n1017:     while ((len = getline(&line, &cap, stdin)) != -1) {\n1018:         if (len > 0 && line[len - 1] == '\\n') {\n1019:             len--;\n1020:         }\n1021:         if (!tl_utf8_valid(line, (size_t)len)) {\n1022:             tl_fail(\"stdin is not valid UTF-8\", \"lines\");\n1023:         }\n1024:         char *bytes = tl_alloc((size_t)len);\n1025:         memcpy(bytes, line, (size_t)len);\n1026:         tl_list_push(&items, (int64_t)tl_str_new(bytes, len));\n1027:     }\n1028:     free(line);\n1029: \n1030:     tl_vec *v = tl_vec_new(items.len, 1);\n1031:     for (int64_t i = 0; i < items.len; i++) {\n1032:         v->cols[0][i] = items.data[i];\n1033:     }\n1034:     return v;\n1035: }\n1036: \n1037: /* Split one Str on a literal delimiter: every occurrence, in order, with the empty string one\n1038:  * empty field and a trailing delimiter a trailing empty field, the same shape `str.split` gives\n1039:  * on every other backend. The delimiter is searched literally, never as a pattern. */\n1040: static tl_vec *tl_split(const tl_str *s, const tl_str *sep) {\n1041:     tl_list items = {NULL, 0, 0};\n1042:     int64_t pos = 0;\n1043:     while (pos <= s->len) {\n1044:         int64_t found = -1;\n1045:         for (int64_t i = pos; i + sep->len <= s->len; i++) {\n1046:             if (memcmp(s->ptr + i, sep->ptr, (size_t)sep->len) == 0) {\n1047:                 found = i;\n1048:                 break;\n1049:             }\n1050:         }\n1051:         int64_t n;\n1052:         if (found == -1) {\n1053:             n = s->len - pos;\n1054:         } else {\n1055:             n = found - pos;\n1056:         }\n1057:         char *bytes = tl_alloc((size_t)n);\n1058:         memcpy(bytes, s->ptr + pos, (size_t)n);\n1059:         tl_list_push(&items, (int64_t)tl_str_new(bytes, n));\n1060:         if (found == -1) {\n1061:             break;\n1062:         }\n1063:         pos = found + sep->len;\n1064:     }\n1065:     tl_vec *v = tl_vec_new(items.len, 1);\n1066:     for (int64_t i = 0; i < items.len; i++) {\n1067:         v->cols[0][i] = items.data[i];\n1068:     }\n1069:     return v;\n1070: }\n1071: \n1072: /* Split every line of a Vec<Str> on the delimiter, one row per line: `dsv(delim)`. The outer\n1073:  * Vec is a single column of inner Vecs, the struct-of-arrays spelling of Vec<Vec<Str>>. */\n1074: tl_vec *tl_split_lines(tl_vec *lines, tl_str *sep) {\n1075:     tl_vec *out = tl_vec_new(lines->len, 1);\n1076:     for (int64_t i = 0; i < lines->len; i++) {\n1077:         out->cols[0][i] = (int64_t)tl_split((tl_str *)lines->cols[0][i], sep);\n1078:     }\n1079:     return out;\n1080: }\n1081: \n1082: /* Gather one element of a Vec of records back into a record.\n1083:  *\n1084:  * The struct-of-arrays layout means an element is spread across columns, and almost nothing in\n1085:  * the language needs it whole: select reads single fields out of the columns, and `.field`\n1086:  * returns a column. Printing is the exception, because rendering an element needs every field\n1087:  * at once. This is the only gather, and it exists for output alone.\n1088:  */\n1089: int64_t *tl_rec_from_vec(const tl_vec *v, int64_t i) {\n1090:     int64_t *rec = tl_rec_new(v->ncols);\n1091:     for (int64_t c = 0; c < v->ncols; c++) {\n1092:         rec[c] = v->cols[c][i];\n1093:     }\n1094:     return rec;\n1095: }\n1096: \n1097: /* Opt: a pointer to a slot, or NULL for absent.\n1098:  *\n1099:  * Boxing rather than a tag pair, because a slot holds any value an Int can and there is no\n1100:  * spare bit pattern to mean absent. Uniform across element types, which is what lets one\n1101:  * function serve them all.\n1102:  */\n1103: int64_t *tl_opt_some(int64_t value) {\n1104:     int64_t *p = tl_alloc(sizeof(int64_t));\n1105:     *p = value;\n1106:     return p;\n1107: }\n1108: \n1109: int64_t tl_opt_is_some(const int64_t *o) {\n1110:     return o != NULL;\n1111: }\n1112: \n1113: int64_t tl_opt_get(const int64_t *o) {\n1114:     return *o;\n1115: }\n1116: \n1117: /* Every element but the first. NULL on an empty Vec -- the same absence encoding tl_opt_some\n1118:  * uses everywhere else, so a caller unwraps it the same way an Index result is unwrapped. */\n1119: int64_t *tl_vec_tail(const tl_vec *v) {\n1120:     if (v->len == 0) {\n1121:         return NULL;\n1122:     }\n1123:     tl_vec *out = tl_vec_new(v->len - 1, v->ncols);\n1124:     for (int64_t c = 0; c < v->ncols; c++) {\n1125:         if (out->len > 0) {\n1126:             memcpy(out->cols[c], v->cols[c] + 1, (size_t)out->len * sizeof(int64_t));\n1127:         }\n1128:     }\n1129:     return tl_opt_some((int64_t)out);\n1130: }\n1131: \n1132: /* Flatten a Vec<Vec<T>> into a Vec<T>. `ncols` is T's column count: passed in rather than read\n1133:  * off an inner Vec, since an empty outer Vec has no inner Vec to read it from. */\n1134: tl_vec *tl_vec_flatten(const tl_vec *vv, int64_t ncols) {\n1135:     int64_t total = 0;\n1136:     for (int64_t i = 0; i < vv->len; i++) {\n1137:         total += ((const tl_vec *)vv->cols[0][i])->len;\n1138:     }\n1139:     tl_vec *out = tl_vec_new(total, ncols);\n1140:     int64_t at = 0;\n1141:     for (int64_t i = 0; i < vv->len; i++) {\n1142:         const tl_vec *inner = (const tl_vec *)vv->cols[0][i];\n1143:         for (int64_t c = 0; c < ncols; c++) {\n1144:             if (inner->len > 0) {\n1145:                 memcpy(out->cols[c] + at, inner->cols[c], (size_t)inner->len * sizeof(int64_t));\n1146:             }\n1147:         }\n1148:         at += inner->len;\n1149:     }\n1150:     return out;\n1151: }\n1152: \n1153: /* `a + b` on two Vecs (kantord/toylang#97, the add-trait reading): join them without going\n1154:  * through the one-level-of-nesting `tl_vec_flatten` does. `ncols` is T's column count, the same\n1155:  * reason `tl_vec_flatten` takes it rather than reading it off either Vec. */\n1156: tl_vec *tl_vec_concat(const tl_vec *a, const tl_vec *b, int64_t ncols) {\n1157:     tl_vec *out = tl_vec_new(a->len + b->len, ncols);\n1158:     for (int64_t c = 0; c < ncols; c++) {\n1159:         if (a->len > 0) {\n1160:             memcpy(out->cols[c], a->cols[c], (size_t)a->len * sizeof(int64_t));\n1161:         }\n1162:         if (b->len > 0) {\n1163:             memcpy(out->cols[c] + a->len, b->cols[c], (size_t)b->len * sizeof(int64_t));\n1164:         }\n1165:     }\n1166:     return out;\n1167: }\n1168: \n1169: /* Ascending by raw int64 value: what backs Int, Int64, and Char, since all three live in the\n1170:  * slot unnarrowed (a Char is a codepoint, and the checker already keeps it from mixing with the\n1171:  * others). qsort's comparator returns the sign of the difference rather than subtracting, since\n1172:  * an int64 difference can itself overflow the int qsort wants back. */\n1173: static int tl_cmp_int64(const void *a, const void *b) {\n1174:     int64_t x = *(const int64_t *)a;\n1175:     int64_t y = *(const int64_t *)b;\n1176:     return x < y ? -1 : (x > y ? 1 : 0);\n1177: }\n1178: \n1179: static int tl_cmp_str_slot(const void *a, const void *b) {\n1180:     const tl_str *x = (const tl_str *)*(const int64_t *)a;\n1181:     const tl_str *y = (const tl_str *)*(const int64_t *)b;\n1182:     return (int)tl_str_cmp(x, y);\n1183: }\n1184: \n1185: /* `sort` over a Vec whose element is Int, Int64, or Char: one column of raw int64 slots, sorted\n1186:  * in place on a copy. Restricted to these by the checker (`orderable`), so there is no column\n1187:  * beyond the one holding the elements themselves to carry along. */\n1188: tl_vec *tl_vec_sort_int(const tl_vec *v) {\n1189:     tl_vec *out = tl_vec_new(v->len, 1);\n1190:     if (v->len > 0) {\n1191:         memcpy(out->cols[0], v->cols[0], (size_t)v->len * sizeof(int64_t));\n1192:         qsort(out->cols[0], (size_t)v->len, sizeof(int64_t), tl_cmp_int64);\n1193:     }\n1194:     return out;\n1195: }\n1196: \n1197: /* The same shape for `Vec<Str>`, whose slots are `tl_str *` rather than raw integers. */\n1198: tl_vec *tl_vec_sort_str(const tl_vec *v) {\n1199:     tl_vec *out = tl_vec_new(v->len, 1);\n1200:     if (v->len > 0) {\n1201:         memcpy(out->cols[0], v->cols[0], (size_t)v->len * sizeof(int64_t));\n1202:         qsort(out->cols[0], (size_t)v->len, sizeof(int64_t), tl_cmp_str_slot);\n1203:     }\n1204:     return out;\n1205: }\n1206: \n1207: /* `reverse`, generic over the element type the way `tl_vec_tail` is: every column's row order\n1208:  * flips together, so a Vec of records or of nested Vecs reverses correctly with no type-specific\n1209:  * code. */\n1210: tl_vec *tl_vec_reverse(const tl_vec *v, int64_t ncols) {\n1211:     tl_vec *out = tl_vec_new(v->len, ncols);\n1212:     for (int64_t c = 0; c < ncols; c++) {\n1213:         for (int64_t i = 0; i < v->len; i++) {\n1214:             out->cols[c][i] = v->cols[c][v->len - 1 - i];\n1215:         }\n1216:     }\n1217:     return out;\n1218: }\n1219: \n1220: /* `sum` over a Vec of Int or Int64 (kantord/toylang#140): the reduction of `+`, each addition\n1221:  * wrapping the way the language's `+` wraps. Both widths live in the same i64 slot, so `narrow`\n1222:  * is what tells Int (32-bit wrap after every addition) from Int64 (none). Accumulated in\n1223:  * uint64_t so no addition can overflow into undefined behaviour; the narrow step truncates to\n1224:  * 32 bits and sign-extends. */\n1225: int64_t tl_vec_sum(const tl_vec *v, int narrow) {\n1226:     uint64_t acc = 0;\n1227:     for (int64_t i = 0; i < v->len; i++) {\n1228:         acc += (uint64_t)v->cols[0][i];\n1229:         if (narrow) {\n1230:             acc = (uint64_t)(int64_t)(int32_t)acc;\n1231:         }\n1232:     }\n1233:     return (int64_t)acc;\n1234: }\n1235: \n1236: /* `max` over a Vec of Int or Int64: the greatest raw slot, Int's already sign-extended so the\n1237:  * i64 comparison orders it correctly. NULL on an empty Vec, the same absence encoding\n1238:  * tl_opt_some uses everywhere else, so a caller unwraps it the way it unwraps an Index result. */\n1239: int64_t *tl_vec_max(const tl_vec *v) {\n1240:     if (v->len == 0) {\n1241:         return NULL;\n1242:     }\n1243:     int64_t m = v->cols[0][0];\n1244:     for (int64_t i = 1; i < v->len; i++) {\n1245:         if (v->cols[0][i] > m) {\n1246:             m = v->cols[0][i];\n1247:         }\n1248:     }\n1249:     return tl_opt_some(m);\n1250: }\n1251: \n1252: /* Collapse one dimension at `i`, `depth` layers down, counting from the end when negative.\n1253:  *\n1254:  * `is_record` decides whether an entry has to be gathered out of the columns. The column count\n1255:  * cannot stand in for that test: a record with one field has one column exactly like a Vec of\n1256:  * scalars does.\n1257:  */\n1258: int64_t *tl_at(const tl_vec *v, int64_t i, int64_t depth, int is_record) {\n1259:     if (depth > 0) {\n1260:         tl_vec *out = tl_vec_new(v->len, 1);\n1261:         for (int64_t k = 0; k < v->len; k++) {\n1262:             const tl_vec *inner = (const tl_vec *)v->cols[0][k];\n1263:             out->cols[0][k] = (int64_t)tl_at(inner, i, depth - 1, is_record);\n1264:         }\n1265:         return (int64_t *)out;\n1266:     }\n1267:     if (i < 0) {\n1268:         i = v->len + i;\n1269:     }\n1270:     if (i < 0 || i >= v->len) {\n1271:         return NULL;\n1272:     }\n1273:     return tl_opt_some(is_record ? (int64_t)tl_rec_from_vec(v, i) : v->cols[0][i]);\n1274: }\n1275: \n1276: /* Narrow a Vec to the [lo, hi) window, clamping out-of-range bounds jq-style rather than\n1277:  * answering absence the way tl_at does: negatives count from the end, both bounds clamp to\n1278:  * [0, len], and a crossed window is empty (kantord/toylang#143). A bound left out is passed\n1279:  * as INT64_MIN for the start or INT64_MAX for the end, each folding to the Vec's own boundary\n1280:  * under the clamp. `depth` is the same layers-down the collapse uses, recursing into each\n1281:  * element when the slice lands below the outermost dimension. */\n1282: tl_vec *tl_vec_slice(const tl_vec *v, int64_t lo, int64_t hi, int64_t depth) {\n1283:     if (depth > 0) {\n1284:         tl_vec *out = tl_vec_new(v->len, 1);\n1285:         for (int64_t k = 0; k < v->len; k++) {\n1286:             const tl_vec *inner = (const tl_vec *)v->cols[0][k];\n1287:             out->cols[0][k] = (int64_t)tl_vec_slice(inner, lo, hi, depth - 1);\n1288:         }\n1289:         return out;\n1290:     }\n1291:     int64_t n = v->len;\n1292:     if (lo < 0) {\n1293:         lo += n;\n1294:     }\n1295:     if (hi < 0) {\n1296:         hi += n;\n1297:     }\n1298:     if (lo < 0) {\n1299:         lo = 0;\n1300:     }\n1301:     if (hi < 0) {\n1302:         hi = 0;\n1303:     }\n1304:     if (lo > n) {\n1305:         lo = n;\n1306:     }\n1307:     if (hi > n) {\n1308:         hi = n;\n1309:     }\n1310:     int64_t len = hi - lo;\n1311:     if (len < 0) {\n1312:         len = 0;\n1313:     }\n1314:     tl_vec *out = tl_vec_new(len, v->ncols);\n1315:     for (int64_t c = 0; c < v->ncols; c++) {\n1316:         if (len > 0) {\n1317:             memcpy(out->cols[c], v->cols[c] + lo, (size_t)len * sizeof(int64_t));\n1318:         }\n1319:     }\n1320:     return out;\n1321: }\n1322: \n1323: /* Insist an Opt is present, `depth` layers down.\n1324:  *\n1325:  * Unlike an index this needs no is_record flag: an Opt already holds a gathered value, so there\n1326:  * is nothing left to collect out of columns.\n1327:  */\n1328: int64_t *tl_unwrap(int64_t *o, int64_t depth) {\n1329:     if (depth > 0) {\n1330:         tl_vec *v = (tl_vec *)o;\n1331:         tl_vec *out = tl_vec_new(v->len, 1);\n1332:         for (int64_t k = 0; k < v->len; k++) {\n1333:             out->cols[0][k] = (int64_t)tl_unwrap((int64_t *)v->cols[0][k], depth - 1);\n1334:         }\n1335:         return (int64_t *)out;\n1336:     }\n1337:     if (o == NULL) {\n1338:         const char *msg = \"toylang: unwrapped a value that is not there\\n\";\n1339:         (void)!write(2, msg, strlen(msg));\n1340:         exit(1);\n1341:     }\n1342:     return (int64_t *)*o;\n1343: }\n1344: \n1345: /* The only way arithmetic can fail. */\n1346: _Noreturn void tl_div_by_zero(void) {\n1347:     const char *msg = \"toylang: divided by zero\\n\";\n1348:     (void)!write(2, msg, strlen(msg));\n1349:     exit(1);\n1350: }\n1351: \n1352: /* Build a Vec of `len` scalars, filled by the caller. `map` produces one column whatever the\n1353:  * source had, since its result element is whatever the body returned. */\n1354: /* The integers from zero up to but not including n. A negative n gives an empty Vec rather\n1355:  * than an error, the same as asking for zero of them. */\n1356: tl_vec *tl_range(int64_t n) {\n1357:     if (n < 0) {\n1358:         n = 0;\n1359:     }\n1360:     tl_vec *v = tl_vec_new(n, 1);\n1361:     for (int64_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n1362:         v->cols[0][i] = i;\n1363:     }\n1364:     return v;\n1365: }\n1366: \n1367: /* Every Unicode scalar value in s, decoded from UTF-8, one codepoint per element -- not one\n1368:  * byte and not one UTF-16 unit, so a character outside the Basic Multilingual Plane is one\n1369:  * element here even where a backend whose own strings are UTF-16 needs a surrogate pair to\n1370:  * spell it. `s->len` is an overallocation (the ASCII-only case needs exactly that many, and\n1371:  * every multi-byte codepoint needs fewer slots than the bytes it decodes from), trimmed to the\n1372:  * real count once decoding is done, the same sizing tl_parse_string already relies on for the\n1373:  * reverse direction. Malformed UTF-8 cannot occur: every tl_str this runtime builds is already\n1374:  * valid UTF-8, whether from a literal or from tl_utf8_encode. */\n1375: tl_vec *tl_chars(const tl_str *s) {\n1376:     tl_vec *v = tl_vec_new(s->len, 1);\n1377:     int64_t n = 0;\n1378:     int64_t i = 0;\n1379:     while (i < s->len) {\n1380:         unsigned char b0 = (unsigned char)s->ptr[i];\n1381:         uint32_t cp;\n1382:         int extra;\n1383:         if (b0 < 0x80) {\n1384:             cp = b0;\n1385:             extra = 0;\n1386:         } else if ((b0 & 0xE0) == 0xC0) {\n1387:             cp = b0 & 0x1F;\n1388:             extra = 1;\n1389:         } else if ((b0 & 0xF0) == 0xE0) {\n1390:             cp = b0 & 0x0F;\n1391:             extra = 2;\n1392:         } else {\n1393:             cp = b0 & 0x07;\n1394:             extra = 3;\n1395:         }\n1396:         i++;\n1397:         for (int k = 0; k < extra; k++) {\n1398:             cp = (cp << 6) | ((unsigned char)s->ptr[i] & 0x3F);\n1399:             i++;\n1400:         }\n1401:         v->cols[0][n++] = (int64_t)cp;\n1402:     }\n1403:     v->len = n;\n1404:     return v;\n1405: }\n\n(End of file - total 1405 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"/* The native backend's runtime.\n *\n * Compiled and linked into every native binary by the `cc` invocation that already links the\n * object file. The generated LLVM IR declares these and calls them; nothing here knows about\n * toylang's types beyond what its signatures say.\n *\n * Nothing frees. Prototype 1.5 leaks deliberately: choosing between refcounting and tracing\n * belongs with the mutation model, and a half-built refcount would be worse than an honest\n * leak in a program that runs once and exits. Keeping every allocation in this file keeps that\n * decision in one visible place.\n */\n\n#include <stdint.h>\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n#include <unistd.h>\n\n/* A toylang Str: bytes and a length, never null-terminated by contract. The bytes a literal\n * points at happen to carry a trailing NUL so that a debugger can print them, but len excludes","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/runtime/toylang.c","text":"/* The native backend's runtime.\n *\n * Compiled and linked into every native binary by the `cc` invocation that already links the\n * object file. The generated LLVM IR declares these and calls them; nothing here knows about\n * toylang's types beyond what its signatures say.\n *\n * Nothing frees. Prototype 1.5 leaks deliberately: choosing between refcounting and tracing\n * belongs with the mutation model, and a half-built refcount would be worse than an honest\n * leak in a program that runs once and exits. Keeping every allocation in this file keeps that\n * decision in one visible place.\n */\n\n#include <stdint.h>\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n#include <unistd.h>\n\n/* A toylang Str: bytes and a length, never null-terminated by contract. The bytes a literal\n * points at happen to carry a trailing NUL so that a debugger can print them, but len excludes\n * it and no code here reads past len. */\ntypedef struct {\n    const char *ptr;\n    int64_t len;\n} tl_str;\n\nstatic void *tl_alloc(size_t n) {\n    void *p = malloc(n);\n    if (p == NULL) {\n        const char *msg = \"toylang: out of memory\\n\";\n        write(2, msg, strlen(msg));\n        exit(1);\n    }\n    return p;\n}\n\nstatic tl_str *tl_str_new(char *bytes, int64_t len) {\n    tl_str *s = tl_alloc(sizeof(tl_str));\n    s->ptr = bytes;\n    s->len = len;\n    return s;\n}\n\ntl_str *tl_concat(const tl_str *a, const tl_str *b) {\n    char *bytes = tl_alloc((size_t)(a->len + b->len));\n    memcpy(bytes, a->ptr, (size_t)a->len);\n    memcpy(bytes + a->len, b->ptr, (size_t)b->len);\n    return tl_str_new(bytes, a->len + b->len);\n}\n\ntl_str *tl_int_to_str(int64_t n) {\n    /* -9223372036854775808 is 20 characters plus a terminator. */\n    char buf[24];\n    int len = snprintf(buf, sizeof buf, \"%lld\", (long long)n);\n    char *bytes = tl_alloc((size_t)len);\n    memcpy(bytes, buf, (size_t)len);\n    return tl_str_new(bytes, len);\n}\n\nint64_t tl_str_eq(const tl_str *a, const tl_str *b) {\n    if (a->len != b->len) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n    return memcmp(a->ptr, b->ptr, (size_t)a->len) == 0;\n}\n\n/* Byte order, which is what Lua does. JavaScript compares UTF-16 code units, so the three\n * backends agree on ASCII and are not guaranteed to beyond it. */\nint64_t tl_str_cmp(const tl_str *a, const tl_str *b) {\n    int64_t shared = a->len < b->len ? a->len : b->len;\n    int diff = memcmp(a->ptr, b->ptr, (size_t)shared);\n    if (diff != 0) {\n        return diff < 0 ? -1 : 1;\n    }\n    if (a->len == b->len) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n    return a->len < b->len ? -1 : 1;\n}\n\n/* One write for the payload and one for the newline, rather than copying to join them. */\nvoid tl_print(const tl_str *s) {\n    if (s->len > 0) {\n        (void)!write(1, s->ptr, (size_t)s->len);\n    }\n    (void)!write(1, \"\\n\", 1);\n}\n\n/* A Vec: a length and `ncols` columns, each holding `len` raw 8-byte slots.\n *\n * The layout is struct of arrays. A Vec of scalars has one column; a Vec of records has one\n * column per field, which is what makes reading a field off a Vec a column rather than a gather.\n *\n * Every scalar toylang has fits one slot: an Int is an i64, and a Str, a record or a nested Vec\n * is a pointer. That is what lets one set of functions serve every element type instead of one\n * per width.\n */\ntypedef struct {\n    int64_t len;\n    int64_t ncols;\n    int64_t **cols;\n} tl_vec;\n\ntl_vec *tl_vec_new(int64_t len, int64_t ncols) {\n    tl_vec *v = tl_alloc(sizeof(tl_vec));\n    v->len = len;\n    v->ncols = ncols;\n    v->cols = tl_alloc((size_t)ncols * sizeof(int64_t *));\n    for (int64_t c = 0; c < ncols; c++) {\n        v->cols[c] = len > 0 ? tl_alloc((size_t)len * sizeof(int64_t)) : NULL;\n    }\n    return v;\n}\n\nint64_t tl_vec_len(const tl_vec *v) {\n    return v->len;\n}\n\nint64_t tl_vec_get(const tl_vec *v, int64_t col, int64_t i) {\n    return v->cols[col][i];\n}\n\nvoid tl_vec_set(tl_vec *v, int64_t col, int64_t i, int64_t value) {\n    v->cols[col][i] = value;\n}\n\n/* One field of a Vec of records, as a Vec of that field's type.\n *\n * The column is shared rather than copied, so `.name` on a Vec<User> costs one small header and\n * no element work. This is the whole reason the layout is struct of arrays.\n */\ntl_vec *tl_vec_column(const tl_vec *v, int64_t col) {\n    tl_vec *out = tl_alloc(sizeof(tl_vec));\n    out->len = v->len;\n    out->ncols = 1;\n    out->cols = tl_alloc(sizeof(int64_t *));\n    out->cols[0] = v->cols[col];\n    return out;\n}\n\n/* select in two passes: count survivors, then fill. Two passes over a mask beats growing an\n * array, and every column is compacted with the same surviving indices. */\ntl_vec *tl_vec_from_mask(const tl_vec *src, const int8_t *keep) {\n    int64_t n = 0;\n    for (int64_t i = 0; i < src->len; i++) {\n        n += keep[i] != 0;\n    }\n    tl_vec *out = tl_vec_new(n, src->ncols);\n    int64_t j = 0;\n    for (int64_t i = 0; i < src->len; i++) {\n        if (keep[i]) {\n            for (int64_t c = 0; c < src->ncols; c++) {\n                out->cols[c][j] = src->cols[c][i];\n            }\n            j++;\n        }\n    }\n    return out;\n}\n\nint8_t *tl_mask_new(int64_t len) {\n    return len > 0 ? tl_alloc((size_t)len) : NULL;\n}\n\nvoid tl_mask_set(int8_t *mask, int64_t i, int64_t value) {\n    mask[i] = value != 0;\n}\n\n/* A record: one slot per field, in the field order the type declares. Records only ever\n * arrive from input, since the language has no expression that builds one. */\nint64_t *tl_rec_new(int64_t nfields) {\n    return tl_alloc((size_t)nfields * sizeof(int64_t));\n}\n\nint64_t tl_rec_get(const int64_t *r, int64_t field) {\n    return r[field];\n}\n\nvoid tl_rec_set(int64_t *r, int64_t field, int64_t value) {\n    r[field] = value;\n}\n\n/* JSON string escaping, matching what the Lua and JavaScript printers emit. Anything below\n * space goes out as \\u00xx, which is what both of the others do for control characters. */\ntl_str *tl_quote(const tl_str *s) {\n    /* Worst case every byte becomes \\u00xx, plus the two quotes. */\n    char *out = tl_alloc((size_t)(s->len * 6 + 2));\n    int64_t n = 0;\n    out[n++] = '\"';\n    for (int64_t i = 0; i < s->len; i++) {\n        unsigned char c = (unsigned char)s->ptr[i];\n        switch (c) {\n            case '\"': out[n++] = '\\\\'; out[n++] = '\"'; break;\n            case '\\\\': out[n++] = '\\\\'; out[n++] = '\\\\'; break;\n            case '\\n': out[n++] = '\\\\'; out[n++] = 'n'; break;\n            case '\\r': out[n++] = '\\\\'; out[n++] = 'r'; break;\n            case '\\t': out[n++] = '\\\\'; out[n++] = 't'; break;\n            default:\n                if (c < 0x20) {\n                    n += snprintf(out + n, 7, \"\\\\u%04x\", c);\n                } else {\n                    out[n++] = (char)c;\n                }\n        }\n    }\n    out[n++] = '\"';\n    return tl_str_new(out, n);\n}\n\n/* Concatenate `parts`, which holds tl_str pointers, between `open` and `close` with `sep`\n * between elements. One allocation, so printing a Vec is not quadratic in its length. */\ntl_str *tl_str_join(const tl_vec *parts, const tl_str *open, const tl_str *sep,\n                    const tl_str *close) {\n    int64_t total = open->len + close->len;\n    for (int64_t i = 0; i < parts->len; i++) {\n        total += ((const tl_str *)tl_vec_get(parts, 0, i))->len;\n        if (i > 0) {\n            total += sep->len;\n        }\n    }\n\n    char *out = tl_alloc((size_t)total);\n    int64_t n = 0;\n    memcpy(out + n, open->ptr, (size_t)open->len);\n    n += open->len;\n    for (int64_t i = 0; i < parts->len; i++) {\n        if (i > 0) {\n            memcpy(out + n, sep->ptr, (size_t)sep->len);\n            n += sep->len;\n        }\n        const tl_str *p = (const tl_str *)tl_vec_get(parts, 0, i);\n        memcpy(out + n, p->ptr, (size_t)p->len);\n        n += p->len;\n    }\n    memcpy(out + n, close->ptr, (size_t)close->len);\n    n += close->len;\n    return tl_str_new(out, n);\n}\n\n/* Reading input.\n *\n * The parser is driven by a type descriptor the compiler emits as one string, so it only ever\n * looks for the shape the program declared. The grammar is:\n *\n *   s            Str\n *   i            Int\n *   b            Bool\n *   [T           Vec of T\n *   {n,name:T,...}   record with n fields, in the type's declared order\n *   e{n,Name,variant,variant:T,...}   enum with n variants, in declaration order (the\n *                variant's position is its tag); a variant with no `:T` is a unit variant.\n *                Name is what a mismatch says it expected, and what `@` names.\n *   @Name        the enum called Name, whose descriptor is still open around this one. A\n *                recursive enum's payload names itself back rather than spelling itself out\n *                again, which would have no end (kantord/toylang#94).\n *\n * It re-validates rather than trusting a pre-checked shape, so a built binary works on its own\n * with `./adults < data.json` and not only under the test harness. A mismatch names the path\n * that failed and exits non-zero, which is what the Rust-side check does too.\n */\n\ntypedef struct {\n    const char *p;\n    const char *end;\n} tl_json;\n\n/* The enums whose descriptors are open around the type being parsed, innermost first. `@Name`\n * resolves by walking up this chain, so a name is always the nearest enclosing enum of that\n * name -- which is what makes two instantiations of one generic enum, nested inside each other,\n * resolve to the right one. */\ntypedef struct tl_open_enum {\n    const char *name;\n    int64_t name_len;\n    /* The `e{...}` this name was opened at, which is what `@Name` parses as. */\n    const char *desc;\n    const struct tl_open_enum *up;\n} tl_open_enum;\n\nstatic void tl_fail(const char *what, const char *path) {\n    char buf[512];\n    int n = snprintf(buf, sizeof buf, \"toylang: input: %s at %s\\n\", what,\n                     path[0] ? path : \"input\");\n    (void)!write(2, buf, (size_t)n);\n    exit(1);\n}\n\nstatic void tl_skip_ws(tl_json *j) {\n    while (j->p < j->end && (*j->p == ' ' || *j->p == '\\t' || *j->p == '\\n' || *j->p == '\\r')) {\n        j->p++;\n    }\n}\n\nstatic void tl_expect(tl_json *j, char c, const char *path) {\n    tl_skip_ws(j);\n    if (j->p >= j->end || *j->p != c) {\n        char what[32];\n        snprintf(what, sizeof what, \"expected `%c`\", c);\n        tl_fail(what, path);\n    }\n    j->p++;\n}\n\n/* A growable list of slots, used while an array's length is still unknown. */\ntypedef struct {\n    int64_t *data;\n    int64_t len;\n    int64_t cap;\n} tl_list;\n\nstatic void tl_list_push(tl_list *l, int64_t v) {\n    if (l->len == l->cap) {\n        int64_t cap = l->cap ? l->cap * 2 : 8;\n        int64_t *data = tl_alloc((size_t)cap * sizeof(int64_t));\n        memcpy(data, l->data, (size_t)l->len * sizeof(int64_t));\n        l->data = data;\n        l->cap = cap;\n    }\n    l->data[l->len++] = v;\n}\n\n/* Advance `t` past one complete type in the descriptor. */\nstatic const char *tl_type_skip(const char *t) {\n    switch (*t) {\n        case 's': case 'i': case 'b':\n            return t + 1;\n        case '[':\n            return tl_type_skip(t + 1);\n        case '@':\n            /* A back-reference is one token: the name runs to whatever delimits the type. */\n            t++;\n            while (*t != ',' && *t != '}') {\n                t++;\n            }\n            return t;\n        case '{': {\n            t++;\n            int64_t n = 0;\n            while (*t != ',') {\n                n = n * 10 + (*t++ - '0');\n            }\n            t++;\n            for (int64_t f = 0; f < n; f++) {\n                while (*t != ':') {\n                    t++;\n                }\n                t = tl_type_skip(t + 1);\n                if (*t == ',') {\n                    t++;\n                }\n            }\n            return t + 1; /* past '}' */\n        }\n        case 'e': {\n            t += 2; /* past \"e{\" */\n            int64_t n = 0;\n            while (*t != ',') {\n                n = n * 10 + (*t++ - '0');\n            }\n            t++;\n            while (*t != ',' && *t != '}') {\n                t++; /* the enum's name */\n            }\n            for (int64_t v = 0; v < n; v++) {\n                t++; /* past ',' */\n                while (*t != ':' && *t != ',' && *t != '}') {\n                    t++;\n                }\n                if (*t == ':') {\n                    t = tl_type_skip(t + 1);\n                }\n            }\n            return t + 1; /* past '}' */\n        }\n        default:\n            tl_fail(\"bad type descriptor\", \"\");\n            return t;\n    }\n}\n\nstatic int64_t tl_parse(tl_json *j, const char *t, const char *path,\n                        const tl_open_enum *scope);\n\n/* Consumes exactly 4 hex digits at j->p and returns them as a UTF-16 code unit. */\nstatic uint32_t tl_hex4(tl_json *j, const char *path) {\n    uint32_t v = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\n        if (j->p >= j->end) {\n            tl_fail(\"unterminated \\\\u escape\", path);\n        }\n        char c = *j->p;\n        int d = (c >= '0' && c <= '9')   ? c - '0'\n                : (c >= 'a' && c <= 'f') ? c - 'a' + 10\n                : (c >= 'A' && c <= 'F') ? c - 'A' + 10\n                                         : -1;\n        if (d < 0) {\n            tl_fail(\"bad \\\\u escape\", path);\n        }\n        v = v * 16 + (uint32_t)d;\n        j->p++;\n    }\n    return v;\n}\n\n/* Appends cp's UTF-8 encoding to out, which tl_parse_string has already sized generously\n * enough: every multi-byte encoding here is shorter than the \\u escape(s) that produced it. */\nstatic void tl_utf8_encode(char *out, int64_t *n, uint32_t cp) {\n    if (cp < 0x80) {\n        out[(*n)++] = (char)cp;\n    } else if (cp < 0x800) {\n        out[(*n)++] = (char)(0xC0 | (cp >> 6));\n        out[(*n)++] = (char)(0x80 | (cp & 0x3F));\n    } else if (cp < 0x10000) {\n        out[(*n)++] = (char)(0xE0 | (cp >> 12));\n        out[(*n)++] = (char)(0x80 | ((cp >> 6) & 0x3F));\n        out[(*n)++] = (char)(0x80 | (cp & 0x3F));\n    } else {\n        out[(*n)++] = (char)(0xF0 | (cp >> 18));\n        out[(*n)++] = (char)(0x80 | ((cp >> 12) & 0x3F));\n        out[(*n)++] = (char)(0x80 | ((cp >> 6) & 0x3F));\n        out[(*n)++] = (char)(0x80 | (cp & 0x3F));\n    }\n}\n\nstatic tl_str *tl_parse_string(tl_json *j, const char *path) {\n    tl_expect(j, '\"', path);\n    char *out = tl_alloc((size_t)(j->end - j->p));\n    int64_t n = 0;\n    while (j->p < j->end && *j->p != '\"') {\n        if (*j->p == '\\\\') {\n            j->p++;\n            if (j->p >= j->end) {\n                tl_fail(\"unterminated escape\", path);\n            }\n            // A control character with no named shorthand (NUL, say) is the only escape\n            // that reaches this in practice: src/lib.rs re-serializes input through\n            // serde_json before any backend sees it, and that writer emits raw UTF-8 for\n            // everything else. A surrogate pair is still combined into one codepoint below,\n            // to be a JSON decoder rather than one tied to that upstream behavior.\n            if (*j->p == 'u') {\n                j->p++;\n                uint32_t cu = tl_hex4(j, path);\n                uint32_t cp;\n                if (cu >= 0xD800 && cu <= 0xDBFF) {\n                    if (j->p + 1 >= j->end || j->p[0] != '\\\\' || j->p[1] != 'u') {\n                        tl_fail(\"unpaired surrogate\", path);\n                    }\n                    j->p += 2;\n                    uint32_t lo = tl_hex4(j, path);\n                    if (lo < 0xDC00 || lo > 0xDFFF) {\n                        tl_fail(\"unpaired surrogate\", path);\n                    }\n                    cp = 0x10000 + ((cu - 0xD800) << 10) + (lo - 0xDC00);\n                } else if (cu >= 0xDC00 && cu <= 0xDFFF) {\n                    tl_fail(\"unpaired surrogate\", path);\n                } else {\n                    cp = cu;\n                }\n                tl_utf8_encode(out, &n, cp);\n                continue;\n            }\n            switch (*j->p) {\n                case '\"': out[n++] = '\"'; break;\n                case '\\\\': out[n++] = '\\\\'; break;\n                case '/': out[n++] = '/'; break;\n                case 'n': out[n++] = '\\n'; break;\n                case 't': out[n++] = '\\t'; break;\n                case 'r': out[n++] = '\\r'; break;\n                case 'b': out[n++] = '\\b'; break;\n                case 'f': out[n++] = '\\f'; break;\n                default: tl_fail(\"unsupported escape\", path);\n            }\n            j->p++;\n        } else {\n            out[n++] = *j->p++;\n        }\n    }\n    tl_expect(j, '\"', path);\n    return tl_str_new(out, n);\n}\n\n/* Skip one JSON value without interpreting it, for fields the type does not declare. */\nstatic void tl_skip_value(tl_json *j, const char *path) {\n    tl_skip_ws(j);\n    if (j->p >= j->end) {\n        tl_fail(\"unexpected end of input\", path);\n    }\n    if (*j->p == '\"') {\n        tl_parse_string(j, path);\n        return;\n    }\n    if (*j->p == '[' || *j->p == '{') {\n        char open = *j->p;\n        char close = open == '[' ? ']' : '}';\n        int depth = 0;\n        while (j->p < j->end) {\n            if (*j->p == '\"') {\n                tl_parse_string(j, path);\n                continue;\n            }\n            if (*j->p == open) {\n                depth++;\n            } else if (*j->p == close) {\n                depth--;\n                if (depth == 0) {\n                    j->p++;\n                    return;\n                }\n            }\n            j->p++;\n        }\n        tl_fail(\"unterminated value\", path);\n    }\n    while (j->p < j->end && *j->p != ',' && *j->p != '}' && *j->p != ']') {\n        j->p++;\n    }\n}\n\nstatic int64_t tl_parse_record(tl_json *j, const char *t, const char *path,\n                               const tl_open_enum *scope) {\n    /* Field names and types, read off the descriptor once. */\n    t++;\n    int64_t n = 0;\n    while (*t != ',') {\n        n = n * 10 + (*t++ - '0');\n    }\n    t++;\n\n    const char *names[64];\n    int64_t name_lens[64];\n    const char *types[64];\n    if (n > 64) {\n        tl_fail(\"too many record fields\", path);\n    }\n    for (int64_t f = 0; f < n; f++) {\n        names[f] = t;\n        while (*t != ':') {\n            t++;\n        }\n        name_lens[f] = t - names[f];\n        types[f] = t + 1;\n        t = tl_type_skip(t + 1);\n        if (*t == ',') {\n            t++;\n        }\n    }\n\n    int64_t *rec = tl_rec_new(n);\n    int8_t seen[64];\n    memset(seen, 0, sizeof seen);\n\n    tl_expect(j, '{', path);\n    tl_skip_ws(j);\n    if (j->p < j->end && *j->p == '}') {\n        j->p++;\n    } else {\n        for (;;) {\n            tl_str *key = tl_parse_string(j, path);\n            tl_expect(j, ':', path);\n\n            int64_t match = -1;\n            for (int64_t f = 0; f < n; f++) {\n                if (key->len == name_lens[f] && memcmp(key->ptr, names[f], (size_t)key->len) == 0) {\n                    match = f;\n                    break;\n                }\n            }\n            if (match < 0) {\n                /* Fields the program did not declare are ignored, matching the Rust-side check. */\n                tl_skip_value(j, path);\n            } else {\n                char sub[256];\n                snprintf(sub, sizeof sub, \"%s.%.*s\", path, (int)key->len, key->ptr);\n                rec[match] = tl_parse(j, types[match], sub, scope);\n                seen[match] = 1;\n            }\n\n            tl_skip_ws(j);\n            if (j->p < j->end && *j->p == ',') {\n                j->p++;\n                continue;\n            }\n            break;\n        }\n        tl_expect(j, '}', path);\n    }\n\n    for (int64_t f = 0; f < n; f++) {\n        if (!seen[f]) {\n            char what[256];\n            snprintf(what, sizeof what, \"missing field `%.*s`\", (int)name_lens[f], names[f]);\n            tl_fail(what, path);\n        }\n    }\n    return (int64_t)rec;\n}\n\n/* One enum value spans two JSON shapes (ADR 0009): a bare string for a unit variant, a\n * single-key object for a payload one. What comes back is the same two-slot box the compiler\n * builds for a constructed enum: slot 0 the variant's declaration index, slot 1 the payload. */\nstatic int64_t tl_parse_enum(tl_json *j, const char *t, const char *path,\n                             const tl_open_enum *scope) {\n    const char *self = t;\n    t += 2; /* past \"e{\" */\n    int64_t n = 0;\n    while (*t != ',') {\n        n = n * 10 + (*t++ - '0');\n    }\n    t++;\n    const char *ename = t;\n    while (*t != ',' && *t != '}') {\n        t++;\n    }\n    int64_t ename_len = t - ename;\n\n    const char *names[64];\n    int64_t name_lens[64];\n    const char *types[64]; /* NULL marks a unit variant */\n    if (n > 64) {\n        tl_fail(\"too many variants\", path);\n    }\n    for (int64_t v = 0; v < n; v++) {\n        t++;\n        names[v] = t;\n        while (*t != ':' && *t != ',' && *t != '}') {\n            t++;\n        }\n        name_lens[v] = t - names[v];\n        if (*t == ':') {\n            types[v] = t + 1;\n            t = tl_type_skip(t + 1);\n        } else {\n            types[v] = NULL;\n        }\n    }\n\n    tl_skip_ws(j);\n    if (j->p < j->end && *j->p == '\"') {\n        tl_str *s = tl_parse_string(j, path);\n        for (int64_t v = 0; v < n; v++) {\n            if (types[v] == NULL && s->len == name_lens[v] &&\n                memcmp(s->ptr, names[v], (size_t)s->len) == 0) {\n                int64_t *box = tl_rec_new(2);\n                box[0] = v;\n                return (int64_t)box;\n            }\n        }\n        char what[256];\n        snprintf(what, sizeof what, \"`%.*s` is not a unit variant of %.*s\", (int)s->len,\n                 s->ptr, (int)ename_len, ename);\n        tl_fail(what, path);\n    }\n    if (j->p < j->end && *j->p == '{') {\n        j->p++;\n        tl_str *key = tl_parse_string(j, path);\n        tl_expect(j, ':', path);\n        for (int64_t v = 0; v < n; v++) {\n            if (types[v] != NULL && key->len == name_lens[v] &&\n                memcmp(key->ptr, names[v], (size_t)key->len) == 0) {\n                char sub[256];\n                snprintf(sub, sizeof sub, \"%s.%.*s\", path, (int)key->len, key->ptr);\n                int64_t *box = tl_rec_new(2);\n                box[0] = v;\n                /* Open around the payload, so a `@` inside it names this enum. */\n                tl_open_enum open = {ename, ename_len, self, scope};\n                box[1] = tl_parse(j, types[v], sub, &open);\n                /* One key is the whole shape, so the wrapper closes right here. */\n                tl_expect(j, '}', path);\n                return (int64_t)box;\n            }\n        }\n        char what[256];\n        snprintf(what, sizeof what, \"`%.*s` is not a payload variant of %.*s\", (int)key->len,\n                 key->ptr, (int)ename_len, ename);\n        tl_fail(what, path);\n    }\n    char what[256];\n    snprintf(what, sizeof what, \"expected %.*s\", (int)ename_len, ename);\n    tl_fail(what, path);\n    return 0;\n}\n\n/* A Vec of records is parsed element by element and then transposed into columns. Filling the\n * columns directly would avoid materialising each record, and is worth doing when the parser\n * stops being the cheapest part of reading input. */\nstatic int64_t tl_parse_vec(tl_json *j, const char *t, const char *path,\n                            const tl_open_enum *scope) {\n    const char *elem = t + 1;\n    /* Whether the element is a record is a separate question from how many columns it has: a\n     * record with one field also has one column, so the count cannot stand in for the test. */\n    int is_record = *elem == '{';\n    int64_t ncols = 1;\n    if (is_record) {\n        const char *c = elem + 1;\n        ncols = 0;\n        while (*c != ',') {\n            ncols = ncols * 10 + (*c++ - '0');\n        }\n    }\n\n    tl_list items = {NULL, 0, 0};\n    tl_expect(j, '[', path);\n    tl_skip_ws(j);\n    if (j->p < j->end && *j->p == ']') {\n        j->p++;\n    } else {\n        for (;;) {\n            char sub[256];\n            snprintf(sub, sizeof sub, \"%s[%lld]\", path, (long long)items.len);\n            tl_list_push(&items, tl_parse(j, elem, sub, scope));\n            tl_skip_ws(j);\n            if (j->p < j->end && *j->p == ',') {\n                j->p++;\n                continue;\n            }\n            break;\n        }\n        tl_expect(j, ']', path);\n    }\n\n    tl_vec *v = tl_vec_new(items.len, ncols);\n    for (int64_t i = 0; i < items.len; i++) {\n        if (!is_record) {\n            v->cols[0][i] = items.data[i];\n        } else {\n            const int64_t *rec = (const int64_t *)items.data[i];\n            for (int64_t c = 0; c < ncols; c++) {\n                v->cols[c][i] = rec[c];\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return (int64_t)v;\n}\n\nstatic int64_t tl_parse(tl_json *j, const char *t, const char *path,\n                        const tl_open_enum *scope) {\n    tl_skip_ws(j);\n    if (j->p >= j->end) {\n        tl_fail(\"unexpected end of input\", path);\n    }\n    switch (*t) {\n        case 's': {\n            if (*j->p != '\"') {\n                tl_fail(\"expected a string\", path);\n            }\n            return (int64_t)tl_parse_string(j, path);\n        }\n        case 'i': {\n            const char *start = j->p;\n            if (j->p < j->end && (*j->p == '-' || *j->p == '+')) {\n                j->p++;\n            }\n            while (j->p < j->end && *j->p >= '0' && *j->p <= '9') {\n                j->p++;\n            }\n            if (j->p == start) {\n                tl_fail(\"expected an integer\", path);\n            }\n            /* A float where Int was declared is an error, not a truncation. */\n            if (j->p < j->end && (*j->p == '.' || *j->p == 'e' || *j->p == 'E')) {\n                tl_fail(\"expected an integer, found a non-integer number\", path);\n            }\n            char buf[32];\n            int64_t n = j->p - start;\n            if (n >= (int64_t)sizeof buf) {\n                tl_fail(\"integer is out of range\", path);\n            }\n            memcpy(buf, start, (size_t)n);\n            buf[n] = 0;\n            return strtoll(buf, NULL, 10);\n        }\n        case 'b': {\n            if (j->end - j->p >= 4 && memcmp(j->p, \"true\", 4) == 0) {\n                j->p += 4;\n                return 1;\n            }\n            if (j->end - j->p >= 5 && memcmp(j->p, \"false\", 5) == 0) {\n                j->p += 5;\n                return 0;\n            }\n            tl_fail(\"expected a boolean\", path);\n            return 0;\n        }\n        case '[':\n            if (*j->p != '[') {\n                tl_fail(\"expected an array\", path);\n            }\n            return tl_parse_vec(j, t, path, scope);\n        case '{':\n            if (*j->p != '{') {\n                tl_fail(\"expected an object\", path);\n            }\n            return tl_parse_record(j, t, path, scope);\n        case 'e':\n            /* Which of its two shapes arrived is tl_parse_enum's own question. */\n            return tl_parse_enum(j, t, path, scope);\n        case '@': {\n            /* The enum this names is open around here, and its descriptor is where to resume. */\n            const char *name = t + 1;\n            int64_t len = 0;\n            while (name[len] != ',' && name[len] != '}') {\n                len++;\n            }\n            for (const tl_open_enum *e = scope; e != NULL; e = e->up) {\n                if (e->name_len == len && memcmp(e->name, name, (size_t)len) == 0) {\n                    return tl_parse(j, e->desc, path, scope);\n                }\n            }\n            tl_fail(\"bad type descriptor\", path);\n            return 0;\n        }\n        default:\n            tl_fail(\"bad type descriptor\", path);\n            return 0;\n    }\n}\n\nint64_t tl_read_input(const tl_str *descriptor) {\n    size_t cap = 1 << 16;\n    size_t len = 0;\n    char *buf = tl_alloc(cap);\n    for (;;) {\n        if (len == cap) {\n            cap *= 2;\n            char *bigger = tl_alloc(cap);\n            memcpy(bigger, buf, len);\n            buf = bigger;\n        }\n        ssize_t n = read(0, buf + len, cap - len);\n        if (n < 0) {\n            tl_fail(\"could not read stdin\", \"\");\n        }\n        if (n == 0) {\n            break;\n        }\n        len += (size_t)n;\n    }\n\n    /* The descriptor is a literal the compiler emitted, so it is NUL-terminated in practice;\n     * copy it anyway so the C string functions above have one for certain. */\n    char *t = tl_alloc((size_t)descriptor->len + 1);\n    memcpy(t, descriptor->ptr, (size_t)descriptor->len);\n    t[descriptor->len] = 0;\n\n    tl_json j = {buf, buf + len};\n    int64_t value = tl_parse(&j, t, \"input\", NULL);\n    tl_skip_ws(&j);\n    if (j.p != j.end) {\n        tl_fail(\"trailing content after the value\", \"input\");\n    }\n    return value;\n}\n\n/* Every remaining JSON value on stdin, one per line, parsed with the same descriptor-driven\n * grammar tl_read_input uses above and assembled into a proper Vec -- spread into columns when\n * the element is a record, the same invariant vec_lit and tl_at already keep, since tl_parse\n * hands back a record as one packed blob rather than already-columnar data.\n *\n * A blank line is skipped rather than fed to tl_parse, which would fail trying to parse nothing.\n */\ntl_vec *tl_read_inputs(const tl_str *descriptor) {\n    char *t = tl_alloc((size_t)descriptor->len + 1);\n    memcpy(t, descriptor->ptr, (size_t)descriptor->len);\n    t[descriptor->len] = 0;\n\n    int is_record = t[0] == '{';\n    int64_t ncols = 1;\n    if (is_record) {\n        const char *p = t + 1;\n        ncols = 0;\n        while (*p != ',') {\n            ncols = ncols * 10 + (*p++ - '0');\n        }\n    }\n\n    tl_list items = {NULL, 0, 0};\n    char *line = NULL;\n    size_t cap = 0;\n    ssize_t len;\n    while ((len = getline(&line, &cap, stdin)) != -1) {\n        tl_json j = {line, line + len};\n        tl_skip_ws(&j);\n        if (j.p == j.end) {\n            continue;\n        }\n        j.p = line;\n        int64_t value = tl_parse(&j, t, \"inputs\", NULL);\n        tl_skip_ws(&j);\n        if (j.p != j.end) {\n            tl_fail(\"trailing content after the value\", \"inputs\");\n        }\n        tl_list_push(&items, value);\n    }\n    free(line);\n\n    tl_vec *out = tl_vec_new(items.len, ncols);\n    for (int64_t i = 0; i < items.len; i++) {\n        if (is_record) {\n            const int64_t *rec = (const int64_t *)items.data[i];\n            for (int64_t c = 0; c < ncols; c++) {\n                out->cols[c][i] = rec[c];\n            }\n        } else {\n            out->cols[0][i] = items.data[i];\n        }\n    }\n    return out;\n}\n\n/* One JSON value from stdin per call, parsed the same descriptor-driven way tl_read_inputs\n * assembles its whole Vec from, but one line at a time and with no Vec ever built: the caller\n * drives its own loop and decides when to stop, which is what lets the native backend fuse\n * `jsonlines(f(inputs))` into a read-one/transform-one/write-one loop (see tir::fusion)\n * instead of collecting everything before printing anything.\n *\n * Returns 0 at EOF (*out untouched) and 1 with *out set otherwise. A separate flag rather than a\n * sentinel value in *out, because a record pointer or an Int result can legitimately be any\n * int64_t, including whatever a sentinel would need to reserve.\n *\n * getline's buffer is not reused across calls the way tl_read_inputs's is within its own single\n * call: this function is called once per record from the caller's own loop, so there is no one\n * long-lived buffer to reuse, and toylang optimises for none of this to begin with. */\nint tl_read_one_input(const tl_str *descriptor, int64_t *out) {\n    char *t = tl_alloc((size_t)descriptor->len + 1);\n    memcpy(t, descriptor->ptr, (size_t)descriptor->len);\n    t[descriptor->len] = 0;\n\n    char *line = NULL;\n    size_t cap = 0;\n    for (;;) {\n        ssize_t len = getline(&line, &cap, stdin);\n        if (len == -1) {\n            free(line);\n            return 0;\n        }\n        tl_json j = {line, line + len};\n        tl_skip_ws(&j);\n        if (j.p == j.end) {\n            continue;\n        }\n        j.p = line;\n        int64_t value = tl_parse(&j, t, \"inputs\", NULL);\n        tl_skip_ws(&j);\n        if (j.p != j.end) {\n            tl_fail(\"trailing content after the value\", \"inputs\");\n        }\n        *out = value;\n        free(line);\n        return 1;\n    }\n}\n\n/* One raw line of stdin at a time: the streaming counterpart of tl_collect_lines below, the\n * same way tl_read_one_input above is tl_read_inputs's. Returns 0 at EOF (*out untouched) and\n * 1 with *out set to the line's tl_str otherwise. Same trailing-newline rule as\n * tl_collect_lines; a blank line is a line, since `lines` keeps them. A non-UTF-8 line is\n * refused rather than carried: a Str is Unicode scalar values (kantord/toylang#102). */\nstatic int tl_utf8_valid(const char *s, size_t len) {\n    size_t i = 0;\n    while (i < len) {\n        unsigned char b = (unsigned char)s[i];\n        if (b < 0x80) {\n            i++;\n        } else if (b >= 0xC2 && b < 0xE0) {\n            if (i + 1 >= len || ((unsigned char)s[i + 1] & 0xC0) != 0x80) return 0;\n            i += 2;\n        } else if (b >= 0xE0 && b < 0xF0) {\n            if (i + 2 >= len || ((unsigned char)s[i + 1] & 0xC0) != 0x80\n                || ((unsigned char)s[i + 2] & 0xC0) != 0x80) return 0;\n            i += 3;\n        } else if (b >= 0xF0 && b < 0xF5) {\n            if (i + 3 >= len || ((unsigned char)s[i + 1] & 0xC0) != 0x80\n                || ((unsigned char)s[i + 2] & 0xC0) != 0x80\n                || ((unsigned char)s[i + 3] & 0xC0) != 0x80) return 0;\n            i += 4;\n        } else {\n            return 0;\n        }\n    }\n    return 1;\n}\n\nint tl_read_one_line(int64_t *out) {\n    char *line = NULL;\n    size_t cap = 0;\n    ssize_t len = getline(&line, &cap, stdin);\n    if (len == -1) {\n        free(line);\n        return 0;\n    }\n    if (len > 0 && line[len - 1] == '\\n') {\n        len--;\n    }\n    if (!tl_utf8_valid(line, (size_t)len)) {\n        tl_fail(\"stdin is not valid UTF-8\", \"lines\");\n    }\n    char *bytes = tl_alloc((size_t)len);\n    memcpy(bytes, line, (size_t)len);\n    *out = (int64_t)tl_str_new(bytes, len);\n    free(line);\n    return 1;\n}\n\n/* One line of stdin at a time via getline, in contrast to tl_read_input just above, which reads\n * the whole stream before anything else can run. getline reuses the same growable buffer across\n * calls, so each line is copied out to its own allocation before being stored; the buffer would\n * otherwise be overwritten, and every previously stored line, on the next call.\n *\n * The trailing newline getline includes is stripped; a final line with none is still yielded,\n * matching wc -l's undercount being the mistake to avoid rather than the convention to follow.\n * A bare \\r is left untouched as ordinary content, matching jq -R and Python's own stdin\n * iteration, neither of which treats CRLF specially. */\ntl_vec *tl_collect_lines(void) {\n    tl_list items = {NULL, 0, 0};\n    char *line = NULL;\n    size_t cap = 0;\n    ssize_t len;\n    while ((len = getline(&line, &cap, stdin)) != -1) {\n        if (len > 0 && line[len - 1] == '\\n') {\n            len--;\n        }\n        if (!tl_utf8_valid(line, (size_t)len)) {\n            tl_fail(\"stdin is not valid UTF-8\", \"lines\");\n        }\n        char *bytes = tl_alloc((size_t)len);\n        memcpy(bytes, line, (size_t)len);\n        tl_list_push(&items, (int64_t)tl_str_new(bytes, len));\n    }\n    free(line);\n\n    tl_vec *v = tl_vec_new(items.len, 1);\n    for (int64_t i = 0; i < items.len; i++) {\n        v->cols[0][i] = items.data[i];\n    }\n    return v;\n}\n\n/* Split one Str on a literal delimiter: every occurrence, in order, with the empty string one\n * empty field and a trailing delimiter a trailing empty field, the same shape `str.split` gives\n * on every other backend. The delimiter is searched literally, never as a pattern. */\nstatic tl_vec *tl_split(const tl_str *s, const tl_str *sep) {\n    tl_list items = {NULL, 0, 0};\n    int64_t pos = 0;\n    while (pos <= s->len) {\n        int64_t found = -1;\n        for (int64_t i = pos; i + sep->len <= s->len; i++) {\n            if (memcmp(s->ptr + i, sep->ptr, (size_t)sep->len) == 0) {\n                found = i;\n                break;\n            }\n        }\n        int64_t n;\n        if (found == -1) {\n            n = s->len - pos;\n        } else {\n            n = found - pos;\n        }\n        char *bytes = tl_alloc((size_t)n);\n        memcpy(bytes, s->ptr + pos, (size_t)n);\n        tl_list_push(&items, (int64_t)tl_str_new(bytes, n));\n        if (found == -1) {\n            break;\n        }\n        pos = found + sep->len;\n    }\n    tl_vec *v = tl_vec_new(items.len, 1);\n    for (int64_t i = 0; i < items.len; i++) {\n        v->cols[0][i] = items.data[i];\n    }\n    return v;\n}\n\n/* Split every line of a Vec<Str> on the delimiter, one row per line: `dsv(delim)`. The outer\n * Vec is a single column of inner Vecs, the struct-of-arrays spelling of Vec<Vec<Str>>. */\ntl_vec *tl_split_lines(tl_vec *lines, tl_str *sep) {\n    tl_vec *out = tl_vec_new(lines->len, 1);\n    for (int64_t i = 0; i < lines->len; i++) {\n        out->cols[0][i] = (int64_t)tl_split((tl_str *)lines->cols[0][i], sep);\n    }\n    return out;\n}\n\n/* Gather one element of a Vec of records back into a record.\n *\n * The struct-of-arrays layout means an element is spread across columns, and almost nothing in\n * the language needs it whole: select reads single fields out of the columns, and `.field`\n * returns a column. Printing is the exception, because rendering an element needs every field\n * at once. This is the only gather, and it exists for output alone.\n */\nint64_t *tl_rec_from_vec(const tl_vec *v, int64_t i) {\n    int64_t *rec = tl_rec_new(v->ncols);\n    for (int64_t c = 0; c < v->ncols; c++) {\n        rec[c] = v->cols[c][i];\n    }\n    return rec;\n}\n\n/* Opt: a pointer to a slot, or NULL for absent.\n *\n * Boxing rather than a tag pair, because a slot holds any value an Int can and there is no\n * spare bit pattern to mean absent. Uniform across element types, which is what lets one\n * function serve them all.\n */\nint64_t *tl_opt_some(int64_t value) {\n    int64_t *p = tl_alloc(sizeof(int64_t));\n    *p = value;\n    return p;\n}\n\nint64_t tl_opt_is_some(const int64_t *o) {\n    return o != NULL;\n}\n\nint64_t tl_opt_get(const int64_t *o) {\n    return *o;\n}\n\n/* Every element but the first. NULL on an empty Vec -- the same absence encoding tl_opt_some\n * uses everywhere else, so a caller unwraps it the same way an Index result is unwrapped. */\nint64_t *tl_vec_tail(const tl_vec *v) {\n    if (v->len == 0) {\n        return NULL;\n    }\n    tl_vec *out = tl_vec_new(v->len - 1, v->ncols);\n    for (int64_t c = 0; c < v->ncols; c++) {\n        if (out->len > 0) {\n            memcpy(out->cols[c], v->cols[c] + 1, (size_t)out->len * sizeof(int64_t));\n        }\n    }\n    return tl_opt_some((int64_t)out);\n}\n\n/* Flatten a Vec<Vec<T>> into a Vec<T>. `ncols` is T's column count: passed in rather than read\n * off an inner Vec, since an empty outer Vec has no inner Vec to read it from. */\ntl_vec *tl_vec_flatten(const tl_vec *vv, int64_t ncols) {\n    int64_t total = 0;\n    for (int64_t i = 0; i < vv->len; i++) {\n        total += ((const tl_vec *)vv->cols[0][i])->len;\n    }\n    tl_vec *out = tl_vec_new(total, ncols);\n    int64_t at = 0;\n    for (int64_t i = 0; i < vv->len; i++) {\n        const tl_vec *inner = (const tl_vec *)vv->cols[0][i];\n        for (int64_t c = 0; c < ncols; c++) {\n            if (inner->len > 0) {\n                memcpy(out->cols[c] + at, inner->cols[c], (size_t)inner->len * sizeof(int64_t));\n            }\n        }\n        at += inner->len;\n    }\n    return out;\n}\n\n/* `a + b` on two Vecs (kantord/toylang#97, the add-trait reading): join them without going\n * through the one-level-of-nesting `tl_vec_flatten` does. `ncols` is T's column count, the same\n * reason `tl_vec_flatten` takes it rather than reading it off either Vec. */\ntl_vec *tl_vec_concat(const tl_vec *a, const tl_vec *b, int64_t ncols) {\n    tl_vec *out = tl_vec_new(a->len + b->len, ncols);\n    for (int64_t c = 0; c < ncols; c++) {\n        if (a->len > 0) {\n            memcpy(out->cols[c], a->cols[c], (size_t)a->len * sizeof(int64_t));\n        }\n        if (b->len > 0) {\n            memcpy(out->cols[c] + a->len, b->cols[c], (size_t)b->len * sizeof(int64_t));\n        }\n    }\n    return out;\n}\n\n/* Ascending by raw int64 value: what backs Int, Int64, and Char, since all three live in the\n * slot unnarrowed (a Char is a codepoint, and the checker already keeps it from mixing with the\n * others). qsort's comparator returns the sign of the difference rather than subtracting, since\n * an int64 difference can itself overflow the int qsort wants back. */\nstatic int tl_cmp_int64(const void *a, const void *b) {\n    int64_t x = *(const int64_t *)a;\n    int64_t y = *(const int64_t *)b;\n    return x < y ? -1 : (x > y ? 1 : 0);\n}\n\nstatic int tl_cmp_str_slot(const void *a, const void *b) {\n    const tl_str *x = (const tl_str *)*(const int64_t *)a;\n    const tl_str *y = (const tl_str *)*(const int64_t *)b;\n    return (int)tl_str_cmp(x, y);\n}\n\n/* `sort` over a Vec whose element is Int, Int64, or Char: one column of raw int64 slots, sorted\n * in place on a copy. Restricted to these by the checker (`orderable`), so there is no column\n * beyond the one holding the elements themselves to carry along. */\ntl_vec *tl_vec_sort_int(const tl_vec *v) {\n    tl_vec *out = tl_vec_new(v->len, 1);\n    if (v->len > 0) {\n        memcpy(out->cols[0], v->cols[0], (size_t)v->len * sizeof(int64_t));\n        qsort(out->cols[0], (size_t)v->len, sizeof(int64_t), tl_cmp_int64);\n    }\n    return out;\n}\n\n/* The same shape for `Vec<Str>`, whose slots are `tl_str *` rather than raw integers. */\ntl_vec *tl_vec_sort_str(const tl_vec *v) {\n    tl_vec *out = tl_vec_new(v->len, 1);\n    if (v->len > 0) {\n        memcpy(out->cols[0], v->cols[0], (size_t)v->len * sizeof(int64_t));\n        qsort(out->cols[0], (size_t)v->len, sizeof(int64_t), tl_cmp_str_slot);\n    }\n    return out;\n}\n\n/* `reverse`, generic over the element type the way `tl_vec_tail` is: every column's row order\n * flips together, so a Vec of records or of nested Vecs reverses correctly with no type-specific\n * code. */\ntl_vec *tl_vec_reverse(const tl_vec *v, int64_t ncols) {\n    tl_vec *out = tl_vec_new(v->len, ncols);\n    for (int64_t c = 0; c < ncols; c++) {\n        for (int64_t i = 0; i < v->len; i++) {\n            out->cols[c][i] = v->cols[c][v->len - 1 - i];\n        }\n    }\n    return out;\n}\n\n/* `sum` over a Vec of Int or Int64 (kantord/toylang#140): the reduction of `+`, each addition\n * wrapping the way the language's `+` wraps. Both widths live in the same i64 slot, so `narrow`\n * is what tells Int (32-bit wrap after every addition) from Int64 (none). Accumulated in\n * uint64_t so no addition can overflow into undefined behaviour; the narrow step truncates to\n * 32 bits and sign-extends. */\nint64_t tl_vec_sum(const tl_vec *v, int narrow) {\n    uint64_t acc = 0;\n    for (int64_t i = 0; i < v->len; i++) {\n        acc += (uint64_t)v->cols[0][i];\n        if (narrow) {\n            acc = (uint64_t)(int64_t)(int32_t)acc;\n        }\n    }\n    return (int64_t)acc;\n}\n\n/* `max` over a Vec of Int or Int64: the greatest raw slot, Int's already sign-extended so the\n * i64 comparison orders it correctly. NULL on an empty Vec, the same absence encoding\n * tl_opt_some uses everywhere else, so a caller unwraps it the way it unwraps an Index result. */\nint64_t *tl_vec_max(const tl_vec *v) {\n    if (v->len == 0) {\n        return NULL;\n    }\n    int64_t m = v->cols[0][0];\n    for (int64_t i = 1; i < v->len; i++) {\n        if (v->cols[0][i] > m) {\n            m = v->cols[0][i];\n        }\n    }\n    return tl_opt_some(m);\n}\n\n/* Collapse one dimension at `i`, `depth` layers down, counting from the end when negative.\n *\n * `is_record` decides whether an entry has to be gathered out of the columns. The column count\n * cannot stand in for that test: a record with one field has one column exactly like a Vec of\n * scalars does.\n */\nint64_t *tl_at(const tl_vec *v, int64_t i, int64_t depth, int is_record) {\n    if (depth > 0) {\n        tl_vec *out = tl_vec_new(v->len, 1);\n        for (int64_t k = 0; k < v->len; k++) {\n            const tl_vec *inner = (const tl_vec *)v->cols[0][k];\n            out->cols[0][k] = (int64_t)tl_at(inner, i, depth - 1, is_record);\n        }\n        return (int64_t *)out;\n    }\n    if (i < 0) {\n        i = v->len + i;\n    }\n    if (i < 0 || i >= v->len) {\n        return NULL;\n    }\n    return tl_opt_some(is_record ? (int64_t)tl_rec_from_vec(v, i) : v->cols[0][i]);\n}\n\n/* Narrow a Vec to the [lo, hi) window, clamping out-of-range bounds jq-style rather than\n * answering absence the way tl_at does: negatives count from the end, both bounds clamp to\n * [0, len], and a crossed window is empty (kantord/toylang#143). A bound left out is passed\n * as INT64_MIN for the start or INT64_MAX for the end, each folding to the Vec's own boundary\n * under the clamp. `depth` is the same layers-down the collapse uses, recursing into each\n * element when the slice lands below the outermost dimension. */\ntl_vec *tl_vec_slice(const tl_vec *v, int64_t lo, int64_t hi, int64_t depth) {\n    if (depth > 0) {\n        tl_vec *out = tl_vec_new(v->len, 1);\n        for (int64_t k = 0; k < v->len; k++) {\n            const tl_vec *inner = (const tl_vec *)v->cols[0][k];\n            out->cols[0][k] = (int64_t)tl_vec_slice(inner, lo, hi, depth - 1);\n        }\n        return out;\n    }\n    int64_t n = v->len;\n    if (lo < 0) {\n        lo += n;\n    }\n    if (hi < 0) {\n        hi += n;\n    }\n    if (lo < 0) {\n        lo = 0;\n    }\n    if (hi < 0) {\n        hi = 0;\n    }\n    if (lo > n) {\n        lo = n;\n    }\n    if (hi > n) {\n        hi = n;\n    }\n    int64_t len = hi - lo;\n    if (len < 0) {\n        len = 0;\n    }\n    tl_vec *out = tl_vec_new(len, v->ncols);\n    for (int64_t c = 0; c < v->ncols; c++) {\n        if (len > 0) {\n            memcpy(out->cols[c], v->cols[c] + lo, (size_t)len * sizeof(int64_t));\n        }\n    }\n    return out;\n}\n\n/* Insist an Opt is present, `depth` layers down.\n *\n * Unlike an index this needs no is_record flag: an Opt already holds a gathered value, so there\n * is nothing left to collect out of columns.\n */\nint64_t *tl_unwrap(int64_t *o, int64_t depth) {\n    if (depth > 0) {\n        tl_vec *v = (tl_vec *)o;\n        tl_vec *out = tl_vec_new(v->len, 1);\n        for (int64_t k = 0; k < v->len; k++) {\n            out->cols[0][k] = (int64_t)tl_unwrap((int64_t *)v->cols[0][k], depth - 1);\n        }\n        return (int64_t *)out;\n    }\n    if (o == NULL) {\n        const char *msg = \"toylang: unwrapped a value that is not there\\n\";\n        (void)!write(2, msg, strlen(msg));\n        exit(1);\n    }\n    return (int64_t *)*o;\n}\n\n/* The only way arithmetic can fail. */\n_Noreturn void tl_div_by_zero(void) {\n    const char *msg = \"toylang: divided by zero\\n\";\n    (void)!write(2, msg, strlen(msg));\n    exit(1);\n}\n\n/* Build a Vec of `len` scalars, filled by the caller. `map` produces one column whatever the\n * source had, since its result element is whatever the body returned. */\n/* The integers from zero up to but not including n. A negative n gives an empty Vec rather\n * than an error, the same as asking for zero of them. */\ntl_vec *tl_range(int64_t n) {\n    if (n < 0) {\n        n = 0;\n    }\n    tl_vec *v = tl_vec_new(n, 1);\n    for (int64_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        v->cols[0][i] = i;\n    }\n    return v;\n}\n\n/* Every Unicode scalar value in s, decoded from UTF-8, one codepoint per element -- not one\n * byte and not one UTF-16 unit, so a character outside the Basic Multilingual Plane is one\n * element here even where a backend whose own strings are UTF-16 needs a surrogate pair to\n * spell it. `s->len` is an overallocation (the ASCII-only case needs exactly that many, and\n * every multi-byte codepoint needs fewer slots than the bytes it decodes from), trimmed to the\n * real count once decoding is done, the same sizing tl_parse_string already relies on for the\n * reverse direction. Malformed UTF-8 cannot occur: every tl_str this runtime builds is already\n * valid UTF-8, whether from a literal or from tl_utf8_encode. */\ntl_vec *tl_chars(const tl_str *s) {\n    tl_vec *v = tl_vec_new(s->len, 1);\n    int64_t n = 0;\n    int64_t i = 0;\n    while (i < s->len) {\n        unsigned char b0 = (unsigned char)s->ptr[i];\n        uint32_t cp;\n        int extra;\n        if (b0 < 0x80) {\n            cp = b0;\n            extra = 0;\n        } else if ((b0 & 0xE0) == 0xC0) {\n            cp = b0 & 0x1F;\n            extra = 1;\n        } else if ((b0 & 0xF0) == 0xE0) {\n            cp = b0 & 0x0F;\n            extra = 2;\n        } else {\n            cp = b0 & 0x07;\n            extra = 3;\n        }\n        i++;\n        for (int k = 0; k < extra; k++) {\n            cp = (cp << 6) | ((unsigned char)s->ptr[i] & 0x3F);\n            i++;\n        }\n        v->cols[0][n++] = (int64_t)cp;\n    }\n    v->len = n;\n    return v;\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":1405,"totalLines":1405,"truncated":false}},"title":"runtime/toylang.c","time":{"start":1788464660310,"end":1788464660340}},"id":"prt_068ccff5100193vpQo8MsrEQo4","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","messageID":"msg_068ccedf7001lh1uRrpfifMEVG"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788464660348,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_0e29565b29bb41869a51289c","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_llvm.rs","limit":120,"offset":1},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_llvm.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! The native backend.\n2: //!\n3: //! Unlike Lua and JavaScript this does not end at a string of source. It ends at an object file,\n4: //! which is not a program: linking is still someone else's job, so a native build shells out to\n5: //! `cc`, which also compiles `runtime/toylang.c` alongside it.\n6: //!\n7: //! Everything it cannot compile yet returns a named error rather than being silently absent, so\n8: //! the gap between this and the other two backends is a visible, shrinking list.\n9: \n10: use std::collections::HashMap;\n11: use std::path::Path;\n12: \n13: use inkwell::basic_block::BasicBlock;\n14: use inkwell::builder::Builder;\n15: use inkwell::context::Context;\n16: use inkwell::module::{Linkage, Module};\n17: use inkwell::targets::{\n18:     CodeModel, FileType, InitializationConfig, RelocMode, Target, TargetMachine,\n19: };\n20: use inkwell::types::{BasicMetadataTypeEnum, BasicTypeEnum, StructType};\n21: use inkwell::values::{\n22:     BasicMetadataValueEnum, BasicValueEnum, FunctionValue, IntValue, PointerValue,\n23: };\n24: use inkwell::{AddressSpace, IntPredicate, OptimizationLevel};\n25: \n26: use crate::ast::{BinOp, LogicOp};\n27: use crate::tir::{self, Builtin, Func, Fusion, Kind, LocalId, Program, Source, Stage, Tir};\n28: use crate::ty::{self, Enums, Type};\n29: \n30: /// The C source linked into every native binary.\n31: ///\n32: /// Embedded rather than read from disk so a built `toylang` does not depend on its own source\n33: /// tree still being there.\n34: pub const RUNTIME_C: &str = include_str!(\"../runtime/toylang.c\");\n35: \n36: fn unsupported(what: &str) -> String {\n37:     format!(\"the native backend cannot compile {what} yet\")\n38: }\n39: \n40: struct Runtime<'ctx> {\n41:     concat: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n42:     int_to_str: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n43:     str_eq: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n44:     str_cmp: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n45:     print: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n46:     quote: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n47:     join: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n48:     vec_new: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n49:     vec_len: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n50:     vec_get: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n51:     vec_set: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n52:     vec_from_mask: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n53:     mask_new: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n54:     mask_set: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n55:     vec_column: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n56:     rec_get: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n57:     rec_new: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n58:     collect_lines: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n59:     split_lines: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n60:     rec_set: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n61:     read_input: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n62:     read_inputs: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n63:     read_one_input: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n64:     read_one_line: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n65:     rec_from_vec: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n66:     at: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n67:     opt_is_some: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n68:     opt_get: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n69:     opt_some: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n70:     unwrap: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n71:     div_by_zero: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n72:     range: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n73:     vec_tail: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n74:     vec_flatten: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n75:     vec_slice: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n76:     vec_concat: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n77:     chars: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n78:     vec_sort_int: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n79:     vec_sort_str: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n80:     vec_reverse: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n81:     vec_sum: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n82:     vec_max: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n83: }\n84: \n85: /// What a compiler-introduced binding holds.\n86: ///\n87: /// `select` over a Vec of records binds `.` to a position rather than to a value, because\n88: /// struct-of-arrays spreads an element across columns and materialising it would undo the point\n89: /// of the layout. A cursor is only ever consumed by field access, which reads one column.\n90: #[derive(Clone, Copy)]\n91: enum Slot<'ctx> {\n92:     Value(BasicValueEnum<'ctx>),\n93:     Cursor {\n94:         vec: PointerValue<'ctx>,\n95:         index: IntValue<'ctx>,\n96:     },\n97: }\n98: \n99: struct Emitter<'ctx, 'p> {\n100:     ctx: &'ctx Context,\n101:     module: Module<'ctx>,\n102:     builder: Builder<'ctx>,\n103:     rt: Runtime<'ctx>,\n104:     funcs: HashMap<String, FunctionValue<'ctx>>,\n105:     locals: HashMap<LocalId, Slot<'ctx>>,\n106:     params: HashMap<String, BasicValueEnum<'ctx>>,\n107:     /// The one parsed stdin value, as the raw slot tl_read_input returns, stored in a global\n108:     /// that main fills before anything else runs. Every other backend binds `input` to a name\n109:     /// read once in its preamble; a call per `Input` node would re-read an already-drained\n110:     /// stdin now that a program can spell `input` more than once.\n111:     input_slot: Option<PointerValue<'ctx>>,\n112:     next_global: usize,\n113:     /// Every enum the program declared, for `ty::variants`: what a `Type::Enum` carries is a\n114:     /// placeholder wherever a recursive enum's payload reaches back to itself.\n115:     enums: &'p Enums,\n116:     /// The printer each recursive enum has of its own, by `Type::ident`. See `declare_printers`.\n117:     printers: HashMap<String, FunctionValue<'ctx>>,\n118: }\n119: \n120: impl<'ctx> Emitter<'ctx, '_> {\n\n(Showing lines 1-120 of 2664. Use offset=121 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! The native backend.\n//!\n//! Unlike Lua and JavaScript this does not end at a string of source. It ends at an object file,\n//! which is not a program: linking is still someone else's job, so a native build shells out to\n//! `cc`, which also compiles `runtime/toylang.c` alongside it.\n//!\n//! Everything it cannot compile yet returns a named error rather than being silently absent, so\n//! the gap between this and the other two backends is a visible, shrinking list.\n\nuse std::collections::HashMap;\nuse std::path::Path;\n\nuse inkwell::basic_block::BasicBlock;\nuse inkwell::builder::Builder;\nuse inkwell::context::Context;\nuse inkwell::module::{Linkage, Module};\nuse inkwell::targets::{\n    CodeModel, FileType, InitializationConfig, RelocMode, Target, TargetMachine,\n};\nuse inkwell::types::{BasicMetadataTypeEnum, BasicTypeEnum, StructType};","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_llvm.rs","text":"//! The native backend.\n//!\n//! Unlike Lua and JavaScript this does not end at a string of source. It ends at an object file,\n//! which is not a program: linking is still someone else's job, so a native build shells out to\n//! `cc`, which also compiles `runtime/toylang.c` alongside it.\n//!\n//! Everything it cannot compile yet returns a named error rather than being silently absent, so\n//! the gap between this and the other two backends is a visible, shrinking list.\n\nuse std::collections::HashMap;\nuse std::path::Path;\n\nuse inkwell::basic_block::BasicBlock;\nuse inkwell::builder::Builder;\nuse inkwell::context::Context;\nuse inkwell::module::{Linkage, Module};\nuse inkwell::targets::{\n    CodeModel, FileType, InitializationConfig, RelocMode, Target, TargetMachine,\n};\nuse inkwell::types::{BasicMetadataTypeEnum, BasicTypeEnum, StructType};\nuse inkwell::values::{\n    BasicMetadataValueEnum, BasicValueEnum, FunctionValue, IntValue, PointerValue,\n};\nuse inkwell::{AddressSpace, IntPredicate, OptimizationLevel};\n\nuse crate::ast::{BinOp, LogicOp};\nuse crate::tir::{self, Builtin, Func, Fusion, Kind, LocalId, Program, Source, Stage, Tir};\nuse crate::ty::{self, Enums, Type};\n\n/// The C source linked into every native binary.\n///\n/// Embedded rather than read from disk so a built `toylang` does not depend on its own source\n/// tree still being there.\npub const RUNTIME_C: &str = include_str!(\"../runtime/toylang.c\");\n\nfn unsupported(what: &str) -> String {\n    format!(\"the native backend cannot compile {what} yet\")\n}\n\nstruct Runtime<'ctx> {\n    concat: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    int_to_str: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    str_eq: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    str_cmp: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    print: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    quote: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    join: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    vec_new: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    vec_len: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    vec_get: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    vec_set: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    vec_from_mask: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    mask_new: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    mask_set: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    vec_column: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    rec_get: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    rec_new: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    collect_lines: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    split_lines: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    rec_set: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    read_input: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    read_inputs: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    read_one_input: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    read_one_line: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    rec_from_vec: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    at: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    opt_is_some: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    opt_get: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    opt_some: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    unwrap: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    div_by_zero: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    range: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    vec_tail: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    vec_flatten: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    vec_slice: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    vec_concat: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    chars: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    vec_sort_int: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    vec_sort_str: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    vec_reverse: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    vec_sum: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    vec_max: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n}\n\n/// What a compiler-introduced binding holds.\n///\n/// `select` over a Vec of records binds `.` to a position rather than to a value, because\n/// struct-of-arrays spreads an element across columns and materialising it would undo the point\n/// of the layout. A cursor is only ever consumed by field access, which reads one column.\n#[derive(Clone, Copy)]\nenum Slot<'ctx> {\n    Value(BasicValueEnum<'ctx>),\n    Cursor {\n        vec: PointerValue<'ctx>,\n        index: IntValue<'ctx>,\n    },\n}\n\nstruct Emitter<'ctx, 'p> {\n    ctx: &'ctx Context,\n    module: Module<'ctx>,\n    builder: Builder<'ctx>,\n    rt: Runtime<'ctx>,\n    funcs: HashMap<String, FunctionValue<'ctx>>,\n    locals: HashMap<LocalId, Slot<'ctx>>,\n    params: HashMap<String, BasicValueEnum<'ctx>>,\n    /// The one parsed stdin value, as the raw slot tl_read_input returns, stored in a global\n    /// that main fills before anything else runs. Every other backend binds `input` to a name\n    /// read once in its preamble; a call per `Input` node would re-read an already-drained\n    /// stdin now that a program can spell `input` more than once.\n    input_slot: Option<PointerValue<'ctx>>,\n    next_global: usize,\n    /// Every enum the program declared, for `ty::variants`: what a `Type::Enum` carries is a\n    /// placeholder wherever a recursive enum's payload reaches back to itself.\n    enums: &'p Enums,\n    /// The printer each recursive enum has of its own, by `Type::ident`. See `declare_printers`.\n    printers: HashMap<String, FunctionValue<'ctx>>,\n}\n\nimpl<'ctx> Emitter<'ctx, '_> {","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":120,"totalLines":2664,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/emit_llvm.rs","time":{"start":1788464660299,"end":1788464660345}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"jq 1.8.2 — max_by exists. Good.\n\nNow — LLVM backend. This is the hardest. Let me look at how the LLVM backend handles Map/Select and the runtime C functions (sort, max, etc.. Let me look at the Runtime struct and sort_runtime and the runtime/toylang.c functions.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_