{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788464678471,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"call_e392e38237ab4b0aafb122f7","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"include":"emit_llvm.rs","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src","pattern":"Kind::Index|fn emit_loop|Kind::Field"},"output":"Found 3 matches\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/emit_llvm.rs:\n  Line 482:     fn emit_loop<F>(&mut self, len: IntValue<'ctx>, mut body: F) -> Result<(), String>\n\n  Line 1393:             Kind::Field { base, name } => self.field(base, name, &t.ty)?,\n\n  Line 1557:             Kind::Index {\n","metadata":{"matches":3,"truncated":false},"title":"Kind::Index|fn emit_loop|Kind::Field","time":{"start":1788464678450,"end":1788464678466}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The LLVM backend's Map binds the param to a Cursor (for record elements) or Value. The body expression reads through the cursor. For sort_by/max_by in LLVM, I need a fundamentally different approach: the key must be computed per element, then sorted. This is a substantial piece of work in LLVM.\n\nThe key insight: the projection body, when the element is a record, reads single fields (through the cursor).. So computing keys per element requires iterating the source and evaluating the body per element — exactly what `map` does. So: compute a key Vec (one column), then sort the indices by the key, then gather the elements (per column) in sorted index order.\n\n \n\nSo for LLVM sort_by:\n1. Compute key for each element (using a map-like loop evaluating body with param bound to cursor/value. Store keys in a Vec (1 column, elem type's slot form.\n.\n2. Build an index Vec [0,1,...len-1]... \n3. Sort the indices by the key column (qsort with a comparator that reads key[idx]... — but qsort's comparator needs a context (the keys array) — qsort can't pass context. So I need an insertion sort or a different approach in C, or build pairs.\n\n\n\nHmm — this is getting heavy. Let me look at how the LLVM backend sorts today (tl_vec_sort_int uses qsort on the column itself, no context needed. For sort_by, the comparator needs the key column. qsort has no context param. Options: (a) build a pairs Vec (key, idx) and sort by key via a global? (b) implement an insertion sort that takes the keys array explicitly. (c) since keys and indices both int64, pack them into one array: each element becomes a single int64 pair... can't pack two i64s. (d) Sort a combined array of structs {key, idx} using a comparator with no context — compare the structs' first fields. YES! Build a Vec of records {key, idx} (two columns), sort it by the key column using qsort with a comparator on the struct's first field. The comparator for int64 keys is tl_cmp_int64 (works on the first column's slot. Actually qsort sorts an array of records — each record is 2 int64s. A comparator that compares the first int64 of each record: `tl_cmp_int64` takes `const void *a` as a pointer to an int64 — for a record, a points at the first field (the key), works directly! Since a record's slots are contiguous int64s, `*(const int64_t*)a` reads the key. \n\nSo: build a Vec of 2 columns (key, idx). qsort it with tl_cmp_int64 (or a str comparator for Str keys — for Str keys, the key is a tl_str* pointer, so a record {tl_str* key, int64 idx} — the comparator needs to deref the key pointer and compare strings. That's tl_cmp_str_slot — reads `*(const tl_str **)a` — the first slot is a tl_str* pointer. Works! Then gather the source elements into the output in sorted idx order (per column...\n\nFor Str keys: the key Vec's column 0 holds tl_str* pointers. The record {key, idx} first slot is a tl_str* — `tl_cmp_str_slot` reads it as `*(const tl_str **)a` → the tl_str*, calls tl_str_cmp. Works.\n\n\n\nFor max_by LLVM:\n1. Compute key Vec (same map-like loop.\n2. Walk indices 1..len-1, track best key and best idx (loop. \n3. If len==0 return NULL (absent opt. Else gather the best element into a record (tl_rec_from_vec... Actually max_by's result is `Opt<T>` — one element of the Vec. Need to return a slot/record value wrapped in tl_opt_some. The result type is Opt<T>. For the LLVM emitter, the expression result needs to be a BasicValue with the right representation. Let me look at how Index (tl_at) produces Opt<T> values and how callers consume them. In LLVM, Opt is \"a pointer to a slot, or NULL\". The result of `tl_at` is an i64 slot value? Let me look at how Index is emitted in LLVM expr... Around line 1458+ there was Unwrap. Let me look at Index emission in LLVM.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068cd4627001Eu2JJ1Sxo5ZxTX","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","messageID":"msg_068cd1d02001EgIAfcxo0gLixC"}}
