{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788464691582,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"call_dd07207e0ed642d8a63eb743","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"include":"ty.rs","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src","pattern":"fn elem|fn as_opt"},"output":"Found 2 matches\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/ty.rs:\n  Line 157:     pub fn elem(&self) -> Option<&Type> {\n\n  Line 176:     pub fn as_opt(&self) -> Option<&Type> {\n","metadata":{"matches":2,"truncated":false},"title":"fn elem|fn as_opt","time":{"start":1788464691572,"end":1788464691580}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"OK. The LLVM backend work is big but doable. Let me now decide the overall approach and write the implementation. Let me plan the TIR shape and each backend's emission.\n\n### TIR Kind design\n\n```rust\n/// `v | sort_by(.key)`: `v`'s entries in ascending order by the value the projection `body`\n/// gives each entry, ties keeping their original order (stable, the way sort_by reads in jq\n/// and every other backend orders by key. Unlike `sort` no total order on `T` is needed:\n/// every backend orders by the projected key instead.\nSortBy {\n    source: Box<Tir>,\n    param: LocalId,\n    body: Box<Tir>,\n},\n/// `v | max_by(.key))`: the entry whose projection `body` is greatest, `Opt<T>` because an\n/// empty Vec has no maximum -- the same absence answer `max` gives (kantord/toylang#140).\n/// Ties keep the first such entry.\nMaxBy {\n    source: Box<Tir>,\n    param: LocalId,\n    body: Box<Tir>,\n},\n```\n\n### Checker\n\nAdd to `call()` dispatch (after map/select, since they need the subject):\n\n```rust\nif func == \"sort_by\" {\n    return sort_by_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?, span);\n}\nif func == \"max_by\" {\n    return max_by_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?, span);\n}\n```\n\nHmm — wait. Should these require a subject (pipe)? sort_by's Vec arrives through the pipe, like map. Let me name the functions sort_by_call/max_by_call mirroring select_call/map_call. \n\nActually — hold on. Let me reconsider whether the Vec should come through the pipe. Look at the issue once more: \"Implement sort_by and max_by as Vec builtins ordering through a scalar projection function, e.g. sort_by(.key) / max_by(.key).\"\n\nThe map docs: \"`map(expr)`: applies expr to every entry of its subject... The subject arrives through a pipe\". So map's signature is `map(expr)` — the expr arg is the body. Same pattern: `sort_by(expr)` — expr arg is the projection. Yes. So `v | sort_by(.key)`. The checker: subject via ctx.subject.\n\nNow implement:\n\n```rust\n/// The projection both `sort_by` and `max_by` order by: a scalar in the same restricted set\n/// `sort`'s element type is restricted to, so every backend can order by key with a\n/// comparator rather than reaching for record ordering.\nfn sort_by_call(ctx: &Ctx, arg: &Expr, span: Span) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n    let Some((subject, id)) = ctx.subject.clone() else {\n        return Err(Error::new(span, \"`sort_by` needs a subject, so it must follow `|`\"));\n    };\n    let Some(elem) = mapper_elem(&subject) else {\n        return Err(Error::new(span, format!(\"`sort_by` needs a Vec or a stream, found {subject}\"))));\n    };\n    let param = ctx.fresh();\n    let inner = mapper_ctx(ctx, elem, param);\n    let body = synth(&inner, arg)?;\n    if !orderable(&body.ty) {\n        return Err(Error::new(arg.span(), format!(\"`sort_by`'s projection must give an Int, Int64, Str, or Char, found {}\", body.ty))));\n    }\n    let source = Tir::new(subject.clone(), Kind::Local(id));\n    Ok(Tir::new(subject, Kind::SortBy { source: Box::new(source), param, body: Box::new(body) }))\n}\n```\n\nWait — sort_by's result type is the subject's type (Vec<T> or Stream<T>?) — map preserves stream-ness. But sort (like reverse) has \"no lawful Stream instance\" — blocking. So sort_by should also refuse a Stream subject? The sort docs: \"One value in and one value out -- the whole Vec has to be there before the first output entry can be, so sort has no lawful Stream instance\". Same for sort_by: it needs the whole Vec before the first output entry. So sort_by should require a Vec, not a Stream. Hmm — mapper_elem accepts both Vec and Stream. For sort_by, I should restrict to Vec only (reject Stream). Like sort_call uses `arg.ty.elem()` — which is Vec-only (`Type::elem` deliberately Vec-only per the comment on runtime_elem: \"the checker's Type::elem deliberately does not agree, keeping the reducers Vec-only at the surface\". \n\nSo for sort_by, use `elem()` (Vec-only. And for the subject, if it's a Stream, error. Let me check what `Type::elem` is. Let me look.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068cd7970001Vk0SUkYxwWu9jR","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","messageID":"msg_068cd55a7001ys8PD6MkpVu3m4"}}
