{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788464706831,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_c1ef20be1c2b43b38a869763","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/tests/tag_corpus.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/tests/tag_corpus.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! Tags every corpus case with the AST shapes it exercises.\n2: //!\n3: //! Runs unconditionally on every `cargo test`, the same way `export_site.rs` regenerates\n4: //! `corpus.json`: the tags are derived from the compiled program, so keeping them fresh is\n5: //! cheaper than a staleness check would be. The patch touches only the `node_types:` line,\n6: //! never the rest of the file, so the comments the corpus authors wrote survive it.\n7: \n8: mod support;\n9: \n10: use std::path::Path;\n11: \n12: #[test]\n13: fn tag_the_corpus_with_node_types() {\n14:     for case in support::cases() {\n15:         let program =\n16:             toylang::compile(&case.program).unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"{}: {e}\", case.name));\n17:         let tags = toylang::tags::node_types(&program);\n18:         let path = support::dir().join(format!(\"{}.yaml\", case.name));\n19:         patch_node_types(&path, &tags);\n20:     }\n21: }\n22: \n23: fn patch_node_types(path: &Path, tags: &[String]) {\n24:     let text = std::fs::read_to_string(path).unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"{}: {e}\", path.display()));\n25:     let line = format!(\"node_types: [{}]\", tags.join(\", \"));\n26: \n27:     let mut lines: Vec<&str> = text.lines().collect();\n28:     let new_text = match lines.iter().position(|l| l.starts_with(\"node_types:\")) {\n29:         Some(i) => {\n30:             lines[i] = &line;\n31:             lines.join(\"\\n\")\n32:         }\n33:         None => format!(\"{}\\n\\n{line}\", text.trim_end()),\n34:     };\n35: \n36:     let new_text = format!(\"{}\\n\", new_text.trim_end());\n37:     if new_text != text {\n38:         std::fs::write(path, new_text).unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"{}: {e}\", path.display()));\n39:     }\n40: }\n\n(End of file - total 40 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! Tags every corpus case with the AST shapes it exercises.\n//!\n//! Runs unconditionally on every `cargo test`, the same way `export_site.rs` regenerates\n//! `corpus.json`: the tags are derived from the compiled program, so keeping them fresh is\n//! cheaper than a staleness check would be. The patch touches only the `node_types:` line,\n//! never the rest of the file, so the comments the corpus authors wrote survive it.\n\nmod support;\n\nuse std::path::Path;\n\n#[test]\nfn tag_the_corpus_with_node_types() {\n    for case in support::cases() {\n        let program =\n            toylang::compile(&case.program).unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"{}: {e}\", case.name));\n        let tags = toylang::tags::node_types(&program);\n        let path = support::dir().join(format!(\"{}.yaml\", case.name));\n        patch_node_types(&path, &tags);\n    }","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/tests/tag_corpus.rs","text":"//! Tags every corpus case with the AST shapes it exercises.\n//!\n//! Runs unconditionally on every `cargo test`, the same way `export_site.rs` regenerates\n//! `corpus.json`: the tags are derived from the compiled program, so keeping them fresh is\n//! cheaper than a staleness check would be. The patch touches only the `node_types:` line,\n//! never the rest of the file, so the comments the corpus authors wrote survive it.\n\nmod support;\n\nuse std::path::Path;\n\n#[test]\nfn tag_the_corpus_with_node_types() {\n    for case in support::cases() {\n        let program =\n            toylang::compile(&case.program).unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"{}: {e}\", case.name));\n        let tags = toylang::tags::node_types(&program);\n        let path = support::dir().join(format!(\"{}.yaml\", case.name));\n        patch_node_types(&path, &tags);\n    }\n}\n\nfn patch_node_types(path: &Path, tags: &[String]) {\n    let text = std::fs::read_to_string(path).unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"{}: {e}\", path.display()));\n    let line = format!(\"node_types: [{}]\", tags.join(\", \"));\n\n    let mut lines: Vec<&str> = text.lines().collect();\n    let new_text = match lines.iter().position(|l| l.starts_with(\"node_types:\")) {\n        Some(i) => {\n            lines[i] = &line;\n            lines.join(\"\\n\")\n        }\n        None => format!(\"{}\\n\\n{line}\", text.trim_end()),\n    };\n\n    let new_text = format!(\"{}\\n\", new_text.trim_end());\n    if new_text != text {\n        std::fs::write(path, new_text).unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"{}: {e}\", path.display()));\n    }\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":40,"totalLines":40,"truncated":false}},"title":"tests/tag_corpus.rs","time":{"start":1788464706820,"end":1788464706827}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"`Type::elem` is Vec-only. Good. So sort_by takes only a Vec subject (via pipe), and max_by too (like max, which takes only Vec. max_by's source via pipe — hmm, max takes `max(v)` directly as its arg. But max_by needs both Vec and projection. Via pipe: `v | max_by(.key)`. OK.\n\n.\n\nNow let me also check: should the subject be allowed as a direct argument `max_by(v, .key)`? No — unary. OK.\n\n \n\nActually — wait. I realize there might be a cleaner spelling. What if sort_by/max_by take a record argument {v: ..., key: ...}? But then `.key` couldn't reference the element. So no. The pipe form is right. \n\nHmm, but let me double-check by looking at how the docs might present it and whether \"sort_by(.key)\" alone (without a pipe) would even parse. A bare `sort_by(.key)` at the program body: `.` is unbound → checker error \"`sort_by` needs a subject, so it must follow `|`\" — same as `map(.x)` alone. Fine.\n\n.\n\nNow — the \"subject\" for sort_by could also be a plain Vec value like `sort_by([3,1,2], ...)`? No unary. OK.\n\n\n\nLet me now also handle: what if someone writes `[3,1,2] | sort_by(.)`? The projection `.` — a bare subject in mapper context — body type = elem type. Works — sorts by the element itself. That's fine (degenerate case. But is `.` allowed in mapper context? In map, `.` is the element. Yes — `map(.)` would work. So `sort_by(.)` = sort. Fine.\n\n\n\nNow let me write the checker functions. Let me also decide the error messages:\n\n- sort_by with no subject: \"`sort_by` needs a subject, so it must follow `|`\" (like map/select.\n- subject not Vec/Stream — but for sort_by, Stream must be refused. Hmm — map/select accept Stream. For sort_by, I want Vec-only. So:\n\n```rust\nlet Some(elem) = subject.elem() else { ... }\n```\n\nBut that error would fire for both non-Vec and Stream subjects. The message should say what's needed. Like sort's: \"`sort_by` needs a Vec, found {subject}\". Good.\n\n\n\nNow the projection must be orderable. Error message style like sort's: \"`sort_by` needs a projection of Int, Int64, Str, or Char, found {}\".\n\n\n\nFor max_by: same, but result is `opt_of(ctx, elem.clone())` — Opt<T>. Projection must be orderable (same four types).\n\n\n\nNow — one more consideration: does the projection need to be checked with `.` rebound? Yes — mapper_ctx, same as map/select. And `in_mapper` blocks source reads. Good.\n\n\n\nNow — the tags. Add Kind::SortBy → \"builtin.sort-by\"? Hmm — tags.rs's `tag()`: Kind::Map → \"map-over\", Kind::Select → \"selection.narrow\". For SortBy/MaxBy, they're separate Kind variants (not Builtin. What tag? The map-over tag for Map is \"map-over\". For SortBy: maybe \"sort-by\". Let me think — CONTEXT.md names? The tag doc says tags draw from tir::Kind with CONTEXT.md names where they exist. Since SortBy is a new Kind, a tag like \"sort-by\" and \"max-by\" seems natural. But — the tags.rs also has `builtin_tag(Builtin::Sort) → \"sort\"`. Hmm — should sort_by's tag be \"builtin.sort\" (no... it's a distinct Kind. I'll use \"sort-by\" and \"max-by\" tags. Wait — but node_types are also used to build the site's case tree (tag_corpus.rs). Let me check how tag_corpus expects tags... Let me look at tests/tag_corpus.rs briefly.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068cdb4e2001vp3QBKucfPhkDv","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","messageID":"msg_068cd7e57001di3lgGUtVZZt1J"}}
