{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788464731317,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_7e2dd919c46446a89ecf6d69","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/offload.rs","limit":50,"offset":1},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/offload.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! The `--explain-offload` diagnostic: which sub-expressions became vectorized kernels and which\n2: //! fell back to per-element processing, and why.\n3: //!\n4: //! The predicate is cardinality, the same one the design's kernel-admissibility mapping draws\n5: //! (draft.md, \"Cardinality is the kernel-admissibility predicate\"): a `Vec` has known extent, so\n6: //! a `map`/`select` over one compiles to a loop that can be vectorized; a `Stream` is unbounded\n7: //! and arrives one entry at a time, so its per-element stages are never kernels. The one other\n8: //! compile-time decision worth reporting is `tir::fusion`: whether the program's `jsonlines` sink\n9: //! ran as a fused read-one/transform-one/write-one loop or eagerly over an already-materialized\n10: //! value.\n11: \n12: use crate::tir::{self, Kind, Program, Source, Tir};\n13: use crate::ty::Type;\n14: \n15: /// The offload report, one line per decision, ending with a newline. The fusion decision first,\n16: /// then one line per `map`/`select` node in source order.\n17: pub fn explain(program: &Program) -> String {\n18:     let mut out = String::new();\n19:     fusion_line(program, &mut out);\n20:     let mut report = |t: &Tir| match &t.kind {\n21:         Kind::Map { source, .. } => kernel_line(\"map\", &source.ty, &mut out),\n22:         Kind::Select { source, .. } => kernel_line(\"select\", &source.ty, &mut out),\n23:         _ => {}\n24:     };\n25:     for f in &program.funcs {\n26:         tir::each_node(&f.body, &mut report);\n27:     }\n28:     tir::each_node(&program.body, &mut report);\n29:     out\n30: }\n31: \n32: /// The program-level decision `tir::fusion` makes, reported as the first line: whether the sink\n33: /// ran as a fused loop, and when it did not, what the argument's type says instead.\n34: fn fusion_line(program: &Program, out: &mut String) {\n35:     if let Some(fusion) = tir::fusion(program) {\n36:         let source = match fusion.source {\n37:             Source::Inputs => \"`inputs`\",\n38:             Source::Lines => \"`lines`\",\n39:             Source::Range(_) => \"`range`\",\n40:         };\n41:         out.push_str(&format!(\n42:             \"the `jsonlines` pipeline fused into a read-one/transform-one/write-one loop over {source}\\n\"\n43:         ));\n44:     } else {\n45:         // `jsonlines` takes a Vec or a stream (checked); a stream argument always fuses above, so\n46:         // what is left here is the materialized case.\n47:         let Kind::Builtin {\n48:             which: tir::Builtin::JsonLines,\n49:             arg,\n50:         } = &program.body.kind\n\n(Showing lines 1-50 of 87. Use offset=51 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! The `--explain-offload` diagnostic: which sub-expressions became vectorized kernels and which\n//! fell back to per-element processing, and why.\n//!\n//! The predicate is cardinality, the same one the design's kernel-admissibility mapping draws\n//! (draft.md, \"Cardinality is the kernel-admissibility predicate\"): a `Vec` has known extent, so\n//! a `map`/`select` over one compiles to a loop that can be vectorized; a `Stream` is unbounded\n//! and arrives one entry at a time, so its per-element stages are never kernels. The one other\n//! compile-time decision worth reporting is `tir::fusion`: whether the program's `jsonlines` sink\n//! ran as a fused read-one/transform-one/write-one loop or eagerly over an already-materialized\n//! value.\n\nuse crate::tir::{self, Kind, Program, Source, Tir};\nuse crate::ty::Type;\n\n/// The offload report, one line per decision, ending with a newline. The fusion decision first,\n/// then one line per `map`/`select` node in source order.\npub fn explain(program: &Program) -> String {\n    let mut out = String::new();\n    fusion_line(program, &mut out);\n    let mut report = |t: &Tir| match &t.kind {","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/src/offload.rs","text":"//! The `--explain-offload` diagnostic: which sub-expressions became vectorized kernels and which\n//! fell back to per-element processing, and why.\n//!\n//! The predicate is cardinality, the same one the design's kernel-admissibility mapping draws\n//! (draft.md, \"Cardinality is the kernel-admissibility predicate\"): a `Vec` has known extent, so\n//! a `map`/`select` over one compiles to a loop that can be vectorized; a `Stream` is unbounded\n//! and arrives one entry at a time, so its per-element stages are never kernels. The one other\n//! compile-time decision worth reporting is `tir::fusion`: whether the program's `jsonlines` sink\n//! ran as a fused read-one/transform-one/write-one loop or eagerly over an already-materialized\n//! value.\n\nuse crate::tir::{self, Kind, Program, Source, Tir};\nuse crate::ty::Type;\n\n/// The offload report, one line per decision, ending with a newline. The fusion decision first,\n/// then one line per `map`/`select` node in source order.\npub fn explain(program: &Program) -> String {\n    let mut out = String::new();\n    fusion_line(program, &mut out);\n    let mut report = |t: &Tir| match &t.kind {\n        Kind::Map { source, .. } => kernel_line(\"map\", &source.ty, &mut out),\n        Kind::Select { source, .. } => kernel_line(\"select\", &source.ty, &mut out),\n        _ => {}\n    };\n    for f in &program.funcs {\n        tir::each_node(&f.body, &mut report);\n    }\n    tir::each_node(&program.body, &mut report);\n    out\n}\n\n/// The program-level decision `tir::fusion` makes, reported as the first line: whether the sink\n/// ran as a fused loop, and when it did not, what the argument's type says instead.\nfn fusion_line(program: &Program, out: &mut String) {\n    if let Some(fusion) = tir::fusion(program) {\n        let source = match fusion.source {\n            Source::Inputs => \"`inputs`\",\n            Source::Lines => \"`lines`\",\n            Source::Range(_) => \"`range`\",\n        };\n        out.push_str(&format!(\n            \"the `jsonlines` pipeline fused into a read-one/transform-one/write-one loop over {source}\\n\"\n        ));\n    } else {\n        // `jsonlines` takes a Vec or a stream (checked); a stream argument always fuses above, so\n        // what is left here is the materialized case.\n        let Kind::Builtin {\n            which: tir::Builtin::JsonLines,\n            arg,\n        } = &program.body.kind","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":50,"totalLines":87,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/offload.rs","time":{"start":1788464731261,"end":1788464731316}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"OK. has_tail_call has a catch-all `_ => false`, so fine. each_node is exhaustive (needs SortBy/MaxBy arms. flatten has catch-all. Good.\n\nNow let me enumerate the exhaustive matches over Kind that need new arms:\n1. `tags.rs` walk (30-106) and tag (109-148)\n2. `check/linearity.rs` stream_uses (31-118), any_node (165-205), calls_in (256-327)\n3. `emit_rs.rs` expr (1175...); also line 795? Let me check 795. And 812 (Kind::Call in a match? — that was tail_stmts maybe with catch-all?.\n4. `emit_py.rs` expr (423...\n5. `emit_js.rs` expr (580?, 706, 789? — some may be show()/printer functions with catch-all. Need to check each.\n\n6. `emit_lua.rs` expr (672, 800...\n7. `emit_jq.rs` expr (193?,362,479...— some are different functions (callees?Each has its own match. Need new arms wherever exhaustive over Kind.\n8. `emit_go.rs` expr (696,1027...— plus others.\n\n9. `emit_llvm.rs` expr (1248...— exhaustive match over Kind (need new arms. And the Runtime/helpers.\n10. `check/mod.rs` 682 — match t.kind over Expr? Let me check that one. Actually check/mod.rs:682 match t.kind — could be over tir Kind in some reorder pass. Let me look.\n\n11. `offload.rs` 20 — match &t.kind with closures— let me check.\n\nLet me look at offload and check/mod:682.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068ce147b001GyJgbCF13kvdKq","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","messageID":"msg_068ce00d6001DrznPPgP293efR"}}
