{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788464754329,"sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_f747bf3e12f84c43a72a47f8","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/tests/euler_real_data.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/tests/euler_real_data.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! The opt-in check that verifies Euler 8, 11, 13 and 18 against real puzzle data.\n2: //!\n3: //! Each problem reads a blob of problem-given data that cannot live in this repo (#39), so the\n4: //! docs pages run the programs below on small synthetic data instead and point here for the\n5: //! real-sized check. This is where the published answers are actually confirmed: the same\n6: //! programs, run against a contributor's own copies of the real texts, failing loudly on a wrong\n7: //! answer rather than skipping.\n8: //!\n9: //! `#[ignore]` keeps it out of `just test`; `just euler-data DIR` runs it with DIR holding your\n10: //! own copies of the four raw data texts, copied from projecteuler.net:\n11: //!\n12: //! - `euler08.txt`: the thousand-digit number, whitespace allowed.\n13: //! - `euler11.txt`: twenty lines, twenty integers each.\n14: //! - `euler13.txt`: a hundred lines, fifty digits each.\n15: //! - `euler18.txt`: fifteen lines, line `i` holding `i + 1` integers.\n16: \n17: mod support;\n18: \n19: use std::env;\n20: use std::path::Path;\n21: \n22: use serde_json::{Value, json};\n23: \n24: const PROGRAM_8: &str = r#\"\n25: fn window(p: {v: Vec<Int>, i: Int, k: Int}) -> Int64 =\n26:     p | .k == 0 -> 1 or i64(p.v[p.i + p.k - 1]!) * window({v: p.v, i: p.i, k: p.k - 1})\n27: \n28: fn max2(p: {a: Int64, b: Int64}) -> Int64 = p | .a > .b -> p.a or p.b\n29: \n30: fn best(p: {v: Vec<Int>, lo: Int, hi: Int}) -> Int64 =\n31:     p | .hi - .lo == 1 -> window({v: p.v, i: p.lo, k: 13}) or\n32:         max2(\n33:             {\n34:                 a: best({v: p.v, lo: p.lo, hi: (p.lo + p.hi) / 2}),\n35:                 b: best({v: p.v, lo: (p.lo + p.hi) / 2, hi: p.hi})\n36:             }\n37:         )\n38: \n39: best({v: input, lo: 0, hi: length(input) - 12})\n40: \"#;\n41: \n42: const PROGRAM_11: &str = r#\"\n43: fn get(p: {g: Vec<Vec<Int>>, r: Int, c: Int}) -> Int = p.g[p.r]![p.c]!\n44: \n45: fn four(p: {g: Vec<Vec<Int>>, r: Int, c: Int, dr: Int, dc: Int}) -> Int =\n46:     get({g: p.g, r: p.r, c: p.c}) * get({g: p.g, r: p.r + p.dr, c: p.c + p.dc}) *\n47:         get({g: p.g, r: p.r + 2 * p.dr, c: p.c + 2 * p.dc}) *\n48:         get({g: p.g, r: p.r + 3 * p.dr, c: p.c + 3 * p.dc})\n49: \n50: fn row_products(p: {g: Vec<Vec<Int>>, r: Int, dr: Int, dc: Int, cmin: Int, cmax: Int}) -> Vec<Int> =\n51:     collect(range(p.cmax))\n52:         | select(. >= p.cmin)\n53:         | map(four({g: p.g, r: p.r, c: ., dr: p.dr, dc: p.dc}))\n54: \n55: fn direction(p: {g: Vec<Vec<Int>>, dr: Int, dc: Int, rmax: Int, cmin: Int, cmax: Int}) -> Vec<Int> =\n56:     flatten(\n57:         collect(range(p.rmax))\n58:             | map(\n59:                   row_products(\n60:                       {\n61:                           g: p.g,\n62:                           r: .,\n63:                           dr: p.dr,\n64:                           dc: p.dc,\n65:                           cmin: p.cmin,\n66:                           cmax: p.cmax\n67:                       }\n68:                   )\n69:               )\n70:     )\n71: \n72: fn maximum_of(p: {v: Vec<Int>, i: Int, best: Int}) -> Int =\n73:     p | .i >= length(.v) -> p.best or\n74:         maximum_of(\n75:             {\n76:                 v: p.v,\n77:                 i: p.i + 1,\n78:                 best: p | .v[.i]! > .best -> p.v[p.i]! or p.best\n79:             }\n80:         )\n81: \n82: fn maximum(v: Vec<Int>) -> Int = maximum_of({v: v, i: 1, best: v[0]!})\n83: \n84: fn largest_product(g: Vec<Vec<Int>>) -> Int =\n85:     maximum(\n86:         direction(\n87:             {\n88:                 g: g,\n89:                 dr: 0,\n90:                 dc: 1,\n91:                 rmax: length(g),\n92:                 cmin: 0,\n93:                 cmax: length(g[0]!) - 3\n94:             }\n95:         ) +\n96:             direction(\n97:                 {\n98:                     g: g,\n99:                     dr: 1,\n100:                     dc: 0,\n101:                     rmax: length(g) - 3,\n102:                     cmin: 0,\n103:                     cmax: length(g[0]!)\n104:                 }\n105:             ) +\n106:             direction(\n107:                 {\n108:                     g: g,\n109:                     dr: 1,\n110:                     dc: 1,\n111:                     rmax: length(g) - 3,\n112:                     cmin: 0,\n113:                     cmax: length(g[0]!) - 3\n114:                 }\n115:             ) +\n116:             direction(\n117:                 {\n118:                     g: g,\n119:                     dr: 1,\n120:                     dc: -1,\n121:                     rmax: length(g) - 3,\n122:                     cmin: 3,\n123:                     cmax: length(g[0]!)\n124:                 }\n125:             )\n126:     )\n127: \n128: largest_product(input)\n129: \"#;\n130: \n131: const PROGRAM_13: &str = r#\"\n132: fn empty() -> Vec<Int> = []\n133: \n134: fn col_sum(p: {nums: Vec<Vec<Int>>, i: Int, k: Int}) -> Int =\n135:     p | .i >= length(.nums) -> 0 or\n136:         p.nums[p.i]![p.k]! + col_sum({nums: p.nums, i: p.i + 1, k: p.k})\n137: \n138: fn column_total(p: {nums: Vec<Vec<Int>>, k: Int, carry: Int}) -> Int =\n139:     col_sum({nums: p.nums, i: 0, k: p.k}) + p.carry\n140: \n141: fn emit_carry(p: {carry: Int, acc: Vec<Int>}) -> Vec<Int> =\n142:     p | .carry == 0 -> p.acc or\n143:         emit_carry({carry: p.carry / 10, acc: [p.carry % 10] + p.acc})\n144: \n145: fn add_digits(p: {nums: Vec<Vec<Int>>, k: Int, carry: Int, acc: Vec<Int>}) -> Vec<Int> =\n146:     p | .k < 0 -> emit_carry({carry: p.carry, acc: p.acc}) or\n147:         add_digits(\n148:             {\n149:                 nums: p.nums,\n150:                 k: p.k - 1,\n151:                 carry: column_total({nums: p.nums, k: p.k, carry: p.carry}) / 10,\n152:                 acc: [column_total({nums: p.nums, k: p.k, carry: p.carry}) % 10] +\n153:                     p.acc\n154:             }\n155:         )\n156: \n157: fn first_ten(v: Vec<Int>) -> Vec<Int> = collect(range(10)) | map(v[.]!)\n158: \n159: fn leading_digits(nums: Vec<Vec<Int>>) -> Vec<Int> =\n160:     first_ten(\n161:         add_digits(\n162:             {nums: nums, k: length(nums[0]!) - 1, carry: 0, acc: empty()}\n163:         )\n164:     )\n165: \n166: leading_digits(input)\n167: \"#;\n168: \n169: const PROGRAM_18: &str = r#\"\n170: fn combine(p: {row: Vec<Int>, below: Vec<Int>, i: Int}) -> Int =\n171:     p.row[p.i]! +\n172:         (\n173:             p | .below[.i]! > .below[.i + 1]! -> p.below[p.i]! or p.below[p.i + 1]!\n174:         )\n175: \n176: fn merge_row(p: {row: Vec<Int>, below: Vec<Int>}) -> Vec<Int> =\n177:     collect(range(length(p.row))) | map(combine({row: p.row, below: p.below, i: .}))\n178: \n179: fn collapse(p: {rows: Vec<Vec<Int>>, i: Int, acc: Vec<Int>}) -> Int =\n180:     p | .i < 0 -> p.acc[0]! or\n181:         collapse(\n182:             {\n183:                 rows: p.rows,\n184:                 i: p.i - 1,\n185:                 acc: merge_row({row: p.rows[p.i]!, below: p.acc})\n186:             }\n187:         )\n188: \n189: fn triangle_max(rows: Vec<Vec<Int>>) -> Int =\n190:     collapse({rows: rows, i: length(rows) - 2, acc: rows[length(rows) - 1]!})\n191: \n192: triangle_max(input)\n193: \"#;\n194: \n195: /// A number split into its digits, one JSON integer per digit.\n196: fn digits(text: &str, what: &str) -> Value {\n197:     Value::Array(\n198:         text.chars()\n199:             .filter(|c| c.is_ascii_digit())\n200:             .map(|c| {\n201:                 json!(i64::from(\n202:                     c.to_digit(10)\n203:                         .unwrap_or_else(|| panic!(\"{what}: not a digit: {c:?}\"))\n204:                 ))\n205:             })\n206:             .collect(),\n207:     )\n208: }\n209: \n210: /// Each line split into its digits, one JSON integer per digit.\n211: fn digit_rows(text: &str, what: &str) -> Value {\n212:     Value::Array(\n213:         text.lines()\n214:             .filter(|l| !l.trim().is_empty())\n215:             .map(|line| {\n216:                 Value::Array(\n217:                     line.trim()\n218:                         .chars()\n219:                         .map(|c| {\n220:                             json!(i64::from(\n221:                                 c.to_digit(10)\n222:                                     .unwrap_or_else(|| panic!(\"{what}: not a digit: {c:?}\"))\n223:                             ))\n224:                         })\n225:                         .collect(),\n226:                 )\n227:             })\n228:             .collect(),\n229:     )\n230: }\n231: \n232: /// Each line split into whitespace-separated integers.\n233: fn int_rows(text: &str, what: &str) -> Value {\n234:     Value::Array(\n235:         text.lines()\n236:             .filter(|l| !l.trim().is_empty())\n237:             .map(|line| {\n238:                 Value::Array(\n239:                     line.split_whitespace()\n240:                         .map(|tok| {\n241:                             json!(\n242:                                 tok.parse::<i64>()\n243:                                     .unwrap_or_else(|_| panic!(\"{what}: not an integer: {tok:?}\"))\n244:                             )\n245:                         })\n246:                         .collect(),\n247:                 )\n248:             })\n249:             .collect(),\n250:     )\n251: }\n252: \n253: /// One problem's fixed program, data file name, parser, and published answer.\n254: type Case = (\n255:     &'static str,\n256:     &'static str,\n257:     &'static str,\n258:     fn(&str, &str) -> Value,\n259:     &'static str,\n260: );\n261: \n262: /// Runs the four Euler programs against the contributor's own copies of the real puzzle data and\n263: /// checks every backend against the published answer. It fails loudly rather than skipping: an\n264: /// unset `EULER_DATA`, a missing or unparseable data file, or a wrong answer all turn red.\n265: #[test]\n266: #[ignore]\n267: fn euler_real_data() {\n268:     let dir = env::var(\"EULER_DATA\").unwrap_or_else(|_| {\n269:         panic!(\n270:             \"EULER_DATA is unset: point it at your own copies of the Euler 8/11/13/18 data \\\n271:              (see the module docs for the file names) and run `just euler-data`\"\n272:         )\n273:     });\n274:     let dir = Path::new(&dir);\n275: \n276:     let cases: [Case; 4] = [\n277:         (\"8\", PROGRAM_8, \"euler08.txt\", digits, \"23514624000\"),\n278:         (\"11\", PROGRAM_11, \"euler11.txt\", int_rows, \"70600674\"),\n279:         (\n280:             \"13\",\n281:             PROGRAM_13,\n282:             \"euler13.txt\",\n283:             digit_rows,\n284:             \"[5,5,3,7,3,7,6,2,3,0]\",\n285:         ),\n286:         (\"18\", PROGRAM_18, \"euler18.txt\", int_rows, \"1074\"),\n287:     ];\n288: \n289:     for (problem, program, file, parse, want) in cases {\n290:         let path = dir.join(file);\n291:         let text = std::fs::read_to_string(&path).unwrap_or_else(|e| {\n292:             panic!(\n293:                 \"{file}: cannot read the Euler {problem} data from {}: {e}\",\n294:                 path.display()\n295:             )\n296:         });\n297:         let input = parse(&text, file).to_string();\n298:         let failures = support::agreement_failures(\n299:             &format!(\"euler/{problem}\"),\n300:             program,\n301:             Some(&input),\n302:             &support::Expect::Output(format!(\"{want}\\n\")),\n303:         );\n304:         assert!(failures.is_empty(), \"{file}: {}\", failures.join(\"\\n\"));\n305:     }\n306: }\n\n(End of file - total 306 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! The opt-in check that verifies Euler 8, 11, 13 and 18 against real puzzle data.\n//!\n//! Each problem reads a blob of problem-given data that cannot live in this repo (#39), so the\n//! docs pages run the programs below on small synthetic data instead and point here for the\n//! real-sized check. This is where the published answers are actually confirmed: the same\n//! programs, run against a contributor's own copies of the real texts, failing loudly on a wrong\n//! answer rather than skipping.\n//!\n//! `#[ignore]` keeps it out of `just test`; `just euler-data DIR` runs it with DIR holding your\n//! own copies of the four raw data texts, copied from projecteuler.net:\n//!\n//! - `euler08.txt`: the thousand-digit number, whitespace allowed.\n//! - `euler11.txt`: twenty lines, twenty integers each.\n//! - `euler13.txt`: a hundred lines, fifty digits each.\n//! - `euler18.txt`: fifteen lines, line `i` holding `i + 1` integers.\n\nmod support;\n\nuse std::env;\nuse std::path::Path;","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-177/tests/euler_real_data.rs","text":"//! The opt-in check that verifies Euler 8, 11, 13 and 18 against real puzzle data.\n//!\n//! Each problem reads a blob of problem-given data that cannot live in this repo (#39), so the\n//! docs pages run the programs below on small synthetic data instead and point here for the\n//! real-sized check. This is where the published answers are actually confirmed: the same\n//! programs, run against a contributor's own copies of the real texts, failing loudly on a wrong\n//! answer rather than skipping.\n//!\n//! `#[ignore]` keeps it out of `just test`; `just euler-data DIR` runs it with DIR holding your\n//! own copies of the four raw data texts, copied from projecteuler.net:\n//!\n//! - `euler08.txt`: the thousand-digit number, whitespace allowed.\n//! - `euler11.txt`: twenty lines, twenty integers each.\n//! - `euler13.txt`: a hundred lines, fifty digits each.\n//! - `euler18.txt`: fifteen lines, line `i` holding `i + 1` integers.\n\nmod support;\n\nuse std::env;\nuse std::path::Path;\n\nuse serde_json::{Value, json};\n\nconst PROGRAM_8: &str = r#\"\nfn window(p: {v: Vec<Int>, i: Int, k: Int}) -> Int64 =\n    p | .k == 0 -> 1 or i64(p.v[p.i + p.k - 1]!) * window({v: p.v, i: p.i, k: p.k - 1})\n\nfn max2(p: {a: Int64, b: Int64}) -> Int64 = p | .a > .b -> p.a or p.b\n\nfn best(p: {v: Vec<Int>, lo: Int, hi: Int}) -> Int64 =\n    p | .hi - .lo == 1 -> window({v: p.v, i: p.lo, k: 13}) or\n        max2(\n            {\n                a: best({v: p.v, lo: p.lo, hi: (p.lo + p.hi) / 2}),\n                b: best({v: p.v, lo: (p.lo + p.hi) / 2, hi: p.hi})\n            }\n        )\n\nbest({v: input, lo: 0, hi: length(input) - 12})\n\"#;\n\nconst PROGRAM_11: &str = r#\"\nfn get(p: {g: Vec<Vec<Int>>, r: Int, c: Int}) -> Int = p.g[p.r]![p.c]!\n\nfn four(p: {g: Vec<Vec<Int>>, r: Int, c: Int, dr: Int, dc: Int}) -> Int =\n    get({g: p.g, r: p.r, c: p.c}) * get({g: p.g, r: p.r + p.dr, c: p.c + p.dc}) *\n        get({g: p.g, r: p.r + 2 * p.dr, c: p.c + 2 * p.dc}) *\n        get({g: p.g, r: p.r + 3 * p.dr, c: p.c + 3 * p.dc})\n\nfn row_products(p: {g: Vec<Vec<Int>>, r: Int, dr: Int, dc: Int, cmin: Int, cmax: Int}) -> Vec<Int> =\n    collect(range(p.cmax))\n        | select(. >= p.cmin)\n        | map(four({g: p.g, r: p.r, c: ., dr: p.dr, dc: p.dc}))\n\nfn direction(p: {g: Vec<Vec<Int>>, dr: Int, dc: Int, rmax: Int, cmin: Int, cmax: Int}) -> Vec<Int> =\n    flatten(\n        collect(range(p.rmax))\n            | map(\n                  row_products(\n                      {\n                          g: p.g,\n                          r: .,\n                          dr: p.dr,\n                          dc: p.dc,\n                          cmin: p.cmin,\n                          cmax: p.cmax\n                      }\n                  )\n              )\n    )\n\nfn maximum_of(p: {v: Vec<Int>, i: Int, best: Int}) -> Int =\n    p | .i >= length(.v) -> p.best or\n        maximum_of(\n            {\n                v: p.v,\n                i: p.i + 1,\n                best: p | .v[.i]! > .best -> p.v[p.i]! or p.best\n            }\n        )\n\nfn maximum(v: Vec<Int>) -> Int = maximum_of({v: v, i: 1, best: v[0]!})\n\nfn largest_product(g: Vec<Vec<Int>>) -> Int =\n    maximum(\n        direction(\n            {\n                g: g,\n                dr: 0,\n                dc: 1,\n                rmax: length(g),\n                cmin: 0,\n                cmax: length(g[0]!) - 3\n            }\n        ) +\n            direction(\n                {\n                    g: g,\n                    dr: 1,\n                    dc: 0,\n                    rmax: length(g) - 3,\n                    cmin: 0,\n                    cmax: length(g[0]!)\n                }\n            ) +\n            direction(\n                {\n                    g: g,\n                    dr: 1,\n                    dc: 1,\n                    rmax: length(g) - 3,\n                    cmin: 0,\n                    cmax: length(g[0]!) - 3\n                }\n            ) +\n            direction(\n                {\n                    g: g,\n                    dr: 1,\n                    dc: -1,\n                    rmax: length(g) - 3,\n                    cmin: 3,\n                    cmax: length(g[0]!)\n                }\n            )\n    )\n\nlargest_product(input)\n\"#;\n\nconst PROGRAM_13: &str = r#\"\nfn empty() -> Vec<Int> = []\n\nfn col_sum(p: {nums: Vec<Vec<Int>>, i: Int, k: Int}) -> Int =\n    p | .i >= length(.nums) -> 0 or\n        p.nums[p.i]![p.k]! + col_sum({nums: p.nums, i: p.i + 1, k: p.k})\n\nfn column_total(p: {nums: Vec<Vec<Int>>, k: Int, carry: Int}) -> Int =\n    col_sum({nums: p.nums, i: 0, k: p.k}) + p.carry\n\nfn emit_carry(p: {carry: Int, acc: Vec<Int>}) -> Vec<Int> =\n    p | .carry == 0 -> p.acc or\n        emit_carry({carry: p.carry / 10, acc: [p.carry % 10] + p.acc})\n\nfn add_digits(p: {nums: Vec<Vec<Int>>, k: Int, carry: Int, acc: Vec<Int>}) -> Vec<Int> =\n    p | .k < 0 -> emit_carry({carry: p.carry, acc: p.acc}) or\n        add_digits(\n            {\n                nums: p.nums,\n                k: p.k - 1,\n                carry: column_total({nums: p.nums, k: p.k, carry: p.carry}) / 10,\n                acc: [column_total({nums: p.nums, k: p.k, carry: p.carry}) % 10] +\n                    p.acc\n            }\n        )\n\nfn first_ten(v: Vec<Int>) -> Vec<Int> = collect(range(10)) | map(v[.]!)\n\nfn leading_digits(nums: Vec<Vec<Int>>) -> Vec<Int> =\n    first_ten(\n        add_digits(\n            {nums: nums, k: length(nums[0]!) - 1, carry: 0, acc: empty()}\n        )\n    )\n\nleading_digits(input)\n\"#;\n\nconst PROGRAM_18: &str = r#\"\nfn combine(p: {row: Vec<Int>, below: Vec<Int>, i: Int}) -> Int =\n    p.row[p.i]! +\n        (\n            p | .below[.i]! > .below[.i + 1]! -> p.below[p.i]! or p.below[p.i + 1]!\n        )\n\nfn merge_row(p: {row: Vec<Int>, below: Vec<Int>}) -> Vec<Int> =\n    collect(range(length(p.row))) | map(combine({row: p.row, below: p.below, i: .}))\n\nfn collapse(p: {rows: Vec<Vec<Int>>, i: Int, acc: Vec<Int>}) -> Int =\n    p | .i < 0 -> p.acc[0]! or\n        collapse(\n            {\n                rows: p.rows,\n                i: p.i - 1,\n                acc: merge_row({row: p.rows[p.i]!, below: p.acc})\n            }\n        )\n\nfn triangle_max(rows: Vec<Vec<Int>>) -> Int =\n    collapse({rows: rows, i: length(rows) - 2, acc: rows[length(rows) - 1]!})\n\ntriangle_max(input)\n\"#;\n\n/// A number split into its digits, one JSON integer per digit.\nfn digits(text: &str, what: &str) -> Value {\n    Value::Array(\n        text.chars()\n            .filter(|c| c.is_ascii_digit())\n            .map(|c| {\n                json!(i64::from(\n                    c.to_digit(10)\n                        .unwrap_or_else(|| panic!(\"{what}: not a digit: {c:?}\"))\n                ))\n            })\n            .collect(),\n    )\n}\n\n/// Each line split into its digits, one JSON integer per digit.\nfn digit_rows(text: &str, what: &str) -> Value {\n    Value::Array(\n        text.lines()\n            .filter(|l| !l.trim().is_empty())\n            .map(|line| {\n                Value::Array(\n                    line.trim()\n                        .chars()\n                        .map(|c| {\n                            json!(i64::from(\n                                c.to_digit(10)\n                                    .unwrap_or_else(|| panic!(\"{what}: not a digit: {c:?}\"))\n                            ))\n                        })\n                        .collect(),\n                )\n            })\n            .collect(),\n    )\n}\n\n/// Each line split into whitespace-separated integers.\nfn int_rows(text: &str, what: &str) -> Value {\n    Value::Array(\n        text.lines()\n            .filter(|l| !l.trim().is_empty())\n            .map(|line| {\n                Value::Array(\n                    line.split_whitespace()\n                        .map(|tok| {\n                            json!(\n                                tok.parse::<i64>()\n                                    .unwrap_or_else(|_| panic!(\"{what}: not an integer: {tok:?}\"))\n                            )\n                        })\n                        .collect(),\n                )\n            })\n            .collect(),\n    )\n}\n\n/// One problem's fixed program, data file name, parser, and published answer.\ntype Case = (\n    &'static str,\n    &'static str,\n    &'static str,\n    fn(&str, &str) -> Value,\n    &'static str,\n);\n\n/// Runs the four Euler programs against the contributor's own copies of the real puzzle data and\n/// checks every backend against the published answer. It fails loudly rather than skipping: an\n/// unset `EULER_DATA`, a missing or unparseable data file, or a wrong answer all turn red.\n#[test]\n#[ignore]\nfn euler_real_data() {\n    let dir = env::var(\"EULER_DATA\").unwrap_or_else(|_| {\n        panic!(\n            \"EULER_DATA is unset: point it at your own copies of the Euler 8/11/13/18 data \\\n             (see the module docs for the file names) and run `just euler-data`\"\n        )\n    });\n    let dir = Path::new(&dir);\n\n    let cases: [Case; 4] = [\n        (\"8\", PROGRAM_8, \"euler08.txt\", digits, \"23514624000\"),\n        (\"11\", PROGRAM_11, \"euler11.txt\", int_rows, \"70600674\"),\n        (\n            \"13\",\n            PROGRAM_13,\n            \"euler13.txt\",\n            digit_rows,\n            \"[5,5,3,7,3,7,6,2,3,0]\",\n        ),\n        (\"18\", PROGRAM_18, \"euler18.txt\", int_rows, \"1074\"),\n    ];\n\n    for (problem, program, file, parse, want) in cases {\n        let path = dir.join(file);\n        let text = std::fs::read_to_string(&path).unwrap_or_else(|e| {\n            panic!(\n                \"{file}: cannot read the Euler {problem} data from {}: {e}\",\n                path.display()\n            )\n        });\n        let input = parse(&text, file).to_string();\n        let failures = support::agreement_failures(\n            &format!(\"euler/{problem}\"),\n            program,\n            Some(&input),\n            &support::Expect::Output(format!(\"{want}\\n\")),\n        );\n        assert!(failures.is_empty(), \"{file}: {}\", failures.join(\"\\n\"));\n    }\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":306,"totalLines":306,"truncated":false}},"title":"tests/euler_real_data.rs","time":{"start":1788464754318,"end":1788464754326}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Good. Docs fragments run on every backend and agree. So my docs pages' examples will be exercised by tests — great verification.\n\nNow — there's also the `docs/examples/euler/26-reciprocal-cycles.md` and `22` which the issue says are unblocked. Should I update those pages? The issue says \"Unblocks docs/examples/euler/26... and docs/examples/euler/22...\". Hmm — \"unblocks\" meaning these pages can now be rewritten to use sort_by/max_by. But 26's page is solved already (with hand-written best_of).. Rewriting it is part of the ergonomics plan (row 7: \"sort_by/max_by -- build, after row 1\"). The \"unblocks\" language in the issue suggests the implementer should also update those pages. But — careful: those pages are in docs/examples/euler/ — and tests/fmt_examples.rs and euler tests may exercise them. Let me check what tests run over docs/examples/euler. \n\nAlso — the brief says: \"Unblocks docs/examples/euler/26-reciprocal-cycles.md (replaces its hand-written best_of comparator)and docs/examples/euler/22-names-scores.md (rank-after-sorting).\" So updating 26 to use max_by is explicitly in scope. And 22 — it's still blocked by the data problem (#129), so its \"unblocking\" may just mean the sort_by ranking part is now possible, but the page stays skipped (until data lands. Hmm. Let me re-read 22: \"Skipped. ... blocked twice over. The names are problem-given data with nowhere to live in this repo... Independently of the data, there is the rank: toylang has no Vec sort\". So sort now exists — the \"rank\" blocker is gone. But the data blocker remains. So 22 stays skipped. I won't rewrite 22 (it has no solution to write. \n\nFor 26: it's a solved page. Updating it to use max_by — replaces best_of/find_best halving recursion? Let me think what the new solution looks like. The problem: find d < 1000 with the longest reciprocal cycle. The natural new solution:\n\n```toylang\nfn strip2... strip5... reduced... cycle_length...\nrange(998) | map({d: . + 2, len: cycle_length(. + 2)}) | max_by(.len) | .d\n```\n\nWait — max_by returns Opt<T> — so `| .d` on an Opt... hmm. The result of max_by is Opt<{d, len}>. `.d` on Opt wouldn't distribute (Field over Opt isn't defined;. So need to unwrap: `... | max_by(.len) | .d!`? Unwrap needs an Opt, `.d!` — field access then unwrap? No — `.d` reads a field of the Opt, which isn't a record. Order: `| .! | .d`? Unwrap the Opt then read .d. Hmm — `max_by(...) | .! | .d`? Let me think: the result type is Opt<{d: Int, len: Int}>. `.` binds to the Opt. `.d` is a field access on Opt — refused. So `.!` first: `max_by(...) | .!` gives {d, len}, then `.d` gives d. So: `... | max_by(.len) | .! | .d`? Hmm — `. !` binds tighter? `.!` unwraps. Actually the chain: `... | max_by(.len) | .! | .d`. Hmm — but wait, can a pipe chain do `| .!`? `.` in the right side is the piped value (Opt. `.!` — Unwrap{base: Subject}. Should work. Then `.d` field read. Let me look at how the docs already write `find_best(...).d` — they use direct field access on the call result. So the new solution could be:\n\n```toylang\nfn best_length(d: Int) -> {d: Int, len: Int} = {d: d, len: cycle_length(d)}\n\ncycle_length(strip5(strip2(...)))... \n```\n\nHmm wait — the current 26 solution uses a halving search to avoid stack depth (cycle walks chunks of 100 digits). With max_by builtin, the reduction costs no stack depth, but `cycle_length` itself still recurses chunk-wise. The page's cycle_length uses cycle_outer (chunked walk. So replacing find_best/best_of with:\n\n```toylang\nrange(998) | map({d: . + 2, len: cycle_length(. + 2)}) | max_by(.len) | .! | .d\n```\n\nBut — `cycle_length(. + 2)` calls a function with `. + 2` (Int — fine. `range(998)` gives 0..997, `.+ 2` gives 2..999. Good. \n\nHmm — but wait: max_by's projection `.len` — `.` is the record {d, len}. Good. Result Opt<{d,len}>. `.!` unwraps → {d, len}. `.d` → d. Output 983. \n\nBut — the `!` on an Opt<Record> in docs... does the printer accept an unwrap at the end? The result type would be Int — prints 983. Good.\n\nActually hold on — let me reconsider whether I should touch the Euler pages at all. The brief explicitly says the issue \"Unblocks docs/examples/euler/26-reciprocal-cycles.md (replaces its hand-written best_of comparator)\". So updating 26 is in scope. I'll do it after the core implementation works.\n\n.\n\nLet me now also check what tests exercise the euler pages (euler_real_data.rs? tests/euler_real_data.rs seems to be a different thing (a test file with real data for some problems?. Let me look at it and how docs/examples/euler pages are tested.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_068ce6e85001lefgN7cL68ZNBa","sessionID":"ses_f97363172ffe9X2xJ1KPM1b5DB","messageID":"msg_068ce3870001BMojXtN8Oe4wQR"}}
